(130, 122, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css basics tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+basics+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+basics+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+basics+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+basics+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+basics+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS</h3><p>Cascading Style Sheets (CSS) is a styling language used to control the layout and appearance of web pages written in HTML or XML. CSS is used to separate the presentation of a document from its structure, making it easier to maintain and update the layout and design of a website.</p><p>With CSS, you can change the font, color, and spacing of text, as well as the size, position, and background of elements on a web page. CSS also allows you to create responsive designs that adapt to different screen sizes and devices.</p><h3>CSS Selectors and Properties</h3><p>CSS selectors are used to target specific elements on a web page, while properties are used to define the styles that are applied to those elements. There are several types of CSS selectors, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Element selectors</strong>: These select elements based on their tag name, such as <code>p</code> or <code>img</code>.</li><li><strong>Class selectors</strong>: These select elements based on their class attribute, such as <code>.header</code> or <code>.footer</code>.</li><li><strong>ID selectors</strong>: These select elements based on their ID attribute, such as <code>#nav</code> or <code>#sidebar</code>.</li></ul><p>CSS properties are used to define the styles that are applied to elements. Some common CSS properties include:</p><ul><li><strong>color</strong>: sets the color of text</li><li><strong>background-color</strong>: sets the background color of an element</li><li><strong>font-size</strong>: sets the size of text</li><li><strong>width</strong> and <strong>height</strong>: set the size of an element</li></ul><h3>Basic CSS Styling</h3><p>Now that we\'ve covered the basics of CSS selectors and properties, let\'s take a look at some basic CSS styling. We\'ll start with a simple example that styles a paragraph of text:</p><p><code>p {</code></p><p><code>  color: blue;</code></p><p><code>  font-size: 18px;</code></p><p><code>  background-color: yellow;</code></p><p><code>}</code></p><p>This code selects all paragraph elements on a web page and applies the following styles:</p><ul><li>sets the text color to blue</li><li>sets the font size to 18 pixels</li><li>sets the background color to yellow</li></ul><p>This is just a small taste of what you can do with CSS. As we continue through this course, we\'ll cover more advanced topics and techniques for styling web pages.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(131, 123, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css layout tutorials</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+layout+tutorials\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+layout+tutorials\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+layout+tutorials/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+layout+tutorials/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+layout+tutorials\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Layout</h3><p>CSS layout is used to control the position and arrangement of elements on a web page. It is a crucial aspect of web development, as it determines how the content is presented to the user. With CSS, you can create complex layouts, including multi-column layouts, flexible boxes, and grids.</p><p>CSS layout is based on the box model, which consists of four main parts: content, padding, border, and margin. Understanding the box model is essential for creating effective layouts.</p><h3>CSS Box Model and Positioning</h3><p>The CSS box model is a fundamental concept in web development. It describes the structure of an HTML element as a rectangular box, with four main parts: content, padding, border, and margin. The <strong>content</strong> area is where the element\'s content is displayed. The <strong>padding</strong> is the space between the content and the border. The <strong>border</strong> is the visible outline of the element. The <strong>margin</strong> is the space between the element and other elements.</p><p>The box model can be controlled using CSS properties such as <code>width</code>, <code>height</code>, <code>padding</code>, <code>border</code>, and <code>margin</code>. For example, to set the width and height of an element, you can use the following code: <code>width: 500px; height: 200px;</code></p><p>Positioning is another important aspect of CSS layout. It determines how an element is positioned within its parent element or the document. There are several positioning schemes, including <strong>static</strong>, <strong>relative</strong>, <strong>absolute</strong>, and <strong>fixed</strong>. Each scheme has its own rules and behaviors.</p><h3>Basic CSS Layout Techniques</h3><p>There are several basic CSS layout techniques that you can use to create effective layouts. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Float</strong>: The float property is used to float an element to the left or right of its parent element. For example: <code>float: left;</code></li><li><strong>Clear</strong>: The clear property is used to clear a floated element. For example: <code>clear: both;</code></li><li><strong>Display</strong>: The display property is used to specify how an element is displayed. For example: <code>display: block;</code></li><li><strong>Position</strong>: The position property is used to specify the positioning scheme of an element. For example: <code>position: relative;</code></li></ul><p>These techniques can be used to create a variety of layouts, including simple layouts, complex layouts, and responsive layouts.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(132, 124, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: web development basics</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=web+development+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/web+development+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/web+development+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/web+development+basics/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=web+development+basics\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>First Week Review and Quiz</h3><p>Welcome to the seventh day of our beginner web development course. In the past six days, we have covered the basics of HTML and CSS. Today, we will review what we have learned so far and introduce you to JavaScript, which is a crucial programming language for web development.</p><p>Let\'s start with a review of HTML. HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language, and it is used to create the structure and content of a web page. Some basic HTML tags include <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>. We use these tags to create headings, paragraphs, images, and links on our web page.</p><ul><li><strong>HTML Structure</strong>: The basic structure of an HTML document includes the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> tag, the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> tag, and the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> tag.</li><li><strong>HTML Tags</strong>: HTML tags are used to define the different elements on a web page. For example, the <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> tag is used to define a heading, while the <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tag is used to define a paragraph.</li></ul><p>Next, let\'s review CSS. CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets, and it is used to control the layout and appearance of a web page. Some basic CSS concepts include selectors, properties, and values. We use these concepts to style our HTML elements and create a visually appealing web page.</p><ul><li><strong>CSS Selectors</strong>: CSS selectors are used to target specific HTML elements on a web page. For example, the <code>h1</code> selector is used to target all <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> elements on a web page.</li><li><strong>CSS Properties and Values</strong>: CSS properties and values are used to style HTML elements. For example, the <code>color</code> property is used to set the color of text, while the <code>background-color</code> property is used to set the background color of an element.</li></ul><p>Now, let\'s introduce you to JavaScript. JavaScript is a programming language that is used to add interactivity to a web page. It is used to create dynamic effects, such as animations and pop-ups, and to respond to user interactions, such as clicks and hover effects.</p><p>For our first week project, we want you to create a simple web page using HTML and CSS. The web page should include a heading, a paragraph, an image, and a link. You should also use CSS to style your HTML elements and create a visually appealing web page.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(133, 125, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: HTML structure diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=HTML+structure+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/HTML+structure+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/HTML+structure+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/HTML+structure+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=HTML+structure+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to HTML Structure</h3><p>HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard markup language used to create web pages. It provides the structure and content of a web page, and is used by web browsers to render the page. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of HTML, including headings and paragraphs, as well as HTML attributes.</p><p>HTML documents are made up of a series of elements, which are represented by tags. Tags are surrounded by angle brackets (<>) and usually come in pairs, with the opening tag preceding the content and the closing tag following the content. For example, the paragraph element is represented by the <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tag.</p><h3>Headings and Paragraphs</h3><p>Headings and paragraphs are two of the most basic elements in HTML. Headings are used to define the title of a page or section, while paragraphs are used to define a block of text. There are six levels of headings in HTML, ranging from <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> (the most important) to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> (the least important). The following is an example of how to use headings and paragraphs in HTML:</p><ul><li><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> - Main heading</li><li><code>&lt;h2&gt;</code> - Subheading</li><li><code>&lt;h3&gt;</code> - Sub-subheading</li><li><code>&lt;p&gt;</code> - Paragraph</li></ul><p>For example: <code>&lt;h1&gt;Main Heading&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph of text.&lt;/p&gt;</code></p><h3>HTML Attributes</h3><p>HTML attributes are used to provide additional information about an element. They are added to the opening tag of an element and consist of a name and a value. For example, the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> element (which is used to create a hyperlink) has an attribute called <code>href</code>, which specifies the URL of the page that the link should go to. The following is an example of how to use attributes in HTML:</p><ul><li><code>href</code> - Specifies the URL of a hyperlink</li><li><code>src</code> - Specifies the URL of an image</li><li><code>title</code> - Specifies the title of a page or element</li></ul><p>For example: <code>&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.example.com&quot;&gt;Visit example.com&lt;/a&gt;</code></p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>HTML is the standard markup language used to create web pages.</li><li>Headings and paragraphs are two of the most basic elements in HTML.</li><li>HTML attributes are used to provide additional information about an element.</li></ul>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(134, 126, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: html lists and links tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=html+lists+and+links+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/html+lists+and+links+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/html+lists+and+links+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/html+lists+and+links+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=html+lists+and+links+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to HTML Lists and Links</h3><p>In this lesson, we will explore the basics of HTML lists and links. HTML lists are used to group a collection of items, while links are used to connect to other web pages or websites.</p><h3>Ordered and Unordered Lists</h3><p>There are two types of lists in HTML: ordered and unordered. Ordered lists are used when the order of the items is important, while unordered lists are used when the order is not important.</p><ul><li><strong>Ordered Lists</strong>: These lists are created using the <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> tag and are used to list items in a specific order. Each item in the list is marked with a number.</li><li><strong>Unordered Lists</strong>: These lists are created using the <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> tag and are used to list items in no particular order. Each item in the list is marked with a bullet point.</li></ul><p>Example of an ordered list:<code>&lt;ol&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item 1&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item 2&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item 3&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ol&gt;</code></p><p>Example of an unordered list:<code>&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item 1&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item 2&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item 3&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;</code></p><h3>Creating Hyperlinks</h3><p>Hyperlinks, or links, are used to connect to other web pages or websites. They are created using the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag.</p><p>The basic syntax of a hyperlink is:<code>&lt;a href=&quot;url&quot;&gt;link text&lt;/a&gt;</code></p><p>Where <code>url</code> is the URL of the page you want to link to, and <code>link text</code> is the text that will be displayed for the link.</p><p>Example:<code>&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.google.com&quot;&gt;Visit Google&lt;/a&gt;</code></p><h3>Linking to External Pages</h3><p>To link to an external page, you simply need to specify the full URL of the page in the <code>href</code> attribute.</p><p>Example:<code>&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.facebook.com&quot;&gt;Visit Facebook&lt;/a&gt;</code></p><p>It\'s also a good practice to include a <code>target</code> attribute to specify where the link should open. For example, to open the link in a new tab, you can use <code>target=&quot;_blank&quot;</code>.</p><p>Example:<code>&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.facebook.com&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;Visit Facebook&lt;/a&gt;</code></p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(135, 127, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css basics tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+basics+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+basics+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+basics+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+basics+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+basics+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Welcome to Introduction to CSS</h3><p>CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets, and it is used to control the layout and appearance of web pages. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of CSS, including selectors and properties, as well as CSS units and values.</p><h3>CSS Basics</h3><p>CSS is used to separate the presentation of a document from its structure. This means that we can use HTML to define the content and structure of a web page, and then use CSS to define the layout, colors, fonts, and other visual aspects of the page.</p><p>To link a CSS file to an HTML document, we use the <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> tag. For example: <code>&lt;link rel=\'stylesheet\' type=\'text/css\' href=\'style.css\'&gt;</code></p><h3>Selectors and Properties</h3><p>In CSS, a selector is used to target a specific element or group of elements on a web page. We can then use properties to define the styles that should be applied to those elements. For example: <code>h1 { color: blue; }</code> This code selects all <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> elements on the page and sets their text color to blue.</p><p>Some common selectors include:</p><ul><li><code>*\"</code> - selects all elements on the page</li><li><code>h1</code> - selects all <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> elements</li><li><code>.class</code> - selects all elements with a class of <code>class</code></li><li><code>#id</code> - selects the element with an id of <code>id</code></li></ul><p>Some common properties include:</p><ul><li><code>color</code> - sets the text color of an element</li><li><code>background-color</code> - sets the background color of an element</li><li><code>font-size</code> - sets the font size of an element</li><li><code>width</code> and <code>height</code> - set the width and height of an element</li></ul><h3>CSS Units and Values</h3><p>In CSS, we use units and values to define the size and other properties of elements. Some common units include:</p><ul><li><code>px</code> - pixels</li><li><code>em</code> - relative to the font size of the element</li><li><code>rem</code> - relative to the font size of the root element</li><li><code>%</code> - percentage</li></ul><p>For example: <code>font-size: 16px;</code> sets the font size to 16 pixels, while <code>width: 50%;</code> sets the width to 50% of the parent element\'s width.</p><p>It\'s <strong>important</strong> to understand how to use units and values correctly in CSS, as this will help you to create web pages that are responsive and look good on different devices and screen sizes.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(136, 128, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css selectors and box model diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+selectors+and+box+model+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+selectors+and+box+model+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+selectors+and+box+model+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+selectors+and+box+model+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+selectors+and+box+model+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Selectors and Box Model</h3><p>Welcome to Day 11 of our web development course. Today, we will be covering CSS selectors and the box model. CSS selectors are used to target specific elements on a web page and apply styles to them. The box model, on the other hand, is a fundamental concept in CSS that helps us understand how elements are laid out on a web page.</p><p>Let\'s start with element selectors. Element selectors are used to target HTML elements based on their tag name. For example, to select all paragraph elements on a web page, you can use the <code>p</code> selector. Here are some examples of element selectors:</p><ul><li><code>h1</code> - selects all heading 1 elements</li><li><code>p</code> - selects all paragraph elements</li><li><code>img</code> - selects all image elements</li><li><code>a</code> - selects all anchor elements</li></ul><p>In addition to element selectors, we also have class and ID selectors. Class selectors are used to target elements based on their class attribute, while ID selectors are used to target elements based on their ID attribute. Here are some examples:</p><ul><li><code>.header</code> - selects all elements with the class <code>header</code></li><li><code>#nav</code> - selects the element with the ID <code>nav</code></li></ul><p>Now, let\'s move on to the box model. The box model is a concept in CSS that describes the structure of an HTML element. It consists of four main parts: the content area, the padding, the border, and the margin. The <strong>content area</strong> is the area where the element\'s content is displayed. The <strong>padding</strong> is the space between the content area and the border. The <strong>border</strong> is the visible outline of the element. The <strong>margin</strong> is the space between the element and other elements on the web page.</p><p>Here is an example of how the box model works:</p><ul><li><code>width</code> and <code>height</code> properties set the size of the content area</li><li><code>padding</code> property sets the size of the padding</li><li><code>border</code> property sets the size and style of the border</li><li><code>margin</code> property sets the size of the margin</li></ul><p>Understanding the box model is crucial for creating well-structured and visually appealing web pages. By using CSS selectors and the box model, you can create complex layouts and designs with ease.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(137, 129, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css layout models</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+layout+models\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+layout+models\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+layout+models/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+layout+models/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+layout+models\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Styling and Layout</h3><p>Welcome to Day 12 of our web development course, where we\'ll be exploring the world of CSS styling and layout. In this lesson, we\'ll cover three essential topics: Colors and Backgrounds, Margins and Padding, and CSS Layout Models.</p><p>First, let\'s dive into <strong>Colors and Backgrounds</strong>. In CSS, you can set the color of an element\'s text using the <code>color</code> property, and the background color using the <code>background-color</code> property. You can also set a background image using the <code>background-image</code> property.</p><ul><li>The <code>color</code> property is used to set the color of an element\'s text.</li><li>The <code>background-color</code> property is used to set the background color of an element.</li><li>The <code>background-image</code> property is used to set a background image for an element.</li></ul><p>Next, let\'s talk about <strong>Margins and Padding</strong>. Margins are the space between an element and its surrounding elements, while padding is the space between an element\'s content and its border. You can set the margin of an element using the <code>margin</code> property, and the padding using the <code>padding</code> property.</p><ul><li>The <code>margin</code> property is used to set the margin of an element.</li><li>The <code>padding</code> property is used to set the padding of an element.</li><li>You can also use shorthand properties, such as <code>margin-top</code>, <code>margin-bottom</code>, <code>padding-left</code>, and <code>padding-right</code>, to set the margin and padding of individual sides.</li></ul><p>Finally, let\'s discuss <strong>CSS Layout Models</strong>. There are several layout models in CSS, including the Box Model, the Flexbox Model, and the Grid Model. The Box Model is the traditional layout model, where elements are laid out in a rectangular box. The Flexbox Model is a more flexible layout model, where elements can be laid out in a flexible container. The Grid Model is a two-dimensional layout model, where elements can be laid out in a grid of rows and columns.</p><ul><li>The <strong>Box Model</strong> is the traditional layout model, where elements are laid out in a rectangular box.</li><li>The <strong>Flexbox Model</strong> is a more flexible layout model, where elements can be laid out in a flexible container.</li><li>The <strong>Grid Model</strong> is a two-dimensional layout model, where elements can be laid out in a grid of rows and columns.</li></ul><p>In conclusion, CSS styling and layout are essential skills for any web developer. By mastering colors and backgrounds, margins and padding, and CSS layout models, you\'ll be able to create visually appealing and user-friendly websites.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(138, 130, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript programming language</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+programming+language\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+programming+language\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+programming+language/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+programming+language/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+programming+language\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to JavaScript</h3><p>JavaScript is a high-level, dynamic, and interpreted programming language that is primarily used for client-side scripting on the web. It allows developers to create interactive web pages, web applications, and mobile applications. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of JavaScript, variables, data types, and basic operators.</p><h3>JavaScript Basics</h3><p>JavaScript is a scripting language that is executed on the client-side, meaning that it runs on the user\'s web browser. It is used to add interactivity to web pages, create animations, and update the content of a web page dynamically. JavaScript is also used in mobile and desktop applications, game development, and server-side programming.</p><p>Some of the key features of JavaScript include:</p><ul><li><strong>Dynamic typing</strong>: JavaScript is dynamically typed, which means that you do not need to declare the data type of a variable before using it.</li><li><strong>First-class functions</strong>: JavaScript functions are first-class citizens, which means that they can be assigned to variables, passed as arguments to other functions, and returned as values from functions.</li><li><strong>Prototype-based inheritance</strong>: JavaScript uses prototype-based inheritance, which allows objects to inherit properties and methods from other objects.</li></ul><h3>Variables and Data Types</h3><p>In JavaScript, a variable is a name given to a value. Variables are used to store and manipulate data in a program. JavaScript has several data types, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Number</strong>: The number data type represents a numeric value, such as 1, 2, or 3. For example: <code>let x = 5;</code></li><li><strong>String</strong>: The string data type represents a sequence of characters, such as \'hello\' or \'goodbye\'. For example: <code>let name = \'John\';</code></li><li><strong>Boolean</strong>: The boolean data type represents a true or false value. For example: <code>let isAdmin = true;</code></li><li><strong>Array</strong>: The array data type represents a collection of values, such as [1, 2, 3] or [\'a\', \'b\', \'c\']. For example: <code>let colors = [\'red\', \'green\', \'blue\'];</code></li><li><strong>Object</strong>: The object data type represents a collection of key-value pairs, such as {name: \'John\', age: 30}. For example: <code>let person = {name: \'John\', age: 30};</code></li></ul><h3>Basic Operators</h3><p>JavaScript has several basic operators that can be used to perform arithmetic, comparison, logical, and assignment operations. Some of the most common operators include:</p><ul><li><strong>Arithmetic operators</strong>: +, -, \\*, /, %, etc. For example: <code>let x = 5; let y = 3; let result = x + y;</code></li><li><strong>Comparison operators</strong>: ==, !=, ===, !==, >, <, >=, <=. For example: <code>let x = 5; let y = 3; let result = x > y;</code></li><li><strong>Logical operators</strong>: &&, ||, !. For example: <code>let x = true; let y = false; let result = x && y;</code></li><li><strong>Assignment operators</strong>: =, +=, -=, \\*=, /=, etc. For example: <code>let x = 5; x += 3;</code></li></ul>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(139, 131, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: javascript functions and control structures</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=javascript+functions+and+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/javascript+functions+and+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/javascript+functions+and+control+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/javascript+functions+and+control+structures/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=javascript+functions+and+control+structures\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript Functions and Control Structures</h3><p>In this lesson, we will cover the basics of JavaScript functions and control structures. Functions are reusable blocks of code that perform a specific task, while control structures determine the flow of your program\'s execution.</p><h3>Functions and Function Calls</h3><p>A function in JavaScript is defined using the <code>function</code> keyword followed by the name of the function and a list of parameters in parentheses. The code to be executed is placed inside the curly brackets.</p><p>For example:</p><code>function greet(name) {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;console.log(\'Hello, \' + name);<br>}</code><p>To call a function, simply use the function name followed by the required parameters in parentheses.</p><code>greet(\'John\');</code><p>This will output <strong>Hello, John</strong> to the console.</p><h3>Conditional Statements</h3><p>Conditional statements are used to execute different blocks of code based on certain conditions. The most common type of conditional statement is the <code>if</code> statement.</p><p>The syntax for an <code>if</code> statement is:</p><code>if (condition) {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;// code to be executed if condition is true<br>}</code><p>For example:</p><code>let age = 25;<br>if (age >= 18) {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;console.log(\'You are an adult.\');<br>}</code><p>This will output <strong>You are an adult.</strong> to the console because the condition <code>age >= 18</code> is true.</p><p>We can also use the <code>else</code> keyword to specify a block of code to be executed if the condition is false.</p><code>let age = 15;<br>if (age >= 18) {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;console.log(\'You are an adult.\');<br>} else {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;console.log(\'You are a minor.\');<br>}</code><p>This will output <strong>You are a minor.</strong> to the console because the condition <code>age >= 18</code> is false.</p><h3>Loops and Iterations</h3><p>Loops are used to execute a block of code repeatedly for a specified number of times. The most common types of loops are <code>for</code> loops, <code>while</code> loops, and <code>do-while</code> loops.</p><p>A <code>for</code> loop is used to iterate over a block of code for a specified number of times. The syntax for a <code>for</code> loop is:</p><code>for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;// code to be executed<br>}</code><p>This will execute the code inside the loop 5 times.</p><p>A <code>while</code> loop is used to execute a block of code as long as a certain condition is true. The syntax for a <code>while</code> loop is:</p><code>let i = 0;<br>while (i < 5) {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;// code to be executed<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;i++;<br>}</code><p>This will also execute the code inside the loop 5 times.</p><p>A <code>do-while</code> loop is similar to a <code>while</code> loop, but it will always execute the code inside the loop at least once. The syntax for a <code>do-while</code> loop is:</p><code>let i = 0;<br>do {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;// code to be executed<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;i++;<br>} while (i < 5);</code><p>This will also execute the code inside the loop 5 times.</p><ul><li>Functions are reusable blocks of code that perform a specific task.</li><li>Conditional statements determine which block of code to execute based on certain conditions.</li><li>Loops are used to execute a block of code repeatedly for a specified number of times.</li></ul>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(140, 131, 'quiz', '29', 2, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(141, 132, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: css selectors tutorial</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=css+selectors+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/css+selectors+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/css+selectors+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/css+selectors+tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=css+selectors+tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to CSS Selectors</h3><p>CSS selectors are used to target and style specific elements on a web page. They are a fundamental part of CSS and are used to apply styles, layouts, and behaviors to HTML elements. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of CSS selectors, the different types of selectors, and best practices for using them.</p><p>CSS selectors can be used to target elements based on their type, class, ID, attributes, and more. They are used in CSS rules to specify which elements to apply the styles to. For example, the selector <code>p</code> would target all paragraph elements on a web page.</p><h3>Selector Types</h3><p>There are several types of CSS selectors, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Element Selectors</strong>: These selectors target elements based on their type. For example, <code>p</code> would target all paragraph elements, while <code>img</code> would target all image elements.</li><li><strong>Class Selectors</strong>: These selectors target elements based on their class attribute. For example, <code>.header</code> would target all elements with the class <code>header</code>.</li><li><strong>ID Selectors</strong>: These selectors target elements based on their ID attribute. For example, <code>#header</code> would target the element with the ID <code>header</code>.</li><li><strong>Attribute Selectors</strong>: These selectors target elements based on their attributes. For example, <code>input[type=\'text\']</code> would target all input elements with the type attribute set to <code>text</code>.</li></ul><p>These are just a few examples of the many types of CSS selectors available. By combining different selectors, you can target specific elements on a web page and apply styles, layouts, and behaviors to them.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><p>When using CSS selectors, there are several best practices to keep in mind:</p><ul><li><strong>Use specific selectors</strong>: Instead of using general selectors like <code>p</code> or <code>div</code>, use more specific selectors like <code>.header p</code> or <code>#nav div</code>.</li><li><strong>Avoid overusing ID selectors</strong>: ID selectors are very specific and can make your CSS rules harder to override. Instead, use class selectors or attribute selectors when possible.</li><li><strong>Use a preprocessor like Sass or Less</strong>: These preprocessors can help you write more efficient and modular CSS code, and can also help you avoid errors and inconsistencies.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices and using CSS selectors effectively, you can write more efficient, modular, and maintainable CSS code.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56');
