(165, 153, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: node.js express.js restful api</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=node.js+express.js+restful+api\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/node.js+express.js+restful+api\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/node.js+express.js+restful+api/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/node.js+express.js+restful+api/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=node.js+express.js+restful+api\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Backend Development</h3><p>Welcome to Day 36 of our web development course, where we\'ll be exploring the world of backend development. In this lesson, we\'ll introduce you to Node.js, Express.js, and RESTful APIs, which are essential tools for building robust and scalable server-side applications.</p><p>First, let\'s start with <strong>Node.js</strong>, a JavaScript runtime environment that allows you to run JavaScript on the server-side. Node.js provides an event-driven, non-blocking I/O model, which makes it lightweight and efficient. With Node.js, you can build fast and scalable network applications, and it\'s widely used in production environments.</p><h3>What is Express.js?</h3><p><strong>Express.js</strong> is a popular Node.js web framework that provides a flexible and modular way to build web applications. It allows you to handle HTTP requests and responses, and provides a robust set of features for building web servers. Express.js is often used to build RESTful APIs, web applications, and microservices.</p><p>Some key features of Express.js include:</p><ul><li>Request and response objects</li><li>Middleware functions</li><li>Routing and URL handling</li><li>Template engine support</li></ul><h3>What are RESTful APIs?</h3><p><strong>RESTful APIs</strong> (Representational State of Resource) are an architectural style for designing networked applications. They\'re based on the idea of resources, which are identified by URIs, and can be manipulated using a fixed set of operations. RESTful APIs are widely used in web development, and provide a simple and consistent way to interact with web services.</p><p>Some key characteristics of RESTful APIs include:</p><ul><li>Resource-based architecture</li><li>Client-server architecture</li><li>Stateless communication</li><li>Cacheable data</li><li>Uniform interface</li></ul><p>Here\'s an example of how you might use Node.js and Express.js to create a simple RESTful API:</p><code>const express = require(\'express\');<br>const app = express();<br><br>app.get(\'/users\', (req, res) => {<br>  res.json([{ name: \'John Doe\', age: 30 }, { name: \'Jane Doe\', age: 25 }]);<br>});<br><br>app.listen(3000, () => {<br>  console.log(\'Server listening on port 3000\');<br>});</code><p>In this example, we\'re using Express.js to create a web server that listens on port 3000, and responds to GET requests to the \'/users\' endpoint with a JSON array of user objects.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56');
