(176, 164, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: material ui custom components</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=material+ui+custom+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/material+ui+custom+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/material+ui+custom+components/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/material+ui+custom+components/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=material+ui+custom+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Building Custom Components with Material-UI</h3><p>Material-UI is a popular React UI framework that provides a wide range of pre-built components. However, in many cases, you may need to create custom components to fit your specific requirements. In this lesson, we will explore how to build custom components with Material-UI, override existing components, and customize the theme.</p><h3>Custom Components</h3><p>To create a custom component with Material-UI, you can use the <code>styled</code> function from the <code>@material-ui/core</code> package. This function allows you to create a new component with a custom style.</p><ul><li>First, import the necessary packages: <code>import { styled } from \'@material-ui/core\';</code></li><li>Then, create a new component using the <code>styled</code> function: <code>const MyButton = styled.button`background-color: #333; color: #fff; padding: 10px 20px;`;</code></li><li>Finally, use the custom component in your React application: <code><MyButton>Click me</MyButton></code></li></ul><h3>Overrides</h3><p>Material-UI provides a way to override the styles of existing components using the <code>makeStyles</code> function. This function allows you to create a custom style for a specific component.</p><ul><li>First, import the necessary packages: <code>import { makeStyles } from \'@material-ui/core\';</code></li><li>Then, create a new style using the <code>makeStyles</code> function: <code>const useStyles = makeStyles((theme) => ({ root: { backgroundColor: \'#333\', color: \'#fff\', padding: \'10px 20px\', }, }));</code></li><li>Finally, use the custom style in your React application: <code>const classes = useStyles(); return <Button className={classes.root}>Click me</Button>;</code></li></ul><h3>Theme Customization</h3><p>Material-UI provides a way to customize the theme of your application using the <code>createMuiTheme</code> function. This function allows you to create a custom theme with your own settings.</p><ul><li>First, import the necessary packages: <code>import { createMuiTheme } from \'@material-ui/core\';</code></li><li>Then, create a new theme using the <code>createMuiTheme</code> function: <code>const theme = createMuiTheme({ palette: { primary: { main: \'#333\', }, }, });</code></li><li>Finally, use the custom theme in your React application: <code>const ThemeProvider = createMuiTheme(theme); return <ThemeProvider><App /></ThemeProvider>;</code></li></ul><p>By using these techniques, you can create custom components, override existing components, and customize the theme of your Material-UI application. This will allow you to create a unique and consistent design for your application.</p><p><strong>Best practices:</strong></p><ul><li>Use the <code>styled</code> function to create custom components.</li><li>Use the <code>makeStyles</code> function to override existing components.</li><li>Use the <code>createMuiTheme</code> function to customize the theme.</li></ul>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(177, 165, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: tailwind css logo</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=tailwind+css+logo\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/tailwind+css+logo\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/tailwind+css+logo/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/tailwind+css+logo/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=tailwind+css+logo\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Tailwind CSS</h3><p>Tailwind CSS is a popular utility-first CSS framework that allows developers to write more concise and maintainable CSS code. In this lesson, we will explore the core concepts of Tailwind CSS and how to get started with it.</p><h3>Utility-first Approach</h3><p>The utility-first approach is a paradigm shift from traditional CSS frameworks that focus on pre-designed components. Instead, Tailwind CSS provides a set of low-level utility classes that can be combined to create custom components. This approach gives developers more flexibility and control over their CSS code.</p><p>For example, instead of using a pre-designed <code>button</code> class, you can use utility classes like <code>bg-blue-500</code>, <code>hover:bg-blue-700</code>, and <code>text-white</code> to create a custom button component.</p><ul><li><strong>Advantages of utility-first approach:</strong></li><li>More flexibility and control over CSS code</li><li>Faster development and prototyping</li><li>Easier maintenance and debugging</li></ul><h3>Configuring Tailwind</h3><p>To get started with Tailwind CSS, you need to configure it in your project. This involves installing the necessary dependencies and creating a configuration file. You can install Tailwind CSS using npm or yarn by running the command <code>npm install tailwindcss</code> or <code>yarn add tailwindcss</code>.</p><p>Once installed, you need to create a configuration file called <code>tailwind.config.js</code> to customize the framework. This file allows you to configure various settings, such as colors, spacing, and typography.</p><p>For example, you can customize the color palette by adding the following code to your <code>tailwind.config.js</code> file:</p><code>module.exports = {<br>  theme: {<br>    colors: {<br>      primary: \'#3498db\',<br>      secondary: \'#f1c40f\',<br>    },<br>  },<br>};</code><h3>Customizing Components</h3><p>One of the key benefits of Tailwind CSS is the ability to customize components using utility classes. You can create custom components by combining different utility classes to achieve the desired design.</p><p>For example, you can create a custom <code>card</code> component by using the following utility classes:</p><code>&lt;div class=&quot;max-w-sm mx-auto bg-white rounded-xl shadow-md p-4&quot;&gt;<br>  &lt;h2 class=&quot;text-2xl font-bold mb-2&quot;&gt;Card Title&lt;/h2&gt;<br>  &lt;p class=&quot;text-gray-600&quot;&gt;Card content&lt;/p&gt;<br>&lt;/div&gt;</code><p>This code creates a custom card component with a maximum width, white background, rounded corners, and a shadow effect.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(178, 166, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: tailwind css custom ui components</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=tailwind+css+custom+ui+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/tailwind+css+custom+ui+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/tailwind+css+custom+ui+components/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/tailwind+css+custom+ui+components/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=tailwind+css+custom+ui+components\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Building Custom UI with Tailwind CSS</h3><p>Tailwind CSS is a popular utility-first CSS framework that allows developers to create custom user interfaces quickly and efficiently. In this lesson, we will explore how to build custom UI components, implement responsive design, and customize themes using Tailwind CSS.</p><h3>Custom Components</h3><p>To build custom UI components with Tailwind CSS, you can use the <code>@apply</code> directive to create custom classes. For example, you can create a custom button class using the following code:</p><code>.btn { @apply bg-orange-500 hover:bg-orange-700 text-white font-bold py-2 px-4 rounded; }</code><p>This code creates a custom button class called <code>.btn</code> that applies the specified styles. You can then use this class in your HTML to create a custom button.</p><h3>Responsive Design</h3><p>Tailwind CSS provides a range of responsive design classes that allow you to create mobile-friendly and responsive UI components. For example, you can use the <code>md:</code> prefix to apply styles only on medium-sized screens and above.</p><ul><li><code>md:flex</code> - applies the <code>flex</code> style on medium-sized screens and above</li><li><code>lg:hidden</code> - hides an element on large-sized screens and above</li><li><code>xl:w-1/2</code> - sets the width of an element to half the parent container on extra-large screens and above</li></ul><p>By using these responsive design classes, you can create UI components that adapt to different screen sizes and devices.</p><h3>Theme Customization</h3><p>Tailwind CSS allows you to customize the default theme by creating a custom <code>tailwind.config.js</code> file. In this file, you can override the default theme settings, such as colors, fonts, and spacing.</p><p>For example, you can customize the default color palette by adding the following code to your <code>tailwind.config.js</code> file:</p><code>module.exports = { theme: { extend: { colors: { \'custom-blue\': \'#3498db\', } } } }</code><p>This code adds a custom blue color to the default color palette, which you can then use in your CSS classes.</p><p>By customizing the theme, you can create a unique and consistent visual identity for your application.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><ul><li>Use the <code>@apply</code> directive to create custom classes</li><li>Use responsive design classes to create mobile-friendly and responsive UI components</li><li>Customize the default theme by creating a custom <code>tailwind.config.js</code> file</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, you can create custom UI components that are consistent, responsive, and visually appealing.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>Tailwind CSS is a utility-first CSS framework that allows developers to create custom UI components quickly and efficiently</li><li>Custom components can be created using the <code>@apply</code> directive</li><li>Responsive design classes can be used to create mobile-friendly and responsive UI components</li><li>The default theme can be customized by creating a custom <code>tailwind.config.js</code> file</li></ul>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
