(180, 167, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design examples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Responsive Web Design</h3><p>Responsive web design is an approach to web design that makes web pages render well on a variety of devices and screen sizes. In this lesson, we will cover the basics of responsive web design, including media queries, flexible grids, and responsive images.</p><h3>Media Queries</h3><p>Media queries are a fundamental component of responsive web design. They allow you to apply different styles based on the characteristics of the device, such as screen size, orientation, or resolution. Media queries are defined using the <code>@media</code> rule in CSS.</p><ul><li><strong>Max-width</strong>: This query applies styles when the screen width is less than or equal to a specified value. For example: <code>@media (max-width: 768px) { ... }</code></li><li><strong>Min-width</strong>: This query applies styles when the screen width is greater than or equal to a specified value. For example: <code>@media (min-width: 1024px) { ... }</code></li><li><strong>Orientation</strong>: This query applies styles based on the device\'s orientation. For example: <code>@media (orientation: landscape) { ... }</code></li></ul><h3>Flexible Grids</h3><p>A flexible grid is a grid system that adjusts to the screen size and device type. It is typically created using CSS and can be used to layout content in a responsive manner. There are several ways to create a flexible grid, including using <code>flexbox</code> or <code>grid</code> in CSS.</p><p>A flexible grid typically consists of a container element and child elements. The container element is given a <code>display</code> property of <code>flex</code> or <code>grid</code>, and the child elements are given <code>flex</code> or <code>grid</code> properties to define their size and position.</p><h3>Responsive Images</h3><p>Responsive images are images that adjust their size and resolution based on the screen size and device type. There are several ways to create responsive images, including using the <code>img</code> tag with a <code>srcset</code> attribute or using CSS to set the image size and resolution.</p><ul><li><strong>Srcset</strong>: The <code>srcset</code> attribute allows you to specify multiple images with different resolutions and sizes. The browser will then choose the best image to display based on the screen size and device type. For example: <code><img src=\'image.jpg\' srcset=\'image-small.jpg 480w, image-medium.jpg 800w, image-large.jpg 1200w\'></code></li><li><strong>Picture</strong>: The <code>picture</code> element allows you to specify multiple images with different resolutions and sizes, as well as different formats such as WebP or JPEG. For example: <code><picture><source media=\'(max-width: 600px)\' srcset=\'image-small.jpg\'><source media=\'(max-width: 800px)\' srcset=\'image-medium.jpg\'><img src=\'image-large.jpg\' alt=\'image\'></picture></code></li></ul>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(181, 168, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web design examples</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+design+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+design+examples/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+design+examples\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Implementing Responsive Design</h3><p>Responsive design is an essential aspect of web development, as it allows websites to adapt to different screen sizes and devices. In this lesson, we will explore the mobile-first approach, viewport meta tag, and responsive navigation.</p><p>The mobile-first approach involves designing for smaller screens first and then scaling up to larger screens. This approach has several benefits, including <strong>improved user experience</strong> and <strong>reduced development time</strong>. To implement the mobile-first approach, you can use CSS media queries to apply different styles based on screen size.</p><h3>Viewport Meta Tag</h3><p>The viewport meta tag is used to control the zooming and scaling of a web page on mobile devices. The basic syntax of the viewport meta tag is:</p><code>&lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0&quot;&gt;</code><p>This tag tells the browser to set the width of the page to the width of the device and to set the initial zoom level to 1.0.</p><h3>Responsive Navigation</h3><p>Responsive navigation involves creating a navigation menu that adapts to different screen sizes. Here are some steps to create a responsive navigation menu:</p><ul><li>Use a <strong>flexible container</strong> to hold the navigation menu items.</li><li>Use <strong>CSS media queries</strong> to apply different styles based on screen size.</li><li>Use a <strong>hamburger menu</strong> icon to toggle the navigation menu on smaller screens.</li></ul><p>For example, you can use the following HTML and CSS code to create a responsive navigation menu:</p><code>&lt;nav&gt;<br>&lt;ul&gt;<br>&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;<br>&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;<br>&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;<br>&lt;/ul&gt;<br>&lt;/nav&gt;</code><code>nav ul {<br>list-style: none;<br>margin: 0;<br>padding: 0;<br>display: flex;<br>flex-wrap: wrap;<br>}<br>nav li {<br>flex: 1 1 100px;<br>}<br>nav a {<br>text-decoration: none;<br>color: #333;<br>}<br>@media only screen and (max-width: 600px) {<br>nav ul {<br>flex-direction: column;<br>}<br>nav li {<br>flex: 1 1 100%;<br>}<br>}</code><p>This code creates a responsive navigation menu that adapts to different screen sizes. On smaller screens, the navigation menu items are stacked vertically, while on larger screens, they are displayed horizontally.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(182, 169, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive design layouts</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+design+layouts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+design+layouts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+design+layouts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+design+layouts/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+design+layouts\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Advanced CSS Techniques for Responsive Design</h3><p>As we continue to build on our web development skills, it\'s essential to learn advanced CSS techniques for creating responsive designs. In this lesson, we\'ll explore three crucial topics: CSS Grid, Flexbox, and CSS Media Queries. These techniques will help you create flexible, adaptable, and visually appealing layouts for your websites.</p><h3>CSS Grid</h3><p>CSS Grid is a powerful layout system that allows you to create complex, two-dimensional grid structures. It\'s ideal for creating responsive designs, as it enables you to define rows and columns and place content within them. To use CSS Grid, you\'ll need to define a container element with the <code>display: grid</code> property.</p><ul><li>Define the grid structure using the <code>grid-template-columns</code> and <code>grid-template-rows</code> properties.</li><li>Use the <code>grid-column</code> and <code>grid-row</code> properties to place content within the grid.</li><li>Utilize the <code>grid-gap</code> property to add space between grid cells.</li></ul><p>For example, to create a simple grid with two columns and two rows, you can use the following code: <code>container { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; grid-template-rows: 1fr 1fr; }</code></p><h3>Flexbox</h3><p>Flexbox is another essential layout system in CSS that allows you to create flexible, one-dimensional layouts. It\'s perfect for creating responsive designs, as it enables you to distribute content evenly within a container. To use Flexbox, you\'ll need to define a container element with the <code>display: flex</code> property.</p><ul><li>Define the flex direction using the <code>flex-direction</code> property.</li><li>Use the <code>justify-content</code> property to align content horizontally.</li><li>Utilize the <code>align-items</code> property to align content vertically.</li></ul><p>For example, to create a simple flexbox layout with horizontal alignment, you can use the following code: <code>container { display: flex; flex-direction: row; justify-content: space-between; }</code></p><h3>CSS Media Queries</h3><p>CSS Media Queries allow you to apply different styles based on specific conditions, such as screen size or device type. They\'re essential for creating responsive designs, as they enable you to adapt your layout to different screen sizes and devices. To use CSS Media Queries, you\'ll need to define a query using the <code>@media</code> rule.</p><ul><li>Define the query condition using the <code>max-width</code> or <code>min-width</code> property.</li><li>Apply styles within the query using the <code>{}</code> syntax.</li><li>Use the <code>and</code> operator to combine multiple conditions.</li></ul><p>For example, to apply a style for screens with a maximum width of 768px, you can use the following code: <code>@media (max-width: 768px) { /* styles */ }</code></p><p>By mastering these advanced CSS techniques, you\'ll be able to create responsive, adaptable, and visually appealing layouts for your websites. Remember to practice and experiment with different scenarios to solidify your understanding of CSS Grid, Flexbox, and CSS Media Queries.</p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong><ul><li>CSS Grid is a powerful layout system for creating complex, two-dimensional grid structures.</li><li>Flexbox is a flexible layout system for creating one-dimensional layouts.</li><li>CSS Media Queries allow you to apply different styles based on specific conditions.</li></ul>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(183, 170, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: front-end frameworks</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/front-end+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/front-end+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=front-end+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Front-end Frameworks</h3><p>Front-end frameworks are pre-built sets of CSS and JavaScript files that provide a set of pre-designed UI components, layouts, and interactions. They help developers to build responsive, mobile-first, and consistent web applications quickly and efficiently. In this lesson, we will introduce three popular front-end frameworks: Bootstrap, Materialize, and Tailwind CSS.</p><h3>Bootstrap</h3><p>Bootstrap is one of the most popular front-end frameworks. It was created by Twitter and is now maintained by a team of developers. Bootstrap provides a wide range of UI components, including navigation bars, alerts, buttons, and more. It also includes a grid system that makes it easy to create responsive layouts.</p><p>To use Bootstrap, you need to include the Bootstrap CSS and JavaScript files in your HTML file. You can do this by adding the following lines of code to the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> section of your HTML file: <code>&lt;link rel=\'stylesheet\' href=\'https://maxcdn.bootstrapcdn.com/bootstrap/4.0.0/css/bootstrap.min.css\'&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;script src=\'https://maxcdn.bootstrapcdn.com/bootstrap/4.0.0/js/bootstrap.min.js\'&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code>.</p><h3>Materialize</h3><p>Materialize is a front-end framework that is based on Google\'s Material Design. It provides a wide range of UI components, including navigation bars, buttons, and cards. Materialize also includes a grid system that makes it easy to create responsive layouts.</p><p>To use Materialize, you need to include the Materialize CSS and JavaScript files in your HTML file. You can do this by adding the following lines of code to the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> section of your HTML file: <code>&lt;link rel=\'stylesheet\' href=\'https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/materialize/1.0.0/css/materialize.min.css\'&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;script src=\'https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/materialize/1.0.0/js/materialize.min.js\'&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code>.</p><h3>Tailwind CSS</h3><p>Tailwind CSS is a utility-first front-end framework that provides a set of pre-designed CSS classes that can be used to style HTML elements. It does not provide any pre-designed UI components, but instead provides a set of classes that can be used to create custom UI components.</p><p>To use Tailwind CSS, you need to include the Tailwind CSS file in your HTML file. You can do this by adding the following line of code to the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> section of your HTML file: <code>&lt;link rel=\'stylesheet\' href=\'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/tailwindcss@2.2.19/dist/tailwind.min.css\'&gt;</code>.</p><p>Here are some key features of each framework: <ul><li><strong>Bootstrap</strong>: provides a wide range of pre-designed UI components, includes a grid system, and is easy to use.</li><li><strong>Materialize</strong>: provides a wide range of pre-designed UI components, includes a grid system, and is based on Google\'s Material Design.</li><li><strong>Tailwind CSS</strong>: provides a set of pre-designed CSS classes, does not provide any pre-designed UI components, and is highly customizable.</li></ul></p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(184, 171, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web application frameworks</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+application+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+application+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+application+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+application+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+application+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Responsive Web Applications</h3><p>Responsive web applications are designed to provide an optimal user experience across a wide range of devices, from desktop computers to mobile phones. To achieve this, developers use various frameworks that provide pre-built CSS and JavaScript components. In this lesson, we will explore three popular frameworks: Bootstrap, Materialize, and Tailwind CSS.</p><h3>Bootstrap Grid System</h3><p>The Bootstrap Grid System is a powerful tool for building responsive layouts. It divides the screen into 12 columns, allowing you to create complex layouts using rows and columns. The grid system is based on the following classes: <code>container</code>, <code>row</code>, and <code>col-</code>. For example, <code>col-md-4</code> would occupy 4 columns on a medium-sized screen.</p><ul><li><strong>Container</strong>: The container class is used to wrap the grid system and provide a maximum width.</li><li><strong>Row</strong>: The row class is used to define a horizontal row in the grid system.</li><li><strong>Column</strong>: The column class is used to define a vertical column in the grid system.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use the Bootstrap Grid System: <code>&lt;div class=&quot;container&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;row&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;col-md-4&quot;&gt;Column 1&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;col-md-4&quot;&gt;Column 2&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;col-md-4&quot;&gt;Column 3&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code></p><h3>Materialize Responsive Design</h3><p>Materialize is a popular front-end framework that provides a responsive design system. It uses a 12-column grid system, similar to Bootstrap, but with some key differences. Materialize uses the <code>container</code> class to define the grid system, and the <code>row</code> class to define a horizontal row. The column classes are defined using the <code>col</code> class, followed by the column size (e.g., <code>col s4</code>).</p><ul><li><strong>Container</strong>: The container class is used to wrap the grid system and provide a maximum width.</li><li><strong>Row</strong>: The row class is used to define a horizontal row in the grid system.</li><li><strong>Column</strong>: The column class is used to define a vertical column in the grid system.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use Materialize Responsive Design: <code>&lt;div class=&quot;container&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;row&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;col s4&quot;&gt;Column 1&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;col s4&quot;&gt;Column 2&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;col s4&quot;&gt;Column 3&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code></p><h3>Tailwind CSS Utilities</h3><p>Tailwind CSS is a utility-first CSS framework that provides a unique approach to building responsive layouts. It uses a set of pre-defined classes to style HTML elements, rather than relying on a grid system. Tailwind CSS provides a wide range of classes for styling elements, including <code>flex</code>, <code>grid</code>, and <code>float</code>.</p><ul><li><strong>Flexbox</strong>: The flexbox classes are used to create flexible layouts.</li><li><strong>Grid</strong>: The grid classes are used to create grid-based layouts.</li><li><strong>Float</strong>: The float classes are used to create floating layouts.</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how to use Tailwind CSS Utilities: <code>&lt;div class=&quot;flex justify-center&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;w-1/2&quot;&gt;Column 1&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;w-1/2&quot;&gt;Column 2&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code></p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(185, 172, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive web development best practices</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+web+development+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+web+development+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+web+development+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+web+development+best+practices/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+web+development+best+practices\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Best Practices for Responsive Web Development</h3><p>As a web developer, it\'s essential to ensure that your website is accessible, compatible, and performs well across different browsers and devices. In this lesson, we\'ll cover the best practices for cross-browser compatibility, accessibility, and performance optimization.</p><h3>Cross-Browser Compatibility</h3><p>Cross-browser compatibility refers to the ability of a website to work seamlessly across different browsers, such as Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, and Safari. To achieve this, you can use the following techniques:</p><ul><li>Use <strong>standard HTML and CSS</strong> to ensure that your website\'s structure and layout are consistent across different browsers.</li><li>Use <strong>browser prefixes</strong> for CSS properties that are not yet widely supported, such as <code>-webkit-</code> for Safari and Chrome, and <code>-moz-</code> for Firefox.</li><li>Test your website on different browsers and devices to identify and fix any compatibility issues.</li></ul><h3>Accessibility</h3><p>Accessibility refers to the ability of a website to be used by people with disabilities. To make your website accessible, you can use the following techniques:</p><ul><li>Use <strong>semantic HTML</strong> to provide a clear structure to your website\'s content, making it easier for screen readers to navigate.</li><li>Use <strong>alt text</strong> for images to provide a description of the image for users who are blind or have low vision.</li><li>Use <strong>high contrast colors</strong> to make your website\'s text and background more readable for users with visual impairments.</li></ul><h3>Performance Optimization</h3><p>Performance optimization refers to the process of improving the speed and efficiency of a website. To optimize your website\'s performance, you can use the following techniques:</p><ul><li>Use <strong>minification and compression</strong> to reduce the size of your website\'s files, making them faster to download.</li><li>Use <strong>lazy loading</strong> to load images and other resources only when they are needed, reducing the amount of data that needs to be transferred.</li><li>Use <strong>caching</strong> to store frequently-used resources in the user\'s browser, reducing the need for repeat downloads.</li></ul><p>By following these best practices, you can ensure that your website is responsive, accessible, and performs well across different browsers and devices.</p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
(186, 173, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: responsive website design</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=responsive+website+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/responsive+website+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/responsive+website+design/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/responsive+website+design/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=responsive+website+design\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Final Project - Building a Fully Responsive Website</h3><p>Welcome to the final project of our web development course. In this project, we will be building a fully responsive website from scratch. We will cover three main topics: project planning, design implementation, and testing and deployment.</p><p>First, let\'s talk about <strong>project planning</strong>. This is the most important step in building a website. We need to define the scope, goals, and target audience of our website. We also need to create a wireframe and a mockup of our website to visualize the layout and design.</p><ul><li>Define the scope and goals of the website</li><li>Identify the target audience</li><li>Create a wireframe and mockup of the website</li></ul><p>Next, let\'s move on to <strong>design implementation</strong>. In this step, we will be writing the HTML, CSS, and JavaScript code for our website. We will use <code>HTML5</code> for structuring our content, <code>CSS3</code> for styling, and <code>JavaScript</code> for adding interactivity.</p><ul><li>Write the HTML structure of the website</li><li>Add CSS styles to the website</li><li>Use JavaScript to add interactivity</li></ul><p>Finally, let\'s talk about <strong>testing and deployment</strong>. We need to test our website on different devices and browsers to ensure that it is fully responsive and works as expected. We also need to deploy our website to a server so that it can be accessed by the public.</p><ul><li>Test the website on different devices and browsers</li><li>Deploy the website to a server</li><li>Configure the website for search engine optimization</li></ul><p>Here is an example of how we can use <code>media queries</code> to make our website responsive: <code>@media only screen and (max-width: 768px) { /* styles for mobile devices */ }</code></p>', 1, '2026-04-03 13:34:56', '2026-04-03 13:34:56'),
