(922, 935, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2fd14d4175e_1781518669.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Introduction to Web Development</h3><p>Today, you will learn the basics of web development and understand its importance in the real world. You will discover what web development is, its history, the various careers available, and why it matters. This knowledge is crucial for any aspiring web developer, as it will help you make informed decisions about your career path and give you a solid foundation to build upon.</p><h4>Concepts you need</h4><p>Let\'s start with the basics. <strong>Web development</strong> refers to the process of building and maintaining websites, web applications, and mobile applications. Think of it like building a house. Just as an architect designs the house, a web developer designs the website or application. The architect uses materials like bricks, wood, and concrete, while the web developer uses programming languages like <code>JavaScript</code>, <code>HTML</code>, and <code>CSS</code>.</p><p>The <strong>history of web development</strong> dates back to the 1980s, when the internet was first introduced. The first website was launched in 1991, and since then, the web has evolved rapidly. It\'s like a tree that has grown from a small seed into a massive forest, with new branches and leaves emerging every day.</p><p><strong>Web development careers</strong> are diverse and plentiful. You can become a front-end developer, back-end developer, full-stack developer, or even a DevOps engineer. Each role has its unique responsibilities and requires different skill sets. For example, a front-end developer focuses on the client-side of the website, making sure it looks and feels great to the user, while a back-end developer works on the server-side, ensuring the website is secure, fast, and scalable.</p><p>The <strong>importance of web development</strong> cannot be overstated. In today\'s digital age, every business, organization, and individual needs a website or online presence to reach their audience. Web development is not just about building websites; it\'s about creating experiences, solving problems, and making people\'s lives easier. It\'s like building a bridge that connects people, businesses, and communities across the globe.</p><h4>Step-by-step task</h4><ol><li>Open a text editor or an integrated development environment (IDE) like Visual Studio Code, Sublime Text, or Atom.</li><li>Create a new file and save it with an <code>.html</code> extension, for example, <code>index.html</code>.</li><li>In the file, add the basic structure of an HTML document, which includes the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> tags.</li><li>Add some content to the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> tag, like a heading, a paragraph, and a link.</li><li>Save the file and open it in a web browser to see the result.</li></ol><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;My First Web Page&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to my website&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> my first web page, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> I am excited to learn more about web development.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;a href=\'https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//www.google.com\'&gt;Visit Google&lt;/a&gt;</span>\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code></pre><p>This code creates a simple web page with a title, heading, paragraph, and link. The <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> tag is the root element of the document, the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> tag contains metadata, and the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> tag contains the content.</p><h4>Deliverable</h4><p>Submit your <code>index.html</code> file, which should contain the basic structure of an HTML document and some content.</p><h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4><ul><li>Forgetting to save the file with an <code>.html</code> extension.</li><li>Not closing tags properly, which can lead to errors and unexpected behavior.</li><li>Not using the correct syntax for HTML tags and attributes.</li><li>Not testing the web page in a browser to see the result.</li></ul><h4>Pro tips</h4><ul><li>Use a code editor or IDE with syntax highlighting and auto-completion to make coding easier and more efficient.</li><li>Use online resources, such as Mozilla Developer Network or W3Schools, to learn more about HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.</li><li>Practice building small projects, like a personal website or a to-do list app, to gain hands-on experience and improve your skills.</li></ul><blockquote>Remember, web development is a continuous learning process. Stay curious, keep practicing, and you will become a proficient web developer in no time.</blockquote>', 'Introduction to Web Development', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 10:22:28'),
(923, 936, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><h3 style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2fd31a97b95_1781519130.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></h3></div><h3>Tools and Software Used in Web Development</h3>\n<p>Today, you will learn about the essential tools and software used in web development. These tools will help you build, test, and maintain your web applications efficiently.</p>\n<h4>Concepts You Need</h4>\n<p>Before we dive into the task, let\'s cover some essential concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Text Editors and IDEs</strong>: A text editor is a program that allows you to write and edit text files. An Integrated Development Environment (IDE) is a text editor that provides additional features such as code completion, debugging, and project management.</li>\n<li><strong>Version Control Systems</strong>: A version control system is a tool that helps you manage changes to your code over time. It allows you to track changes, collaborate with others, and revert to previous versions if needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Web Browsers and Debugging Tools</strong>: A web browser is a program that allows you to view and interact with web pages. Debugging tools are used to identify and fix errors in your code.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and Graphics Software</strong>: Design and graphics software are used to create visual elements for your web application, such as logos, icons, and graphics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let\'s start with text editors and IDEs. A text editor is like a word processor, but instead of formatting text, it helps you write and edit code. Some popular text editors include Notepad++, Sublime Text, and Atom. An IDE, on the other hand, is like a text editor on steroids. It provides features such as code completion, debugging, and project management. Some popular IDEs include Visual Studio Code, IntelliJ IDEA, and Eclipse.</p>\n<h4>Step-by-Step Task</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Install a text editor or IDE on your computer. For this task, we will use Visual Studio Code, a popular and free IDE.</p></li>\n<li><p>Open Visual Studio Code and create a new project by clicking on the \"Create a new folder\" button in the start page.</p></li>\n<li><p>Name your project \"MyWebApp\" and click on the \"Create\" button.</p></li>\n<li><p>Open the \"Explorer\" panel in Visual Studio Code by clicking on the \"Explorer\" button in the left sidebar.</p></li>\n<li><p>Create a new file called \"index.html\" in the \"MyWebApp\" folder by right-clicking on the folder and selecting \"New File\" from the context menu.</p></li>\n<li><p>Open the \"index.html\" file and add the following code:<code>\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;My Web App&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Hello, World!&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code>\n</p><p>This code creates a basic HTML page with a title and a paragraph of text.</p></li>\n<li><p>Save the \"index.html\" file by clicking on the \"File\" menu and selecting \"Save\" from the dropdown menu.</p></li>\n<li><p>Open the \"index.html\" file in a web browser by clicking on the \"Run Code\" button in Visual Studio Code or by copying the file path and pasting it into the address bar of a web browser.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>For this task, you will submit a screenshot of the \"index.html\" file open in a web browser. Make sure to include the title and paragraph of text in the screenshot.</p>\n<h4>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Not saving the \"index.html\" file correctly</strong>: Make sure to save the file with the correct file extension (.html) and in the correct location (the \"MyWebApp\" folder).</li>\n<li><strong>Not opening the \"index.html\" file in a web browser correctly</strong>: Make sure to copy the file path correctly and paste it into the address bar of a web browser.</li>\n<li><strong>Not including the title and paragraph of text in the screenshot</strong>: Make sure to include the title and paragraph of text in the screenshot to ensure that you have completed the task correctly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro Tips</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use the Visual Studio Code debugger to identify and fix errors in your code</strong>: The Visual Studio Code debugger is a powerful tool that allows you to step through your code and identify errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Use a version control system to manage changes to your code</strong>: A version control system such as Git allows you to track changes to your code and collaborate with others.</li>\n<li><strong>Use design and graphics software to create visual elements for your web application</strong>: Design and graphics software such as Adobe Creative Cloud allows you to create visual elements such as logos, icons, and graphics.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4><br></h4><ul>\n</ul>', 'Tools and Software Used in Web Development', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 12:11:12'),
(924, 937, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2fd48c7d52b_1781519500.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Web Development Careers and Specializations</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ll learn about different careers and specializations in web development. This knowledge will help you understand the roles you can take on in a web development project and what skills you need to develop to succeed in each role.</p>\n\n<h4>Concepts you need:</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Front-end Developer</strong>: The front-end developer is responsible for creating the user interface and user experience of a website or application. They focus on the client-side of the web, using programming languages like HTML, CSS, and JavaScript to create interactive and visually appealing interfaces.</li>\n  <li><strong>Back-end Developer</strong>: The back-end developer is responsible for creating the server-side logic, database integration, and API connectivity for a website or application. They focus on the server-side of the web, using programming languages like PHP, Ruby, Python, and Java to create the underlying structure of the website or application.</li>\n  <li><strong>Full-stack Developer</strong>: The full-stack developer is a jack-of-all-trades in web development. They have a broad range of skills and can work on both the front-end and back-end of a website or application. They can handle everything from user interface design to server-side logic and database integration.</li>\n  <li><strong>Web Designer</strong>: The web designer is responsible for creating the visual aspects of a website or application, including the layout, color scheme, and typography. They use design tools like Sketch, Figma, and Adobe XD to create wireframes, prototypes, and high-fidelity designs.</li>\n  <li><strong>UI/UX Specialist</strong>: The UI/UX specialist is responsible for creating user-centered designs that are both functional and aesthetically pleasing. They use design thinking principles to research, ideate, prototype, and test user interfaces and user experiences.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task:</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li>Create a new HTML file called <code>index.html</code> and add the following code inside it:<code>\n&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Web Development Careers and Specializations&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Web Development Careers and Specializations&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Front-end Developer&lt;/strong&gt;: The front-end developer is responsible for creating the user interface and user experience of a website or application.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Back-end Developer&lt;/strong&gt;: The back-end developer is responsible for creating the server-side logic, database integration, and API connectivity for a website or application.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Full-stack Developer&lt;/strong&gt;: The full-stack developer is a jack-of-all-trades in web development.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Web Designer&lt;/strong&gt;: The web designer is responsible for creating the visual aspects of a website or application.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;UI/UX Specialist&lt;/strong&gt;: The UI/UX specialist is responsible for creating user-centered designs that are both functional and aesthetically pleasing.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code>\n  <p>This code creates a basic HTML structure with a title, heading, and unordered list containing the different web development careers and specializations.</p>\n  </li><li>Open the <code>index.html</code> file in a web browser and view the result. You should see a basic web page with a title, heading, and unordered list containing the different web development careers and specializations.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable:</h3>\n<p>Submit the <code>index.html</code> file as your deliverable for this task. Make sure to include the code we created in the step-by-step task.</p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid:</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Not including all the necessary concepts and specializations in the unordered list.</li>\n  <li>Not using the correct HTML structure and syntax.</li>\n  <li>Not testing the code in a web browser.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips:</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use a code editor like Visual Studio Code or Sublime Text to write and edit your code.</li>\n  <li>Use a web browser like Google Chrome or Mozilla Firefox to test your code.</li>\n  <li>Use online resources like W3Schools or Mozilla Developer Network to learn more about HTML and web development.</li>\n</ul>', 'Web Development Careers and Specializations', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 10:33:32'),
(925, 938, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2fd76185403_1781520225.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you will learn how software projects are planned and managed in the real world. By the end of the day you’ll be able to explain the Waterfall model, Agile methodology, Scrum framework, and Kanban board, and you’ll create a simple visual board that shows how a team might move work from idea to finished product.</p><h3>Concepts you need</h3><h4>Waterfall Model</h4><p>The Waterfall model is a linear way of building software. Think of it like a waterfall that flows from one step to the next: <em>Requirements → Design → Implementation → Testing → Deployment → Maintenance</em>. Each phase must finish before the next one starts, and going back is difficult and costly.</p><h4>Agile Methodology</h4><p>Agile is a philosophy that values people, collaboration, and quick delivery. It says: <em>Build small pieces, get feedback often, and adapt.</em> It is the opposite of the rigid Waterfall approach.</p><h4>Scrum Framework</h4><p>Scrum is a specific way to practice Agile. It breaks work into short cycles called <strong>sprints</strong> (usually 2‑4 weeks). The team holds daily <strong>stand‑ups</strong>, a sprint <strong>planning</strong> meeting, a sprint <strong>review</strong>, and a sprint <strong>retrospective</strong>. The product owner owns the backlog of tasks.</p><h4>Kanban Board</h4><p>A Kanban board is a visual tool that shows the flow of work. It usually has columns like <em>Backlog, To‑Do, In‑Progress, Review, Done</em>. Tasks move from left to right as they get completed. It helps teams see bottlenecks and keep work flowing smoothly.</p><h3>Step‑by‑step task</h3><ol><li><strong>Define a simple project</strong><p>Imagine you are building a small website that lists your favorite books. Write down the main features: <em>Home page, Book list, Book details, Contact form.</em></p></li><li><strong>Write a Waterfall plan</strong><p>Create a short table (you can type it in a text file) that lists the phases and what will happen in each phase for your project.</p></li><li><strong>Write an Agile plan</strong><p>List the same features as items in a backlog. Each item should be small enough to finish in a sprint.</p></li><li><strong>Design a Scrum sprint schedule</strong><p>Decide that you will use 2‑week sprints. For the first sprint, pick 2 backlog items (e.g. Home page and Book list). Write a sprint goal: <em>Deliver a working home page with a list of books.</em></p></li><li><strong>Create a Kanban board</strong><p>Use a simple HTML page to represent the board. Copy the code below into a file called kanban.html. Open it in a browser to see the board.</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;&lt;html&gt;&lt;head&gt;&lt;title&gt;Kanban Board&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/head&gt;&lt;body&gt;&lt;h3&gt;My Project Kanban&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'display:flex;gap:10px;\'</span>&gt;&lt;div style=\'border:1px solid <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#000;padding:10px;width:150px;\'&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Backlog&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;div style=\'background:#eee;padding:5px;margin:5px 0;\'&gt;Create Home Page&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=\'background:#eee;padding:5px;margin:5px 0;\'&gt;Create Book List&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=\'border:1px solid #000;padding:10px;width:150px;\'&gt;&lt;h4&gt;To‑Do&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;div style=\'background:#ffeb3b;padding:5px;margin:5px 0;\'&gt;Create Home Page&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=\'border:1px solid #000;padding:10px;width:150px;\'&gt;&lt;h4&gt;In‑Progress&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;div style=\'background:#03a9f4;padding:5px;margin:5px 0;\'&gt;Create Book List&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=\'border:1px solid #000;padding:10px;width:150px;\'&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Review&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=\'border:1px solid #000;padding:10px;width:150px;\'&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Done&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</span></code></pre></li><li><strong>Explain the board</strong><p>Write a short paragraph (in a text file) describing how each column works and how a task moves from Backlog to Done.</p></li></ol><h3>Deliverable</h3><p>Submit the following:</p><ul><li>A text file called waterfall.txt containing your Waterfall phase table.</li><li>A text file called backlog.txt listing your Agile backlog items.</li><li>A text file called sprint_plan.txt describing your first sprint goal and selected items.</li><li>The kanban.html file you created.</li><li>A one‑page PDF or Word document titled “Project Planning Summary” that includes:</li><ul><li>A short comparison of Waterfall vs Agile.</li><li>The Scrum sprint schedule.</li><li>A screenshot of your Kanban board (you can paste the image from the browser).</li></ul></ul><h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3><ul><li>Mixing up the order of Waterfall phases – remember that each phase must finish before the next starts.</li><li>Creating backlog items that are too large – keep them small and testable.</li><li>Forgetting to move tasks across the Kanban board – the board is only useful if it reflects real progress.</li><li>Using double quotes in your HTML – always use single quotes for attributes to keep the platform happy.</li></ul><h3>Pro tips</h3><blockquote><p>Use a real project management tool (like Trello or Jira) for a more realistic Kanban experience.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>During the sprint review, ask a peer to play the role of a stakeholder and give feedback on the demo.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Keep your sprint backlog in a spreadsheet so you can easily track time estimates and actual effort.</p></blockquote>', 'Web Development Process and Methodologies', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 10:43:57'),
(926, 939, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2fd84cb2827_1781520460.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 5! Today, we\'re stepping back from direct coding for a moment to explore the bigger picture: the exciting world of the web development industry itself. You\'ll learn about the latest trends, what the future holds, and how emerging technologies are shaping our field. Understanding these aspects is crucial for any aspiring developer; it helps you make informed career choices, build relevant skills, and create websites that are modern and impactful in the real world.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<p>Before we dive into the task, let\'s understand some key concepts about the web development landscape.</p>\n\n<h4>What is the Web Development Industry?</h4>\n<p>Imagine all the people, companies, and tools involved in creating and maintaining every website and web application you use daily. That\'s the web development industry! It\'s a vast and dynamic sector where professionals build everything from simple personal blogs to complex online banking systems and social media platforms. Understanding its trends helps you stay relevant and build websites that truly matter.</p>\n\n<h4>Current Web Development Trends</h4>\n<p>These are the popular approaches and technologies that many developers are using right now to build modern websites.</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Responsive Web Design (RWD):</strong>\n        <p>Think of responsive web design like a chameleon changing its colours to blend in with different backgrounds. In web terms, it means designing websites that automatically adjust and look good on any device, whether it\'s a large desktop monitor, a tablet, or a small smartphone screen.</p>\n        <blockquote>\n            <em><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Most people access the internet from their phones. If your website isn\'t responsive, it will be hard to use on a mobile device, and users will quickly leave. It\'s about providing a great experience for everyone, everywhere.</em>\n        </blockquote>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Single Page Applications (SPAs):</strong>\n        <p>Imagine you\'re using a desktop application like Microsoft Word or a game. When you click something, the whole application doesn\'t reload; only the specific part you interacted with changes. SPAs work similarly in your web browser. Instead of reloading an entirely new page every time you click a link, only the necessary content is updated dynamically. This makes the website feel much faster and smoother, like a native app.</p>\n        <blockquote>\n            <em><strong>Examples:</strong> Gmail, Google Maps, Facebook, and many modern dashboards are SPAs. You load the main application once, and then navigate within it without full page refreshes.</em>\n        </blockquote>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Progressive Web Apps (PWAs):</strong>\n        <p>PWAs are like a superhero hybrid: they combine the best features of a traditional website with the best features of a mobile app. You access them through a web browser, just like any website, but they can offer app-like capabilities such as working offline, sending push notifications, and even being \'installed\' to your device\'s home screen without going through an app store.</p>\n        <blockquote>\n            <em><strong>Benefit:</strong> They provide a reliable, fast, and engaging user experience, bridging the gap between web and native mobile applications.</em>\n        </blockquote>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Dark Mode:</strong>\n        <p>Dark mode is a user interface option where the colour scheme uses a dark background with light-coloured text, instead of the traditional light background with dark text. Many operating systems and applications now offer this as a user preference.</p>\n        <blockquote>\n            <em><strong>Why it\'s popular:</strong> It can reduce eye strain, especially in low-light conditions, save battery life on devices with OLED screens, and many users simply find it more aesthetically pleasing.</em>\n        </blockquote>\n    </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Future of Web Development</h4>\n<p>The web is always evolving! Here\'s a glimpse into what\'s coming next.</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Artificial Intelligence (AI) &amp; Machine Learning (ML) in Web Dev:</strong>\n        <p>AI and ML are no longer just for sci-fi movies; they\'re becoming powerful tools in web development. They can help automate tasks for developers (like writing basic code or testing), and they can also enhance user experiences with features like personalized content recommendations (think Netflix or Amazon), intelligent chatbots for customer support, and advanced search functionalities.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Voice User Interfaces (VUIs):</strong>\n        <p>Imagine talking to a website instead of typing! VUIs allow users to interact with websites and applications using voice commands. With the rise of smart speakers (like Amazon Alexa and Google Assistant), integrating voice commands into web experiences is becoming more common, offering new ways for users to access information and services.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Web3 and Blockchain:</strong>\n        <p>Web3 represents a potential next generation of the internet, built on decentralized technologies like blockchain. Instead of data being stored on central servers controlled by large companies, blockchain allows for decentralized, secure, and transparent data management. This opens up possibilities for new types of applications where users have more ownership and control over their data and digital assets (like cryptocurrencies and NFTs).</p>\n    </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Web Development Industry Outlook</h4>\n<p>What does the future hold for web developers?</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>\n        <strong>High Demand:</strong> The need for skilled web developers continues to grow rapidly across almost all industries. Businesses constantly need new websites, updates to existing ones, and complex web applications.</li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Specialization:</strong> While \'full-stack\' developers (who handle both frontend and backend) are valuable, there\'s also a strong trend towards specialization. You might focus on frontend development (what users see), backend development (server, database logic), UI/UX design (user interface/experience), or even specific areas like cybersecurity or cloud deployment.</li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Remote Work:</strong> Web development is one of the fields where remote work is very common and accepted, offering flexibility and access to a global talent pool.</li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Continuous Learning:</strong> The web development landscape changes incredibly fast. To stay relevant and successful, developers must be lifelong learners, constantly updating their skills and knowledge.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Emerging Technologies in Web Development</h4>\n<p>These are newer technologies that are gaining traction and promise to change how we build for the web.</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>\n        <strong>WebAssembly (Wasm):</strong>\n        <p>Think of WebAssembly as a super-efficient engine that allows code written in other programming languages (like C++, Rust, or Go) to run directly inside web browsers at near-native speeds. Traditionally, browsers primarily run JavaScript. Wasm opens the door for highly performance-intensive applications, like advanced games, video editors, or complex scientific simulations, to run smoothly in a web browser.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Low-Code/No-Code Platforms:</strong>\n        <p>These platforms are like building with LEGO blocks instead of designing each individual brick. They allow people to create websites and applications with minimal or no traditional coding, using visual drag-and-drop interfaces and pre-built components. While they don\'t replace custom coding for complex projects, they empower non-developers and speed up development for simpler applications.</p>\n        <blockquote>\n            <em><strong>Examples:</strong> Tools like Webflow, Bubble, or even advanced WordPress page builders fall into this category.</em>\n        </blockquote>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <strong>Jamstack:</strong>\n        <p>Jamstack is a modern web development architecture that focuses on delivering faster, more secure, and scalable websites. It stands for <strong>J</strong>avaScript, <strong>A</strong>PIs, and <strong>M</strong>arkup. Essentially, it means building websites where the content is pre-built (static) and served quickly, with dynamic features added through JavaScript and external APIs (Application Programming Interfaces). This approach often leads to better performance and security compared to traditional server-rendered websites.</p>\n    </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<p>Today, your task is to create a simple HTML page that summarizes what you\'ve learned about web development trends and the industry outlook. This will help you solidify your understanding and practice basic HTML structure.</p>\n\n<ol>\n    <li>\n        <h4>Create a new HTML file:</h4>\n        <p>Open your text editor (like VS Code) and create a new file named <code>day5_trends.html</code>.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <h4>Set up the basic HTML structure:</h4>\n        <p>Add the fundamental HTML boilerplate to your file. Remember to include the <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code> declaration, <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> tags.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'en\'</span>&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'UTF-8\'</span>&gt;\n    &lt;meta name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'viewport\'</span> content=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\'</span>&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Web Development Trends - Day <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n    \n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code></pre>\n        <p><em>Explanation:</em> This code sets up the basic document type, specifies the language as English, defines character encoding, ensures proper scaling on various devices, and sets the title that appears in the browser tab.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <h4>Add a main heading:</h4>\n        <p>Inside the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> tags, add an <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> tag for your page title.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n&lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Exploring Web Development Trends <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> Industry Outlook&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n</code></pre>\n        <p><em>Explanation:</em> The <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> tag is used for the most important heading on a page.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <h4>Summarize Current Trends:</h4>\n        <p>Add an <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code> for \'Current Web Development Trends\' and then use an unordered list (<code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>) with list items (<code>&lt;li&gt;</code>) to briefly describe <strong>at least three</strong> current trends you learned about today. For each trend, include its name and a very short, one-sentence explanation.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n&lt;h2&gt;Current Web Development Trends&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;ul&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Responsive Web Design:&lt;/strong&gt; Websites that adapt to different screen sizes <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span> optimal viewing.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Single Page Applications (SPAs):&lt;/strong&gt; Web apps that load once <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> dynamically update content without full page reloads.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Progressive Web Apps (PWAs):&lt;/strong&gt; Websites offering app-like features such <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">as</span> offline access <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> push notifications.&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;\n</code></pre>\n        <p><em>Explanation:</em> <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code> is a subheading. <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> creates a bulleted list, and each <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> is an item in that list. <code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> makes text bold.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <h4>Summarize Future Trends and Emerging Technologies:</h4>\n        <p>Add another <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code> for \'Future &amp; Emerging Technologies\' and use another unordered list to summarize <strong>at least three</strong> future trends or emerging technologies. Again, include the name and a brief explanation for each.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n&lt;h2&gt;Future &amp; Emerging Technologies&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;ul&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AI &amp; Machine Learning:&lt;/strong&gt; Enhancing user experience <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> aiding developers <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">with</span> automation.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;WebAssembly (Wasm):&lt;/strong&gt; Allows high-performance code to run <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> browsers at near-native speeds.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Low-Code/No-Code Platforms:&lt;/strong&gt; Tools <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span> building apps <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">with</span> minimal <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">or</span> no manual coding.&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;\n</code></pre>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <h4>Add a concluding thought on the Industry Outlook:</h4>\n        <p>Finish with a paragraph (<code>&lt;p&gt;</code>) summarizing your key takeaway about the web development industry outlook (e.g., high demand, importance of continuous learning).</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n&lt;h2&gt;Industry Outlook&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;The web development industry continues to be a field of high demand <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> constant innovation. To succeed, continuous learning <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> adapting to <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">new</span> technologies are essential <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span> every developer.&lt;/p&gt;\n</code></pre>\n        <p><em>Explanation:</em> The <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> tag is used for paragraphs of text.</p>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <h4>Save and view your file:</h4>\n        <p>Save <code>day5_trends.html</code> and open it in your web browser to see your summary page.</p>\n    </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit your <code>day5_trends.html</code> file. It should contain a well-structured HTML page summarizing at least three current web development trends, at least three future/emerging technologies, and a concluding thought on the industry outlook, using the HTML tags learned today.</p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Forgetting HTML boilerplate:</strong> Always start with <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>. These are the foundation of any HTML page.</li>\n    <li><strong>Not closing tags:</strong> Every opening tag (e.g., <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>) needs a closing tag (<code>&lt;/p&gt;</code>). Forgetting this can break your page\'s layout.</li>\n    <li><strong>Using incorrect heading levels:</strong> Remember that <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> is for the main title, <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code> for major sections, and so on. Don\'t use them just for size; use them for semantic structure.</li>\n    <li><strong>Copy-pasting without understanding:</strong> While you\'re using provided code examples, make sure you understand what each tag and attribute does. This is how you truly learn.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n    <li>\n        <blockquote>\n            <em><strong>Stay curious:</strong> The web development world changes quickly. Make it a habit to read tech blogs, follow industry leaders, and explore new tools. This proactive learning will keep your skills sharp.</em>\n        </blockquote>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <blockquote>\n            <em><strong>Think about user experience:</strong> As you learn about trends like Responsive Design and PWAs, always consider the end-user. How will these technologies make a website better, faster, or easier to use for real people?</em>\n        </blockquote>\n    </li>\n    <li>\n        <blockquote>\n            <em><strong>Don\'t get overwhelmed:</strong> There are many trends and technologies. You don\'t need to master them all at once. Focus on understanding the basics and then pick one or two areas that genuinely interest you to dive deeper into.</em>\n        </blockquote>\n    </li>\n</ul>', 'Web Development Industry and Trends', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 10:48:24'),
(927, 940, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; <img src=\"https://internshipadda.com/public/uploads/images/media_6a2fd8c1605c8_1781520577.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 8px 0px; border-radius: 8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you will build your first complete HTML web page from scratch. You will learn how HTML gives web pages structure and meaning, which is the foundation of all websites. Understanding these basics will help you create job-ready web pages and prepare for advanced topics like CSS and JavaScript.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n\n<h4>What is HTML?</h4>\n<p>HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language. Think of it as the skeleton of a website - it defines what content exists on the page and how it should be organized. Unlike programming languages that make things happen, HTML is a markup language that describes the structure of information.</p>\n\n<h4>Basic HTML Structure</h4>\n<p>All HTML documents follow a standard structure like a house blueprint. The main parts are:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>&lt;html&gt;</strong> - The container for everything on the page</li>\n  <li><strong>&lt;head&gt;</strong> - Contains metadata (information about the page)</li>\n  <li><strong>&lt;body&gt;</strong> - Contains the content users see</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>HTML Document Type Declaration</h4>\n<p>The  declaration is like a label that tells browsers, \"This is an HTML5 document.\" It must appear at the very top of every HTML file to ensure browsers render the page correctly.</p>\n\n<h4>HTML Elements and Tags</h4>\n<p>HTML elements are the building blocks of web pages. They are written using tags like &lt;h1&gt; or &lt;p&gt;. Tags often come in pairs - an opening tag &lt;h1&gt; and closing tag &lt;/h1&gt;. The content between them becomes a heading. Some tags like &lt;br&gt; are self-closing and don\'t need a closing tag.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><p>Create a new file called <strong>my-first-page.html</strong> using your text editor</p></li>\n  \n  <li><p>Add the document type declaration at the top:</p>\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code></pre>\n  <p>This tells browsers to use HTML5 standards</p></li>\n  \n  <li><p>Add the basic HTML structure:</p>\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;My First Web Page&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n  <p>The &lt;title&gt; tag sets what appears in the browser tab</p></li>\n  \n  <li><p>Add content inside the body:</p>\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;h1&gt;Welcome to My Website&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> a paragraph of text.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;a href=\'https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//example.com\'&gt;Visit Example&lt;/a&gt;</span>\n&lt;img src=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'image.jpg\'</span> alt=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Description\'</span>&gt;</code></pre>\n  <p>This adds a heading, paragraph, link, and image</p></li>\n  \n  <li><p>Save the file and open it in your browser to see your page</p></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit your <strong>my-first-page.html</strong> file with:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Correct  declaration</li>\n  <li>Proper HTML structure with &lt;html&gt;, &lt;head&gt;, and &lt;body&gt; tags</li>\n  <li>At least one heading, paragraph, link, and image</li>\n  <li>Meaningful text in the &lt;title&gt; tag</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Forgetting the  declaration at the top</li>\n  <li>Mismatched or missing closing tags like missing &lt;/h1&gt;</li>\n  <li>Using double quotes (\"), always use single quotes (\') in HTML attributes</li>\n  <li>Putting content directly inside &lt;html&gt; without &lt;body&gt;</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Use a code editor:</strong> Tools like VS Code highlight matching tags to help you avoid errors</li>\n  <li><strong>Validate in browser:</strong> Press F12 to open developer tools and check for HTML errors</li>\n  <li><strong>Start simple:</strong> Begin with one element at a time when learning new tags</li>\n  <li><strong>Indent consistently:</strong> Use 2 spaces per level to make your code easy to read</li>\n</ul>', 'Introduction to HTML', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 10:51:28');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(928, 941, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: HTML structure example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=HTML+structure+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/HTML+structure+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/HTML+structure+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/HTML+structure+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=HTML+structure+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you will build a simple webpage with core HTML elements. This is essential for organizing content clearly on any website, like creating sections for navigation, articles, or product listings.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n\n<h4>1. HTML Head and Body Elements</h4>\n<p>HTML documents have two main sections: the <strong>&lt;head&gt;</strong> and <strong>&lt;body&gt;</strong>. Think of the &lt;head&gt; as a recipe’s ingredients list (metadata like page title) and the &lt;body&gt; as the actual dish (visible content).</p>\n\n<h4>2. Headings and Paragraphs</h4>\n<p>Headings (<strong>&lt;h1&gt;</strong> to &lt;h6&gt;) are like chapter titles in a book. Paragraphs (&lt;p&gt;) are blocks of text, like sentences in a story. &lt;h1&gt; is the most important heading (usually one per page).</p>\n\n<h4>3. Links and Images</h4>\n<p>Links (&lt;a&gt;) let users jump to other pages, like roads connecting cities. Images (&lt;img&gt;) display pictures but need a <em>source URL</em> (like a photo’s location).</p>\n\n<h4>4. Lists and Tables</h4>\n<p>Lists (&lt;ul&gt; for bullet points, &lt;ol&gt; for numbered items) organize related items. Tables (&lt;table&gt;) display structured data like a spreadsheet, with rows and columns.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>Create a new file called <code>index.html</code> and open it in a code editor.</li>\n  <li>Write the basic HTML structure:\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;My First Webpage&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n  <p>This wraps your page in the main HTML tags. The &lt;title&gt; sets what appears in the browser tab.</p>\n\n  <li>Add headings and paragraphs:\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;h1&gt;Welcome to My Site&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> a paragraph explaining the page’s purpose.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>\n  <p>Place this inside the &lt;body&gt;. The &lt;h1&gt; is the main heading, followed by a paragraph.</p>\n\n  <li>Add a link to another page:\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;a href=\'https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//example.com\'&gt;Visit Example&lt;/a&gt;</span></code></pre>\n  <p>This creates a clickable link labeled “Visit Example.” The <code>href</code> attribute is the destination URL.</p>\n\n  <li>Add an image:\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;img src=\'https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//example.com/image.jpg\' alt=\'Description\' /&gt;</span></code></pre>\n  <p>The <code>src</code> is the image location. The <code>alt</code> describes the image for accessibility and when it fails to load.</p>\n\n  <li>Create a bullet list:\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre>\n  <p>Use &lt;ul&gt; for unordered (bulleted) lists and &lt;li&gt; for each item.</p>\n\n  <li>Create a numbered list:\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;ol&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Step <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Step <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ol&gt;</code></pre>\n  <p>Use &lt;ol&gt; for ordered (numbered) lists. The browser automatically numbers the items.</p>\n\n  <li>Add a table:\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;table&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n    &lt;th&gt;Month&lt;/th&gt;\n    &lt;th&gt;Sales&lt;/th&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n    &lt;td&gt;January&lt;/td&gt;\n    &lt;td&gt;$<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">100</span>&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n&lt;/table&gt;</code></pre>\n  <p>&lt;tr&gt; creates a table row, &lt;th&gt; for header cells (bolded), and &lt;td&gt; for regular cells.</p>\n\n  <li>Preview your page in a browser to check all elements work.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit your complete <code>index.html</code> file showing all elements from the task. Include at least one heading, paragraph, link, image, list, and table.</p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Forgetting to close tags (e.g., missing &lt;/p&gt; after a paragraph).</li>\n  <li>Using double quotes (<code>&quot;</code>) instead of single quotes in attributes.</li>\n  <li>Placing images or links outside the &lt;body&gt; section.</li>\n  <li>Mixing up &lt;ul&gt; and &lt;ol&gt; for lists.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><blockquote>Indent nested elements (e.g., &lt;li&gt; inside &lt;ul&gt;) for readability.</blockquote></li>\n  <li><blockquote>Always add an <code>alt</code> attribute to images for accessibility.</blockquote></li>\n  <li><blockquote>Use &lt;h1&gt; only once per page as the main heading.</blockquote></li>\n</ul>', 'Basic HTML Structure and Syntax', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(929, 942, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2fda0279dc2_1781520898.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today, you will learn about text elements in web development, which are essential for creating content on websites. You will understand how to use headings, paragraphs, line breaks, text formatting, and special characters to make your web pages more engaging and user-friendly. Mastering these skills will help you build professional-looking websites and make you a more competitive web developer in the job market.</p><h3>Concepts you need</h3><p>Let\'s start with the basics. In web development, text elements are used to add content to a website. Think of them like the words and sentences you write in a document, but with more formatting options. There are several key concepts to understand:</p><ul><li><strong>Headings and Subheadings</strong>: These are like titles and subtitles in a book. They help organize content and make it easier to read. In web development, we use &lt;h1&gt;, &lt;h2&gt;, &lt;h3&gt;, &lt;h4&gt;, &lt;h5&gt;, and &lt;h6&gt; tags to create headings, with &lt;h1&gt; being the most important and &lt;h6&gt; being the least important.</li><li><strong>Paragraphs and Line Breaks</strong>: These are like the paragraphs and lines in a letter. We use &lt;p&gt; tags to create paragraphs and &lt;br&gt; tags to create line breaks.</li><li><strong>Text Formatting and Styles</strong>: These are like the bold, italic, and underline options in a word processor. We use &lt;b&gt;, &lt;i&gt;, and &lt;u&gt; tags to make text bold, italic, and underlined, respectively.</li><li><strong>Special Characters and Entities</strong>: These are like special symbols in a document, such as copyright symbols and arrows. We use &amp;#xxx; or &amp;name; to represent these characters in web development, where xxx is the character\'s Unicode code point and name is the character\'s name.</li></ul><h3>Step-by-step task</h3><ol><li>Create a new file called index.html and add the basic structure: &lt;html&gt;, &lt;head&gt;, and &lt;body&gt; tags.</li><li>Add a heading using the &lt;h1&gt; tag: &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to my website&lt;/h1&gt;.</li><li>Add a paragraph using the &lt;p&gt; tag: &lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph of text.&lt;/p&gt;.</li><li>Add a line break using the &lt;br&gt; tag: &lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph of text.&lt;br&gt;This is another line of text.&lt;/p&gt;.</li><li>Format some text using the &lt;b&gt;, &lt;i&gt;, and &lt;u&gt; tags: &lt;p&gt;This text is &lt;b&gt;bold&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;i&gt;italic&lt;/i&gt;, and &lt;u&gt;underlined&lt;/u&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;.</li><li>Add a special character using the &amp;#xxx; or &amp;name; syntax: &lt;p&gt;Copyright &amp;#169; 2024.&lt;/p&gt;.</li></ol><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;<br>&lt;head&gt;<br>&lt;title&gt;My Website&lt;/title&gt;<br>&lt;/head&gt;<br>&lt;body&gt;<br>&lt;h1&gt;Welcome to my website&lt;/h1&gt;<br>&lt;p&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> a paragraph of text.&lt;/p&gt;<br>&lt;p&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> a paragraph of text.&lt;br&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> another line of text.&lt;/p&gt;<br>&lt;p&gt;This text <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> &lt;b&gt;bold&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;i&gt;italic&lt;/i&gt;, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> &lt;u&gt;underlined&lt;/u&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;<br>&lt;p&gt;Copyright &amp;<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#169; 2024.&lt;/p&gt;<br>&lt;/body&gt;<br>&lt;/html&gt;<br></span></code></pre><h3>Deliverable</h3><p>Submit your completed index.html file, which should include all the text elements and formatting options covered in today\'s task.</p><h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3><ul><li>Forgetting to close tags, which can cause formatting issues and errors.</li><li>Using the wrong tag for a specific text element, such as using &lt;b&gt; for a heading instead of &lt;h1&gt;.</li><li>Not using special characters and entities correctly, which can result in incorrect display or errors.</li><li>Not testing your code in different browsers and devices to ensure compatibility.</li></ul><h3>Pro tips</h3><ul><li>Use headings and subheadings consistently to organize your content and improve accessibility.</li><li>Use text formatting and styles sparingly to avoid overwhelming the user and to maintain a clear visual hierarchy.</li><li>Test your code regularly to catch errors and ensure that your website looks and functions as intended.</li></ul><blockquote>Remember, practice makes perfect. The more you work with text elements and formatting options, the more comfortable you will become with using them to create engaging and user-friendly websites.</blockquote>', 'HTML Text Elements', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 10:56:45'),
(930, 943, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2fdb5535ec6_1781521237.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today, you will learn about links and anchors in web development. You will build a simple web page with links to other pages, email addresses, and phone numbers. Understanding how to create links is essential for a real job as a web developer because it allows you to connect different parts of your website and make it easy for users to navigate.</p><h3>Concepts you need</h3><p>Let\'s start with the basics. A <strong>hyperlink</strong>, or simply <strong>link</strong>, is a clickable element on a web page that takes you to another page, website, or location on the same page. Think of it like a map that shows you how to get from one place to another.</p><p>A <strong>link syntax</strong> is the way you write a link in your code. It consists of the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag, which is short for <strong>anchor</strong>. The <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag has several <strong>attributes</strong>, which are like properties that provide more information about the link. The most important attribute is <code>href</code>, which stands for <strong>hypertext reference</strong> and specifies the URL of the page you want to link to.</p><p>Another important concept is <strong>link targets</strong>. This refers to where the linked page will open when you click on the link. You can set the target to be a new window, a new tab, or the same window. This is useful when you want to keep the user on the same page, but also provide them with more information.</p><p><strong>Link relations</strong> describe the relationship between the current page and the linked page. For example, you can specify that the linked page is a stylesheet, a script, or a license agreement.</p><p>Finally, let\'s talk about <strong>email and phone links</strong>. These are special types of links that allow users to send an email or make a phone call directly from your website. Email links use the <code>mailto:</code> protocol, while phone links use the <code>tel:</code> protocol.</p><h3>Step-by-step task</h3><ol><li>Create a new file called <code>index.html</code> and add the basic structure of an HTML document, including the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> tags.</li><li>Add a link to another page called <code>about.html</code> using the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag. The link should have the text <code>Learn more about us</code> and should open in the same window. The code should look like this: <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;a href=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'about.html\'</span>&gt;Learn more about us&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre></li><li>Add a link to an email address using the <code>mailto:</code> protocol. The link should have the text <code>Send us an email</code> and should open in the default email client. The code should look like this: <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;a href=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'mailto:info@example.com\'</span>&gt;Send us an email&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre></li><li>Add a link to a phone number using the <code>tel:</code> protocol. The link should have the text <code>Call us</code> and should open in the default phone app. The code should look like this: <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;a href=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'tel:1234567890\'</span>&gt;Call us&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre></li><li>Test your links by opening the <code>index.html</code> file in a web browser and clicking on each link.</li></ol><h3>Deliverable</h3><p>Your task is to create a simple web page with links to another page, an email address, and a phone number. The page should have the following structure:</p><ul><li>A link to another page called <code>about.html</code> with the text <code>Learn more about us</code></li><li>A link to an email address with the text <code>Send us an email</code></li><li>A link to a phone number with the text <code>Call us</code></li></ul><h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3><ul><li>Forgetting to close the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag, which can cause the link to not work properly.</li><li>Using the wrong protocol for email or phone links, which can cause the link to not work at all.</li><li>Not testing your links, which can cause you to miss errors or broken links.</li><li>Not using the <code>href</code> attribute, which is required for the link to work.</li></ul><h3>Pro tips</h3><ul><li>Use descriptive text for your links, rather than just using the URL as the text. This makes it easier for users to understand where the link will take them.</li><li>Use the <code>title</code> attribute to provide a tooltip or additional information about the link.</li><li>Consider using an <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag with an <code>alt</code> attribute to provide a visual representation of the link, especially for users who may have difficulty reading text.</li></ul><blockquote>Remember to always test your links and make sure they are working as expected. This will help you catch any errors or broken links before your website goes live.</blockquote>', 'HTML Links and Anchors', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 11:00:58'),
(931, 944, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2fe93659a53_1781524790.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Day 10: HTML Images and Multimedia</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 10 of our web development internship. Today, we\'re going to explore how to add images and multimedia to our web pages. This is a crucial skill for any web developer, as images and multimedia can make our web pages more engaging and interactive.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need:</h3>\n<h4>1. Introduction to HTML Images</h4>\n<p>HTML images are a way to add pictures to our web pages. Just like how we use words to convey our message, images can help us convey a message in a more visual way. Think of it like a picture book - instead of reading a story, we can look at pictures and understand the story.</p>\n<h4>2. Image Syntax and Attributes</h4>\n<p>When we add an image to our web page, we use the <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag. The <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag has several attributes that we can use to customize the image. Some common attributes include <code>src</code> (source), <code>alt</code> (alternative text), and <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> (image size).</p>\n<h4>3. Image Mapping and Accessibility</h4>\n<p>Image mapping is a technique where we can create a clickable area on an image. This can be useful for creating interactive images, such as a map with clickable regions. Accessibility is also an important consideration when adding images to our web pages. We should make sure that our images have alternative text, so that screen readers can read the text to users who are visually impaired.</p>\n<h4>4. Audio and Video Elements</h4>\n<p>Audio and video elements are used to add multimedia to our web pages. We can use the <code>&lt;audio&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> tags to add audio and video to our web pages. These elements have several attributes that we can use to customize the audio and video, such as <code>src</code> (source), <code>controls</code> (whether to display controls), and <code>autoplay</code> (whether to play the audio or video automatically).</p>\n<h3>Step-by-step task:</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new HTML file called <code>image.html</code> and add the following code:<code>\n&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Image Example&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Image Example&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;img src=\'image.jpg\' alt=\'A picture of a cat\' width=\'200\' height=\'200\'&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code>\n<p>This code adds an image to our web page with the source <code>image.jpg</code>, alternative text <code>A picture of a cat</code>, and a width and height of 200 pixels.</p>\n</li><li>Add an image map to the image by adding the following code inside the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> tag:<code>\n&lt;map name=\'map\'&gt;\n  &lt;area shape=\'rect\' coords=\'10,10,50,50\' href=\'#.\'&gt;\n&lt;/map&gt;\n</code>\n<p>This code adds an image map with a rectangular shape and coordinates (10,10) to (50,50). The <code>href</code> attribute is set to <code>#.</code> to make the image map clickable.</p>\n</li><li>Add an audio element to our web page by adding the following code inside the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> tag:<code>\n&lt;audio controls&gt;\n  &lt;source src=\'audio.mp3\' type=\'audio/mpeg\'&gt;\n  Your browser does not support the audio element.\n&lt;/audio&gt;\n</code>\n<p>This code adds an audio element with the source <code>audio.mp3</code> and type <code>audio/mpeg</code>. The <code>controls</code> attribute is set to <code>true</code> to display controls for the audio element.</p>\n</li><li>Add a video element to our web page by adding the following code inside the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> tag:<code>\n&lt;video width=\'320\' height=\'240\' controls&gt;\n  &lt;source src=\'video.mp4\' type=\'video/mp4\'&gt;\n  Your browser does not support the video element.\n&lt;/video&gt;\n</code>\n<p>This code adds a video element with the source <code>video.mp4</code> and type <code>video/mp4</code>. The <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> attributes are set to 320 and 240 pixels respectively. The <code>controls</code> attribute is set to <code>true</code> to display controls for the video element.</p>\n</li></ol>\n<h3>Deliverable:</h3>\n<p>Today, you should create a new HTML file called <code>image.html</code> and add the code from the step-by-step task. You should also make sure that the image, audio, and video elements work correctly in your browser.</p>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Not adding alternative text to images, which can make it difficult for screen readers to read the text.</li>\n<li>Not using the <code>controls</code> attribute for audio and video elements, which can make it difficult for users to control the playback.</li>\n<li>Not testing the image, audio, and video elements in different browsers and devices, which can make it difficult to identify and fix issues.</li>\n<li>Not optimizing the image, audio, and video files for web use, which can make them slow to load and consume too much bandwidth.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use the <code>alt</code> attribute to add alternative text to images, which can make it easier for screen readers to read the text.</li>\n<li>Use the <code>controls</code> attribute to display controls for audio and video elements, which can make it easier for users to control the playback.</li>\n<li>Use the <code>autoplay</code> attribute to play audio and video elements automatically, which can make it easier for users to start playing the content.</li>\n<li>Use the <code>loop</code> attribute to play audio and video elements continuously, which can make it easier for users to listen or watch the content continuously.</li>\n</ul>', 'HTML Images and Multimedia', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 12:36:46'),
(932, 945, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2ff24d77ad5_1781527117.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>HTML Lists and Tables</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 11 of your web development internship! Today, we\'re going to learn about HTML lists and tables. These are essential elements in building a website, and you\'ll use them frequently in your future projects.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>Before we dive into the task, let\'s cover the necessary concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ordered and Unordered Lists</strong>: These are lists that display items in a specific order. Ordered lists are numbered, while unordered lists are bullet-pointed.</li>\n<li><strong>List Styles and Attributes</strong>: We\'ll learn how to customize the appearance of our lists using styles and attributes.</li>\n<li><strong>Introduction to HTML Tables</strong>: Tables are used to display data in a table format. We\'ll cover the basic structure and syntax of HTML tables.</li>\n<li><strong>Table Structure and Syntax</strong>: We\'ll dive deeper into the structure and syntax of HTML tables, including rows, columns, and cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let\'s start with ordered and unordered lists. Think of a list of items you need to buy at the grocery store. You can either number the items or use bullet points. That\'s the difference between ordered and unordered lists.</p>\n\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<p>For this task, you\'ll create a simple webpage that displays a list of items and a table with some data. Follow these steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Start by creating a new HTML file called <code>&lt;a href=\'lists_and_tables.html\'&gt;lists_and_tables.html&lt;/a&gt;</code>.</li>\n<li>Create an unordered list of your favorite foods. Use the <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> tag to create the list, and the <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> tag to create each list item. For example:<code>\n&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Pizza&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Sushi&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Tacos&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;\n</code>\n<p>This will create a bullet-pointed list of your favorite foods.</p>\n</li><li>Create an ordered list of your favorite sports. Use the <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> tag to create the list, and the <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> tag to create each list item. For example:<code>\n&lt;ol&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Soccer&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Basketball&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Football&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ol&gt;\n</code>\n<p>This will create a numbered list of your favorite sports.</p>\n</li><li>Now, let\'s create a table with some data. Start by creating a table with two columns and three rows:<code>\n&lt;table&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n    &lt;th&gt;Name&lt;/th&gt;\n    &lt;th&gt;Age&lt;/th&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n    &lt;td&gt;John Doe&lt;/td&gt;\n    &lt;td&gt;25&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n    &lt;td&gt;Jane Doe&lt;/td&gt;\n    &lt;td&gt;30&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n&lt;/table&gt;\n</code>\n<p>This will create a basic table with two columns and three rows.</p>\n</li><li>Finally, let\'s add some styles to our list and table. We can use the <code>style</code> attribute to add some basic styling. For example:<code>\n&lt;ul style=\'color: blue;\'&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Pizza&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Sushi&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Tacos&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;\n</code>\n<p>This will change the text color of the list to blue.</p>\n</li></ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>For this task, you should submit a single HTML file called <code>lists_and_tables.html</code> that contains the following elements:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An unordered list of your favorite foods</li>\n<li>An ordered list of your favorite sports</li>\n<li>A table with two columns and three rows</li>\n<li>Basic styling using the <code>style</code> attribute</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<p>Here are some common mistakes to avoid when working with lists and tables:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Not closing list items (<code>&lt;/li&gt;</code>) or table rows (<code>&lt;/tr&gt;</code>)</li>\n<li>Using the wrong list type (<code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>)</li>\n<li>Not using table headers (<code>&lt;th&gt;</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<p>Here are some pro tips for working with lists and tables:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use the <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> tag for unordered lists and the <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> tag for ordered lists.</li>\n<li>Use the <code>&lt;th&gt;</code> tag for table headers and the <code>&lt;td&gt;</code> tag for table cells.</li>\n<li>Use the <code>style</code> attribute to add basic styling to your lists and tables.</li>\n</ul><br>', 'HTML Lists and Tables', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 12:24:57'),
(933, 946, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Table with headers and footers</p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&amp;q=Table+with+headers+and+footers\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Table+with+headers+and+footers\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Table+with+headers+and+footers/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Table+with+headers+and+footers/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Table+with+headers+and+footers\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\n</div></div><h3>HTML Table Advanced Topics</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ll learn how to add headers and footers to tables, style them, and make them accessible to everyone. This is a crucial skill for any web developer, as tables are often used to display data and information in a structured way.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>Before we start, let\'s cover some essential concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Table Headers and Footers</strong>: These are rows that contain labels or titles for the columns or rows in a table. Think of them like a table of contents.</li>\n  <li><strong>Table Rows and Columns</strong>: These are the individual units that make up a table. Rows are horizontal, while columns are vertical.</li>\n  <li><strong>Table Styles and Attributes</strong>: These are used to customize the appearance and behavior of a table. Think of them like a set of instructions for how to display the table.</li>\n  <li><strong>Table Accessibility</strong>: This is the practice of making sure that tables are usable by everyone, including people with disabilities. This includes using alternative text for images, providing clear labels, and making sure the table can be navigated using a keyboard.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<p>Now that we\'ve covered the concepts, let\'s create a table that demonstrates each of these features. Follow these steps:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Create a basic table with two rows and two columns:<code>&lt;table&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n  &lt;td&gt;Column 1&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;td&gt;Column 2&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n  &lt;td&gt;Row 1&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;td&gt;Row 2&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;/table&gt;</code></li>\n  <li>Add a table header to the first row:<code>&lt;table&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n  &lt;th&gt;Column 1&lt;/th&gt;\n  &lt;th&gt;Column 2&lt;/th&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n  &lt;td&gt;Row 1&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;td&gt;Row 2&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;/table&gt;</code></li>\n  <li>Add a table footer to the last row:<code>&lt;table&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n  &lt;th&gt;Column 1&lt;/th&gt;\n  &lt;th&gt;Column 2&lt;/th&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n  &lt;td&gt;Row 1&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;td&gt;Row 2&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n  &lt;td colspan=\'2\'&gt;Table footer&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;/table&gt;</code></li>\n  <li>Add some basic styles to the table:<code>&lt;style&gt;\n  table {\n  border-collapse: collapse;\n  }\n  &lt;/style&gt;\n  &lt;table&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n  &lt;th&gt;Column 1&lt;/th&gt;\n  &lt;th&gt;Column 2&lt;/th&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n  &lt;td&gt;Row 1&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;td&gt;Row 2&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n  &lt;td colspan=\'2\'&gt;Table footer&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;/table&gt;</code></li>\n  <li>Add some alternative text to the table:<code>&lt;table&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n  &lt;th&gt;Column 1&lt;/th&gt;\n  &lt;th&gt;Column 2&lt;/th&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n  &lt;td&gt;Row 1&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;td&gt;Row 2&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;\n  &lt;td colspan=\'2\'&gt;Table footer&lt;/td&gt;\n  &lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;!-- alternative text for screen readers --&gt;\n  &lt;figcaption&gt;This is a table with two columns and two rows.&lt;/figcaption&gt;\n  &lt;/table&gt;</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>Today, you should submit a table that demonstrates each of the features we covered. This should include:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A table header with two columns and two rows.</li>\n  <li>A table footer with two columns and one row.</li>\n  <li>Borders and basic styles to make the table look nice.</li>\n  <li>Alternative text for screen readers to make the table accessible.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<p>Here are some common mistakes to watch out for:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Forgetting to add a table header or footer</strong>: Make sure to include both a header and a footer in your table.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not using alternative text for screen readers</strong>: Don\'t forget to add alternative text to make your table accessible.</li>\n  <li><strong>Using tables for layout</strong>: Tables should be used for displaying data, not for layout. Avoid using tables to create complex layouts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not testing for accessibility</strong>: Make sure to test your table for accessibility using a screen reader or other accessibility tools.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<p>Here are some pro tips to help you master tables:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Use the `border-collapse` property to make borders look nice</strong>: This property helps to make borders look nice and consistent.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use the `colspan` attribute to merge cells</strong>: This attribute helps to merge cells and create a nice layout.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use the `figcaption` element to add alternative text</strong>: This element helps to add alternative text for screen readers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><br></h3>', 'HTML Table Advanced Topics', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 12:24:36');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(934, 947, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><br></p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\n<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2ff331e8507_1781527345.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"><br>\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you will build a complete HTML form that collects user data, validates it, and is accessible to everyone. In a real job, forms are the main way a website talks to its users, so mastering them is essential.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HTML Form</strong> – Think of a form as a paper questionnaire that you can fill out on a computer. It sends data back to a server when you press submit.</li>\n<li><strong>Form Syntax</strong> – A form is created with the <code>form</code> tag, and you can set where the data goes with the <code>action</code> attribute and how it is sent with the <code>method</code> attribute.</li>\n<li><strong>Form Attributes</strong> – <code>action</code>, <code>method</code>, <code>enctype</code>, <code>target</code>, and <code>novalidate</code> control the form’s behavior.</li>\n<li><strong>Input Types</strong> – Each <code>input</code> element can be a text box, a checkbox, a radio button, a file picker, etc. The <code>type</code> attribute tells the browser what kind of data to expect.</li>\n<li><strong>Input Attributes</strong> – <code>placeholder</code>, <code>required</code>, <code>minlength</code>, <code>maxlength</code>, <code>pattern</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>step</code>, and <code>autocomplete</code> help guide the user and enforce rules.</li>\n<li><strong>Form Validation</strong> – The browser can automatically check that required fields are filled or that an email looks like an email before letting you submit.</li>\n<li><strong>Accessibility</strong> – Using <code>label</code>, <code>aria-*</code> attributes, and semantic HTML makes the form usable for screen readers and people with disabilities.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Create the file</strong>\n<p>Open your code editor and create a new file called <code>form-example.html</code>. Add the basic HTML skeleton:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'utf-8\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Form Example&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;!<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">-- Your form will go here --&gt;</span>\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Add the form element</strong>\n<p>Inside <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> insert a <code>form</code> tag. Use <code>action</code> to point to a placeholder URL and <code>method</code> to use POST:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;form action=\'https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//example.com/submit\' method=\'post\'&gt;</span>\n  &lt;!<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">-- form fields go here --&gt;</span>\n&lt;/form&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>This tells the browser where to send the data when the user clicks submit.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Add a text input for the name</strong>\n<p>Use a <code>label</code> for accessibility and an <code>input</code> of type text. The <code>for</code> attribute on the label must match the <code>id</code> of the input:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'name\'</span>&gt;Full Name:&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'text\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'name\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'name\'</span> placeholder=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'John Doe\'</span> required&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>The <code>required</code> attribute makes the browser refuse to submit if this field is empty.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Add an email input</strong>\n<p>Use <code>type=\'email\'</code> so the browser checks the format. Add a <code>placeholder</code> and <code>required</code>:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'email\'</span>&gt;Email:&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'email\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'email\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'email\'</span> placeholder=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'you@example.com\'</span> required&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Add a password input</strong>\n<p>Passwords should be hidden. Use <code>type=\'password\'</code> and set a minimum length:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'password\'</span>&gt;Password:&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'password\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'password\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'password\'</span> minlength=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'8\'</span> required&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Add a number input for age</strong>\n<p>Use <code>type=\'number\'</code> and set <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> to restrict values:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'age\'</span>&gt;Age:&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'number\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'age\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'age\'</span> min=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'18\'</span> max=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'99\'</span> required&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Add a date input for birthdate</strong>\n<p>Use <code>type=\'date\'</code> so the browser shows a calendar picker:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'dob\'</span>&gt;Date of Birth:&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'date\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'dob\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'dob\'</span> required&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Add a textarea for comments</strong>\n<p>For longer text, use <code>textarea</code>:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'comments\'</span>&gt;Comments:&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;textarea id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'comments\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'comments\'</span> rows=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'4\'</span> cols=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'50\'</span> placeholder=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Your thoughts...\'</span>&gt;&lt;/textarea&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Add checkboxes for interests</strong>\n<p>Group related checkboxes with a fieldset:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;fieldset&gt;\n  &lt;legend&gt;Interests&lt;/legend&gt;\n  &lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'music\'</span>&gt;&lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'checkbox\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'music\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'interests\'</span> value=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'music\'</span>&gt; Music&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'sports\'</span>&gt;&lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'checkbox\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'sports\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'interests\'</span> value=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'sports\'</span>&gt; Sports&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'reading\'</span>&gt;&lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'checkbox\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'reading\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'interests\'</span> value=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'reading\'</span>&gt; Reading&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;/fieldset&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Add radio buttons for gender</strong>\n<p>Radio buttons let the user pick one option:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;fieldset&gt;\n  &lt;legend&gt;Gender&lt;/legend&gt;\n  &lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'male\'</span>&gt;&lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'radio\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'male\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'gender\'</span> value=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'male\'</span> required&gt; Male&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'female\'</span>&gt;&lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'radio\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'female\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'gender\'</span> value=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'female\'</span> required&gt; Female&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'other\'</span>&gt;&lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'radio\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'other\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'gender\'</span> value=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'other\'</span> required&gt; Other&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;/fieldset&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Add a file input for profile picture</strong>\n<p>Use <code>enctype=\'multipart/form-data\'</code> on the form to allow file uploads. Add an input of type file:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'profile\'</span>&gt;Profile Picture:&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'file\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'profile\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'profile\'</span> accept=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'image/*\'</span>&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Add a submit button</strong>\n<p>Finish the form with a button that submits the data:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;button <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'submit\'</span>&gt;Send&lt;/button&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Enhance accessibility</strong>\n<p>Wrap each input in a <code>label</code> or use <code>aria-label</code> if the visual label is missing. For example, add <code>aria-describedby</code> to the email field to reference a small help text:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'email\'</span>&gt;Email:&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'email\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'email\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'email\'</span> aria-describedby=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'emailHelp\'</span> required&gt;\n&lt;small id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'emailHelp\'</span>&gt;We\'ll never share your email.&lt;/small&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Test the form</strong>\n<p>Open the file in a browser, try submitting with missing fields, wrong email format, and see the built‑in validation messages. Make sure the form submits to the placeholder URL (you won’t see a real response, but the request should go out).</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit a single file named <code>form-example.html</code> that contains a fully functional form with all the input types, validation rules, and accessibility features described above.</p>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Using double quotes inside attribute values – always use single quotes.</li>\n<li>Missing <code>label</code> tags or mismatching <code>for</code> and <code>id</code> – this breaks screen‑reader navigation.</li>\n<li>Forgetting the <code>required</code> attribute on mandatory fields – the form will submit empty data.</li>\n<li>Not escaping special characters in code examples – this can break the HTML rendering.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<blockquote>\n  <p><strong>Use <code>autocomplete</code></strong> to let the browser remember common values and speed up form filling.</p>\n  <p><strong>Group related inputs with <code>fieldset</code> and <code>legend</code></strong> for better semantics and accessibility.</p>\n  <p><strong>Test across browsers</strong> because some older browsers may not support newer input types like <code>date</code> or <code>email</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>', 'HTML Forms and Input Types', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 12:43:11'),
(935, 948, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: html form validation example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=html+form+validation+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/html+form+validation+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/html+form+validation+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/html+form+validation+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=html+form+validation+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you\'ll build a complete contact form with advanced features like validation, security, and error handling. This is one of the most common tasks for real web developers - every website needs forms for user interaction!</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n\n<h4>Form Labels and Placeholders</h4>\n<p><strong>Labels</strong> are like road signs - they tell users what information to provide. <strong>Placeholders</strong> are like example notes in a notebook that disappear when you start writing.</p>\n\n<h4>Form Buttons and Submit</h4>\n<p>A <code>submit</code> button is like a doorbell - when you press it, the form sends its information to a server. Regular buttons can do other actions, but submit buttons are special.</p>\n\n<h4>Form Validation</h4>\n<p>Validation is like a bouncer at a club checking IDs. HTML5 has built-in validation that checks if users filled things in correctly before sending the form.</p>\n\n<h4>Error Handling</h4>\n<p>Error messages are like friendly corrections. When validation fails, browsers show messages like \"Please fill this out\" to help users fix mistakes.</p>\n\n<h4>Form Security</h4>\n<p>Security is like a lock on your door. We\'ll add basic protections against common attacks and learn how to mark sensitive fields correctly.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>Create a new HTML file and add this basic form structure:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;form action=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'process-form.php\'</span> method=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'POST\'</span> novalidate&gt;\n      &lt;!<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">-- Fields will go here --&gt;</span>\n    &lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>Explanation: <code>action</code> tells where to send data, <code>method</code> specifies how, and <code>novalidate</code> disables browser validation temporarily</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>Add a name field with label and placeholder:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'name\'</span>&gt;Full Name*&lt;/label&gt;\n    &lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'text\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'name\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'name\'</span> placeholder=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'John Doe\'</span> required&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>Explanation: <code>for</code> and <code>id</code> connect label to input, <code>required</code> makes it mandatory</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>Add email field with validation pattern:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'email\'</span>&gt;Email*&lt;/label&gt;\n    &lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'email\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'email\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'email\'</span> \n           placeholder=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'your@email.com\'</span> required\n           pattern=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'[a-z0-9._%+-]+@[a-z0-9.-]+\\.[a-z]{2,}$\'</span>&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>Explanation: <code>type=\'email\'</code> checks basic format, <code>pattern</code> adds custom regex validation</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>Add a message textarea:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'message\'</span>&gt;Your Message&lt;/label&gt;\n    &lt;textarea id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'message\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'message\'</span> rows=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'5\'</span>&gt;&lt;/textarea&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>Explanation: <code>textarea</code> lets users write multiple lines</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>Add submit button:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;button <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'submit\'</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'submit-btn\'</span>&gt;Send Message&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>Explanation: <code>type=\'submit\'</code> is required for the button to work correctly</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>Test validation by leaving fields empty and clicking submit</li>\n\n  <li>Add CSRF protection (concept only):\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'hidden\'</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'csrf_token\'</span> value=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'server-generated-token\'</span>&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>Explanation: This hidden field helps protect against fake form submissions</p>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n\n<p>Submit your complete HTML file named <code>contact-form.html</code> that includes:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Name field with label and placeholder</li>\n  <li>Email field with validation</li>\n  <li>Message textarea</li>\n  <li>Submit button</li>\n  <li>At least one validation rule</li>\n  <li>CSRF protection field</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Forgetting to connect labels with matching <code>for</code> and <code>id</code> attributes</li>\n  <li>Using double quotes inside HTML attributes (always use single quotes in this task)</li>\n  <li>Mixing up <code>required</code> and <code>pattern</code> attributes</li>\n  <li>Not testing form validation by submitting empty fields</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><blockquote>Use browser developer tools (F12) to inspect form elements and see validation messages</blockquote></li>\n  <li><blockquote>Always include labels - they improve accessibility and user experience</blockquote></li>\n  <li><blockquote>Use <code>type=\'email\'</code> instead of regular text fields for email addresses</blockquote></li>\n</ul>', 'HTML Form Advanced Topics', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(936, 949, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><br></p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&amp;q=HTML+semantic+elements+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Imag<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2ff3aba2b6c_1781527467.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></a>\n<br>\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you\'ll build a complete webpage using HTML semantic elements to organize content clearly. Semantic HTML helps search engines, screen readers, and developers understand your page structure—this is essential for professional web projects.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n\n<h4>What is Semantic HTML?</h4>\n<p>Semantic HTML means using tags that describe their meaning, not just their appearance. Think of it like labeling boxes in a storage room: <code>&lt;header&gt;</code> is like a \"Front Door\" label, while <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> is a \"Map\" to other rooms.</p>\n\n<h4>Header and Nav Elements</h4>\n<p>The <code>&lt;header&gt;</code> contains introductory content (like a book cover), and <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> holds navigation links (like a table of contents). Together they help users find their way around your website.</p>\n\n<h4>Main and Section Elements</h4>\n<p><code>&lt;main&gt;</code> is the central content area (the book\'s body), while <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> divides it into logical parts (like chapters). Each section can have its own heading.</p>\n\n<h4>Article and Aside Elements</h4>\n<p><code>&lt;article&gt;</code> is for self-contained content (like a blog post), and <code>&lt;aside&gt;</code> holds related but independent content (like sidebars with extra info). They help separate primary vs supporting material.</p>\n\n<h4>Footer Element</h4>\n<p><code>&lt;footer&gt;</code> contains closing information (like a book\'s back cover)—copyright, author, or links. It always appears at the bottom of a page or section.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>Create a new HTML file named <strong>semantic.html</strong></li>\n  <li>Write the basic HTML structure:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'UTF-8\'</span>&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;My Semantic Page&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;!<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">-- Add semantic elements here --&gt;</span>\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>This sets up your document container</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>Add a header with navigation:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;header&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;My Website&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;nav&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/\'</span>&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/about\'</span>&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/nav&gt;\n&lt;/header&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>The <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> element tells browsers these links help navigate the site</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>Create the main content area:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;main&gt;\n  &lt;section&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;Welcome&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> the main content area.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/section&gt;\n&lt;/main&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p><code>&lt;main&gt;</code> should contain your page\'s unique content</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>Add an article and aside:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;article&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;Interesting Topic&lt;/h3&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> a <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">self</span>-contained piece of content.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/article&gt;\n\n&lt;aside&gt;\n  &lt;h4&gt;Did You Know?&lt;/h4&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;Extra information about the main content.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/aside&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p><code>&lt;article&gt;</code> should stand alone, while <code>&lt;aside&gt;</code> provides supplementary info</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>Create a footer:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;footer&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;&amp;copy; <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2024</span> My Website | Contact: example@email.com&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/footer&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>Footers often include legal info and contact details</p>\n</li></ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit your <strong>semantic.html</strong> file showing all semantic elements properly used. Your page should have:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>&lt;header&gt;</code> with site title and navigation</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;main&gt;</code> containing at least one <code>&lt;section&gt;</code></li>\n  <li><code>&lt;article&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;aside&gt;</code> elements</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;footer&gt;</code> with copyright information</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Using <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> instead of semantic elements when the content has clear meaning</li>\n  <li>Mixing up <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;article&gt;</code> – remember <code>&lt;article&gt;</code> is self-contained</li>\n  <li>Forgetting to include a <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> element for the primary content area</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use semantic elements even for simple pages – they improve accessibility and SEO</li>\n  <li>Validate your HTML using <a href=\"https://validator.w3.org/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">W3C Validator</a> to check for errors</li>\n  <li>Think of semantic elements as \"labels\" that help both humans and machines understand your content</li>\n</ul>', 'HTML Semantic Elements', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 12:44:42'),
(937, 950, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2ff47c2b11c_1781527676.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Day 16: Introduction to CSS</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 16 of your web development internship! Today, you\'ll learn the basics of CSS, which stands for Cascading Style Sheets. CSS is used to control the layout and visual styling of web pages. You\'ll learn how to use CSS to make your web pages look professional and engaging.</p>\n<h3>Concepts You Need to Know:</h3>\n<p>CSS is a styling language that is used to control the layout and visual styling of web pages. It\'s like a set of instructions that tells the web browser how to display the content of a web page.</p>\n<h4>CSS Syntax and Basics</h4>\n<p>CSS syntax is made up of three main parts: selectors, properties, and values. A CSS rule consists of a selector, followed by a set of properties and values.</p>\n<p>Here\'s an example of a CSS rule:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">h1 {\n  color: blue;\n  font-size: 36px;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>In this example, <code>h1</code> is the selector, <code>color</code> and <code>font-size</code> are the properties, and <code>blue</code> and <code>36px</code> are the values.</p>\n<p>There are several types of selectors in CSS, including:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Element selectors: These selectors select elements based on their HTML element name.</li>\n  <li>Class selectors: These selectors select elements based on their class attribute.</li>\n  <li>ID selectors: These selectors select elements based on their ID attribute.</li>\n  <li>Pseudo-class selectors: These selectors select elements based on their state or position.</li>\n  <li>Pseudo-element selectors: These selectors select elements based on their position or appearance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>CSS Selectors and Properties</h4>\n<p>CSS selectors are used to select the HTML elements that you want to style. There are several types of selectors, including element selectors, class selectors, ID selectors, pseudo-class selectors, and pseudo-element selectors.</p>\n<p>CSS properties are used to control the styling of the selected elements. There are several types of properties, including:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Color properties: These properties control the color of the text or background.</li>\n  <li>Font properties: These properties control the font face, size, and style.</li>\n  <li>Layout properties: These properties control the layout of the elements, including margin, padding, and border.</li>\n  <li>Background properties: These properties control the background image or color.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>CSS Values and Units</h4>\n<p>CSS values are used to specify the values of the properties. There are several types of values, including:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Length values: These values specify the length of a measurement, such as pixels or inches.</li>\n  <li>Color values: These values specify the color of the text or background.</li>\n  <li>Percentage values: These values specify a percentage value, such as 50% or 75%.</li>\n  <li>Keyword values: These values specify a keyword value, such as <code>inherit</code> or <code>initial</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>CSS Comments and Syntax Rules</h4>\n<p>CSS comments are used to add comments to your CSS code. Comments are surrounded by <code>/*</code> and <code>*/</code> characters.</p>\n<p>CSS syntax rules are used to define the structure and organization of the CSS code. Some common syntax rules include:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Indentation: CSS code should be indented using four spaces.</li>\n  <li>Line breaks: CSS code should use line breaks to separate different rules and properties.</li>\n  <li;property order:=\"\" css=\"\" properties=\"\" should=\"\" be=\"\" listed=\"\" in=\"\" a=\"\" specific=\"\" order,=\"\" such=\"\" as=\"\" color,=\"\" font-size,=\"\" and=\"\" font-family.<=\"\" li=\"\">\n</li;property></ul>\n<h3>Step-by-Step Task:</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li>Create a new HTML file and add a heading element (h1) to it.</li>\n  <li>Add a CSS rule to the CSS file to style the heading element.</li>\n  <li>Use a color property to change the color of the heading element to blue.</li>\n  <li>Use a font-size property to change the font size of the heading element to 36 pixels.</li>\n  <li>Save both the HTML and CSS files and open them in a web browser to see the styled heading element.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable:</h3>\n<p>The deliverable for today\'s task is to create a new HTML file and a new CSS file, and add a CSS rule to style the heading element.</p>\n<h3>Common Mistakes to Avoid:</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Misusing the <code>class</code> attribute in HTML.</li>\n  <li>Misusing the <code>id</code> attribute in HTML.</li>\n  <li>Not using the correct syntax for CSS selectors and properties.</li>\n  <li>Not using the correct values and units for CSS properties.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro Tips:</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use the <code>!important</code> keyword to override CSS properties.</li>\n  <li>Use the <code>inherit</code> keyword to inherit CSS properties from a parent element.</li>\n  <li>Use the <code>initial</code> keyword to reset CSS properties to their initial value.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Code Example:</h3>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;h1&gt;Hello World!&lt;/h1&gt;\n\n&lt;style&gt;\n  h1 {\n    color: blue;\n    font-size: 36px;\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;\n</code></pre><br>', 'Introduction to CSS', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 12:25:56'),
(938, 951, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; <img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2ff5328084b_1781527858.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>CSS Selectors</h3>\n<p>In today\'s task, you\'ll learn how to select elements on a web page using CSS selectors. This is a fundamental concept in web development, and you\'ll use it to style and layout your web pages. You\'ll learn about different types of selectors, including element selectors, class selectors, ID selectors, attribute selectors, and combinator selectors.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<p><strong>CSS Element Selectors:</strong> An element selector selects an HTML element based on its tag name. For example, if we have an HTML element &lt;p&gt;, we can select it using the CSS selector p.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> &lt;pre&gt;<code>p { background-color: yellow; }\n</code></p><p><code><strong>CSS Class Selectors:</strong> A class selector selects an HTML element based on its class attribute. In HTML, we can add a class to an element using the class attribute, like this: &lt;p class=\'para\'&gt;. Then, in CSS, we can select all elements with that class using the dot notation, like this: .para.</code></p><code>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> &lt;pre&gt;<code>.para { color: blue; }\n</code></p><p><code><strong>CSS ID Selectors:</strong> An ID selector selects an HTML element based on its ID attribute. In HTML, we can add an ID to an element using the id attribute, like this: &lt;p id=\'para1\'&gt;. Then, in CSS, we can select that element using the hash notation, like this: #para1.</code></p><code>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> &lt;pre&gt;<code>#para1 { font-size: 24px; }\n</code></p><p><code><strong>CSS Attribute Selectors:</strong> An attribute selector selects an HTML element based on the presence or value of an attribute. For example, we can select all elements with a class attribute using the selector [class].</code></p><code>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> &lt;pre&gt;<code>[class] { background-color: red; }\n</code></p><p><code><strong>CSS Combinator Selectors:</strong> A combinator selector selects multiple elements based on their relationship to each other. There are three types of combinator selectors: descendant, child, and adjacent sibling.</code></p><code>\n<p><strong>Descendant Combinator:</strong> The descendant combinator selects elements that are descendants of another element. We can use the space character to separate the two selectors, like this: p .para.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> &lt;pre&gt;<code>p .para { color: green; }\n</code></p><p><code><strong>Child Combinator:</strong> The child combinator selects elements that are direct children of another element. We can use the greater-than symbol (&gt;) to separate the two selectors, like this: p &gt; .para.</code></p><code>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> &lt;pre&gt;<code>p &gt; .para { font-size: 18px; }\n</code></p><p><code><strong>Adjacent Sibling Combinator:</strong> The adjacent sibling combinator selects elements that are adjacent to each other. We can use the plus sign (+) to separate the two selectors, like this: p + .para.</code></p><code>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> &lt;pre&gt;<code>p + .para { background-color: purple; }\n</code></p><h3><code>Step-by-step task</code></h3><code>\n<ol>\n<li>Create an HTML file called <code>&lt;a href=\'index.html\'&gt;index.html&lt;/a&gt;</code> and add the following HTML code:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;CSS Selectors&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> a paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'para\'</span>&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> another paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'para1\'</span>&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> a paragraph <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">with</span> an ID.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'para\'</span>&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> another paragraph <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">with</span> a <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;a href=\'<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#\' class=\'link\'&gt;Link&lt;/a&gt;</span>\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<li>Create a CSS file called <code>&lt;a href=\'style.css\'&gt;style.css&lt;/a&gt;</code> and add the following CSS code:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">p { background-color: yellow; }\n.para { color: blue; }\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#para1 { font-size: 24px; }</span>\n[<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>] { background-color: red; }\np .para { color: green; }\np &gt; .para { font-size: 18px; }\np + .para { background-color: purple; }\n</code></pre>\n<li>Link the CSS file to the HTML file by adding the following line of code to the HTML file:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;link rel=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'stylesheet\'</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'text/css\'</span> href=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'style.css\'</span>&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<li>Open the HTML file in a web browser to see the effects of the CSS selectors.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit the HTML and CSS files you created in this task. Make sure to include a brief explanation of how you used each type of CSS selector.</p>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Don\'t forget to link the CSS file to the HTML file.</li>\n<li>Make sure to use the correct syntax for each type of CSS selector.</li>\n<li>Don\'t use the same selector for multiple styles.</li>\n<li>Be careful when using the descendant combinator, as it can select more elements than you expect.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use the browser\'s developer tools to inspect the elements on the page and see how the CSS selectors are applied.</li>\n<li>Use the CSS selector tool in the browser\'s developer tools to test and debug your CSS selectors.</li>\n<li>Use the CSS specificity calculator to determine the specificity of each CSS selector.</li>\n</ul></code></code></code></code></code></code></code>', 'CSS Selectors', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 12:51:24');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(939, 952, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2ff5b464684_1781527988.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>CSS Properties</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 18 of your web development internship! Today, we\'re going to explore the world of CSS properties. You\'ll learn how to control the color, background, text, font, and border of your HTML elements. These properties are essential for creating visually appealing and user-friendly websites.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need:</h4>\n<p>CSS properties are used to define the appearance and behavior of HTML elements. They are divided into several categories, including color, background, text, font, and border.</p>\n<h4>Color Properties:</h4>\n<p>The color of an HTML element is controlled by the color property. You can specify a color using a color name, a hex code, or an RGB value.</p>\n<p>For example, you can use the color name \'red\' to set the color of an element:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'color: red;\'</span>&gt;This text <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> red&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>You can also use a hex code to specify a color. A hex code is a six-digit code that starts with a \'#\' symbol. For example, the hex code \'#ff0000\' represents the color red:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=\'color: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#ff0000;\'&gt;This text is red&lt;/div&gt;</span></code></pre>\n<p>Finally, you can use an RGB value to specify a color. RGB stands for Red, Green, and Blue, and each value ranges from 0 to 255. For example, the RGB value \'rgb(255, 0, 0)\' represents the color red:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'color: rgb(255, 0, 0);\'</span>&gt;This text <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> red&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<h4>Background Properties:</h4>\n<p>The background of an HTML element is controlled by the background-color, background-image, and background-repeat properties.</p>\n<p>The background-color property sets the color of the background:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=\'background-color: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#f2f2f2;\'&gt;This text has a gray background&lt;/div&gt;</span></code></pre>\n<p>The background-image property sets the image used as the background:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'background-image: url(\'</span>background.jpg<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\');\'</span>&gt;This text has a background image&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>The background-repeat property sets whether the background image is repeated or not:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'background-image: url(\'</span>background.jpg<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'); background-repeat: no-repeat;\'</span>&gt;This text has a background image that <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">not</span> repeated&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<h4>Text Properties:</h4>\n<p>The text of an HTML element is controlled by the font-size, font-family, and text-align properties.</p>\n<p>The font-size property sets the size of the text:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'font-size: 24px;\'</span>&gt;This text <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">24</span> pixels high&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>The font-family property sets the font used for the text:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'font-family: Arial;\'</span>&gt;This text <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> Arial font&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>The text-align property sets the alignment of the text:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'text-align: center;\'</span>&gt;This text <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> centered&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<h4>Font Properties:</h4>\n<p>The font of an HTML element is controlled by the font-style, font-variant, and font-weight properties.</p>\n<p>The font-style property sets the style of the font:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'font-style: italic;\'</span>&gt;This text <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> italic font&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>The font-variant property sets the variant of the font:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'font-variant: small-caps;\'</span>&gt;This text <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> small-caps font&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>The font-weight property sets the weight of the font:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'font-weight: bold;\'</span>&gt;This text <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> bold font&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<h4>Border Properties:</h4>\n<p>The border of an HTML element is controlled by the border-width, border-style, and border-color properties.</p>\n<p>The border-width property sets the width of the border:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'border-width: 2px;\'</span>&gt;This text has a <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>-pixel border&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>The border-style property sets the style of the border:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'border-style: solid;\'</span>&gt;This text has a solid border&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>The border-color property sets the color of the border:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'border-color: red;\'</span>&gt;This text has a red border&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<h3>Step-by-step task:</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Create an HTML file and add a div element with the id \'container\'.</li>\n<li>Add a style attribute to the div element and set the background-color to #f2f2f2.</li>\n<li>Add a font-size property to the style attribute and set it to 24px.</li>\n<li>Add a font-family property to the style attribute and set it to Arial.</li>\n<li>Add a text-align property to the style attribute and set it to center.</li>\n<li>Add a border-width property to the style attribute and set it to 2px.</li>\n<li>Add a border-style property to the style attribute and set it to solid.</li>\n<li>Add a border-color property to the style attribute and set it to red.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Here\'s the code:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n&lt;div id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'container\'</span> style=\'background-color: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#f2f2f2; font-size: 24px; font-family: Arial; text-align: center; border-width: 2px; border-style: solid; border-color: red;\'&gt;</span>\nThis text <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> Arial font <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> has a red border\n&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n<h3>Deliverable:</h3>\n<p>Upload your HTML file to the platform and share the file with your mentor.</p>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Not using the correct syntax for the style attribute.</li>\n<li>Not setting the correct values for the properties.</li>\n<li>Not testing the code in different browsers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use the browser\'s developer tools to inspect the elements and see the styles applied.</li>\n<li>Use the CSS preprocessor to write more efficient and modular CSS code.</li>\n<li>Use the CSS grid system to create responsive and flexible layouts.</li>\n</ul>', 'CSS Properties', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 12:53:20'),
(940, 953, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2ff6079b87d_1781528071.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you will learn how the browser draws every element on a page using the CSS Box Model. Understanding this model is essential because it lets you control spacing, borders, and layout in real projects, from simple blogs to complex dashboards.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Box Model</strong> – Think of each element as a box that has content, padding, a border, and margin. The box’s total size is the sum of these parts.</li>\n<li><strong>Margin</strong> – The transparent space outside the border that separates an element from its neighbors. It’s like the buffer zone around a person in a crowded room.</li>\n<li><strong>Padding</strong> – The space between the content and the border inside the box. Imagine a cushion around the text inside a framed picture.</li>\n<li><strong>Border</strong> – The visible line that wraps the content and padding. It’s the frame of the picture.</li>\n<li><strong>Width &amp; Height</strong> – The dimensions of the content area. They determine how much space the text or image occupies.</li>\n<li><strong>Box-sizing</strong> – A property that changes how width and height are calculated. The default is <code>content-box</code>, which excludes padding and border from the specified width/height. <code>border-box</code> includes them.</li>\n<li><strong>Box Model Examples</strong> – Practical snippets that show how margin, padding, border, and width interact.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Create a new HTML file</strong> called <code>box-model.html</code> and open it in your editor.</li>\n<li><strong>Set up the basic structure</strong>:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"> &lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n &lt;html&gt;\n &lt;head&gt;\n   &lt;title&gt;Box Model Demo&lt;/title&gt;\n &lt;/head&gt;\n &lt;body&gt;\n   &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'box\'</span>&gt;Hello, world!&lt;/div&gt;\n &lt;/body&gt;\n &lt;/html&gt;\n</code></pre>\nThis creates a single <code>div</code> with the class <code>box</code> that we will style.</li>\n<li><strong>Add internal CSS</strong> inside a <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> tag in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"> &lt;style&gt;\n   .box {\n     width: 200px;\n     height: 100px;\n     padding: 20px;\n     margin: 30px;\n     border: 5px solid <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#3498db;</span>\n     background-color: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#ecf0f1;</span>\n     font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\n   }\n &lt;/style&gt;\n</code></pre>\nExplain each line:\n<ul>\n<li><code>width</code> and <code>height</code> set the content size.</li>\n<li><code>padding</code> adds space inside the border.</li>\n<li><code>margin</code> pushes the box away from other elements.</li>\n<li><code>border</code> draws a blue line around the box.</li>\n<li><code>background-color</code> fills the content area.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Open the file in a browser</strong> and observe the box. Notice that the total width is 200px + 2×20px padding + 2×5px border + 2×30px margin.</li>\n<li><strong>Experiment with <code>box-sizing</code></strong>:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"> .box {\n   box-sizing: border-box;\n }\n</code></pre>\nNow the specified <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> include padding and border. The box’s outer size remains 200px × 100px.</li>\n<li><strong>Create two boxes side by side</strong> to see margin collapsing and inline-block behavior:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"> &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'box\'</span>&gt;Box <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'box\'</span>&gt;Box <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n</code></pre>\nAdd <code>display: inline-block;</code> to <code>.box</code> and <code>vertical-align: top;</code> to keep them aligned. Observe how margins affect spacing between them.\n</li>\n<li><strong>Save your work</strong> and refresh the browser to confirm all changes.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Your final <code>box-model.html</code> should contain:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <code>div</code> with class <code>box</code> that displays text.</li>\n<li>Internal CSS that sets width, height, padding, margin, border, background, font, and <code>box-sizing</code>.</li>\n<li>Two boxes side by side demonstrating margin and inline-block layout.</li>\n<li>All code properly escaped and readable.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Using double quotes inside attribute values – always use single quotes or no quotes.</li>\n<li>Forgetting to escape &lt; and &gt; inside code blocks, which can break the HTML display.</li>\n<li>Assuming <code>width</code> includes padding and border unless <code>box-sizing: border-box</code> is set.</li>\n<li>Overlooking that <code>margin</code> on adjacent elements can collapse, reducing the expected space.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Set <code>* { box-sizing: border-box; }</code> globally to simplify layout calculations.</li>\n<li>Use <code>margin: auto;</code> on a block element with a fixed width to center it horizontally.</li>\n<li>When designing responsive layouts, combine <code>padding</code> with <code>max-width</code> to keep content readable on all screens.</li>\n</ul>', 'CSS Box Model', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 12:54:52'),
(941, 954, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a2ff78053d70_1781528448.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\"><br>\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you will create a responsive webpage layout using CSS positioning and layout techniques. You\'ll learn how browsers arrange elements by default, and how to override this behavior to build complex designs like sidebars, navigation menus, and modal windows.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n\n<h4>Normal Document Flow</h4>\n<p>Imagine books on a shelf - browsers place elements one after another by default. Block elements (like <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>) take full width and stack vertically. Inline elements (like <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>) sit next to each other horizontally.</p>\n\n<h4>Float and Clear Properties</h4>\n<p>Float is like a boat that moves to the left/right side of a river (container). Other elements can flow around it. <code>clear:both</code> is like a rock that stops the river from flowing around any boats.</p>\n\n<h4>Position Property</h4>\n<p>This lets you park elements like cars in different parking spots:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>static</code>: Default parking spot (normal flow)</li>\n<li><code>relative</code>: Parked relative to where it would normally be</li>\n<li><code>absolute</code>: Parked relative to nearest positioned ancestor</li>\n<li><code>fixed</code>: Stuck to browser window (like GPS navigation)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Display Property</h4>\n<p>This controls visibility and layout:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>block</code>: Takes full width (like a paragraph)</li>\n<li><code>inline</code>: Fits content width (like words in a sentence)</li>\n<li><code>none</code>: Makes element disappear completely</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Overflow Property</h4>\n<p>Imagine water in a glass: <code>overflow:auto</code> adds scrollbars when content spills out. <code>overflow:hidden</code> cuts off extra content like a picture frame.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n\n<ol>\n<li>Create <code>index.html</code> with this basic structure:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;style&gt;\n  /* We\'ll add styles here */\n  &lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"header\"</span>&gt;Header&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"sidebar\"</span>&gt;Sidebar&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"content\"</span>&gt;Main Content&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"footer\"</span>&gt;Footer&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"modal\"</span>&gt;Popup!&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n</li>\n\n<li>Add these styles to see normal flow:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">body { font-family: sans-serif; }\n.header { background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#4CAF50; padding: 20px; color: white; }</span>\n.sidebar { width: 200px; background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#f1f1f1; padding: 10px; }</span>\n.content { margin-left: 220px; padding: 10px; }\n.footer { background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#333; color: white; padding: 10px; }</span>\n.modal { display: none; }</code></pre>\n</li>\n\n<li>Make sidebar float left:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.sidebar {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">float</span>: left;\n  width: 200px;\n  background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#f1f1f1;</span>\n  padding: 10px;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>This lets the sidebar sit on the left while main content flows next to it.</p>\n</li>\n\n<li>Clear floats to prevent footer overlap:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.content::after {\n  content: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"\"</span>;\n  display: table;\n  clear: both;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>This adds an invisible \"rock\" to stop content from wrapping around floated elements.</p>\n</li>\n\n<li>Create a fixed position navigation bar:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.navbar {\n  position: fixed;\n  top: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>;\n  width: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">100</span>%;\n  background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#333;</span>\n  color: white;\n  padding: 10px;\n  text-align: center;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Add this HTML above your header: <code>&lt;div class=\"navbar\"&gt;Navigation&lt;/div&gt;</code></p>\n</li>\n\n<li>Make modal window appear:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.modal {\n  position: absolute;\n  top: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">50</span>%;\n  left: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">50</span>%;\n  transform: translate(-<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">50</span>%, -<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">50</span>%);\n  background: white;\n  padding: 20px;\n  border: 1px solid <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#ccc;</span>\n  display: none;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Add JavaScript later to show/hide it (we\'ll cover JS in later days).</p>\n</li>\n\n<li>Handle overflow with scrolling:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.content {\n  margin-left: 220px;\n  padding: 10px;\n  height: 300px;\n  overflow: auto;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>This adds scrollbars when content is too long.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit your complete <code>index.html</code> file showing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Floated sidebar with cleared content</li>\n<li>Fixed position navigation bar</li>\n<li>Absolute position modal window</li>\n<li>Scrollable content area</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Forgetting to clear floats which causes layout overlap</li>\n<li>Using <code>position: absolute</code> without positioning parent elements first</li>\n<li>Not setting proper container widths when using float</li>\n<li>Mixing different positioning methods without understanding how they interact</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use flexbox/grid instead of float:</strong> Modern layout methods are easier for complex designs</li>\n<li><strong>Check overflow behavior:</strong> Test with different content lengths to see how scrollbars appear</li>\n<li><strong>Use browser dev tools:</strong> Right-click elements and inspect their positioning in real-time</li>\n</ul>', 'CSS Layout and Positioning', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 13:01:12'),
(942, 955, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS display visibility example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+display+visibility+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+display+visibility+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+display+visibility+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+display+visibility+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+display+visibility+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you\'ll build a responsive product card layout using CSS display properties and visibility controls. Understanding these tools helps you create adaptive web designs that work on any device, a critical skill for modern web development.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>Display Property Values</h4>\n<p><strong>display</strong> controls how elements are rendered:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>none</code>: Removes element completely (like deleting a book from a shelf)</li>\n  <li><code>block</code>: Stacks elements vertically (like books on a shelf)</li>\n  <li><code>inline</code>: Flows elements horizontally (like words in a sentence)</li>\n  <li><code>inline-block</code>: Combines block spacing with inline flow</li>\n  <li><code>table</code>: Creates table layouts for structured data</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Visibility Property</h4>\n<p><code>visibility: hidden</code> makes elements invisible but keeps their space (like covering a book with a blank sheet of paper).</p>\n\n<h4>Opacity Property</h4>\n<p><code>opacity: 0.5</code> makes elements transparent (like looking through frosted glass) but they still take space.</p>\n\n<h4>CSS Tables</h4>\n<p>The <code>display: table</code> family creates structured layouts with <code>table-row</code>, <code>table-cell</code>, etc. Useful for data grids that need consistent alignment.</p>\n\n<h4>Inline vs Block Elements</h4>\n<p><em>Inline elements</em> (like <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>) flow with text. <em>Block elements</em> (like <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>) create new lines. Think of inline as puzzle pieces and block as building blocks.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li>Create <code>index.html</code> with this basic structure:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;&lt;title&gt;Product Card&lt;/title&gt;&lt;link rel=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'stylesheet\'</span> href=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'style.css\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'card\'</span>&gt;\n      &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'image\'</span>&gt;Image&lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'details\'</span>&gt;\n        &lt;h2&gt;Product Name&lt;/h2&gt;\n        &lt;p&gt;Description&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n  </li>\n  \n  <li>Create <code>style.css</code> with baseline styles:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.card {\n  width: 300px;\n  border: 1px solid <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#ccc;</span>\n  padding: 16px;\n  margin: 16px;\n}</code></pre>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>Make the image container a block element:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.image {\n  display: block;\n  background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#eee;</span>\n  height: 150px;\n  margin-bottom: 12px;\n}</code></pre>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>Experiment with display values:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.details {\n  display: inline-block;\n  width: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">100</span>%;\n}</code></pre>\n  <p>Notice how <code>inline-block</code> lets us set width while keeping vertical flow</p>\n\n  <li>Create a hidden element:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.hidden {\n  display: none;\n}</code></pre>\n  <p>Add this class to the image container to test hiding</p>\n\n  <li>Try visibility vs opacity:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.image {\n  visibility: hidden;\n  /* opacity: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0.5</span>; */\n}</code></pre>\n  <p>Compare how space is preserved when using visibility</p>\n\n  <li>Make a table layout:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.card {\n  display: table;\n  width: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">100</span>%;\n}\n.card .image {\n  display: table-row;\n}\n.image::before {\n  content: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Image Area\'</span>;\n  display: table-cell;\n  padding: 8px;\n}</code></pre>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit both <code>index.html</code> and <code>style.css</code> files showing:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A product card with visible image and details</n  <li>At least three different display property experiments</li>\n  <li>A working CSS table layout variation</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Using <code>visibility: hidden</code> when you need <code>display: none</code> (elements still take space with visibility)</li>\n  <li>Forgetting that <code>inline-block</code> elements create gaps between them</li>\n  <li>Mixing table display with actual HTML tables (use display tables for layout, real tables for data)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<blockquote>\n  <p>Use browser developer tools to toggle display/visibility properties live</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n  <p>For complex layouts, <code>display: grid</code> is more powerful than table displays</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n  <p>Use <code>opacity</code> for visual effects, <code>visibility</code> for conditional display</p>\n</blockquote>', 'CSS Display and Visibility', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(943, 956, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3002f2c520c_1781531378.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\"><br>\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ll learn CSS Flexbox to create responsive, flexible layouts. Flexbox is essential for modern web design, allowing elements to adjust automatically on different screen sizes—perfect for real-world projects like navigation bars or product grids.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>What is Flexbox?</h4>\n<p>Flexbox is a CSS layout model that arranges elements in a <strong>row</strong> or <strong>column</strong> and automatically adjusts their size and spacing. Think of it like a stretchy shopping bag that rearranges its contents to fit perfectly.</p>\n\n<h4>Flex Container and Flex Items</h4>\n<p>A <strong>Flex Container</strong> is the parent element (like the bag). Its children are <strong>Flex Items</strong> (the items in the bag). Flexbox properties are applied to the container, and item-specific properties control individual elements.</p>\n\n<h4>Flex Container Properties</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>display: flex</code>: Makes an element a Flex container.</li>\n  <li><code>flex-direction</code>: Sets the direction (row or column).</li>\n  <li><code>justify-content</code>: Controls horizontal spacing (e.g., <code>space-between</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>align-items</code>: Controls vertical alignment (e.g., <code>center</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Flex Item Properties</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>flex-grow</code>: Lets items expand to fill space.</li>\n  <li><code>flex-shrink</code>: Lets items shrink if needed.</li>\n  <li><code>order</code>: Changes the visual order of items.</li>\n  <li><code>align-self</code>: Overrides container alignment for a single item.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li>Create an HTML file with this basic structure:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'container\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'item\'</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'item\'</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'item\'</span>&gt;Item <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n  </li>\n  \n  <li>Add CSS to make the container a Flexbox row:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;style&gt;\n.container {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-direction: row;\n  justify-content: space-between;\n  align-items: center;\n  padding: 20px;\n  border: 1px solid <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#ccc;</span>\n}\n&lt;/style&gt;</code></pre>\n  <p>This creates a horizontal layout with space between items and vertical centering.</p>\n\n  </li><li>Make items grow equally:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.item {\n  flex-grow: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>;\n  padding: 10px;\n  text-align: center;\n  border: 1px solid <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#999;</span>\n}</code></pre>\n  <p>Each item now expands to fill the container evenly.</p>\n\n  </li><li>Test responsiveness: Resize your browser window and watch how the items adjust. Add this to see how shrinking works:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.item {\n  flex-shrink: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>;\n  min-width: 100px;\n}</code></pre>\n  <p>Items will shrink below 100px on small screens.</p>\n</li></ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit a single HTML file that:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Shows 3 Flex items in a row</li>\n  <li>Uses <code>justify-content: space-between</code></li>\n  <li>Has centered text in each item</li>\n  <li>Includes a visible border around the container</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Forgetting <code>display: flex</code> on the container</li>\n  <li>Mixing up <code>flex-direction</code> and <code>justify-content</code> (direction sets the axis, justify controls spacing)</li>\n  <li>Using <code>flex-shrink</code> without <code>flex-grow</code> - always pair them for predictable results</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use browser DevTools to inspect Flex properties in real-time</li>\n  <li>Start with <code>flex-direction: row</code> and change as needed</li>\n  <li>Remember: Flexbox is one-dimensional (for grids, use CSS Grid instead)</li>\n</ul>', 'CSS Flexbox', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 13:50:05');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(944, 957, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a30035134613_1781531473.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you will build a responsive portfolio layout using CSS Grid. This skill is critical for real-world jobs where designers need clean, flexible layouts for websites like dashboards, photo galleries, and product pages.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>What is CSS Grid?</h4>\n<p>CSS Grid is like a spreadsheet: it lets you divide a webpage into rows and columns. Imagine a chessboard - each square is a spot where content can live.</p>\n\n<h4>Grid Container</h4>\n<p>This is the parent element that holds all grid items. When you apply <code>display: grid</code> to a container, all its direct children become grid items.</p>\n\n<h4>Grid Items</h4>\n<p>These are the individual elements inside the grid container. They automatically take their place in the grid unless you move them.</p>\n\n<h4>Grid Lines</h4>\n<p>These are the dividing lines between rows and columns. You count them like this: line 1 is the first row/column, line 2 is the second, etc.</p>\n\n<h4>Grid Tracks</h4>\n<p>A track is the space between two grid lines. A row track is a row height, a column track is a column width.</p>\n\n<h4>Grid Areas</h4>\n<p>These let you name sections of your grid (like \"header\" or \"sidebar\") to make positioning easier.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Create an HTML file with this basic structure:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'grid-container\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'item header\'</span>&gt;My Portfolio&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'item nav\'</span>&gt;Menu&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'item main\'</span>&gt;Projects&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'item sidebar\'</span>&gt;About&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'item footer\'</span>&gt;Contact&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>This creates 5 grid items that we\'ll position on our grid.</p>\n\n</li><li>Add CSS to make the container a grid:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.grid-container {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr 200px;\n  grid-template-rows: 100px auto 100px;\n  grid-gap: 10px;\n}</code></pre>\n<p><code>display: grid</code> activates the grid system. <code>grid-template-columns</code> creates 3 columns (200px sidebar, flexible main content, 200px right sidebar). <code>grid-template-rows</code> makes 3 rows (100px header, auto-sized main content, 100px footer). <code>grid-gap</code> adds space between items.</p>\n\n</li><li>Position each item using grid lines:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">.header {\n  grid-column: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>;\n  grid-row: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>;\n}\n\n.nav {\n  grid-column: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>;\n  grid-row: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>;\n}\n\n.main {\n  grid-column: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>;\n  grid-row: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>;\n}\n\n.sidebar {\n  grid-column: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>;\n  grid-row: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>;\n}\n\n.footer {\n  grid-column: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>;\n  grid-row: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>;\n}</code></pre>\n<p><code>grid-column: START / END</code> tells items where to go. The header spans all 3 columns (1 to 4) in row 1. The nav takes column 1 in row 2.</p>\n\n</li><li>Make it responsive:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">@media (max-width: 768px) {\n  .grid-container {\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr;\n    grid-template-rows: auto;\n  }\n\n  .header { grid-column: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>; }\n  .nav { grid-column: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>; }\n  .main { grid-column: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>; }\n  .sidebar { grid-column: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>; }\n  .footer { grid-column: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span> / <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>; }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>This changes the layout to a single column on small screens. All items stack vertically.</p>\n</li></ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit two files: <code>index.html</code> with your HTML structure and <code>styles.css</code> with all the CSS code you wrote. Your layout must show the grid structure clearly in both desktop and mobile views.</p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Forgetting to set <code>display: grid</code> on the container before positioning items</li>\n<li>Mixing up column and row numbers - remember rows are horizontal, columns are vertical</li>\n<li>Using <code>grid-gap</code> without checking browser support (it works in modern browsers, but always test)</li>\n<li>Not testing your layout on different screen sizes</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use browser dev tools:</strong> Right-click on your page and select \"Inspect\" to see grid lines and adjust properties live</li>\n<li><strong>Start simple:</strong> Begin with 2-3 columns before trying complex layouts</li>\n<li><strong>Use named areas:</strong> Instead of line numbers, try <code>grid-template-areas</code> for easier visual positioning</li>\n</ul>', 'CSS Grid', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 13:51:24'),
(945, 958, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3003c3b3af5_1781531587.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>CSS Responsive Design</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ll learn how to make your website look great on different devices, like desktops, laptops, tablets, and smartphones. This is called responsive design. Your task will be to create a simple website that adapts to different screen sizes.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need:</h4>\n<p>Responsive design is all about making your website work well on different devices. To achieve this, you\'ll use media queries and responsive images.</p>\n<h4>Media Queries</h4>\n<p>Imagine you\'re at a restaurant, and you want to order a meal that suits your taste. You tell the waiter, \'I want something spicy, but not too spicy.\' The waiter asks you, \'How spicy do you like it?\' You say, \'Not very spicy, just a little.\' The waiter then brings you a dish that\'s spicy, but not too spicy. This is similar to how media queries work.</p>\n<p>A media query is like asking the browser, \'What\'s the screen size?\' The browser responds with the screen size, and you can then apply styles based on that screen size. This way, your website can adapt to different screen sizes.</p>\n<h4>Responsive Images</h4>\n<p>Responsive images are images that resize themselves based on the screen size. This is useful because it saves bandwidth and makes your website load faster.</p>\n<h4>Mobile-First Design</h4>\n<p>Mobile-first design is a way of designing your website that prioritizes the mobile version first. This means that you design the website for small screens first and then add more features for larger screens.</p>\n<h4>Step-by-step task:</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new HTML file called <code>index.html</code> and add the following code:<code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n&lt;title&gt;Responsive Design&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph of text.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code>\n<p>This code creates a basic HTML document with a title and a paragraph of text.</p>\n</li><li>Add the following CSS code to a new file called <code>styles.css</code>:<code>body {\n  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\n  max-width: 800px;\n  margin: 0 auto;\n}\n\n@media (max-width: 600px) {\n  body {\n    max-width: 300px;\n  }\n}\n\n@media (max-width: 400px) {\n  body {\n    max-width: 200px;\n  }\n}</code>\n<p>This code adds some basic styles to the body element and uses media queries to change the maximum width of the body element based on the screen size.</p>\n</li><li>Link the CSS file to the HTML file by adding the following code to the head section of the HTML file:<code>&lt;link rel=\'stylesheet\' type=\'text/css\' href=\'styles.css\'&gt;</code>\n<p>This code links the CSS file to the HTML file, so the styles are applied to the document.</p>\n</li><li>Save both files and open the HTML file in a browser.</li>\n<li>Resize the browser window to see how the website adapts to different screen sizes.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable:</h4>\n<p>You should have an HTML file called <code>index.html</code> and a CSS file called <code>styles.css</code>. The website should adapt to different screen sizes, and the paragraph of text should resize accordingly.</p>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Not using media queries to change the styles based on screen size.</li>\n<li>Not using responsive images to save bandwidth and improve page load times.</li>\n<li>Not testing the website on different devices and screen sizes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Use the <code>max-width</code> property to set the maximum width of an element, and use media queries to change the maximum width based on screen size.</li>\n<li>Use the <code>min-width</code> property to set the minimum width of an element, and use media queries to change the minimum width based on screen size.</li>\n<li>Use the <code>flexbox</code> layout to create flexible and responsive layouts.</li>\n</ul>', 'CSS Responsive Design', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 13:53:18'),
(946, 959, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CSS preprocessors and frameworks</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CSS+preprocessors+and+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CSS+preprocessors+and+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CSS+preprocessors+and+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CSS+preprocessors+and+frameworks/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CSS+preprocessors+and+frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>CSS Preprocessors and Frameworks</h3>\n<p>In this task, you\'ll learn how to use CSS preprocessors and frameworks to write more efficient and maintainable CSS code. You\'ll understand the basics of Sass and Less, and how to use Bootstrap and Tailwind CSS in your projects.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>Introduction to Sass and Less</h4>\n<p>Sass (Syntactically Awesome StyleSheets) and Less are CSS preprocessors that allow you to write CSS code in a more efficient and modular way. They provide features like variables, nesting, and mixins that make it easier to write and maintain CSS code.</p>\n<p>Think of Sass and Less like a recipe book for cooking. Just as a recipe book provides a set of instructions and ingredients to make a dish, Sass and Less provide a set of instructions and ingredients to make a CSS style.</p>\n\n<h4>CSS Frameworks Overview</h4>\n<p>CSS frameworks are pre-built sets of CSS styles and classes that you can use to create a consistent design for your website. They provide a set of pre-defined classes and styles that you can use to create a layout, typography, and other visual elements.</p>\n<p>Think of CSS frameworks like a set of LEGO blocks. Just as LEGO blocks provide a set of pre-built pieces that you can use to create a structure, CSS frameworks provide a set of pre-built styles and classes that you can use to create a design.</p>\n\n<h4>Bootstrap and Tailwind CSS</h4>\n<p>Bootstrap and Tailwind CSS are two popular CSS frameworks that you can use to create a consistent design for your website. Bootstrap is a more traditional CSS framework that provides a set of pre-defined classes and styles for layout, typography, and other visual elements. Tailwind CSS is a more utility-first CSS framework that provides a set of pre-defined classes and styles for layout, typography, and other visual elements.</p>\n<p>Think of Bootstrap and Tailwind CSS like two different sets of LEGO blocks. Just as you can use different sets of LEGO blocks to create different structures, you can use different CSS frameworks to create different designs.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li>Create a new HTML file called <code>index.html</code> and add the following code:</pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;CSS Preprocessors and Frameworks&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre></li>\n  <li>Create a new CSS file called <code>styles.css</code> and add the following code:</pre><code>body {\n  background-color: #f2f2f2;\n}\n\nh1 {\n  color: #333;\n}</code></pre></li>\n  <li>Install Sass and Less using npm or yarn by running the following command:</pre><code>npm install sass less</code></pre></li>\n  <li>Create a new file called <code>styles.scss</code> and add the following code:</pre><code>$primary-color: #333;\n\nbody {\n  background-color: $primary-color;\n}\n\nh1 {\n  color: $primary-color;\n}</code></pre></li>\n  <li>Compile the Sass code using the following command:</pre><code>sass styles.scss styles.css</code></pre></li>\n  <li>Link the compiled CSS file to your HTML file by adding the following code:</pre><code>&lt;link rel=\'stylesheet\' href=\'styles.css\'&gt;</code></pre></li>\n  <li>Install Bootstrap using npm or yarn by running the following command:</pre><code>npm install bootstrap</code></pre></li>\n  <li>Import Bootstrap in your CSS file by adding the following code:</pre><code>@import \'bootstrap/dist/css/bootstrap.css\';</code></pre></li>\n  <li>Use Bootstrap classes in your HTML file to create a layout:</pre><code>&lt;div class=\'container\'&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\'row\'&gt;\n    &lt;div class=\'col-md-12\'&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre></li>\n  <li>Install Tailwind CSS using npm or yarn by running the following command:</pre><code>npm install tailwindcss</code></pre></li>\n  <li>Import Tailwind CSS in your CSS file by adding the following code:</pre><code>@import \'tailwindcss/base\';\n@import \'tailwindcss/components\';\n@import \'tailwindcss/utilities\';</code></pre></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit the following files:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>index.html</code></li>\n  <li><code>styles.css</code></li>\n  <li><code>styles.scss</code></li>\n  <li><code>styles.css</code> (compiled from <code>styles.scss</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Not installing Sass and Less correctly.</li>\n  <li>Not compiling the Sass code correctly.</li>\n  <li>Not linking the compiled CSS file to the HTML file correctly.</li>\n  <li>Not using Bootstrap classes correctly.</li>\n  <li>Not importing Tailwind CSS correctly.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use Sass and Less to write more efficient and maintainable CSS code.</li>\n  <li>Use Bootstrap and Tailwind CSS to create a consistent design for your website.</li>\n  <li>Use the <code>@import</code> directive to import CSS files in your Sass and Less code.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>\n    {\"question\":\"What is the main purpose of CSS preprocessors like Sass and Less?\",\"options\":[\"To write more efficient and maintainable CSS code\",\"To create a consistent design for a website\",\"To import CSS files in a project\"],\"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"CSS preprocessors like Sass and Less allow you to write CSS code in a more efficient and modular way.\"}\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    {\"question\":\"What is the main difference between Bootstrap and Tailwind CSS?\",\"options\":[\"Bootstrap is more traditional and Tailwind CSS is more utility-first\",\"Bootstrap is more utility-first and Tailwind CSS is more traditional\",\"Both are traditional and utility-first\",\"Neither is traditional or utility-first\"],\"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"Bootstrap is a more traditional CSS framework, while Tailwind CSS is a more utility-first CSS framework.\"}\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    {\"question\":\"How do you compile Sass code?\",\"options\":[\"Using the <code>sass</code> command\",\"Using the <code>less</code> command\",\"Using the <code>bootstrap</code> command\",\"Using the <code>tailwindcss</code> command\"],\"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"You can compile Sass code using the <code>sass</code> command.\"}\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    {\"question\":\"How do you import Bootstrap in your CSS file?\",\"options\":[\"Using the <code>@import</code> directive\",\"Using the <code><link rel=\'stylesheet\'></code> tag\",\"Using the <code><style></style></code> tag\",\"Using the <code><script></script></code> tag\"],\"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"You can import Bootstrap in your CSS file using the <code>@import</code> directive.\"}\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    {\"question\":\"How do you use Bootstrap classes in your HTML file?\",\"options\":[\"Using the <code><div class=\'container\'></code> tag\",\"Using the <code><div class=\'row\'></code> tag\",\"Using the <code><div class=\'col-md-12\'></code> tag\",\"Using all of the above\"],\"correct_index\":3,\"explanation\":\"You can use Bootstrap classes in your HTML file using the <code><div class=\'container\'></code>, <code><div class=\'row\'></code>, and <code><div class=\'col-md-12\'></code> tags.\"}\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    {\"question\":\"How do you import Tailwind CSS in your CSS file?\",\"options\":[\"Using the <code>@import</code> directive\",\"Using the <code><link rel=\'stylesheet\'></code> tag\",\"Using the <code><style></style></code> tag\",\"Using the <code><script></script></code> tag\"],\"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"You can import Tailwind CSS in your CSS file using the <code>@import</code> directive.\"}\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    {\"question\":\"What is the main purpose of CSS frameworks like Bootstrap and Tailwind CSS?\",\"options\":[\"To write more efficient and maintainable CSS code\",\"To create a consistent design for a website\",\"To import CSS files in a project\"],\"correct_index\":1,\"explanation\":\"CSS frameworks like Bootstrap and Tailwind CSS provide a set of pre-defined classes and styles that you can use to create a consistent design for your website.\"}\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    {\"question\":\"How do you link the compiled CSS file to your HTML file?\",\"options\":[\"Using the <code><link rel=\'stylesheet\'></code> tag\",\"Using the <code><style></style></code> tag\",\"Using the <code><script></script></code> tag\",\"Using the <code>@import</code> directive\"],\"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"You can link the compiled CSS file to your HTML file using the <code><link rel=\'stylesheet\'></code> tag.\"}\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    {\"question\":\"What is the main difference between the <code>sass</code> and <code>less</code> commands?\",\"options\":[\"The <code>sass</code> command compiles Sass code, while the <code>less</code> command compiles Less code\",\"The <code>sass</code> command compiles Less code, while the <code>less</code> command compiles Sass code\",\"Both commands compile Sass and Less code\",\"Neither command compiles Sass or Less code\"],\"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"The <code>sass</code> command compiles Sass code, while the <code>less</code> command compiles Less code.\"}\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    {\"question\":\"How do you use the <code>@import</code> directive to import CSS files in your Sass and Less code?\",\"options\":[\"Using the <code>@import \'bootstrap/dist/css/bootstrap.css\';</code> directive\",\"Using the <code>@import \'tailwindcss/base\';</code> directive\",\"Using both directives\",\"Using neither directive\"],\"correct_index\":3,\"explanation\":\"You can use the <code>@import</code> directive to import CSS files in your Sass and Less code using the <code>@import \'bootstrap/dist/css/bootstrap.css\';</code> and <code>@import \'tailwindcss/base\';</code> directives.\"}\n</ul>', 'CSS Preprocessors and Frameworks', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(947, 960, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a30050b9aed8_1781531915.webp\" alt=\"image\" style=\"border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\">&nbsp;</p></div><h3>Introduction to JavaScript</h3>\n<p>Today, you will learn the basics of JavaScript, a programming language used to create interactive web pages. You will understand its history, syntax, and how it is used in web development. By the end of this task, you will be able to create a simple JavaScript program.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need to know:</h4>\n<p>Before we dive into the task, let\'s cover some essential concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>What is JavaScript?</strong> JavaScript is a high-level, dynamic programming language that is primarily used for client-side scripting on the web. It allows you to create interactive web pages by adding dynamic effects, animations, and responding to user interactions.</li>\n<li><strong>JavaScript History</strong> JavaScript was first introduced in 1995 by Brendan Eich, a developer at Netscape Communications. Initially called \"Mocha,\" it was later renamed to JavaScript and became a standard feature in web browsers.</li>\n<li><strong>JavaScript Syntax and Basics</strong> JavaScript uses a syntax similar to C and Java. It consists of variables, data types, operators, control structures, functions, and objects. We will cover these basics in this task.</li>\n<li><strong>JavaScript in Web Development</strong> JavaScript is used extensively in web development to create interactive web pages. It is used for client-side scripting, which means it runs on the client\'s web browser, rather than on the server. This allows for faster and more dynamic web applications.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Step-by-Step Task</h3>\n<p>Now that we have covered the essential concepts, let\'s move on to the task. Your task is to create a simple JavaScript program that asks the user for their name and then greets them with a personalized message.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Create a new HTML file called <code>index.html</code> and add the following code:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;JavaScript Greeting&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;JavaScript Greeting&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;button id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"get-name\"</span>&gt;Get Name&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;div id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"greeting\"</span>&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;script src=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"script.js\"</span>&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code creates a basic HTML structure with a heading, a button, and a div element to display the greeting.</p></li>\n<li><p>Create a new JavaScript file called <code>script.js</code> and add the following code:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">document.addEventListener(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"DOMContentLoaded\"</span>, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> () {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> getNameButton = document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"get-name\"</span>);\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> greetingDiv = document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"greeting\"</span>);\n\n  getNameButton.addEventListener(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"click\"</span>, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> () {\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> name = prompt(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Please enter your name:\"</span>);\n    greetingDiv.textContent = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`Hello, ${name}!`</span>;\n  });\n});\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code listens for the button click event and prompts the user to enter their name. When the user clicks the button, it updates the greeting div with a personalized message.</p></li>\n<li><p>Open the <code>index.html</code> file in your web browser and click the \"Get Name\" button. Enter your name and click the button again to see the personalized greeting.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Your deliverable for this task is to create a simple JavaScript program that asks the user for their name and then greets them with a personalized message. You should have two files: <code>index.html</code> and <code>script.js</code>.</p>\n<h3>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Incorrect syntax</strong> Make sure to use the correct syntax for JavaScript and HTML.</li>\n<li><strong>Missing event listeners</strong> Ensure that you add event listeners to the correct elements to respond to user interactions.</li>\n<li><strong>Incorrect variable names</strong> Use descriptive variable names to avoid confusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro Tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use the browser\'s developer tools</strong> to inspect and debug your code.</li>\n<li><strong>Use a code editor with syntax highlighting and auto-completion</strong> to make coding easier and faster.</li>\n<li><strong>Test your code thoroughly</strong> to ensure it works as expected.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<p>There is no quiz for this task. However, you can test your understanding of the concepts covered in this task by trying to modify the code to ask for the user\'s age and display a personalized message based on their age.</p>', 'Introduction to JavaScript', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 13:59:06'),
(948, 961, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a30056ebf4a3_1781532014.webp\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you will learn how to store, manipulate, and understand data in JavaScript. By mastering variables, data types, type conversion, scope, and hoisting, you’ll be able to write clear, bug‑free code that runs in browsers and on servers.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>Variables in JavaScript</h4>\n<p>A variable is a named container that holds a value. Think of it like a labeled jar that can hold different kinds of items. In JavaScript you create a variable with the keywords <code>var</code>, <code>let</code>, or <code>const</code>.\n</p><ul>\n<li><code>var</code> is the old way and has function‑level scope.</li>\n<li><code>let</code> is block‑level and can be reassigned.</li>\n<li><code>const</code> is block‑level and cannot be reassigned after declaration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<h4>Data Types in JavaScript</h4>\n<p>JavaScript has several built‑in data types that describe the kind of value a variable can hold:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Number</strong> – integers and decimals (e.g. 42, 3.14).</li>\n<li><strong>String</strong> – text surrounded by single or double quotes (e.g. \'hello\').</li>\n<li><strong>Boolean</strong> – true or false.</li>\n<li><strong>Undefined</strong> – a variable that has been declared but not assigned.</li>\n<li><strong>Null</strong> – an intentional absence of any object value.</li>\n<li><strong>Object</strong> – collections of key/value pairs (e.g. {name: \'Alice\'}).</li>\n<li><strong>Array</strong> – ordered lists of values (e.g. [1, 2, 3]).</li>\n<li><strong>Symbol</strong> – unique identifiers (rarely used in day‑to‑day code).</li>\n<li><strong>BigInt</strong> – numbers larger than 2^53-1.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Type Conversion and Coercion</h4>\n<p>JavaScript can automatically convert values from one type to another (coercion) or you can explicitly convert them (type conversion). A common example is adding a number to a string: <code>1 + \'2\'</code> results in the string <code>\'12\'</code> because the number is coerced to a string. Explicit conversion uses functions like <code>Number()</code>, <code>String()</code>, or <code>Boolean()</code>.\n</p>\n<h4>Variable Scope</h4>\n<p>Scope determines where a variable can be accessed. There are two main scopes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Global scope</strong> – variables declared outside any function are available everywhere.</li>\n<li><strong>Local (block or function) scope</strong> – variables declared inside a block ({}) or a function are only accessible inside that block or function.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Think of scope like rooms in a house. A variable in the living room can be seen by everyone, but a variable in the bedroom is only visible to the person inside.</p>\n<h4>Hoisting</h4>\n<p>Hoisting is JavaScript’s behavior of moving variable and function declarations to the top of their scope before code runs. With <code>var</code>, the declaration is hoisted but the assignment stays where it is. With <code>let</code> and <code>const</code>, the declaration is hoisted but the variable is in a <em>temporal dead zone</em> until the line of code that assigns it.</p>\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Declare variables using each keyword.</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">var</span> oldVar = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> newLet = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'hello\'</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> fixedConst = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">true</span>;</code></pre>\n<p>Check the values in the console with <code>console.log</code> to confirm they hold the expected data.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Experiment with reassignment.</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">oldVar = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">20</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// newLet = \'world\'; // this works</span>\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// fixedConst = false; // this will throw an error</span></code></pre>\n<p>Try uncommenting the lines one at a time and observe the console errors.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Explore data types.</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> num = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">42</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">str</span> = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'JavaScript\'</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">bool</span> = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">false</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> undef;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> nul = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">null</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> obj = {name: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Alice\'</span>, age: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">30</span>};\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> arr = [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>];\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> sym = Symbol(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'id\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> big = 9007199254740991n; <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// BigInt literal</span></code></pre>\n<p>Use <code>typeof</code> to log each type: <code>console.log(typeof num); // number</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Practice type conversion.</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> numStr = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'5\'</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> sum = Number(numStr) + <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>; <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// explicit conversion</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> concat = numStr + <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>; <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// implicit coercion</span>\nconsole.log(sum); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// 15</span>\nconsole.log(concat); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// \'510\'</span></code></pre>\n<p>Explain why the results differ.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Test variable scope.</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> globalVar = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'I am global\'</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> testScope() {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> localVar = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'I am local\'</span>;\n  console.log(globalVar); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// accessible</span>\n  console.log(localVar); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// accessible</span>\n}\n\ntestScope();\nconsole.log(localVar); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// ReferenceError</span></code></pre>\n<p>Describe what happens when you try to log <code>localVar</code> outside the function.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Observe hoisting.</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">console.log(hoistedVar); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// undefined, not ReferenceError</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">var</span> hoistedVar = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>;\n\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// console.log(temporalVar); // ReferenceError</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> temporalVar = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>;</code></pre>\n<p>Explain the difference between the two console.log statements.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Write a small script that uses all concepts.</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> calculateTotal(price, taxRate) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> tax = Number(taxRate) * price;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> total = price + tax;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> total;\n}\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> price = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">100</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> taxRate = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'0.08\'</span>; <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// string</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> total = calculateTotal(price, taxRate);\nconsole.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Total with tax: $\'</span> + total);</code></pre>\n<p>Ensure the script runs without errors and outputs the correct total.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit a single <code>index.html</code> file that contains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The script tags with all the code from the steps above.</li>\n<li>A brief comment block at the top explaining the purpose of the file.</li>\n<li>Console output that demonstrates each concept (you can capture the console logs and paste them into a separate <code>output.txt</code> file if you prefer).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Using <code>var</code> when <code>let</code> or <code>const</code> would be clearer.</li>\n<li>Assuming <code>undefined</code> and <code>null</code> are the same – they are distinct values.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that <code>const</code> variables cannot be reassigned.</li>\n<li>Relying solely on implicit coercion; explicit conversion is safer and more predictable.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Keep your variables named descriptively – <code>userAge</code> is better than <code>u</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Use <code>strict mode</code> (<code>\'use strict\';</code>) to catch accidental global variable declarations.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>When debugging scope issues, use <code>console.dir(window)</code> in browsers to inspect global variables.</p>\n</blockquote>', 'JavaScript Variables and Data Types', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 14:00:29');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(949, 962, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a30065b81526_1781532251.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p>\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\"><br></p></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you will build a simple tip calculator that uses JavaScript functions, conditional statements, loops, and arrow functions.  In a real job you often write reusable pieces of code that can be called from many places; mastering functions and control structures lets you keep your code clean, testable, and maintainable.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Function</strong> – A block of code that performs a specific task and can be run whenever you need it.  Think of a kitchen recipe: the recipe itself is the function, and you can make as many dishes as you like by following it again.</li>\n<li><strong>Function Parameters</strong> – The inputs you give a function so it can work with different data.  Parameters are like the ingredients you put into a recipe; each one changes the final dish.</li>\n<li><strong>Return Type</strong> – The value a function gives back after it finishes.  It’s the finished dish that comes out of the kitchen.</li>\n<li><strong>Conditional Statements</strong> – Code that chooses between different paths based on conditions.  It’s like a traffic light: red stops you, green lets you go, yellow warns you.</li>\n<li><strong>Loops</strong> – Repeating a block of code multiple times.  Imagine a conveyor belt that keeps adding items until it reaches a certain count.</li>\n<li><strong>Function Expression</strong> – Defining a function and assigning it to a variable.  It’s like writing a recipe on a sticky note and putting that note in a drawer for later use.</li>\n<li><strong>Arrow Functions</strong> – A shorter syntax for function expressions, introduced in ES6.  They’re like a shorthand way of writing the same recipe on a post‑it: quicker and often easier to read.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Step‑by‑step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>Create a new folder called <code>day28</code> and open a file named <code>index.html</code> inside it.  Add the basic HTML skeleton:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'utf-8\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Tip Calculator&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Tip Calculator&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'bill\'</span>&gt;Bill amount:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'number\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'bill\'</span> placeholder=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'0.00\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;br&gt;\n  &lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'people\'</span>&gt;Number of people:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'number\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'people\'</span> placeholder=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'1\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;br&gt;\n  &lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'service\'</span>&gt;Service quality:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;select id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'service\'</span>&gt;\n    &lt;option value=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'excellent\'</span>&gt;Excellent&lt;/option&gt;\n    &lt;option value=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'good\'</span>&gt;Good&lt;/option&gt;\n    &lt;option value=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'fair\'</span>&gt;Fair&lt;/option&gt;\n  &lt;/select&gt;\n  &lt;br&gt;\n  &lt;button id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'calculate\'</span>&gt;Calculate&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;p id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'result\'</span>&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;script src=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'script.js\'</span>&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Create a file named <code>script.js</code> in the same folder.  Inside, start by selecting the DOM elements you’ll need:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> billInput = document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'bill\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> peopleInput = document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'people\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> serviceSelect = document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'service\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> calculateBtn = document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'calculate\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> resultPara = document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'result\'</span>);\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Write a function called <code>calculateTip</code> that takes three parameters: <code>bill</code>, <code>people</code>, and <code>service</code>.  Inside the function, use a <code>switch</code> statement to determine the tip percentage based on the service quality:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> calculateTip(bill, people, service) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> tipPercent;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">switch</span> (service) {\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">case</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'excellent\'</span>:\n      tipPercent = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0.20</span>;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">break</span>;\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">case</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'good\'</span>:\n      tipPercent = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0.15</span>;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">break</span>;\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">case</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'fair\'</span>:\n      tipPercent = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0.10</span>;\n      <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">break</span>;\n    default:\n      tipPercent = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>;\n  }\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> tip = bill * tipPercent;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> total = bill + tip;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> perPerson = total / people;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> { tip, total, perPerson };\n}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Add an event listener to the <code>calculateBtn</code> that reads the user input, converts it to numbers, and calls <code>calculateTip</code>:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">calculateBtn.addEventListener(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'click\'</span>, () =&gt; {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> bill = parseFloat(billInput.value) || <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> people = parseInt(peopleInput.value) || <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> service = serviceSelect.value;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> result = calculateTip(bill, people, service);\n  resultPara.textContent = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`Tip: $${result.tip.toFixed(2)} | Total: $${result.total.toFixed(2)} | Each: $${result.perPerson.toFixed(2)}`</span>;\n});\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Now refactor the <code>calculateTip</code> function into a function expression assigned to a constant, and then rewrite it again as an arrow function.  Replace the original function definition with the arrow version:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> calculateTip = (bill, people, service) =&gt; {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> tipPercent = {\n    excellent: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0.20</span>,\n    good: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0.15</span>,\n    fair: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0.10</span>\n  }[service] || <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> tip = bill * tipPercent;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> total = bill + tip;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> perPerson = total / people;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> { tip, total, perPerson };\n};\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Test the calculator by entering different values.  Verify that the tip, total, and per‑person amounts update correctly.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>You should submit a zip file containing the <code>day28</code> folder with <code>index.html</code> and <code>script.js</code>.  The page must load in a browser and display the tip calculator with functioning buttons and correct calculations.</p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Using <code>var</code> instead of <code>let</code> or <code>const</code> can lead to unexpected variable hoisting.</li>\n<li>Forgetting to convert input values to numbers causes string concatenation instead of arithmetic.</li>\n<li>Missing a <code>break</code> in a <code>switch</code> can let the code fall through to the next case.</li>\n<li>Returning an object without curly braces inside an arrow function may be misinterpreted as a block; wrap the object in parentheses.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Use <code>const</code> for functions and variables that never change; it protects against accidental reassignment.</li>\n<li>Keep the <code>calculateTip</code> function pure: it should not modify external state, only compute and return values.</li>\n<li>When debugging, log intermediate values with <code>console.log</code> to see exactly what the function receives and returns.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n', 'JavaScript Functions and Control Structures', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 14:04:40'),
(950, 963, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\"><a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&amp;q=DOM+manipulation+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3006bd36759_1781532349.webp\" alt=\"image\" style=\"border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></a>\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you will learn how to change a web page while it is running. By learning to manipulate the Document Object Model (DOM) you can build interactive sites that react to clicks, keystrokes, and other user actions. In real jobs, this skill lets you create dynamic dashboards, single‑page apps, and responsive UI components.</p><h3>Concepts you need</h3><h4>Document Object Model (DOM)</h4><p>The DOM is the browser’s representation of your HTML file. Think of it as a tree where each node is a part of the page – the <code>html</code> root, <code>body</code> children, and so on. The browser builds this tree automatically when it loads your page.</p><h4>DOM Elements and Nodes</h4><p>Every part of the tree is a <strong>node</strong>. Some nodes are <strong>elements</strong> (like <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>), others are <strong>text nodes</strong> that hold the visible text. Elements can have attributes, children, and styles.</p><h4>DOM Methods and Properties</h4><p>JavaScript can query and modify the tree using methods such as <code>getElementById</code>, <code>querySelector</code>, <code>appendChild</code>, and properties like <code>innerText</code> or <code>style</code>. These are the tools you use to read and change the page.</p><h4>DOM Events and Event Listeners</h4><p>Events are actions that happen on the page – a click, a key press, or a mouse move. An <strong>event listener</strong> is a function you attach to an element so that it runs when a specific event occurs. The pattern is <code>element.addEventListener(\'click\', handler)</code>.</p><h3>Step‑by‑step task</h3><ol><li><strong>Create the HTML skeleton</strong><p>Make a file called <code>index.html</code> with a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> that contains a paragraph and a button.</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;<br>&lt;html&gt;<br>&lt;head&gt;<br>  &lt;title&gt;DOM Demo&lt;/title&gt;<br>&lt;/head&gt;<br>&lt;body&gt;<br>  &lt;div id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'content\'</span>&gt;<br>    &lt;p id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'text\'</span>&gt;Hello, world!&lt;/p&gt;<br>    &lt;button id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'toggleBtn\'</span>&gt;Toggle Text&lt;/button&gt;<br>  &lt;/div&gt;<br>&lt;/body&gt;<br>&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre></li><li><strong>Link a JavaScript file</strong><p>Add a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag that points to <code>script.js</code> at the end of the body.</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script src=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'script.js\'</span>&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre></li><li><strong>Grab elements in JavaScript</strong><p>In <code>script.js</code>, use <code>document.getElementById</code> to store references to the button and paragraph.</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> btn = document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'toggleBtn\'</span>);<br><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> para = document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'text\'</span>);</code></pre></li><li><strong>Add a click event listener</strong><p>When the button is clicked, toggle the paragraph’s visibility by changing its <code>style.display</code> property.</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">btn.addEventListener(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'click\'</span>, () =&gt; {<br>  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (para.style.display === <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'none\'</span>) {<br>    para.style.display = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'block\'</span>;<br>  } <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">else</span> {<br>    para.style.display = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'none\'</span>;<br>  }<br>});</code></pre></li><li><strong>Change text color on hover</strong><p>Use <code>addEventListener</code> for <code>mouseenter</code> and <code>mouseleave</code> to change the paragraph’s color.</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">para.addEventListener(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'mouseenter\'</span>, () =&gt; {<br>  para.style.color = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'blue\'</span>;<br>});<br>para.addEventListener(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'mouseleave\'</span>, () =&gt; {<br>  para.style.color = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'black\'</span>;<br>});</code></pre></li><li><strong>Log actions to the console</strong><p>Inside each event handler, add a <code>console.log</code> message so you can see when events fire.</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Button clicked\'</span>);</code></pre></li><li><strong>Test in the browser</strong><p>Open <code>index.html</code> in a browser, click the button, hover over the paragraph, and watch the console.</p></li></ol><h3>Deliverable</h3><p>Submit the two files: <code>index.html</code> and <code>script.js</code>. They should work together to toggle the paragraph and change its color on hover, with console logs for each action.</p><h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3><ul><li>Using <code>innerHTML</code> instead of <code>innerText</code> when you only need plain text.</li><li>Adding event listeners before the DOM is fully loaded – always place the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag at the end of <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> or use <code>DOMContentLoaded</code>.</li><li>Mixing up <code>display: none</code> with <code>visibility: hidden</code>; the former removes the element from layout.</li><li>Forgetting to use single quotes in HTML attributes inside code blocks; double quotes will break the JSON.</li></ul><h3>Pro tips</h3><blockquote><p><strong>Use <code>classList</code> for toggling classes</strong> – it keeps style changes in CSS instead of inline styles.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p><strong>Debug with <code>console.dir</code></strong> to inspect element objects and see all available properties.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p><strong>Keep event handlers small</strong> – separate logic into named functions for readability.</p></blockquote>', 'JavaScript DOM Manipulation', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-15 14:06:09'),
(951, 964, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a311f35c6d54_1781604149.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\">&nbsp;</p></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you will learn how to move around the HTML structure with JavaScript. By mastering DOM traversal you can dynamically change any part of a page, a skill that every front‑end developer uses to build interactive applications.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>DOM Child and Parent Nodes</h4>\n<p>Think of the HTML document as a family tree. Every element has a <strong>parent</strong> (the element that contains it) and may have <strong>children</strong> (elements inside it). For example, a &lt;ul&gt; element is the parent of all its &lt;li&gt; items.</p>\n<h4>DOM Sibling Nodes</h4>\n<p>Siblings are like cousins in the family tree – they share the same parent. In the DOM, <strong>nextSibling</strong> and <strong>previousSibling</strong> let you move left or right among these cousins.</p>\n<h4>DOM Node Lists</h4>\n<p>When you ask the DOM for many elements, it returns a <strong>NodeList</strong>, a collection that behaves like an array but is not exactly one. You can loop through it with <strong>forEach</strong> or a classic <strong>for</strong> loop.</p>\n<h4>DOM Query Selectors</h4>\n<p>Query selectors are the fastest way to grab elements. <strong>querySelector</strong> returns the first match, while <strong>querySelectorAll</strong> returns a NodeList of all matches. They use CSS selector syntax, so you can pick by tag, class, id, attribute, and more.</p>\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Create the HTML skeleton</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!doctype html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'utf-8\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;DOM Traversal Demo&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;div id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'container\'</span>&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;Fruit List&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;ul id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'fruit-list\'</span>&gt;\n      &lt;li <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'fruit\'</span>&gt;Apple&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'fruit\'</span>&gt;Banana&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'fruit\'</span>&gt;Cherry&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;script src=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'app.js\'</span>&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>This sets up a simple list you will manipulate.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Open app.js and select the list container</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> container = document.querySelector(\'<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#container\');</span></code></pre>\n<p>We use querySelector to grab the div with id container.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Find the first fruit item (child of the list)</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> firstFruit = container.querySelector(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'.fruit\'</span>);</code></pre>\n<p>Here we use a CSS class selector to get the first &lt;li&gt;.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Navigate to the parent of that fruit</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> listParent = firstFruit.parentNode;</code></pre>\n<p>parentNode gives the &lt;ul&gt; element that contains the fruit.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Move to the next sibling (the second fruit)</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> secondFruit = firstFruit.nextElementSibling;</code></pre>\n<p>nextElementSibling skips text nodes and returns the next &lt;li&gt;.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Loop through all fruits using a NodeList</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> allFruits = document.querySelectorAll(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'.fruit\'</span>);\nallFruits.forEach((item, index) =&gt; {\n  console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`Fruit ${index + 1}: ${item.textContent}`</span>);\n});</code></pre>\n<p>querySelectorAll returns a NodeList; forEach lets us iterate.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Change the text of the last fruit using sibling navigation</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> lastFruit = secondFruit.nextElementSibling;\nlastFruit.textContent = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Date\'</span>;</code></pre>\n<p>We moved from the second fruit to its next sibling and updated the content.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Insert a new fruit before the first one using parent manipulation</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> newFruit = document.createElement(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'li\'</span>);\nnewFruit.className = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'fruit\'</span>;\nnewFruit.textContent = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Elderberry\'</span>;\nlistParent.insertBefore(newFruit, firstFruit);</code></pre>\n<p>createElement makes a new node; insertBefore places it before the first fruit.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Remove the second fruit using parentNode.removeChild</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">listParent.removeChild(secondFruit);</code></pre>\n<p>This deletes the Banana item from the DOM.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Verify the final structure by logging the innerHTML of the list</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">console.log(container.innerHTML);</code></pre>\n<p>Check that the list now contains Elderberry, Apple, and Date.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit the following files:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>index.html – the HTML skeleton from step 1.</li>\n<li>app.js – the JavaScript file that performs all traversal tasks described above.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your console should display the fruit order before and after each manipulation, and the final list should read: Elderberry, Apple, Date.</p>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Using <code>nextSibling</code> instead of <code>nextElementSibling</code> – the former can return text nodes like line breaks.</li>\n<li>Assuming <code>querySelectorAll</code> returns a real array – it is a NodeList, so <code>map</code> and <code>filter</code> are not available without converting.</li>\n<li>Modifying the DOM inside a loop that changes the structure – this can skip elements or cause errors.</li>\n<li>Forgetting to escape HTML in code blocks – use &lt; and &gt; to keep the page valid.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Use <code>closest</code> to find the nearest ancestor that matches a selector, which is handy for event delegation.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>When adding many elements, build a document fragment first and append it once to reduce reflows.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Always test your selectors in the browser console first to confirm they match the intended elements.</p>\n</blockquote>\n', 'JavaScript DOM Traversal', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:02:39'),
(952, 965, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; <img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a311fb77bc52_1781604279.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p>\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\"><br></p></div><h3>JavaScript DOM Modification</h3>\n\n<p>Today you will build a dynamic to-do list app that lets users add, remove, and style tasks using JavaScript. This skill is essential for real jobs where web pages need to update instantly without reloading.</p>\n\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n\n<p><strong>Creating and Appending Elements</strong> is like building with LEGO bricks - you create new pieces and attach them to your structure. Use <code>document.createElement()</code> to make new elements, then <code>appendChild()</code> to add them.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Removing and Replacing Elements</strong> works like editing a document: use <code>removeChild()</code> to delete nodes, or <code>replaceChild()</code> to swap elements. Think of it as replacing a broken lightbulb with a new one.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Modifying Attributes and Styles</strong> is like customizing a car. Use <code>element.setAttribute()</code> to change HTML attributes, and <code>element.style</code> to apply CSS styles directly. For example, you can make a button glow or change its size.</p>\n\n<p><strong>DOM Fragment and Template</strong> are like blueprints and cookie cutters. A <code>DocumentFragment</code> lets you build elements in memory before adding them to the page. The <code>template</code> tag stores HTML templates that you can clone and reuse efficiently.</p>\n\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>Create a basic HTML file with this structure:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;&lt;title&gt;My To-Do List&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'text\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'taskInput\'</span> placeholder=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'New task\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;button id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'addBtn\'</span>&gt;Add&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;ul id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'taskList\'</span>&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;script src=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'script.js\'</span>&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>Add JavaScript to create and append elements:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> addBtn = document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'addBtn\'</span>);\naddBtn.addEventListener(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'click\'</span>, () =&gt; {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> taskText = document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'taskInput\'</span>).value;\n  \n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> li = document.createElement(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'li\'</span>);\n  li.textContent = taskText;\n  \n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> deleteBtn = document.createElement(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'button\'</span>);\n  deleteBtn.textContent = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Delete\'</span>;\n  deleteBtn.onclick = () =&gt; li.remove();\n  \n  li.appendChild(deleteBtn);\n  document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'taskList\'</span>).appendChild(li);\n});</code></pre>\n    This creates list items with delete buttons when the Add button is clicked.\n  </li>\n\n  <li>Modify element styles and attributes:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">li.style.backgroundColor = \'<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#f0f0f0\';</span>\nli.setAttribute(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'data-created\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">new</span> Date().toISOString());</code></pre>\n    This adds a light gray background and stores creation time in a custom attribute.\n  </li>\n\n  <li>Replace an element:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> oldItem = document.querySelector(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'li\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> newItem = document.createElement(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'li\'</span>);\nnewItem.textContent = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Updated Task\'</span>;\noldItem.replaceWith(newItem);</code></pre>\n    This finds the first list item and replaces it with a new one.\n  </li>\n\n  <li>Use a template for efficient element creation:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;template id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'taskTemplate\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;\n    &lt;span <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'task-text\'</span>&gt;{{text}}&lt;/span&gt;\n    &lt;button <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'delete-btn\'</span>&gt;Delete&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>\n\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> createTaskElement(text) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> template = document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'taskTemplate\'</span>);\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> clone = document.importNode(template.content, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">true</span>);\n  clone.querySelector(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'.task-text\'</span>).textContent = text;\n  clone.querySelector(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'.delete-btn\'</span>).onclick = () =&gt; clone.host.remove();\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> clone;\n}</code></pre>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>Optimize with DocumentFragment:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span> (<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> i = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>; i &lt; <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>; i++) {\n  fragment.appendChild(createTaskElement(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`Task ${i+1}`</span>));\n}\ntaskList.appendChild(fragment);</code></pre>\n    This adds 5 tasks in one operation for better performance.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n\n<p>Submit a single HTML file named <code>todo-list.html</code> that:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Allows adding tasks with an input field and Add button</li>\n  <li>Displays each task as a list item with a delete button</li>\n  <li>Styles tasks with background color and creation timestamp</li>\n  <li>Includes a template for task elements</li>\n  <li>Uses DocumentFragment for batch additions</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Forgetting to append elements to the DOM - created elements exist in memory until added to the page</li>\n  <li>Not using <code>DocumentFragment</code> when adding multiple elements (can cause performance issues)</li>\n  <li>Modifying templates directly instead of using <code>importNode()</code> to clone them</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Use <code>dataset</code> property instead of <code>setAttribute()</code> for custom data: <code>element.dataset.created = \'2023-09-01\'</code></li>\n  <li>Clone templates with <code>document.importNode()</code> for clean reusable components</li>\n  <li>Group multiple DOM changes in a fragment first to avoid page reflow</li>\n</ul>', 'JavaScript DOM Modification', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:05:10'),
(953, 966, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a31200765c74_1781604359.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>JavaScript Events and Listeners</h3>\n\n<h4>Intro</h4>\n<p>Today you will build an interactive webpage that responds to user actions like clicks, keypresses, and form inputs. This is how real websites let users type, submit forms, and interact with buttons—events are the foundation of dynamic web experiences.</p>\n\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n\n<p><strong>Events</strong> are actions users take (like clicking a button) or things that happen in the browser (like a page loading). Think of them as \"signals\" your website can listen for.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Event Types</strong> are categories of events. Common types include:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>click</code> (button presses)</li>\n  <li><code>input</code> (typing in a text field)</li>\n  <li><code>submit</code> (form submission)</li>\n  <li><code>keydown</code> (any key pressed)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Event Listeners</strong> are JavaScript \"watchers\" that run code when an event happens. Adding one is like hiring a guard to shout, \"Hey, the user just clicked this button!\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Event Propagation</strong> describes how events travel through nested HTML elements. Imagine dropping a pebble in a pond—the ripple (event) spreads outward from where it happened.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bubbling</strong> is when an event on a child element also triggers its parent elements. For example, clicking a button inside a <code>div</code> will also trigger a <code>click</code> event on the <code>div</code>.</p>\n\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>\n    <p>Create an HTML file with this structure:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;button id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'colorButton\'</span>&gt;Click Me&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;p id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'output\'</span>&gt;Waiting <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span> input...&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;script src=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'script.js\'</span>&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>This creates a button and a paragraph to show results.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>\n    <p>In <code>script.js</code>, add this code to listen for a button click:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> button = document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'colorButton\'</span>);\nbutton.addEventListener(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'click\'</span>, () =&gt; {\n  button.style.backgroundColor = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'lightblue\'</span>;\n});</code></pre>\n    <p>This uses <code>addEventListener</code> to change the button’s background color when clicked.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>\n    <p>Add a form input that shows what the user types:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> input = document.createElement(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'input\'</span>);\ninput.id = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'userInput\'</span>;\ndocument.body.appendChild(input);\n\ninput.addEventListener(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'input\'</span>, (event) =&gt; {\n  document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'output\'</span>).textContent = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'You typed: \'</span> + event.target.value;\n});</code></pre>\n    <p>The <code>input</code> event updates the paragraph in real time as the user types.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>\n    <p>Demo event bubbling with nested elements:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'parent\'</span> style=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'padding: 20px; border: 1px solid black\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;button id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'child\'</span>&gt;Nested Button&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">document.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'parent\'</span>).addEventListener(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'click\'</span>, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Parent clicked!\'</span>);\n});\n\ndocument.getElementById(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'child\'</span>).addEventListener(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'click\'</span>, (event) =&gt; {\n  console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Child clicked!\'</span>);\n  event.stopPropagation(); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Stop the event from reaching the parent</span>\n});</code></pre>\n    <p>Clicking the button logs \"Child clicked!\" only. <code>stopPropagation()</code> prevents the event from bubbling up to the parent.</p>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>Submit three files: your HTML file, CSS (if used), and JavaScript file. Include screenshots of your working example and the browser console showing event logs from Step 4.</p>\n\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Forgetting to use <code>event.target.value</code> when reading form inputs</li>\n  <li>Mixing up <code>addEventListener</code> parameters (event type first, function second)</li>\n  <li>Not removing event listeners with <code>removeEventListener</code> when elements are deleted</li>\n  <li>Accidentally allowing event bubbling to trigger multiple handlers</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use <code>event.preventDefault()</code> to stop default actions (like form submission refreshing the page)</li>\n  <li>For complex pages, use <strong>event delegation</strong>: add one listener to a parent element instead of many to child elements</li>\n  <li>Always check MDN’s <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Events\" target=\"_blank\">Event reference</a> for full event type lists</li>\n</ul>', 'JavaScript Events and Listeners', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:06:14');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(954, 967, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: calculator error handling</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=calculator+error+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/calculator+error+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/calculator+error+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/calculator+error+handling/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=calculator+error+handling\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript Error Handling</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 33 of your web development internship! Today, we\'re going to learn about error handling in JavaScript, a crucial skill for any web developer. Error handling is essential to ensure that our applications behave correctly even when something unexpected happens.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>To complete today\'s task, you need to understand the following concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Try-catch blocks</strong>: A try-catch block is a way to handle errors in JavaScript. It consists of a try block, where you put the code that might throw an error, and a catch block, where you handle the error.</li>\n<li><strong>Error types and messages</strong>: In JavaScript, there are several types of errors, including syntax errors, runtime errors, and reference errors. Each type of error has a specific message that helps you understand what went wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>Error handling best practices</strong>: Error handling best practices include using try-catch blocks, logging errors, and providing meaningful error messages to users.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<p>Now that you\'ve learned the concepts, let\'s put them into practice. Your task is to create a simple calculator that takes two numbers as input and returns their sum. However, if the user enters something that\'s not a number, the calculator should display an error message.</p>\n<p>Here are the steps to complete the task:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new HTML file called <code>calculator.html</code> and add the following code:</pre><code>\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n&lt;title&gt;Calculator&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n&lt;h1&gt;Calculator&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;input id=\"num1\" type=\"number\" placeholder=\"Number 1\"&gt;\n&lt;input id=\"num2\" type=\"number\" placeholder=\"Number 2\"&gt;\n&lt;button onclick=\"calculate()\">Calculate&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;div id=\"result\"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;script src=\"script.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code></pre></li>\n<li>Create a new JavaScript file called <code>script.js</code> and add the following code:</pre><code>\nfunction calculate() {\n  try {\n    const num1 = parseFloat(document.getElementById(\'num1\').value);\n    const num2 = parseFloat(document.getElementById(\'num2\').value);\n    const result = num1 + num2;\n    document.getElementById(\'result\').innerHTML = `The result is ${result}`;\n  } catch (error) {\n    if (error instanceof SyntaxError) {\n      document.getElementById(\'result\').innerHTML = \'Error: Invalid input\';\n    } else if (error instanceof TypeError) {\n      document.getElementById(\'result\').innerHTML = \'Error: Invalid input\';\n    } else {\n      document.getElementById(\'result\').innerHTML = `Error: ${error.message}`;\n    }\n  }\n}\n</code></pre></li>\n<li>Open <code>calculator.html</code> in a web browser and test the calculator by entering different inputs. Observe how the calculator handles errors.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>Your deliverable for today is the completed <code>calculator.html</code> and <code>script.js</code> files. Make sure to test the calculator thoroughly to ensure it handles errors correctly.</p>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Not using try-catch blocks</strong>: Failing to use try-catch blocks can lead to unhandled errors that can crash your application.</li>\n<li><strong>Not logging errors</strong>: Not logging errors can make it difficult to debug issues in your application.</li>\n<li><strong>Providing meaningless error messages</strong>: Providing meaningless error messages can confuse users and make it harder for them to understand what went wrong.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use console.error() to log errors</strong>: You can use console.error() to log errors in your application, making it easier to debug issues.</li>\n<li><strong>Use try-catch blocks in production code</strong>: Even in production code, it\'s essential to use try-catch blocks to handle errors and prevent application crashes.</li>\n<li><strong>Provide meaningful error messages to users</strong>: Providing meaningful error messages to users can help them understand what went wrong and how to fix the issue.</li>\n</ul>', 'JavaScript Error Handling', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(955, 968, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a31207adeafb_1781604474.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>JavaScript Debugging Techniques</h3>\n<p>As a web developer, you\'ll encounter errors in your code. Debugging is the process of finding and fixing these errors. In this task, you\'ll learn how to use JavaScript debugging techniques to identify and resolve issues in your code.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>In this task, you\'ll learn about the following concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Introduction to Debugging</li>\n<li>Console Logging and Debugging</li>\n<li>Debugger Tools and Features</li>\n<li>Common Debugging Mistakes</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Introduction to Debugging</h4>\n<p>Debugging is the process of finding and fixing errors in your code. When you write code, you\'re trying to achieve a specific goal. However, sometimes your code doesn\'t work as expected, and you need to identify the issue. Debugging helps you find the problem and fix it.</p>\n<p>Imagine you\'re a detective trying to solve a mystery. You have clues, and you need to follow them to find the solution. In debugging, the clues are the errors in your code, and you need to follow them to find the solution.</p>\n<h4>Console Logging and Debugging</h4>\n<p>Console logging is a technique used to print messages to the console. You can use it to see what\'s happening in your code and identify issues. The console is like a detective\'s notebook, where you can write notes and track your progress.</p>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how you can use console logging:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">console.log(&amp;<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Hello, World!\');</span>\n</code></pre>\n<p>This will print the message \"Hello, World!\" to the console.</p>\n<p>You can also use console logging to see the values of variables:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> name = &amp;<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;John\';</span>\nconsole.log(name);\n</code></pre>\n<p>This will print the value of the variable \"name\" to the console.</p>\n<h4>Debugger Tools and Features</h4>\n<p>Debugger tools are software tools that help you debug your code. They provide features such as breakpoints, step-through execution, and variable inspection.</p>\n<p>A breakpoint is a point in your code where the debugger will pause execution. You can then inspect the variables and see what\'s happening.</p>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how you can set a breakpoint in Chrome DevTools:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Set a breakpoint in the console</span>\ndebugger;\n</code></pre>\n<p>This will pause the execution of your code at the current line.</p>\n<p>Step-through execution allows you to run your code line by line. You can step through the code and see what\'s happening at each line.</p>\n<p>Variable inspection allows you to see the values of variables at a specific point in your code.</p>\n<h4>Common Debugging Mistakes</h4>\n<p>Here are some common debugging mistakes to avoid:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Not using console logging to see what\'s happening in your code.</li>\n<li>Not using debugger tools to inspect variables and see what\'s happening.</li>\n<li>Not testing your code thoroughly before deploying it.</li>\n<li>Not keeping track of your changes and updates.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<p>In this task, you\'ll practice using console logging and debugger tools to debug a simple JavaScript program.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new HTML file and add the following code:</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> name = &amp;<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;John\';</span>\nconsole.log(name);\nconsole.log(&amp;<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Hello, World!\');</span>\n&lt;/script&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<li>Open the HTML file in a browser and inspect the console. You should see the messages \"John\" and \"Hello, World!\" printed to the console.</li>\n<li>Now, let\'s add a breakpoint in the code. Open the Chrome DevTools and set a breakpoint at the line `console.log(name);`. Then, run the code and see what happens.</li>\n<li>Next, let\'s use step-through execution to see what\'s happening in the code. Run the code and step through it line by line. You should see the value of the variable `name` printed to the console.</li>\n<li>Finally, let\'s use variable inspection to see the value of the variable `name` at a specific point in the code. Run the code and inspect the variable `name` at the line `console.log(name);`. You should see the value `John` printed to the console.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>For this task, you need to submit the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A screenshot of the console showing the messages \"John\" and \"Hello, World!\" printed to the console.</li>\n<li>A screenshot of the Chrome DevTools showing the breakpoint set at the line `console.log(name);`.</li>\n<li>A screenshot of the Chrome DevTools showing the step-through execution of the code.</li>\n<li>A screenshot of the Chrome DevTools showing the variable inspection of the variable `name` at the line `console.log(name);`.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<p>Here are some pro tips to help you become a better debugger:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Always use console logging to see what\'s happening in your code.</li>\n<li>Always use debugger tools to inspect variables and see what\'s happening.</li>\n<li>Always test your code thoroughly before deploying it.</li>\n<li>Always keep track of your changes and updates.</li>\n</ul>', 'JavaScript Debugging Techniques', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:08:03'),
(956, 969, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: code review checklist</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=code+review+checklist\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/code+review+checklist\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/code+review+checklist/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/code+review+checklist/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=code+review+checklist\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>JavaScript Best Practices and Optimization</h3>\n<p>Today, you will learn about code organization, performance optimization, security considerations, and code review. These are essential skills for any web developer, and mastering them will make you a more efficient and reliable programmer.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<p>Before we start, let\'s cover some key concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Code Organization</strong>: This refers to the way you structure your code to make it easy to read, understand, and maintain. Think of it like organizing your closet: you want to put similar items together, so you can find what you need quickly.</li>\n<li><strong>Performance Optimization</strong>: This is the process of making your code run faster and more efficiently. Imagine you\'re at a restaurant, and the kitchen is slow. You would want to optimize the kitchen workflow to get your food faster.</li>\n<li><strong>Security Considerations</strong>: This refers to the measures you take to protect your code and users from potential threats. Think of it like locking your doors and windows to prevent burglars.</li>\n<li><strong>Code Review</strong>: This is the process of reviewing your code and others\' code to ensure it\'s correct, efficient, and secure. It\'s like having a friend review your essay before you submit it.</li>\n<li><strong>Code Review Checklist</strong>: This is a list of items to check when reviewing code. It\'s like a checklist for your essay review.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<p>Now, let\'s get started with the task:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Create a Code Organization Plan</strong>: Think about how you want to structure your code. Consider using a modular approach, where each module has its own purpose and is easy to understand.</li>\n<li><strong>Write a Simple JavaScript Module</strong>:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// module.js</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> greet(name) {\n  console.log(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Hello, &#39; + name);</span>\n}\nexport { greet };\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is a simple module that exports a function called `greet`. You can use this module in another file by importing it.</li>\n<li><strong>Use a Code Review Checklist</strong>: Create a list of items to check when reviewing code. Here\'s an example:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// code-review-checklist.js</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> checklist = [\n  &<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Is the code readable?&#39;,</span>\n  &<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Is the code efficient?&#39;,</span>\n  &<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;Is the code secure?&#39;</span>\n];\nexport { checklist };\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is a simple checklist that you can use to review code.</li>\n<li><strong>Optimize a Simple Function</strong>:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// optimize.js</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> sum(numbers) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> total = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span> (<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">let</span> i = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>; i &lt; numbers.length; i++) {\n    total += numbers[i];\n  }\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> total;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function adds up an array of numbers. To optimize it, you can use the `reduce` method, which is more efficient:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// optimized.js</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> sum(numbers) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> numbers.reduce((a, b) =&gt; a + b, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This optimized function uses `reduce` to add up the numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Secure a Simple Function</strong>:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// insecure.js</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> getSecret() {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> &<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;my secret&#39;;</span>\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function returns a secret string. To secure it, you can use a secure random number generator and store the secret in a secure location:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// secure.js</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> crypto = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;crypto&#39;);</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> getSecret() {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> secret = crypto.randomBytes(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">16</span>).toString(&<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#39;hex&#39;);</span>\n  <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// store secret in a secure location</span>\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> secret;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This secure function uses a secure random number generator to generate a secret string.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit the following files:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>module.js</li>\n<li>code-review-checklist.js</li>\n<li>optimize.js</li>\n<li>optimized.js</li>\n<li>secure.js</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Not following a code organization plan</strong>: This can lead to messy and hard-to-understand code.</li>\n<li><strong>Not using a code review checklist</strong>: This can lead to missed errors and security vulnerabilities.</li>\n<li><strong>Not optimizing code</strong>: This can lead to slow performance and wasted resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Not securing code</strong>: This can lead to data breaches and security vulnerabilities.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use a linter to enforce coding standards</strong>: A linter can help you catch errors and enforce coding standards.</li>\n<li><strong>Use a code formatter to make code readable</strong>: A code formatter can help you make your code more readable and consistent.</li>\n<li><strong>Use a code coverage tool to measure code quality</strong>: A code coverage tool can help you measure how much of your code is being executed and identify areas for improvement.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n{\"question\":\"What is code organization?\", \"options\":[\"A. The process of making code run faster\", \"B. The process of making code more secure\", \"C. The process of structuring code to make it easy to read and understand\", \"D. The process of reviewing code\"], \"correct_index\":2,\"explanation\":\"Code organization is the process of structuring code to make it easy to read and understand.\"}\n</li>\n<li>\n{\"question\":\"What is performance optimization?\", \"options\":[\"A. The process of making code more secure\", \"B. The process of making code run faster and more efficiently\", \"C. The process of reviewing code\", \"D. The process of structuring code\"], \"correct_index\":1,\"explanation\":\"Performance optimization is the process of making code run faster and more efficiently.\"}\n</li>\n<li>\n{\"question\":\"What is a code review checklist?\", \"options\":[\"A. A list of items to check when reviewing code\", \"B. A list of items to check when writing code\", \"C. A list of items to check when optimizing code\", \"D. A list of items to check when securing code\"], \"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"A code review checklist is a list of items to check when reviewing code.\"}\n</li>\n<li>\n{\"question\":\"What is a linter?\", \"options\":[\"A. A tool that enforces coding standards\", \"B. A tool that makes code more readable\", \"C. A tool that measures code quality\", \"D. A tool that optimizes code\"], \"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"A linter is a tool that enforces coding standards.\"}\n</li>\n<li>\n{\"question\":\"What is a code formatter?\", \"options\":[\"A. A tool that enforces coding standards\", \"B. A tool that makes code more readable\", \"C. A tool that measures code quality\", \"D. A tool that optimizes code\"], \"correct_index\":1,\"explanation\":\"A code formatter is a tool that makes code more readable.\"}\n</li>\n<li>\n{\"question\":\"What is a code coverage tool?\", \"options\":[\"A. A tool that enforces coding standards\", \"B. A tool that makes code more readable\", \"C. A tool that measures code quality\", \"D. A tool that optimizes code\"], \"correct_index\":2,\"explanation\":\"A code coverage tool is a tool that measures code quality.\"}\n</li>\n<li>\n{\"question\":\"What is the purpose of a code review checklist?\", \"options\":[\"A. To enforce coding standards\", \"B. To make code more readable\", \"C. To measure code quality\", \"D. To ensure that code is correct, efficient, and secure\"], \"correct_index\":3,\"explanation\":\"The purpose of a code review checklist is to ensure that code is correct, efficient, and secure.\"}\n</li>\n<li>\n{\"question\":\"What is the purpose of a linter?\", \"options\":[\"A. To make code more readable\", \"B. To measure code quality\", \"C. To optimize code\", \"D. To enforce coding standards\"], \"correct_index\":3,\"explanation\":\"The purpose of a linter is to enforce coding standards.\"}\n</li>\n<li>\n{\"question\":\"What is the purpose of a code formatter?\", \"options\":[\"A. To enforce coding standards\", \"B. To measure code quality\", \"C. To optimize code\", \"D. To make code more readable\"], \"correct_index\":3,\"explanation\":\"The purpose of a code formatter is to make code more readable.\"}\n</li>\n<li>\n{\"question\":\"What is the purpose of a code coverage tool?\", \"options\":[\"A. To enforce coding standards\", \"B. To make code more readable\", \"C. To optimize code\", \"D. To measure code quality\"], \"correct_index\":3,\"explanation\":\"The purpose of a code coverage tool is to measure code quality.\"}\n</li>\n<li>\n{\"question\":\"What is the purpose of a code review?\", \"options\":[\"A. To enforce coding standards\", \"B. To make code more readable\", \"C. To measure code quality\", \"D. To ensure that code is correct, efficient, and secure\"], \"correct_index\":3,\"explanation\":\"The purpose of a code review is to ensure that code is correct, efficient, and secure.\"}\n</li>\n</ul>', 'JavaScript Best Practices and Optimization', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(957, 970, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3120a78d948_1781604519.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Day 36: Introduction to Front-end Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 36 of our web development internship. Today, we\'re going to explore front-end frameworks, which are essential tools for building efficient and scalable web applications. By the end of this day, you\'ll understand the benefits of using front-end frameworks and be able to choose the right one for your project.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<p>A front-end framework is a collection of pre-written code that helps developers build web applications more quickly and efficiently. Think of it like a toolbox that contains all the necessary tools to build a house. Just as a carpenter wouldn\'t build a house from scratch every time, a web developer wouldn\'t start from scratch every time they build a web application.</p>\n<p>Now, let\'s talk about the types of front-end frameworks. There are two main types:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Client-side frameworks: These frameworks run on the client-side (i.e., in the user\'s browser). Examples include React, Angular, and Vue.js.</li>\n  <li>Server-side frameworks: These frameworks run on the server-side and generate HTML, CSS, and JavaScript code for the client-side. Examples include Express.js and Ruby on Rails.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Benefits of Using Front-end Frameworks</p>\n<p>Front-end frameworks offer several benefits, including:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Speed: Front-end frameworks help developers build web applications faster by providing pre-written code and reusable components.</li>\n  <li>Efficiency: Front-end frameworks improve the efficiency of web applications by reducing the amount of code that needs to be written and maintained.</li>\n  <li>Scalability: Front-end frameworks make it easier to scale web applications by providing tools for handling large amounts of data and traffic.</li>\n  <li>Reusability: Front-end frameworks enable developers to reuse code across multiple projects, reducing the time and effort required to build new applications.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Popular Front-end Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Here are some popular front-end frameworks:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>React: A JavaScript library for building user interfaces.</li>\n  <li>Angular: A JavaScript framework for building complex web applications.</li>\n  <li>Vue.js: A progressive and flexible JavaScript framework for building web applications.</li>\n  <li>Ember.js: A JavaScript framework for building complex web applications.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<p>Today\'s task is to build a simple web application using React. Follow these steps:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Create a new React project</strong>: Run the command `npx create-react-app my-app` in your terminal to create a new React project called `my-app`.</li>\n  <li><strong>Install required packages</strong>: Run the command `npm install` in your terminal to install the required packages for the project.</li>\n  <li><strong>Write your first React component</strong>: Create a new file called `App.js` in the `src` directory and add the following code:<code>\n&lt;div&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n</code><p>This code creates a simple React component that displays the text \"Hello World\".</p>\n  </li><li><strong>Render the component</strong>: Open the `index.js` file in the `src` directory and add the following code:<code>\nimport React from \'react\';\nimport ReactDOM from \'react-dom\';\nimport App from \'./App\';\n\nReactDOM.render(&lt;App /&gt;, document.getElementById(\'root\'));\n</code><p>This code renders the `App` component to the DOM.</p>\n  </li><li><strong>Start the development server</strong>: Run the command `npm start` in your terminal to start the development server.</li>\n  <li><strong>Open the application in your browser</strong>: Open your web browser and navigate to `http://localhost:3000` to see the application in action.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Your deliverable for today is a simple React application that displays the text \"Hello World\".</p>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<p>Here are some common mistakes to avoid when working with front-end frameworks:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Not following the official documentation for the framework.</li>\n  <li>Not using the correct syntax for the framework.</li>\n  <li>Not understanding the concept of state and props in React.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<p>Here are some pro tips for working with front-end frameworks:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use the official documentation for the framework to learn more about its features and best practices.</li>\n  <li>Experiment with different frameworks to find the one that best suits your needs.</li>\n  <li>Join online communities and forums to connect with other developers who are using the framework.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>What\'s next?</h3>\n<p>Tomorrow, we\'ll explore state management in React. Be sure to complete today\'s task before moving on to the next day.</p>', 'Introduction to Front-end Frameworks', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:08:48'),
(958, 971, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; <img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3120e498e81_1781604580.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Bootstrap Basics</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ll learn the basics of Bootstrap, a popular front-end framework for building responsive and mobile-first websites. You\'ll create a simple website layout using Bootstrap\'s grid system, CSS components, and JavaScript components. This will help you understand how to build a website from scratch using Bootstrap.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need:</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Introduction to Bootstrap</strong>: Bootstrap is a front-end framework that helps you build responsive and mobile-first websites. It provides a set of pre-designed CSS and JavaScript components that you can use to build your website.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bootstrap Grid System</strong>: The grid system is a fundamental concept in Bootstrap that helps you create responsive layouts. It\'s based on a 12-column grid that you can use to arrange your content.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bootstrap CSS Components</strong>: Bootstrap provides a set of pre-designed CSS components that you can use to build your website. These components include navigation bars, buttons, forms, and more.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bootstrap JavaScript Components</strong>: Bootstrap also provides a set of pre-designed JavaScript components that you can use to add interactivity to your website. These components include modals, tooltips, and carousels.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task:</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li>Create a new HTML file called <code>index.html</code> and add the following code inside the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> section:<code>\n&lt;link rel=\'stylesheet\' href=\'https://maxcdn.bootstrapcdn.com/bootstrap/4.0.0/css/bootstrap.min.css\'&gt;\n&lt;script src=\'https://maxcdn.bootstrapcdn.com/bootstrap/4.0.0/js/bootstrap.min.js\'&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n</code>\n    <p>This code links to the Bootstrap CSS and JavaScript files.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>Create a new HTML file called <code>index.html</code> and add the following code inside the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> section:<code>\n&lt;div class=\'container\'&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\'row\'&gt;\n    &lt;div class=\'col-md-4\'&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;Column 1&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;div class=\'col-md-4\'&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;Column 2&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;div class=\'col-md-4\'&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;Column 3&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n</code>\n    <p>This code creates a simple grid layout using Bootstrap\'s grid system.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>Add a navigation bar to your website by adding the following code inside the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> section:<code>\n&lt;nav class=\'navbar navbar-expand-lg navbar-light bg-light\'&gt;\n  &lt;a class=\'navbar-brand\' href=\'#\'&gt;Navbar&lt;/a&gt;\n  &lt;button class=\'navbar-toggler\' type=\'button\' data-toggle=\'collapse\' data-target=\'#navbarSupportedContent\' aria-controls=\'navbarSupportedContent\' aria-expanded=\'false\' aria-label=\'Toggle navigation\'&gt;\n    &lt;span class=\'navbar-toggler-icon\'&gt;&lt;/span&gt;\n  &lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\'collapse navbar-collapse\' id=\'navbarSupportedContent\'&gt;\n    &lt;ul class=\'navbar-nav mr-auto\'&gt;\n      &lt;li class=\'nav-item active\'&gt;\n        &lt;a class=\'nav-link\' href=\'#\'&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;\n      &lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li class=\'nav-item\'&gt;\n        &lt;a class=\'nav-link\' href=\'#\'&gt;Link&lt;/a&gt;\n      &lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li class=\'nav-item\'&gt;\n        &lt;a class=\'nav-link disabled\' href=\'#\' tabindex=\'-1\' aria-disabled=\'true\'&gt;Disabled&lt;/a&gt;\n      &lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;\n</code>\n    <p>This code adds a navigation bar to your website using Bootstrap\'s CSS components.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>Add a modal to your website by adding the following code inside the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> section:<code>\n&lt;button type=\'button\' class=\'btn btn-primary\' data-toggle=\'modal\' data-target=\'#exampleModal\'&gt;Launch demo modal&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;div class=\'modal fade\' id=\'exampleModal\' tabindex=\'-1\' role=\'dialog\' aria-labelledby=\'exampleModalLabel\' aria-hidden=\'true\'&gt;\n  &lt;div class=\'modal-dialog\' role=\'document\'&gt;\n    &lt;div class=\'modal-content\'&gt;\n      &lt;div class=\'modal-header\'&gt;\n        &lt;h5 class=\'modal-title\' id=\'exampleModalLabel\'&gt;Modal title&lt;/h5&gt;\n        &lt;button type=\'button\' class=\'close\' data-dismiss=\'modal\' aria-label=\'Close\'&gt;\n          &lt;span aria-hidden=\'true\'&gt;&amp;times;&lt;/span&gt;\n        &lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;div class=\'modal-body\'&gt;\n        &lt;p&gt;Modal body text goes here.&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;div class=\'modal-footer\'&gt;\n        &lt;button type=\'button\' class=\'btn btn-secondary\' data-dismiss=\'modal\'&gt;Close&lt;/button&gt;\n        &lt;button type=\'button\' class=\'btn btn-primary\'&gt;Save changes&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n</code>\n    <p>This code adds a modal to your website using Bootstrap\'s JavaScript components.</p>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable:</h4>\n<p>You should have a simple website layout using Bootstrap\'s grid system, CSS components, and JavaScript components. You should have a navigation bar, a modal, and a responsive layout.</p>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid:</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Not linking to the Bootstrap CSS and JavaScript files.</li>\n  <li>Not using the correct classes for the grid system, CSS components, and JavaScript components.</li>\n  <li>Not using the correct attributes for the grid system, CSS components, and JavaScript components.</li>\n  <li>Not testing the website on different devices and screen sizes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips:</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use the Bootstrap documentation to learn more about the grid system, CSS components, and JavaScript components.</li>\n  <li>Use the Bootstrap inspector to inspect the HTML and CSS of the components.</li>\n  <li>Use the Bootstrap examples to learn how to use the components in different scenarios.</li>\n</ul>', 'Bootstrap Basics', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:09:56');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(959, 972, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a31212673b8e_1781604646.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you will build a responsive website with interactive elements using Bootstrap components. You\'ll create navigation menus, alerts, popups, and sliders—common features in real-world web projects like dashboards, product pages, and user interfaces.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>1. Bootstrap Navigation and Menu</h4><p>A navigation bar helps users find content on your site. Think of it like a restaurant menu—visitors use it to choose where to go. Bootstrap provides ready-made classes to create responsive menus that work on phones and computers.</p>\n\n<h4>2. Bootstrap Alerts and Notifications</h4><p>Alerts show messages like \"Success!\" or \"Error.\" They’re like traffic signs—visual signals to guide users. Bootstrap lets you color-code alerts (green for success, red for danger) and add icons or buttons.</p>\n\n<h4>3. Bootstrap Modal and Popup</h4><p>A modal is a popup window that appears on top of your page. Imagine a lightbox that shows a photo when you click a thumbnail. Modals are used for forms, images, or extra information without leaving the current page.</p>\n\n<h4>4. Bootstrap Carousel and Slider</h4><p>A carousel displays images or content in a sliding format. Picture a rotating display of products in a store window. Bootstrap carousels let users swipe or click through items automatically.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Set up your HTML file</strong>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;\n      &lt;head&gt;\n        &lt;link href=\'https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.3/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css\' rel=\'stylesheet\'&gt;</span>\n        &lt;title&gt;Bootstrap Practice&lt;/title&gt;\n      &lt;/head&gt;\n      &lt;body&gt;\n        <!--<span style=\"color:#5c6370\"-->-- Components will go here --&gt;\n        &lt;script src=\'https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.3/dist/js/bootstrap.bundle.min.js\'&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</span>\n      &lt;/body&gt;\n    &lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>This includes Bootstrap’s CSS and JavaScript files needed for all components.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Create a responsive navigation bar</strong>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;nav <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'navbar navbar-expand-lg navbar-light bg-light mb-4\'</span>&gt;\n      &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'container-fluid\'</span>&gt;\n        &lt;a <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'navbar-brand\'</span> href=\'<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#\'&gt;MySite&lt;/a&gt;</span>\n        &lt;button <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'navbar-toggler\'</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'button\'</span> data-bs-toggle=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'collapse\'</span> data-bs-target=\'<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#navbarNav\'&gt;</span>\n          &lt;span <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'navbar-toggler-icon\'</span>&gt;&lt;/span&gt;\n        &lt;/button&gt;\n        &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'collapse navbar-collapse\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'navbarNav\'</span>&gt;\n          &lt;ul <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'navbar-nav\'</span>&gt;\n            &lt;li <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'nav-item\'</span>&gt;&lt;a <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'nav-link\'</span> href=\'<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#\'&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;</span>\n            &lt;li <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'nav-item\'</span>&gt;&lt;a <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'nav-link\'</span> href=\'<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#\'&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;</span>\n          &lt;/ul&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/nav&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>This creates a menu with a \"hamburger\" button for mobile devices.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Add alerts and notifications</strong>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'alert alert-success\'</span> role=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'alert\'</span>&gt;\n      Your form was submitted successfully!\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'alert alert-danger\'</span> role=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'alert\'</span>&gt;\n      Please fix the errors below.\n    &lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>Use <code>alert-success</code> for good messages and <code>alert-danger</code> for errors. Add icons with <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> if you want extra flair.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Build a modal popup</strong>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;!<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">-- Button to open modal --&gt;</span>\n    &lt;button <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'button\'</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'btn btn-primary mb-3\'</span> data-bs-toggle=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'modal\'</span> data-bs-target=\'<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#exampleModal\'&gt;</span>\n      Open Popup\n    &lt;/button&gt;\n\n    &lt;!<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">-- Modal itself --&gt;</span>\n    &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'modal fade\'</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'exampleModal\'</span> tabindex=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'-1\'</span>&gt;\n      &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'modal-dialog\'</span>&gt;\n        &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'modal-content\'</span>&gt;\n          &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'modal-header\'</span>&gt;\n            &lt;h5 <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'modal-title\'</span>&gt;Modal Title&lt;/h5&gt;\n            &lt;button <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'button\'</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'btn-close\'</span> data-bs-dismiss=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'modal\'</span>&gt;&lt;/button&gt;\n          &lt;/div&gt;\n          &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'modal-body\'</span>&gt;\n            This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> a popup message!\n          &lt;/div&gt;\n          &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'modal-footer\'</span>&gt;\n            &lt;button <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'button\'</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'btn btn-secondary\'</span> data-bs-dismiss=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'modal\'</span>&gt;Close&lt;/button&gt;\n          &lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>Clicking the button opens the modal. The <code>fade</code> class adds a smooth transition.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Create a carousel slider</strong>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'carouselExample\'</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'carousel slide\'</span>&gt;\n      &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'carousel-inner\'</span>&gt;\n        &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'carousel-item active\'</span>&gt;\n          &lt;img src=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'image1.jpg\'</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'d-block w-100\'</span> alt=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Slide 1\'</span>&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n        &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'carousel-item\'</span>&gt;\n          &lt;img src=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'image2.jpg\'</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'d-block w-100\'</span> alt=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Slide 2\'</span>&gt;\n        &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;button <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'carousel-control-prev\'</span> data-bs-target=\'<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#carouselExample\' data-bs-slide=\'prev\'&gt;</span>\n        &lt;span <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'carousel-control-prev-icon\'</span>&gt;&lt;/span&gt;\n      &lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;button <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'carousel-control-next\'</span> data-bs-target=\'<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#carouselExample\' data-bs-slide=\'next\'&gt;</span>\n        &lt;span <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'carousel-control-next-icon\'</span>&gt;&lt;/span&gt;\n      &lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>Replace <code>image1.jpg</code> and <code>image2.jpg</code> with your own images. Add <code>data-bs-interval=\'3000\'</code> to control slide speed.</p>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Create one HTML file named <code>bootstrap-components.html</code> that includes:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A responsive navigation bar</li>\n  <li>Two alerts (success and danger)</li>\n  <li>A working modal popup</li>\n  <li>A carousel with at least two images</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Forgetting to include Bootstrap’s JavaScript file—your modal and carousel won’t work without it.</li>\n  <li>Missing <code>data-bs-</code> attributes in modals and carousels—they control functionality.</li>\n  <li>Using double quotes in code examples (always use single quotes as shown).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Test on mobile:</strong> Use your phone to check how the navigation bar collapses and how modals look on small screens.</li>\n  <li><strong>Add icons:</strong> Use Bootstrap Icons (https://icons.getbootstrap.com/) in alerts or buttons for visual polish.</li>\n  <li><strong>Customize colors:</strong> Replace <code>bg-light</code> with <code>bg-primary</code> or other Bootstrap color classes to change background colors.</li>\n</ul>', 'Bootstrap Advanced Topics', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:10:55'),
(960, 973, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3121621a858_1781604706.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you\'ll learn Tailwind CSS, a utility-first CSS framework used by companies like Netflix and Shopify. You\'ll create a responsive layout and customize colors, learning how to speed up real-world web development workflows.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n\n<h4>What is Tailwind CSS?</h4>\n<p><strong>Tailwind</strong> is like a toolbox for web design. Instead of writing custom CSS, you use pre-made classes like <code>text-center</code> or <code>bg-blue-500</code> directly in your HTML. It’s like using LEGO blocks to build a house instead of carving wood from scratch.</p>\n\n<h4>Installation</h4>\n<p>To use Tailwind, you need to <em>install</em> it like software. There are two main methods: using a CDN (like a public library of code) or installing via npm (a package manager for JavaScript). We’ll use the CDN today for simplicity.</p>\n\n<h4>Classes and Utilities</h4>\n<p>Tailwind replaces custom CSS with <strong>utility classes</strong>. For example, <code>text-xl</code> makes text large, and <code>rounded-lg</code> rounds corners. These are like pre-made tools in a toolbox — no need to build them yourself.</p>\n\n<h4>Customization and Themes</h4>\n<p>Tailwind lets you change default values (like colors or fonts) using a <code>tailwind.config.js</code> file. Think of it like customizing a template — you adjust the base colors, and all your buttons and text inherit the new style.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>\n    <p><strong>Create a new HTML file</strong> and add the Tailwind CDN link in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;script src=\'https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//cdn.tailwindcss.com\'&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</span>\n&lt;/head&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>This loads Tailwind’s tools into your project.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p><strong>Build a sample layout</strong> with utility classes. Add this to your <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'text-center p-8 bg-blue-100 rounded-lg max-w-md mx-auto\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;h1 <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'text-3xl font-bold text-blue-800\'</span>&gt;Welcome!&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;p <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'mt-4 text-gray-700\'</span>&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> a Tailwind-powered card.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;button <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'mt-6 px-6 py-2 bg-blue-600 text-white rounded hover:bg-blue-700\'</span>&gt;Click Me&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>Here, classes like <code>text-center</code> and <code>bg-blue-100</code> control layout, colors, and spacing.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p><strong>Install Tailwind via npm</strong> (for customization). In your terminal, run:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">npx tailwindcss init -i</code></pre>\n    <p>This creates a <code>tailwind.config.js</code> file and a <code>input.css</code> file for advanced projects. (You’ll need Node.js installed for this step.)</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p><strong>Customize colors</strong> in <code>tailwind.config.js</code>. Add this code:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">module.exports = {\n  theme: {\n    extend: {\n      colors: {\n        <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'brand-blue\'</span>: \'<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#2563eb\',</span>\n        <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'brand-gray\'</span>: \'<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#64748b\',</span>\n      },\n    },\n  },\n  content: [],\n}</code></pre>\n    <p>Now you can use <code>text-brand-blue</code> or <code>bg-brand-gray</code> in your HTML.</p>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit two files:\n</p><ul>\n  <li>An HTML file with Tailwind classes (using the CDN method)</li>\n  <li>A <code>tailwind.config.js</code> file with your custom colors (even if you can’t test it without the CLI)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Forgetting to escape <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> in code examples (always use &amp;lt; and &amp;gt; in HTML files)</li>\n  <li>Using double quotes in code examples — always use single quotes for attributes like <code>class=\'text-center\'</code></li>\n  <li>Not running <code>npx tailwindcss init -i</code> in the correct project folder when installing via npm</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use the <a href=\"https://tailwindcss.com/docs\" target=\"_blank\">Tailwind Docs</a> as your cheat sheet — every class has a live preview</li>\n  <li>Start with the CDN for quick prototypes, then switch to npm for customization</li>\n  <li>Customize one value at a time in <code>tailwind.config.js</code> to avoid confusion</li>\n</ul>', 'Tailwind CSS Introduction', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:12:03'),
(961, 974, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3121c0ede76_1781604800.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\">&nbsp;</p></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you\'ll build a simple React app with interactive components that use state, props, and events. This teaches you how to create reusable UI elements that respond to user actions—skills used daily by React developers in real projects.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n\n<h4>React</h4>\n<p>React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces. Think of it as a toolbox for creating websites where parts can update independently, like how Legos let you build and modify structures without rebuilding everything.</p>\n\n<h4>JSX</h4>\n<p>JSX is a syntax that lets you write HTML-like code in JavaScript. It\'s like a universal translator between HTML and JavaScript that React understands.</p>\n\n<h4>Components</h4>\n<p>Components are reusable building blocks. Imagine a house built from separate rooms (components) you can reuse in different houses.</p>\n\n<h4>Props</h4>\n<p>Props are data passed to components. Like a recipe (component) that gets ingredients (props) from the chef (parent component).</p>\n\n<h4>State</h4>\n<p>State is a component\'s internal data that can change. Think of it as a thermostat (state) that changes temperature when you adjust it.</p>\n\n<h4>Events</h4>\n<p>Events are user actions like clicks or keypresses. They\'re like buttons on a vending machine that trigger actions when pressed.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Create a React project</strong><br>\nRun these commands in your terminal:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">npx create-react-app react-day40\ncd react-day40\ncode .</code></pre>\nThis creates a new React app called \"react-day40\" and opens it in your code editor.</li>\n\n<li><strong>Build a Toggle Button component</strong><br>\nReplace the contents of <code>App.js</code> with this code:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">import</span> React, { useState } <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'react\'</span>;\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> App() {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> [isOn, setIsOn] = useState(<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">false</span>);\n\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> (\n    &lt;div style={{ padding: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'20px\'</span>, fontFamily: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Arial\'</span> }}&gt;\n      &lt;ToggleButton isOn={isOn} onToggle={() =&gt; setIsOn(!isOn)} /&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;\n        The button <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> {isOn ? <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'ON\'</span> : <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'OFF\'</span>}.\n      &lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> ToggleButton({ isOn, onToggle }) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> (\n    &lt;button\n      onClick={onToggle}\n      style={{\n        backgroundColor: isOn ? \'<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#4CAF50\' : \'#f44336\',</span>\n        color: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'white\'</span>,\n        padding: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'10px 20px\'</span>,\n        border: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'none\'</span>,\n        borderRadius: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'5px\'</span>,\n        cursor: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'pointer\'</span>\n      }}\n    &gt;\n      {isOn ? <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'ON\'</span> : <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'OFF\'</span>}\n    &lt;/button&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</code></pre>\nThis creates a button that toggles between ON and OFF states.</li>\n\n<li><strong>Test your app</strong><br>\nRun <code>npm start</code> in your terminal. You should see a button that changes color and text when clicked, with a message below showing the current state.</li>\n\n<li><strong>Add a greeting component</strong><br>\nUpdate your <code>App.js</code> like this:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> App() {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> [isOn, setIsOn] = useState(<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">false</span>);\n\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> (\n    &lt;div style={{ padding: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'20px\'</span>, fontFamily: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Arial\'</span> }}&gt;\n      &lt;Greeting name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Alice\'</span> /&gt;\n      &lt;br /&gt;\n      &lt;ToggleButton isOn={isOn} onToggle={() =&gt; setIsOn(!isOn)} /&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;The button <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> {isOn ? <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'ON\'</span> : <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'OFF\'</span>}.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> Greeting({ name }) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> &lt;h2&gt;Hello, {name}! Welcome to React.&lt;/h2&gt;;\n}</code></pre>\nThis adds a greeting that shows the user\'s name passed as a prop.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n\n<p>1. A GitHub repository link showing your completed code.<br>\n2. A working demo of your app (either through GitHub Pages or a CodeSandbox link).</p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Forgetting to import React in components</li>\n<li>Using double quotes (\" \") instead of single quotes (\') in JSX attributes</li>\n<li>Trying to modify state directly instead of using the setter function</li>\n<li>Not using curly braces {} to include JavaScript expressions in JSX</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Use <code>console.log()</code> to debug component props and state values</li>\n<li>Install React Developer Tools for your browser to inspect component hierarchies</li>\n<li>Break large components into smaller ones when they become hard to manage</li>\n</ul>', 'React Basics', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:13:34'),
(962, 975, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; <img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a31221a86d68_1781604890.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you will create simple apps using Angular and Vue.js to understand their basic structures. This helps you decide which framework to use for different projects in real jobs.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>What is Angular?</h4>\n<p>Angular is a full-featured framework for building complex web apps. Think of it like a full-service restaurant: it provides everything you need (like databases, menus, and staff) to create large applications.</p>\n\n<h4>What is Vue.js?</h4>\n<p>Vue.js is a progressive framework that starts small and grows with your needs. Imagine it as a modular kitchen: you can use just the basics first, then add appliances (features) as your project grows.</p>\n\n<h4>Components</h4>\n<p>Both frameworks use components—reusable pieces of UI. Like LEGO blocks, components let you build apps by combining smaller parts.</p>\n\n<h4>Directives</h4>\n<p>Directives are special attributes that tell the framework to do something. For example, <code>*ngIf</code> in Angular shows/hides elements conditionally.</p>\n\n<h4>Reactivity</h4>\n<p>Vue.js uses reactivity to automatically update the screen when data changes. It works like a thermostat: if the temperature (data) changes, the heater (UI) updates instantly.</p>\n\n<h4>Two-way binding</h4>\n<p>This connects data and UI so changes in one update the other. Think of a live chat: when you type a message, it appears instantly for everyone.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Create an Angular app:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">ng <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">new</span> angular-demo <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">--no-style</span></code></pre>\n<p>This command creates a new Angular project without CSS stylesheets.</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Edit <code>src/app/app.component.ts</code> with this code:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">import</span> { Component } <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'@angular/core\'</span>;\n\n@Component({\n  selector: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'app-root\'</span>,\n  template: `<h1>Hello, {{ name }}!</h1>\n           <input [(ngmodel)]=\"&lt;span\" style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'name\'&gt;`,\n})\nexport <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span> AppComponent {\n  name = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Angular\'</span>;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>This creates a component that shows a greeting and lets users change the name.</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Create a Vue.js app:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">vue create vue-demo</code></pre>\n<p>This command generates a new Vue.js project.</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Edit <code>src/App.vue</code> with this code:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Hello, {{ name }}!&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;input v-model=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'name\'</span>&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;script&gt;\nexport default {\n  data() {\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> {\n      name: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Vue.js\'</span>\n    }\n  }\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>This Vue component works similarly to the Angular one but uses Vue\'s own syntax.</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Run both apps:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">cd angular-demo\nng serve <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">--open</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// In another terminal:</span>\ncd vue-demo\nnpm run serve</code></pre>\n<p>This starts both apps in separate browser tabs to compare their behavior.</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Create a comparison table in a text file, like this:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">| Feature        | Angular         | Vue.js          |\n|<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">----------------|-----------------|-----------------|</span>\n| Learning curve | Steeper         | Easier          |\n| Size           | Larger          | Smaller         |\n| Flexibility    | Less flexible   | More flexible   |</code></pre></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Angular app code with the greeting component</li>\n<li>The Vue.js app code with the greeting component</li>\n<li>Your comparison table as a text file</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Forgetting to install Angular CLI or Vue CLI before creating projects</li>\n<li>Mixing up Angular\'s <code>[(ngModel)]</code> with Vue\'s <code>v-model</code> syntax</li>\n<li>Not running apps in separate browser tabs to compare them</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use Angular</strong> for large enterprise apps that need strict structure</li>\n<li><strong>Use Vue.js</strong> for smaller projects or when you need more flexibility</li>\n<li>Check official documentation first when stuck: <a href=\"https://angular.io\" target=\"_blank\">angular.io</a> and <a href=\"https://vuejs.org\" target=\"_blank\">vuejs.org</a></li>\n</ul>', 'Angular and Vue.js Introduction', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:15:06'),
(963, 976, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: front-end frameworks comparison</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=front-end+frameworks+comparison\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/front-end+frameworks+comparison\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/front-end+frameworks+comparison/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/front-end+frameworks+comparison/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=front-end+frameworks+comparison\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Front-end Frameworks Comparison</h3>\n<p>In this task, you\'ll learn about the most popular front-end frameworks and how to choose the right one for your project. This is an essential skill to master as a web developer, as it will help you to create efficient, scalable, and maintainable front-end applications.</p>\n\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>Before we dive into the task, let\'s cover some key concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Front-end framework</strong>: A front-end framework is a library or collection of libraries that helps you build the user interface and user experience of a web application. It provides pre-built components, layout structures, and tools to make development faster and easier.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bootstrap</strong>: Bootstrap is a popular front-end framework that provides a set of pre-built CSS and HTML components, as well as JavaScript plugins. It\'s designed to be responsive, meaning it adapts to different screen sizes and devices.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tailwind CSS</strong>: Tailwind CSS is another popular front-end framework that provides a utility-first approach to styling. It allows you to write custom CSS classes to style your components, making it highly customizable.</li>\n  <li><strong>React</strong>: React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces. It\'s a view library, meaning it only handles the view layer of your application. It\'s highly popular and widely used in industry.</li>\n  <li><strong>Angular</strong>: Angular is a JavaScript framework for building complex web applications. It\'s a full-fledged framework that includes a templating engine, dependency injection, and other features.</li>\n  <li><strong>Vue.js</strong>: Vue.js is a progressive and flexible JavaScript framework for building web applications. It\'s a view library, similar to React, but with a more comprehensive set of features.</li>\n  <li><strong>Component-based architecture</strong>: A component-based architecture is a design pattern where your application is broken down into smaller, independent components. Each component has its own logic, state, and behavior.</li>\n  <li><strong>Virtual DOM</strong>: The Virtual DOM is a lightweight in-memory representation of your application\'s UI. It\'s used by React and other libraries to optimize rendering and improve performance.</li>\n  <li><strong>Template literals</strong>: Template literals are a feature in JavaScript that allows you to create strings with embedded expressions. They\'re useful for building dynamic HTML content.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<p>Now that we\'ve covered the key concepts, let\'s move on to the task:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Create a new project using your preferred front-end framework (Bootstrap, Tailwind CSS, React, Angular, or Vue.js).</li>\n  <li>Build a simple landing page with the following components:</li>\n  <ul>\n    <li>A header with a navigation menu</li>\n    <li>A hero section with a background image and a call-to-action button</li>\n    <li>A features section with three cards</li>\n    <li>A call-to-action section with a button</li>\n  </ul>\n  <li>Use template literals to build dynamic HTML content for the navigation menu and the call-to-action button.</li>\n  <li>Use the Virtual DOM to optimize rendering and improve performance. (Note: This step is only applicable to React and other libraries that use the Virtual DOM.)</li>\n  <li>Use a component-based architecture to break down your application into smaller, independent components.</li>\n  <li>Compare the performance and scalability of your application using different front-end frameworks.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how you can use template literals to build dynamic HTML content for the navigation menu:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> navigationMenu = [\n  { label: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Home\'</span>, href: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/\'</span> },\n  { label: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'About\'</span>, href: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/about\'</span> },\n  { label: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Contact\'</span>, href: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/contact\'</span> }\n];\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> html = navigationMenu.map((item) => {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`<li><a href=\'${item.href}\'>${item.label}</a></li>`</span>;\n}).join(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'\'</span>);\n\nconsole.log(html);\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code creates an array of navigation menu items and uses the `map()` method to create a string of HTML elements. The `join()` method is then used to concatenate the HTML elements into a single string.</p>\n\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>For this task, you\'ll need to submit the following:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A working project that demonstrates the use of different front-end frameworks (Bootstrap, Tailwind CSS, React, Angular, or Vue.js)</li>\n  <li>A landing page with the specified components (header, hero section, features section, and call-to-action section)</li>\n  <li>A comparison of the performance and scalability of your application using different front-end frameworks</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<p>Here are some common mistakes to avoid when working with front-end frameworks:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Overusing CSS classes</strong>: While front-end frameworks provide a lot of pre-built CSS classes, it\'s easy to overuse them and end up with a cluttered and inefficient CSS file.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not using a component-based architecture</strong>: Failing to break down your application into smaller, independent components can lead to a complex and hard-to-maintain codebase.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not optimizing rendering and performance</strong>: Failing to use the Virtual DOM or other optimization techniques can lead to slow and unresponsive applications.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<p>Here are some pro tips to help you master front-end frameworks:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Use a consistent naming convention</strong>: Use a consistent naming convention for your CSS classes, JavaScript variables, and HTML elements to make your code easier to read and maintain.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use a code linter</strong>: Use a code linter to catch errors and warnings in your code and improve its overall quality.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use a build tool</strong>: Use a build tool like Webpack or Rollup to optimize and bundle your code for production.</li>\n</ul>', 'Front-end Frameworks Comparison', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(964, 977, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a31227a4e22a_1781604986.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>React Components</h3>\n<p>In this task, you will learn about React components and build a simple counter application. You will learn about React functional components, React class components, component lifecycle methods, props and state, and React hooks.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>Before we start, let\'s cover some essential concepts. A <strong>component</strong> is a reusable piece of code that represents a UI element. Think of it like a LEGO brick - you can use it to build different structures, but the brick itself remains the same.</p>\n<p>A <strong>functional component</strong> is a type of component that takes in props and returns JSX (a syntax extension for JavaScript). It\'s like a function that takes in some data and returns a UI element.</p>\n<p>A <strong>class component</strong> is a type of component that uses a class to define its behavior. It\'s like a blueprint for a building - you define the structure and the rules for how it should work.</p>\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new React app using <code>create-react-app</code>. If you haven\'t installed it yet, run <code>npx create-react-app counter-app</code> in your terminal.</li>\n<li>Open the project in your code editor and navigate to the <code>src</code> folder.</li>\n<li>Create a new file called <code>Counter.js</code> inside the <code>src</code> folder.</li>\n<li>In <code>Counter.js</code>, create a functional component called <code>Counter</code> that takes in a <code>count</code> prop and returns a JSX element with the count.</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">import</span> React <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'react\'</span>;\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> Counter({ count }) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Count: {count}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n</code></pre>\n<li>Export the <code>Counter</code> component from the <code>Counter.js</code> file.</li>\n<li>In the <code>App.js</code> file, import the <code>Counter</code> component and render it with a <code>count</code> prop set to 0.</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">import</span> React <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'react\'</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">import</span> Counter <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'./Counter\'</span>;\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> App() {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;Counter count={<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>} /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;\n</code></pre>\n<li>Run the app using <code>npm start</code> and open it in your browser. You should see a page with the count 0.</li>\n<li>Update the <code>Counter</code> component to use a class component instead of a functional component.</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">import</span> React, { Component } <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'react\'</span>;\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span> Counter <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">extends</span> Component {\n  constructor(props) {\n    super(props);\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">this</span>.state = {\n      count: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>,\n    };\n  }\n\n  render() {\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> (\n      &lt;div&gt;\n        &lt;p&gt;Count: {<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">this</span>.state.count}&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    );\n  }\n}\n\nexport default Counter;\n</code></pre>\n<li>Update the <code>App.js</code> file to use the new class-based <code>Counter</code> component.</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">import</span> React <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'react\'</span>;\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">import</span> Counter <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'./Counter\'</span>;\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> App() {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;Counter /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;\n</code></pre>\n<li>Run the app again and open it in your browser. You should see a page with the count 0.</li>\n<li>Update the <code>Counter</code> component to use React hooks instead of a class component.</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">import</span> React, { useState } <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'react\'</span>;\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> Counter() {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> [count, setCount] = useState(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>);\n\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Count: {count}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Counter;\n</code></pre>\n<li>Run the app again and open it in your browser. You should see a page with the count 0.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>Submit your updated <code>Counter.js</code> file with the class-based and hook-based implementations.</p>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Don\'t forget to export the component from the <code>Counter.js</code> file.</li>\n<li>Make sure to import the <code>Counter</code> component in the <code>App.js</code> file.</li>\n<li>Use the correct syntax for JSX elements, such as <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> instead of <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Use the <code>useState</code> hook to manage state in functional components.</li>\n<li>Use the <code>useEffect</code> hook to handle side effects in functional components.</li>\n<li>Use the <code>useContext</code> hook to access context in functional components.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4><br></h4>', 'React Components', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 12:34:19'),
(965, 978, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3122b497433_1781605044.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you\'ll build a simple <strong>theme toggle app</strong> that switches between light/dark mode using React state management tools. This teaches you how to manage data flow in real apps, which is essential for jobs like building dynamic user interfaces.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>React State Introduction</h4><p>State is like a \"memory\" for your app. When state changes, React updates the screen automatically. Think of it as a variable that React watches closely.</p>\n\n<h4>useState Hook</h4><p>A tool to create state variables. Like a magic pencil that writes values React can track. Example: <code>const [count, setCount] = useState(0)</code> creates a counter that updates the screen when changed.</p>\n\n<h4>useReducer Hook</h4><p>For complex state logic (like a calculator). You define \"actions\" (buttons) and a \"reducer\" function that decides what happens when each action is pressed.</p>\n\n<h4>Context API</h4><p>A way to share state between many components without passing props manually. Like a shared notebook all team members can read/write.</p>\n\n<h4>Redux Basics</h4><p>A library for managing app-wide state. Imagine a central library where every component can check out books (data) and return updated versions.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Create a counter with useState</strong>\n<pre><code>&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;import { useState } from \'react\';\n\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      Count: {count}\n      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setCount(count + 1)}&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setCount(count - 1)}&gt;-&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;\n<p>This creates a counter that updates when buttons are clicked. The state variable <code>count</code> changes when buttons trigger <code>setCount</code>.</p>\n</code></pre></li><code>\n\n<li><strong>Convert counter to use useReducer</strong>\n<pre><code>&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;import { useReducer } from \'react\';\n\nfunction reducer(state, action) {\n  if (action.type === \'increment\') {\n    return { count: state.count + 1 };\n  }\n  if (action.type === \'decrement\') {\n    return { count: state.count - 1 };\n  }\n  return state;\n}\n\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { count: 0 });\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      Count: {state.count}\n      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; dispatch({ type: \'increment\' })}&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; dispatch({ type: \'decrement\' })}&gt;-&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;\n<p>The <code>reducer</code> function decides how state changes based on actions. This is better for complex state with many rules.</p>\n</code></pre></li><code>\n\n<li><strong>Create a Theme Context</strong>\n<pre><code>&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;import { useState, createContext } from \'react\';\n\n// Create context\nexport const ThemeContext = createContext();\n\nexport function ThemeProvider({ children }) {\n  const [isDarkMode, setIsDarkMode] = useState(false);\n\n  const toggleTheme = () =&gt; {\n    setIsDarkMode(!isDarkMode);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    &lt;ThemeContext.Provider value={{ isDarkMode, toggleTheme }}&gt;\n      {children}\n    &lt;/ThemeContext.Provider&gt;\n  );\n}\n&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;\n<p>This creates a shared \"theme memory\" that any component can access. Wrap your app with <code>&lt;ThemeProvider&gt;</code> to share it.</p>\n</code></pre></li><code>\n\n<li><strong>Add Redux for Todos</strong>\n<pre><code>&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;// store.js\nimport { configureStore, createSlice } from \'@reduxjs/toolkit\';\n\nconst todoSlice = createSlice({\n  name: \'todos\',\n  initialState: [],\n  reducers: {\n    addTodo: (state, action) =&gt; {\n      state.push(action.payload);\n    },\n    removeTodo: (state, action) =&gt; {\n      return state.filter(todo =&gt; todo.id !== action.payload.id);\n    }\n  }\n});\n\nexport const { addTodo, removeTodo } = todoSlice.actions;\nexport default configureStore({ reducer: todoSlice.reducer });\n&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;\n<p>This creates a Redux store for managing todos. Use <code>useDispatch</code> in components to send actions like <code>addTodo</code> or <code>removeTodo</code>.</p>\n</code></pre></li><code>\n</code></code></code></code></ol><code><code>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit:</p><ul><li>A GitHub link to your complete app with all 4 features</li><li>A 2-3 sentence summary explaining when to use each state management technique</li></ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Using <code>useState</code> for complex state instead of <code>useReducer</code></li>\n<li>Forgetting to wrap components with <code>ThemeProvider</code> when using Context API</li>\n<li>Not providing a default value when creating Context</li>\n<li>Mutating Redux state directly instead of returning new state</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n</code><ul><code>\n</code><li><code>Start with <code>useState</code> for simple components</code>\n</li><li>Use Context API when 3+ components need the same state</li>\n<li>Only use Redux for large apps with many shared state pieces</li>\n</ul></code>', 'React State Management', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 12:40:03'),
(966, 979, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: React routing diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=React+routing+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/React+routing+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/React+routing+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/React+routing+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=React+routing+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you\'ll build a single-page application with multiple pages using React Router. You\'ll learn how to create navigation menus, pass dynamic data through URLs, and control access to pages – essential skills for real-world web apps like dashboards or e-commerce sites.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>React Router</h4><p>Like a traffic cop for your website. It decides which part of your app to show based on the URL in the browser address bar.</p>\n\n<h4>Route Configuration</h4><p>Creating a map that connects URLs like \'/users\' to your UserList component. It\'s like setting up signs that say &quot;This road leads to the User List&quot;.</p>\n\n<h4>Link &amp; NavLink</h4><p>Special buttons that change the URL without reloading the whole page. NavLink adds active styles to show which page is currently open.</p>\n\n<h4>Route Parameters</h4><p>Dynamic parts of URLs like \'/users/123\' where \'123\' can be any user ID. It\'s like having a sign that says &quot;User [NUMBER]\'s Profile&quot; where [NUMBER] changes.</p>\n\n<h4>Navigation Guards</h4><p>Security checkpoints that say &quot;You can only go here if...&quot;. For example, &quot;You must be logged in to see this page&quot;.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Install React Router: Run <code>npm install react-router-dom</code> in your project folder</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Create your main router file (App.js):</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;BrowserRouter&gt;\n  &lt;Switch&gt;\n    &lt;Route exact path=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/\'</span> component={Home} /&gt;\n    &lt;Route path=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/about\'</span> component={About} /&gt;\n    &lt;Route path=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/users/:id\'</span> component={User} /&gt;\n    &lt;Route path=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'*\'</span> component={NotFound} /&gt;\n  &lt;/Switch&gt;\n&lt;/BrowserRouter&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>This creates 3 main routes and a 404 fallback. The <code>:id</code> part is a parameter that will work with any value after /users/</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Add navigation links in your main layout:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;nav&gt;\n  &lt;Link to=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/\'</span>&gt;Home&lt;/Link&gt;\n  &lt;Link to=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/about\'</span>&gt;About&lt;/Link&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>These links change the URL without reloading the whole page</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Create a User component to display route parameters:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> User({ match }) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> &lt;p&gt;Showing user ID: {match.params.id}&lt;/p&gt;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The <code>match.params</code> object contains any route parameters from the URL</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Add navigation guards in components:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> ProtectedPage({ history }) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> isLoggedIn = checkAuth();\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (!isLoggedIn) {\n    history.push(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/login\'</span>);\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">null</span>;\n  }\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> &lt;p&gt;Secret info!&lt;/p&gt;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>This code checks if the user is logged in, and if not, redirects them to the login page</p></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit a zip folder with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A working React app with 3+ routes</li>\n<li>At least one route using parameters</li>\n<li>Navigation links between pages</li>\n<li>A working navigation guard example</li>\n<li>A 404 error page for unmatched routes</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Forgetting to wrap your app in &lt;BrowserRouter&gt; - without this, routing won\'t work at all</li>\n<li>Using regular &lt;a&gt; tags instead of &lt;Link&gt; - regular links will reload the whole page</li>\n<li>Not adding the exact prop to the home route - it might show other routes incorrectly</li>\n<li>Forgetting to add a catch-all route for 404 errors</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use NavLink\'s activeClassName to highlight the current page in your navigation menu</li>\n<li>Organize your routes in a separate file for large apps</li>\n<li>Use route parameters for things like user IDs, product IDs, or article slugs</li>\n</ul>', 'React Routing and Navigation', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(967, 980, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; <img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a31230fd1e98_1781605135.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you\'ll learn the foundation of back-end development by creating a simple API endpoint that returns data. This skill is critical for real jobs because back-end code handles everything users don\'t see - like storing data, authenticating users, and connecting systems.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n\n<h4>What is Back-end Development?</h4>\n<p>Think of back-end as the kitchen of a restaurant. While customers (users) see the dining room (front-end), the kitchen handles food preparation (data processing). Back-end developers create the systems that manage databases, authenticate users, and power API connections.</p>\n\n<h4>Back-end Development Roles</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Full-Stack Developer</strong>: Handles both front-end and back-end work</li>\n<li><strong>Back-End Specialist</strong>: Focuses only on server logic and databases</li>\n<li><strong>API Developer</strong>: Creates interfaces for different systems to communicate</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Server-side Programming Languages</h4>\n<p>These run on servers, not in browsers. Common options include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>JavaScript (Node.js)</li>\n<li>Python</li>\n<li>Ruby</li>\n<li>PHP</li>\n<li>Java</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Analogy: If front-end languages are like the restaurant menu, server-side languages are the secret recipes only kitchen staff can access.</p>\n\n<h4>Back-end Frameworks and Libraries</h4>\n<p>Frameworks are like toolkits that make development faster. For Node.js, Express is the most popular framework. It provides:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Routing (directing requests)</li>\n<li>Middleware (processing requests)</li>\n<li>Request/response handling</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>API Introduction</h4>\n<p>API stands for Application Programming Interface. It\'s like a restaurant\'s ordering system - customers (front-end) send orders (requests) and get food (responses) without knowing how it\'s made.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;blockquote&gt;\nPro Tip: APIs use HTTP methods like GET (read), POST (create), PUT (update), <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> DELETE (remove) to control data.\n&lt;/blockquote&gt;</code></pre>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n\n<ol>\n<li><p>Open terminal/command prompt and install Node.js if you haven\'t already. Verify with:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">node -v</code></pre></li>\n\n<li><p>Create project folder and initialize:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">mkdir backend-api\n cd backend-api\n npm init -y</code></pre></li>\n\n<li><p>Install Express framework:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">npm install express</code></pre></li>\n\n<li><p>Create <code>index.js</code> with this code:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> express = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'express\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> app = express();\n\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Simple API endpoint</span>\napp.get(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/api/users\'</span>, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.json([\n    { id: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, name: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Alice\'</span> },\n    { id: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, name: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Bob\'</span> }\n  ]);\n});\n\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Start server</span>\napp.listen(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3000</span>, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(\'Server running on http:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//localhost:3000\');</span>\n});</code></pre>\n<p>This creates a server that responds to <code>/api/users</code> with a list of users.</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Run the server:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">node index.js</code></pre></li>\n\n<li><p>Test in browser or using Postman by visiting:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">http:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//localhost:3000/api/users</span></code></pre>\n<p>You should see JSON data with two user objects.</p></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n\n<p>Submit:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Screen recording showing your server running and API response</li>\n<li>Code files including <code>index.js</code> and <code>package.json</code></li>\n<li>Short explanation (3-5 sentences) of how your API works</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Forgetting to install dependencies before running code</li>\n<li>Typing port numbers wrong (must match <code>app.listen()</code> value)</li>\n<li>Not using <code>res.json()</code> - using regular <code>res.send()</code> will return plain text</li>\n<li>Trying to access endpoints without starting the server first</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use nodemon</strong>: Install <code>npm install -g nodemon</code> for automatic server restarts when code changes</li>\n<li><strong>Test with Postman</strong>: This API tool makes testing endpoints much easier than browser refreshes</li>\n<li><strong>Plan your routes first</strong>: Sketch out all your API endpoints before coding</li>\n</ul>', 'Introduction to Back-end Development', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:19:10'),
(968, 981, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3123492abe2_1781605193.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Day 47: Node.js and npm Basics</h3>\n\n<h4>Intro</h4>\n<p>Today, you\'ll learn the basics of Node.js and npm, the two essential tools for any JavaScript developer. You\'ll understand how to install Node.js, create a new project, manage dependencies, and write your first Node.js script. This is a fundamental step in your journey to becoming a proficient web developer.</p>\n\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<h5>1. Node.js Introduction</h5>\n<p>Node.js is a JavaScript runtime environment that allows you to run JavaScript on the server-side. Think of it like a special kind of computer that can execute JavaScript code. It\'s built on Chrome\'s V8 JavaScript engine and is designed to handle high-traffic, real-time applications.</p>\n<p>Imagine you\'re at a restaurant, and you order food. In a traditional web application, you would send a request to the kitchen (server), and the kitchen would prepare your food (generate the response). With Node.js, the kitchen (server) can also prepare the food (generate the response) while you\'re waiting for your order. This is called asynchronous programming.</p>\n<h5>2. npm Introduction</h5>\n<p>npm stands for Node Package Manager. It\'s a package manager for JavaScript, similar to pip for Python or Maven for Java. npm allows you to easily install and manage dependencies for your project.</p>\n<p>Think of dependencies like ingredients for a recipe. Just as you need flour, sugar, and eggs to bake a cake, your JavaScript project needs certain libraries and modules to function correctly. npm helps you manage these dependencies, so you don\'t have to download and install them manually.</p>\n<h5>3. Package.json and Dependencies</h5>\n<p>When you create a new project, npm generates a file called package.json. This file contains metadata about your project, including its name, version, and dependencies.</p>\n<p>Here\'s an example of a package.json file:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">{\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"name\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"my-project\"</span>,\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"version\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"1.0.0\"</span>,\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"dependencies\"</span>: {\n    <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"express\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"^4.17.1\"</span>,\n    <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"nodemon\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"^2.0.12\"</span>\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>In this example, the project depends on two libraries: express and nodemon. Express is a popular web framework for Node.js, and nodemon is a tool that helps you automatically restart your server when you make changes to your code.</p>\n<h5>4. Node.js Modules and Imports</h5>\n<p>Node.js modules are files that contain reusable code. You can import these modules into your project using the require() function or the import statement.</p>\n<p>Here\'s an example of importing the express module:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> express = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'express\'</span>);</code></pre>\n<p>Or, using the import statement:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">import</span> express <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'express\'</span>;</code></pre>\n<h5>5. npm Scripts</h5>\n<p>npm scripts are commands that you can run from the terminal to perform specific tasks. You can define scripts in the scripts section of your package.json file.</p>\n<p>Here\'s an example of a package.json file with scripts:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">{\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"name\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"my-project\"</span>,\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"version\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"1.0.0\"</span>,\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"scripts\"</span>: {\n    <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"start\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"node server.js\"</span>,\n    <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"test\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"jest\"</span>\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>In this example, the project has two scripts: start and test. The start script runs the server.js file using node, and the test script runs the Jest testing framework.</p>\n\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li>Create a new project using npm by running the command <code>npm init</code> in your terminal.</li>\n  <li>Answer the prompts to create a new package.json file.</li>\n  <li>Install the express library by running the command <code>npm install express</code>.</li>\n  <li>Create a new file called server.js and add the following code:\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> express = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'express\'</span>);</code></pre>\n  <p>This code imports the express module and assigns it to a variable called express.</p>\n  </li><li>Add the following code to create an Express server:\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> app = express();</code></pre>\n  <p>This code creates a new Express application instance.</p></li>\n  <li>Add the following code to start the server:\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> port = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3000</span>;\n  app.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n    console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">`Server started on port ${port}`</span>);\n  });</code></pre>\n  <p>This code starts the server on port 3000 and logs a message to the console when it\'s started.</p></li>\n  <li>Run the server by running the command <code>node server.js</code> in your terminal.</li>\n  <li>Open your web browser and navigate to <code>http://localhost:3000</code> to see the server in action.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>For this task, you need to create a new project with a package.json file and install the express library. You also need to create a server.js file and add the code to create an Express server and start it on port 3000. Finally, you need to run the server and test it by navigating to <code>http://localhost:3000</code> in your web browser.</p>\n\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Not installing the express library correctly.</li>\n  <li>Not creating a new file called server.js.</li>\n  <li>Not adding the code to create an Express server and start it on port 3000.</li>\n  <li>Not running the server correctly.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use the <code>npm install</code> command to install dependencies, and the <code>npm start</code> command to run your server.</li>\n  <li>Use the <code>require</code> function to import modules, and the <code>import</code> statement to import ES6 modules.</li>\n  <li>Use the <code>console.log</code> function to log messages to the console.</li>\n</ul><br>', 'Node.js and npm Basics', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 12:35:16'),
(969, 982, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312394dec75_1781605268.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Express.js Fundamentals</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ll learn the basics of Express.js, a popular Node.js web framework used to build web applications. You\'ll understand how to create routes, handle requests and responses, and use middleware functions to enhance your application\'s functionality.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>Before diving into the task, let\'s cover some essential concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Express.js</strong>: A Node.js web framework that simplifies the process of building web applications.</li>\n<li><strong>Node.js</strong>: A JavaScript runtime environment that allows you to run JavaScript on the server-side.</li>\n<li><strong>Routing</strong>: The process of defining URLs and mapping them to specific controller functions or actions.</li>\n<li><strong>Middleware</strong>: Functions that execute during the request-response cycle, allowing you to perform tasks such as authentication, logging, or data validation.</li>\n<li><strong>Templates</strong>: Pre-defined HTML templates that can be populated with dynamic data using a templating engine like EJS or Pug.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let\'s break down each concept:</p>\n<h4>Express.js Introduction</h4>\n<p>Express.js is a lightweight, flexible framework that enables you to build web applications quickly and efficiently. It provides a robust set of features, including routing, middleware, and template engines, making it a popular choice among developers.</p>\n<h4>Express.js Installation</h4>\n<p>To install Express.js, run the following command in your terminal:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">npm install express</code></pre>\n<p>This will install Express.js and its dependencies in your project directory.</p>\n<h4>Request and Response Objects</h4>\n<p>When a client sends a request to your server, Express.js creates a request object that contains information about the request, such as the request method, URL, and headers. The response object is used to send a response back to the client.</p>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how to access the request and response objects:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> express = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'express\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> app = express();\n\napp.get(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/\'</span>, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  console.log(req.method); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Output: GET</span>\n  console.log(req.url); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Output: /</span>\n  console.log(res.statusCode); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Output: 200</span>\n});\n</code></pre>\n<p>In this example, we\'re using the `req` object to access the request method and URL, and the `res` object to access the response status code.</p>\n<h4>Routing and Middleware</h4>\n<p>Routing is the process of defining URLs and mapping them to specific controller functions or actions. Middleware functions execute during the request-response cycle, allowing you to perform tasks such as authentication, logging, or data validation.</p>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how to define a route and use middleware:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> express = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'express\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> app = express();\n\napp.use(express.json()); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Use JSON middleware to parse JSON requests</span>\n\napp.post(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/users\'</span>, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> user = req.body;\n  console.log(user); <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Output: { name: \'John Doe\', email: \'johndoe@example.com\' }</span>\n  res.send(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'User created successfully!\'</span>);\n});\n</code></pre>\n<p>In this example, we\'re using the `express.json()` middleware to parse JSON requests, and then defining a route for creating a new user.</p>\n<h4>Express.js Templates</h4>\n<p>Express.js templates are pre-defined HTML templates that can be populated with dynamic data using a templating engine like EJS or Pug.</p>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how to use EJS templates:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> express = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'express\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> app = express();\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> ejs = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'ejs\'</span>);\n\napp.set(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'view engine\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'ejs\'</span>);\n\napp.get(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/\'</span>, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> name = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'John Doe\'</span>;\n  res.render(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'index\'</span>, { name: name });\n});\n</code></pre>\n<p>In this example, we\'re setting the view engine to EJS and defining a route that renders the `index` template with a dynamic value for the `name` variable.</p>\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<p>Now that you\'ve learned the basics of Express.js, it\'s time to put your knowledge into practice. Follow these steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new Node.js project using `npm init`.</li>\n<li>Install Express.js using `npm install express`.</li>\n<li>Create a new file called `app.js` and require Express.js.</li>\n<li>Define a route for the root URL (`/`) that logs the request method and URL to the console.</li>\n<li>Use the `express.json()` middleware to parse JSON requests and define a route for creating a new user.</li>\n<li>Use EJS templates to render a dynamic value for the `name` variable.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>Submit your completed `app.js` file and a screenshot of the output in the console.</p>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<p>Here are some common mistakes to avoid:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Don\'t forget to install Express.js</strong>: Make sure to run `npm install express` before starting your project.</li>\n<li><strong>Use the correct middleware**: Use the `express.json()` middleware to parse JSON requests, and the `express.urlencoded()` middleware to parse URL-encoded requests.</strong></li><strong>\n<li><strong>Don\'t forget to set the view engine**: Set the view engine to EJS or Pug using `app.set(\'view engine\', \'ejs\');` or `app.set(\'view engine\', \'pug\');`.</strong></li><strong>\n</strong></strong></ul><strong><strong>\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<p>Here are some pro tips to help you improve your Express.js skills:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use middleware functions to enhance your application\'s functionality**: Middleware functions can perform tasks such as authentication, logging, or data validation.</strong></li><strong>\n<li><strong>Use EJS templates to render dynamic data**: EJS templates can be populated with dynamic data using a templating engine like EJS or Pug.</strong></li><strong>\n<li><strong>Use the `express.static()` middleware to serve static files**: The `express.static()` middleware can be used to serve static files, such as images or CSS files.</strong></li><strong>\n</strong></strong></strong></ul></strong></strong>', 'Express.js Fundamentals', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:21:19');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(970, 983, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3123c567e7b_1781605317.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>RESTful API Design</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ll learn how to design a RESTful API, which is a crucial skill for any web developer. You\'ll understand how to create API endpoints, handle HTTP requests and responses, secure your API, and document it for others to use.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need:</h4>\n<p>A RESTful API is based on the Representational State of Resource (REST) architectural style. It\'s like a library where you can borrow books (resources) and return them when you\'re done. Each book has a unique ID, and you can perform different actions on it, like borrowing or returning.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>API Endpoints:</strong> These are the URLs that users will use to access your API. For example, <code>/users</code> might be an endpoint for accessing a list of users.</li>\n<li><strong>HTTP Methods:</strong> These are the actions that can be performed on an API endpoint. For example, you might use the <code>GET</code> method to retrieve a list of users, the <code>POST</code> method to create a new user, or the <code>DELETE</code> method to remove a user.</li>\n<li><strong>Request and Response Bodies:</strong> These are the data that\'s sent with the HTTP request and returned with the HTTP response. For example, you might send a JSON object with a <code>username</code> and <code>password</code> to create a new user.</li>\n<li><strong>API Security and Authentication:</strong> This is how you protect your API from unauthorized access. You might use a username and password, a token, or even OAuth to authenticate users.</li>\n<li><strong>API Documentation:</strong> This is how you explain how to use your API. You might use tools like Swagger or API Blueprint to create documentation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task:</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new API endpoint for accessing a list of users. Use the <code>/users</code> URL and the <code>GET</code> method.</li>\n<li>Use the following JSON object as the response body:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">{\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"users\"</span>: [\n    {<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"id\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"name\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"John Doe\"</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"email\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"john@example.com\"</span>},\n    {<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"id\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"name\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Jane Doe\"</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"email\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"jane@example.com\"</span>}\n  ]\n}</code></pre>\n</li><li>Secure your API by adding a username and password to the request body. Use the following JSON object:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">{\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"username\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"myuser\"</span>,\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"password\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"mypassword\"</span>\n}</code></pre>\n</li><li>Use a tool like Swagger or API Blueprint to create documentation for your API.</li>\n<li>Test your API using a tool like Postman or cURL.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable:</h4>\n<p>Submit a JSON object with the following properties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>apiEndpoint:</strong> The URL of the API endpoint you created.</li>\n<li><strong>httpMethod:</strong> The HTTP method you used for the API endpoint.</li>\n<li><strong>requestBody:</strong> The JSON object you used as the request body.</li>\n<li><strong>responseBody:</strong> The JSON object you used as the response body.</li>\n<li><strong>documentation:</strong> A link to your API documentation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Don\'t forget to secure your API with a username and password.</li>\n<li>Don\'t use a simple password like \"mypassword\". Use a strong password or a token instead.</li>\n<li>Don\'t forget to test your API using a tool like Postman or cURL.</li>\n<li>Don\'t skip documenting your API.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Use a tool like Swagger or API Blueprint to create documentation for your API.</li>\n<li>Test your API using a tool like Postman or cURL.</li>\n<li>Use a strong password or a token instead of a simple password.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Example code:</h4>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how you might create an API endpoint using Node.js and Express.js:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;?xml version=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"1.0\"</span> encoding=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"UTF-8\"</span>?&gt;\n&lt;?php\n  $app = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">new</span> Express();\n  $app-&gt;get(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/users\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span>(req, res) {\n    res.json([\n      {<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"id\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"name\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"John Doe\"</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"email\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"john@example.com\"</span>},\n      {<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"id\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"name\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Jane Doe\"</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"email\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"jane@example.com\"</span>}\n    ]);\n  });\n  $app-&gt;listen(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3000</span>, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span>() {\n    console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Server listening on port 3000\'</span>);\n  });\n?&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code creates an API endpoint at <code>/users</code> that returns a list of users in JSON format.</p>\n<h4><br></h4>', 'RESTful API Design', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 12:35:36'),
(971, 984, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3126bc10bfa_1781606076.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>RESTful API Implementation</h3>\n<p>Today, you will learn how to design and implement a RESTful API. This is a crucial skill for any web developer, as it allows you to interact with servers and retrieve or send data. In a real job, you will use RESTful APIs to fetch data from external services, such as weather APIs, social media APIs, or even internal company APIs.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>A RESTful API is based on a set of architectural principles that make it easy to interact with. Let\'s break down the key concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>API Endpoint Design</strong>: This refers to the URL that you use to access a specific resource on the server. Think of it like a street address for a website. Just as you need to know the address to find a specific building, you need to know the API endpoint to access a specific resource.</li>\n  <li><strong>HTTP Request and Response Methods</strong>: When you send a request to a server, you need to specify the type of request you are making. This is done using HTTP methods like GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE. Think of it like sending a letter: you need to specify the type of letter you are sending (e.g. a request for information, a request to create a new resource, etc.).</li>\n  <li><strong>API Request and Response Body</strong>: When you send a request to a server, you may need to include additional data in the request body. This can be in the form of JSON, XML, or other formats. Think of it like including a package with your letter: you need to specify what\'s inside the package.</li>\n  <li><strong>API Error Handling</strong>: When something goes wrong with your request, the server will return an error message. This can be in the form of a JSON object or a plain text error message. Think of it like receiving a letter with a return address: you need to know how to handle the error message.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li>Create a new Node.js project using the command <code>npm init -y</code>. This will create a new folder with the basic files you need to start a new project.</li>\n  <li>Install the Express.js framework using the command <code>npm install express</code>. This will allow you to create a server and handle HTTP requests.</li>\n  <li>Create a new file called <code>server.js</code> and add the following code:<code>\nconst express = require(\'express\');\nconst app = express();\n\napp.get(\'/users\', (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const users = [\n    { id: 1, name: \'John Doe\' },\n    { id: 2, name: \'Jane Doe\' }\n  ];\n  res.json(users);\n});\n\napp.listen(3000, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(\'Server listening on port 3000\');\n});\n</code>\n<p>This code creates a new Express.js server that listens on port 3000 and responds to GET requests to the <code>/users</code> endpoint.</p>\n</li><li>Start the server using the command <code>node server.js</code> and open a new terminal window. Use the command <code>curl http://localhost:3000/users</code> to send a GET request to the <code>/users</code> endpoint. You should see the following response:<code>\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": 1,\n    \"name\": \"John Doe\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 2,\n    \"name\": \"Jane Doe\"\n  }\n]\n</code>\n<p>This is the response body of the API in JSON format.</p>\n</li><li>Create a new endpoint that responds to POST requests. Add the following code to the <code>server.js</code> file:<code>\napp.post(\'/users\', (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const { name } = req.body;\n  const user = { id: 3, name };\n  users.push(user);\n  res.json(user);\n});\n</code>\n<p>This code creates a new endpoint that listens for POST requests to the <code>/users</code> endpoint. It extracts the <code>name</code> property from the request body and creates a new user object. It then adds the new user to the <code>users</code> array and returns the new user as the response body.</p>\n</li><li>Use the command <code>curl -X POST -H \'Content-Type: application/json\' -d \'{\"name\": \"Bob Smith\"}\' http://localhost:3000/users</code> to send a POST request to the <code>/users</code> endpoint. You should see the following response:<code>\n{\n  \"id\": 3,\n  \"name\": \"Bob Smith\"\n}\n</code>\n<p>This is the response body of the API in JSON format.</p>\n</li></ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>Your task is to create a new endpoint that responds to GET requests and returns a list of all users. You should also create a new endpoint that responds to POST requests and creates a new user. You should use the Express.js framework and handle errors using try-catch blocks.</p>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Forgetting to handle errors: Make sure to use try-catch blocks to handle errors and return a meaningful error message.</li>\n  <li>Not validating user input: Make sure to validate user input to prevent SQL injection or cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks.</li>\n  <li>Not using a consistent naming convention: Use a consistent naming convention throughout your code to make it easier to read and understand.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use a code editor with auto-completion and syntax highlighting to make coding faster and easier.</li>\n  <li>Use a version control system like Git to track changes to your code and collaborate with others.</li>\n  <li>Test your code thoroughly to ensure it works as expected and handle edge cases.</li>\n</ul>', 'RESTful API Implementation', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:34:46'),
(972, 985, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a31272fbeaa2_1781606191.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Back-end Security Fundamentals</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 51 of your web development internship! Today, we\'re going to explore the fundamentals of back-end security. As a web developer, it\'s essential to understand how to protect your applications from common vulnerabilities and ensure the security of user data.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Back-end Security:</strong> Back-end security refers to the measures taken to protect a web application\'s server-side logic, database, and user data from unauthorized access, use, disclosure, disruption, modification, or destruction.</li>\n<li><strong>Common Back-end Vulnerabilities:</strong> These are weaknesses in the code or design of a web application that can be exploited by attackers to gain unauthorized access or disrupt the application\'s functionality.</li>\n<li><strong>Secure Coding Practices:</strong> These are coding techniques and guidelines that help prevent common vulnerabilities and ensure the security of a web application.</li>\n<li><strong>Input Validation and Sanitization:</strong> These are techniques used to ensure that user input is safe and secure by validating and sanitizing it before processing or storing it.</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS):</strong> XSS is a type of attack where an attacker injects malicious code into a web application, which is then executed by the user\'s browser.</li>\n<li><strong>SQL Injection:</strong> SQL injection is a type of attack where an attacker injects malicious SQL code into a web application\'s database, which can be used to extract or modify sensitive data.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task:</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Identify Common Back-end Vulnerabilities:</strong> Research and list some common back-end vulnerabilities, such as SQL injection, cross-site scripting (XSS), and cross-site request forgery (CSRF).</li>\n<li><strong>Understand Secure Coding Practices:</strong> Learn about secure coding practices, such as input validation and sanitization, error handling, and secure password storage.</li>\n<li><strong>Implement Input Validation and Sanitization:</strong> Write a simple PHP script that demonstrates input validation and sanitization. For example:<code>\n&lt;?php\n$input = $_POST[\'input\'];\n$filtered_input = filter_var($input, FILTER_SANITIZE_STRING);\necho $filtered_input;\n?&gt;\n</code><p>This script uses the <code>filter_var</code> function to sanitize the user input and prevent XSS attacks.</p>\n</li><li><strong>Test Your Code:</strong> Test your code by entering malicious input, such as HTML tags or JavaScript code, to ensure that it is properly sanitized and prevented from executing.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable:</h4>\n<p>Submit a written report that includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A list of common back-end vulnerabilities and their descriptions.</li>\n<li>A brief explanation of secure coding practices and their importance.</li>\n<li>A PHP script that demonstrates input validation and sanitization.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Not validating user input:</strong> Failing to validate user input can lead to XSS and SQL injection attacks.</li>\n<li><strong>Not sanitizing user input:</strong> Failing to sanitize user input can lead to XSS and SQL injection attacks.</li>\n<li><strong>Using insecure password storage:</strong> Using insecure password storage can lead to password breaches and unauthorized access.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><blockquote>Always validate and sanitize user input to prevent XSS and SQL injection attacks.</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>Use secure password storage mechanisms, such as bcrypt or Argon2, to protect user passwords.</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>Keep your dependencies and libraries up-to-date to prevent known vulnerabilities.</blockquote></li>\n</ul>\n<h4><br></h4>', 'Back-end Security Fundamentals', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 12:35:56'),
(973, 986, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a31275cd120d_1781606236.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Day 52: Back-end Authentication and Authorization</h3>\n<p>In today\'s task, you\'ll learn how to implement back-end authentication and authorization using JSON Web Tokens (JWT) and role-based access control. This is a crucial skill for any web developer, as it ensures that users can only access the resources they\'re entitled to.</p>\n\n<h4>Concepts You Need</h4>\n<p>Before we dive into the task, let\'s cover the key concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Authentication</strong>: This is the process of verifying a user\'s identity. Think of it like a bouncer at a nightclub: you need to show your ID to get in.</li>\n  <li><strong>Authorization</strong>: This is the process of deciding what actions a user can perform once they\'re inside the club. For example, some users might be allowed to buy drinks, while others might only be able to watch the performance.</li>\n  <li><strong>JSON Web Tokens (JWT)</strong>: These are a way to securely pass information between the client and server. Think of them like a special ticket that lets you get past the bouncer and into the club.</li>\n  <li><strong>Session-based Authentication</strong>: This is a way to store user information on the server-side, so that the server can remember who the user is. Think of it like a sticky note on the bouncer\'s desk, reminding them who\'s allowed in.</li>\n  <li><strong>Role-based Access Control</strong>: This is a way to decide what actions a user can perform based on their role. For example, a manager might be able to create new users, while a regular user might only be able to view existing users.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Step-by-Step Task</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Create a new Node.js project</strong>: Run the following command in your terminal: <code>npm init -y</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Install the required packages</strong>: Run the following commands in your terminal:<br><code>npm install express jsonwebtoken bcrypt</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Create a new file called <code>auth.js</code></strong>: This file will contain the authentication logic. Create a new file called <code>auth.js</code> in the root of your project, and add the following code:<br><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> express = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'express\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> jwt = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'jsonwebtoken\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> bcrypt = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'bcrypt\'</span>);\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> app = express();\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> users = [\n  { id: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, username: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'john\'</span>, password: bcrypt.hashSync(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'hello\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>) },\n  { id: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, username: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'jane\'</span>, password: bcrypt.hashSync(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'world\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>) },\n];\n\napp.post(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/login\'</span>, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> { username, password } = req.body;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> user = users.find((user) =&gt; user.username === username);\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (!user) {\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> res.status(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">401</span>).send({ message: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Invalid username or password\'</span> });\n  }\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> isValidPassword = bcrypt.compareSync(password, user.password);\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (!isValidPassword) {\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> res.status(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">401</span>).send({ message: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Invalid username or password\'</span> });\n  }\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> token = jwt.sign({ userId: user.id }, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'secretkey\'</span>, { expiresIn: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'1h\'</span> });\n  res.send({ token });\n});\n</code></pre></li>\n  <li><strong>Start the server</strong>: Run the following command in your terminal: <code>node auth.js</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Test the login endpoint</strong>: Use a tool like Postman to send a POST request to <code>http://localhost:3000/login</code> with the following JSON body:<br><code>{ \"username\": \"john\", \"password\": \"hello\" }</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Verify the JWT token</strong>: Use a tool like jwt.io to verify the token you received in the previous step.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>For this task, you\'ll need to submit the following:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The <code>auth.js</code> file with the authentication logic</li>\n  <li>A screenshot of the JWT token you received when you tested the login endpoint</li>\n  <li>A screenshot of the verification result on jwt.io</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Don\'t store passwords in plaintext</strong>: Always use a library like bcrypt to hash and store passwords securely.</li>\n  <li><strong>Don\'t use a weak secret key</strong>: Use a strong secret key to sign and verify JWT tokens.</li>\n  <li><strong>Don\'t forget to handle errors</strong>: Always handle errors and exceptions properly to prevent your application from crashing.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Pro Tips</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Use a library like Passport.js to simplify authentication</strong>: Passport.js is a popular library that can help you implement authentication and authorization in your Node.js application.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use a database instead of an in-memory array</strong>: In a real-world application, you\'ll want to store user data in a database instead of an in-memory array.</li>\n  <li><strong>Implement rate limiting and IP blocking</strong>: To prevent brute-force attacks, you should implement rate limiting and IP blocking in your application.</li>\n</ul>', 'Back-end Authentication and Authorization', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:37:22'),
(974, 987, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; <img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a31277ee7523_1781606270.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Introduction to Database-driven Back-end</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 53 of your web development internship! Today, we\'ll introduce you to the world of database-driven development. This is a crucial concept in web development, as it allows you to store and manage data efficiently. A real-world example of this is an e-commerce website, where you need to store information about products, customers, and orders.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need:</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Database-driven Development</strong>: This is a way of developing software that uses a database to store and manage data.</li>\n  <li><strong>Database Modeling and Schema Design</strong>: This is the process of creating a conceptual representation of the data and its relationships.</li>\n  <li><strong>Database Querying and CRUD Operations</strong>: This is the process of retrieving and manipulating data in a database.</li>\n  <li><strong>Database Indexing and Optimization</strong>: This is the process of improving the performance of database queries by creating indexes and optimizing database configuration.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task:</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Create a database schema</strong> for an e-commerce website using MySQL. The schema should include the following tables:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Products</li>\n      <li>Customers</li>\n      <li>Orders</li>\n      <li>Order Items</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><code>&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;\nCREATE TABLE Products (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  name VARCHAR(255),\n  price DECIMAL(10, 2)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE Customers (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  name VARCHAR(255),\n  email VARCHAR(255)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE Orders (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  customer_id INT,\n  order_date DATE,\n  total DECIMAL(10, 2),\n  FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES Customers(id)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE OrderItems (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  order_id INT,\n  product_id INT,\n  quantity INT,\n  FOREIGN KEY (order_id) REFERENCES Orders(id),\n  FOREIGN KEY (product_id) REFERENCES Products(id)\n);\n&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;</code>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Write a SQL query</strong> to retrieve the total amount spent by each customer in the last 30 days. The query should include the customer name and the total amount spent.\n    <code>&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;\nSELECT c.name, SUM(oi.quantity * p.price) AS total_spent\nFROM Customers c\nJOIN Orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id\nJOIN OrderItems oi ON o.id = oi.order_id\nJOIN Products p ON oi.product_id = p.id\nWHERE o.order_date &gt; DATE_SUB(CURDATE(), INTERVAL 30 DAY)\nGROUP BY c.name;\n&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;</code>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Explain</strong> how database indexing can improve the performance of the query in step 3.\n    <p>Database indexing is a technique used to improve the performance of database queries by creating a data structure that allows for faster lookup and retrieval of data. In the query in step 3, we can create an index on the `order_date` column to improve the performance of the query. This is because the query is filtering data based on the `order_date` column, and an index on this column can speed up the filtering process.</p>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable:</h4>\n<p>Submit the following:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The database schema created in step 1.</li>\n  <li>The SQL query written in step 3.</li>\n  <li>A brief explanation of how database indexing can improve the performance of the query in step 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid:</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Not normalizing the database schema</strong>: Normalization is the process of organizing data in a database to minimize data redundancy and improve data integrity. Failing to normalize the database schema can lead to data inconsistencies and errors.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not using indexes correctly</strong>: Indexes can improve the performance of database queries, but they can also slow down write operations. It\'s essential to use indexes judiciously and to monitor their impact on database performance.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not optimizing database configuration</strong>: Database configuration can significantly impact database performance. Failing to optimize database configuration can lead to slow query performance and other issues.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips:</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Use database modeling tools</strong> to create a conceptual representation of the data and its relationships. This can help you identify potential issues and improve data integrity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use indexes and views</strong> to improve database performance and simplify complex queries.</li>\n  <li><strong>Monitor database performance</strong> regularly to identify potential issues and optimize database configuration.</li>\n</ul>', 'Introduction to Database-driven Back-end', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:38:02'),
(975, 988, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3127b465c2c_1781606324.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you will connect your back-end application to a database, enabling it to store and retrieve real data. This is essential for building functional apps like user logins, shopping carts, or blogs.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>Database Integration</h4>\n<p>Think of a database as a digital filing cabinet. Integration means teaching your app to open files (read data), add new files (write data), and organize them properly.</p>\n\n<h4>Database Connection and Configuration</h4>\n<p>This is like plugging your computer into a printer. You need the right cable (driver) and to enter the printer’s name and password (configuration) before they can work together.</p>\n\n<h4>Database Querying and Data Retrieval</h4>\n<p>A query is a question you ask the database. For example: \"Show me all users who signed up this week.\" Your app sends this question and gets the answer as data it can use.</p>\n\n<h4>Database Error Handling</h4>\n<p>Errors happen when the database can’t understand your question or has no power. Error handling is like checking if your printer has paper before printing—your app should catch problems and show helpful messages.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Create a database</strong><br>\nUse SQLite (a lightweight database) and run this command in your terminal:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">sqlite3 mydatabase.db</code></pre>\nThen create a table with:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE TABLE users (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  name TEXT,\n  email TEXT\n);</code></pre>\nAdd sample data with:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES\n(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Alice\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'alice@example.com\'</span>),\n(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Bob\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'bob@example.com\'</span>);</code></pre>\n</li>\n\n<li><strong>Connect your back-end</strong><br>\nInstall the SQLite3 package in your Node.js project:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">npm install sqlite3</code></pre>\nCreate a <code>db.js</code> file with this code:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> sqlite3 = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'sqlite3\'</span>).verbose();\n\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Create a database connection</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> db = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">new</span> sqlite3.Database(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'./mydatabase.db\'</span>, (err) =&gt; {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (err) {\n    console.error(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Database connection error:\'</span>, err.message);\n  } <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">else</span> {\n    console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Connected to the database\'</span>);\n  }\n});\n\nmodule.exports = db;</code></pre>\nThis code tells your app where to find the database and checks if the connection works.\n</li>\n\n<li><strong>Query the database</strong><br>\nIn your main server file (e.g., <code>server.js</code>), add this route to get user data:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> express = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'express\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> db = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'./db\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> app = express();\n\napp.get(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/users\'</span>, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  db.all(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'SELECT * FROM users\'</span>, (err, rows) =&gt; {\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (err) {\n      res.status(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">500</span>).json({ error: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Database error\'</span> });\n    } <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">else</span> {\n      res.json(rows);\n    }\n  });\n});\n\napp.listen(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3000</span>, () =&gt; console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Server running on port 3000\'</span>));</code></pre>\nThis route sends an SQL query to the database and returns the results as JSON.</li>\n\n<li><strong>Add error handling</strong><br>\nUpdate the <code>/users</code> route to handle specific errors:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">app.get(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/users\'</span>, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  db.all(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'SELECT * FROM users\'</span>, (err, rows) =&gt; {\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (err) {\n      console.error(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Query error:\'</span>, err.message);\n      res.status(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">500</span>).json({ error: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Could not retrieve users\'</span> });\n    } <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">else</span> {\n      res.json(rows);\n    }\n  });\n});</code></pre>\nThis logs errors for debugging but shows users a friendly message.</li>\n\n<li><strong>Test your work</strong><br>\nStart your server with <code>node server.js</code>, then visit <code>http://localhost:3000/users</code> in your browser. You should see the user data from your database.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit: <strong>(1)</strong> Your complete code files (<code>db.js</code>, <code>server.js</code>, and <code>mydatabase.db</code>), and <strong>(2)</strong> a screenshot of your browser showing the user data from <code>/users</code>.</p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Forgetting to close database connections:</strong> Always use <code>db.close()</code> when your app shuts down to prevent resource leaks.</li>\n<li><strong>Hardcoding passwords:</strong> Store database credentials in environment variables (we’ll learn this later), not directly in code.</li>\n<li><strong>Ignoring SQL injection:</strong> Never insert user input directly into queries (we’ll cover safe methods in future days).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use a database GUI:</strong> Tools like <em>DBeaver</em> or <em>SQLite Browser</em> let you view and edit databases visually.</li>\n<li><strong>Start with simple queries:</strong> Practice <code>SELECT</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, and <code>UPDATE</code> commands in the database terminal before using them in code.</li>\n<li><strong>Log errors to the console:</strong> Add <code>console.error(err)</code> in your error handlers to see detailed error messages during debugging.</li>\n</ul>', 'Database Integration with Back-end', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:38:51'),
(976, 989, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3127fa5686b_1781606394.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you\'ll learn how to deploy a back-end app to the internet and scale it for many users. You\'ll deploy a simple Node.js app using cloud services, and understand how to handle growth using containers and load balancing. This is critical for real jobs where apps must stay online and handle traffic spikes.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>Back-end Deployment</h4>\n<p>Deployment is like moving into a new house: you prepare your app (your belongings), choose a hosting service (the house), and make sure everything works there. For back-end apps, this means putting your server code on a remote machine that\'s always on.</p>\n\n<h4>Deployment Options and Strategies</h4>\n<p><strong>Deployment options</strong> are like different types of houses: you can rent a ready-made one (cloud platforms like Heroku), build your own (virtual machines on AWS), or use shared space (shared hosting). <strong>Deployment strategies</strong> are plans for updating your app without breaking it. For example, \"blue-green deployment\" is like having two houses and switching keys between them.</p>\n\n<h4>Scaling and Load Balancing</h4>\n<p>Scaling is like adding more cashiers to a store during a sale. <strong>Vertical scaling</strong> adds more power to one server (bigger cashier), while <strong>horizontal scaling</strong> adds more servers (more cashiers). <strong>Load balancing</strong> is a traffic director who sends visitors to the least busy cashier.</p>\n\n<h4>Containerization and Orchestration</h4>\n<p><strong>Containers</strong> are like magic boxes that pack your app with all its dependencies. Docker is the most common tool. <strong>Orchestration</strong> (like Kubernetes) is a team of workers who manage many containers automatically, moving them between servers and fixing broken ones.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Create a simple Node.js app:\n</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> express = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'express\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> app = express();\n\napp.get(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/\'</span>, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.send(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Hello from the cloud!\'</span>);\n});\n\napp.listen(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3000</span>, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Server running on port 3000\'</span>);\n});</code></pre>\nSave this as <code>app.js</code> and run <code>npm init -y</code> to create a project folder.<p></p></li>\n\n<li><p>Deploy to Heroku (basic deployment):\n</p><ol>\n<li>Create a <code>Procfile</code> with <code>web: node app.js</code></li>\n<li>Install Heroku CLI and run:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">heroku create\nheroku login\ngit init\nheroku git:remote -a your-app-name\ngit add .\ngit commit -m <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Initial commit\'</span>\ngit push heroku main</code></pre></li>\n<li>Test your live app at <code>https://your-app-name.herokuapp.com</code></li>\n</ol></li>\n\n<li><p>Try a scaling strategy: Blue-Green Deployment\n</p><ol>\n<li>Deploy a new version with <code>git push heroku main</code> while keeping the old version running</li>\n<li>Use Heroku\'s <code>heroku ps:scale web=2</code> to run two app instances</li>\n</ol></li>\n\n<li><p>Create a Docker container:\n</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">FROM node:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">18</span>\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">package</span>*.json .\nRUN npm install\nCOPY . .\nEXPOSE <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3000</span>\nCMD [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"node\"</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"app.js\"</span>]</code></pre>\nSave this as <code>Dockerfile</code>. Build with <code>docker build -t my-app .</code><p></p></li>\n\n<li><p>Test local container:\n</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">docker run -p <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4000</span>:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3000</span> my-app</code></pre>\nVisit <code>http://localhost:4000</code> to see your app running in a container</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A working Heroku app URL</li>\n<li>Screenshots of your app in browser and Docker running locally</li>\n<li>A text file listing 3 deployment strategies and 2 scaling types with brief explanations</li>\n<li>Your completed Dockerfile</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Forgetting to test your app locally with <code>node app.js</code> before deployment</li>\n<li>Using double quotes in Dockerfile commands (always use single quotes)</li>\n<li>Not setting environment variables for cloud services</li>\n<li>Trying to run containers without first building them</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use CI/CD:</strong> Set up GitHub Actions or GitLab CI to automate deployments</li>\n<li><strong>Monitor performance:</strong> Add tools like New Relic or use cloud provider metrics</li>\n<li><strong>Start small:</strong> Begin with simple deployments before trying complex orchestration</li>\n</ul>', 'Back-end Deployment and Scaling', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:40:09');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(977, 990, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: database diagram example</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=database+diagram+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/database+diagram+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/database+diagram+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/database+diagram+example/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=database+diagram+example\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you\'ll build a simple database for a blog platform to manage users and posts. This skill is essential for real-world jobs where apps need to store, organize, and retrieve data efficiently.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n\n<h4>1. Database Management System (DBMS)</h4>\n<p>A <strong>DBMS</strong> is software that lets you create, manage, and query databases. Think of it as a digital filing cabinet with built-in tools for organizing and finding documents.</p>\n\n<h4>2. Database Types</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Relational Databases</strong> (like MySQL, PostgreSQL): Store data in tables with rows and columns. Like a spreadsheet system where sheets connect to each other.</li>\n<li><strong>NoSQL Databases</strong> (like MongoDB): Store data in flexible formats (documents, key-value pairs). Like a basket that can hold apples (one shape) or mixed fruits (many shapes).</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>3. Database Design and Modeling</h4>\n<p>This is about creating a \"blueprint\" for your data. You\'ll define tables, fields, and relationships. For example, in a blog system: Users → Posts → Comments.</p>\n\n<h4>4. Normalization and Denormalization</h4>\n<p><strong>Normalization</strong> reduces data redundancy by splitting tables (like organizing a messy room into labeled boxes). <strong>Denormalization</strong> duplicates data for faster queries (like keeping a copy of a frequently used document in multiple places).</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Create an Entity-Relationship Diagram (ERD)</strong>\n<ol>\n<li>Draw two entities: <code>User</code> (id, name, email) and <code>Post</code> (id, title, content, user_id).</li>\n<li>Show the relationship: One User can have Many Posts.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n\n<li><strong>Set up SQLite database</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">sqlite3 blog.db</code></pre>\n<p>This creates a new database file named <code>blog.db</code>.</p>\n</li>\n\n<li><strong>Create tables with SQL</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE TABLE User (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  name TEXT NOT NULL,\n  email TEXT UNIQUE\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE Post (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  title TEXT NOT NULL,\n  content TEXT,\n  user_id INTEGER,\n  FOREIGN KEY(user_id) REFERENCES User(id)\n);</code></pre>\n<p><code>PRIMARY KEY</code> uniquely identifies each row. <code>FOREIGN KEY</code> links posts to users.</p>\n</li>\n\n<li><strong>Insert sample data</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">INSERT INTO User (name, email) VALUES\n(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Alice\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'alice@example.com\'</span>),\n(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Bob\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'bob@example.com\'</span>);\n\nINSERT INTO Post (title, content, user_id) VALUES\n(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'First Post\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Hello world!\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>),\n(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'My Journey\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Day 1...\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>);</code></pre>\n</li>\n\n<li><strong>Query the database</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">SELECT Post.title, User.name \nFROM Post\nJOIN User ON Post.user_id = User.id;</code></pre>\n<p>This shows all posts with their authors by connecting the two tables.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n\n<ul>\n<li>1. Your ERD as a PNG image (draw on paper and scan, or use a tool like draw.io)</li>\n<li>2. A text file showing your SQL code and query output</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Forgetting <code>PRIMARY KEY</code> in tables (every table needs one)</li>\n<li>Not setting <code>FOREIGN KEY</code> constraints (important for data relationships)</li>\n<li>Using spaces in column names (use underscores instead)</li>\n<li>Writing queries without testing them step by step</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Start with normalization, then denormalize only if needed for performance</li>\n<li>Use <code>DESCRIBE TABLE</code> (MySQL) or <code>.schema</code> (SQLite) to check your table structure</li>\n<li>Practice with SQLite Browser for a visual interface</li>\n</ul>', 'Introduction to Database Management', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(978, 991, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3128507f9a2_1781606480.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\">&nbsp;</p></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you\'ll create a simple SQL database to organize a library\'s book collection. This skill is crucial for real jobs where databases store customer info, product inventories, and more.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>What is SQL?</h4>\n<p><strong>SQL</strong> (Structured Query Language) is like a special language for talking to databases. Think of it as the rules for asking questions to a library catalog system.</p>\n\n<h4>SQL Syntax</h4>\n<p>SQL syntax is the grammar of the language. Just like recipes have specific steps, SQL commands follow patterns like:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">SELECT * FROM books;</code></pre>\n<p>This means \"show me all data from the books table\".</p>\n\n<h4>Data Types</h4>\n<p>Data types define what kind of information goes in each column. Common types include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>TEXT</code> - for words (book titles)</li>\n<li><code>INTEGER</code> - for numbers (book IDs)</li>\n<li><code>BOOLEAN</code> - for yes/no (available/checked out)</li>\n<li><code>DATE</code> - for dates (publish dates)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>SQL Queries</h4>\n<p>Queries are questions you ask the database. A simple query might be:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">SELECT title FROM books WHERE author = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'J.K. Rowling\'</span>;</code></pre>\n<p>This asks: \"Show me all book titles where the author is J.K. Rowling\"</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Create a new SQL file named <code>library.sql</code></p></li>\n\n<li><p>Start with creating a database:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE DATABASE library;</code></pre>\n<p>This builds a new container to hold all our library data</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Create a books table:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE TABLE books (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  title TEXT NOT NULL,\n  author TEXT NOT NULL,\n  available BOOLEAN DEFAULT <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">true</span>,\n  publish_date DATE\n);</code></pre>\n<p>This creates a table with columns for book information. <code>PRIMARY KEY</code> makes <code>id</code> the unique identifier</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Add some books:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">INSERT INTO books (id, title, author, available, publish_date)\nVALUES\n  (<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Harry Potter\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'J.K. Rowling\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">true</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'1997-06-26\'</span>),\n  (<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'The Hobbit\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'J.R.R. Tolkien\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">false</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'1937-09-21\'</span>);</code></pre>\n<p>This adds two books to our collection</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Try different queries:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">SELECT * FROM books;\nSELECT title FROM books WHERE available = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">true</span>;\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM books;</code></pre>\n<p>These show all data, available books, and count of books respectively</p></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit your completed <code>library.sql</code> file containing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Database creation</li>\n<li>Table definition</li>\n<li>At least 3 INSERT statements</li>\n<li>3 different SELECT queries</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Forgetting semicolons at the end of each statement</li>\n<li>Mixing up <code>TEXT</code> and <code>DATE</code> data types</li>\n<li>Using uppercase for SQL keywords (should be uppercase for readability)</li>\n<li>Not matching column names exactly when inserting data</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use aliases:</strong> Add <code>AS</code> to rename columns in results: <code>SELECT title AS Book FROM books;</code></li>\n<li><strong>Comment your code:</strong> Use <code>--</code> for notes: <code>-- This is a comment</code></li>\n<li><strong>Practice queries:</strong> Try filtering by dates: <code>SELECT * FROM books WHERE publish_date &gt; \'2000-01-01\';</code></li>\n</ul>', 'SQL Database Fundamentals', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:41:50'),
(979, 992, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3128a8c847c_1781606568.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>NoSQL Database Introduction</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 58 of your web development internship! Today, you\'ll learn about NoSQL databases, which are used to store and manage large amounts of unstructured or semi-structured data. This is a crucial skill to have in your toolkit, as many modern web applications rely on NoSQL databases to scale efficiently.</p>\n\n<h4>Concepts you need:</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>NoSQL Database Types: Relational databases vs. NoSQL databases</li>\n  <li>NoSQL Database Models: Document, Key-Value, and Graph databases</li>\n  <li>NoSQL Data Modeling: Designing NoSQL databases for efficient data storage and retrieval</li>\n  <li>NoSQL Advantages: Scalability, flexibility, and high performance</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>NoSQL Database Types</h4>\n<p>NoSQL databases differ from traditional relational databases in several ways. While relational databases use tables with fixed schemas to store and manage data, NoSQL databases use flexible data models that can adapt to changing data structures.</p>\n\n<p>Think of a relational database like a library with books on shelves. Each book has a fixed title, author, and publication date, and the shelves are organized by category. In contrast, a NoSQL database is like a digital storage unit where you can store files of different types and sizes, without worrying about their structure.</p>\n\n<h4>NoSQL Database Models</h4>\n<p>NoSQL databases come in various models, each designed to handle specific types of data. Let\'s explore three common models:</p>\n\n<h4>Document Database</h4>\n<p>A document database stores data in self-contained documents, similar to JSON objects. Each document represents a single record, and the data is stored in a flexible format.</p>\n\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">{\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"name\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"John Doe\"</span>,\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"email\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"johndoe@example.com\"</span>,\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"address\"</span>: {\n    <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"street\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"123 Main St\"</span>,\n    <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"city\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Anytown\"</span>,\n    <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"state\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"CA\"</span>,\n    <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"zip\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"12345\"</span>\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n\n<h4>Key-Value Database</h4>\n<p>A key-value database stores data as a collection of key-value pairs. Each key is unique, and the value associated with it can be a string, number, or even another key-value pair.</p>\n\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">{\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"user_id\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>,\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"username\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"johndoe\"</span>,\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"email\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"johndoe@example.com\"</span>\n}</code></pre>\n\n<h4>Graph Database</h4>\n<p>A graph database stores data as a collection of nodes connected by edges. Each node represents an entity, and the edges represent relationships between entities.</p>\n\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">{\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"nodes\"</span>: [\n    {\n      <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"id\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>,\n      <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"name\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"John Doe\"</span>\n    },\n    {\n      <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"id\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>,\n      <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"name\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Jane Doe\"</span>\n    }\n  ],\n  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"edges\"</span>: [\n    {\n      <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"from\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>,\n      <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"to\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>,\n      <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"type\"</span>: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"friend\"</span>\n    }\n  ]\n}</code></pre>\n\n<h4>NoSQL Data Modeling</h4>\n<p>NoSQL data modeling involves designing the structure of your NoSQL database to efficiently store and retrieve data. Consider the following best practices:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Use a flexible data model that can adapt to changing data structures.</li>\n  <li>Use indexing to improve query performance.</li>\n  <li>Use data partitioning to scale your database horizontally.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>NoSQL Advantages</h4>\n<p>NoSQL databases offer several advantages over traditional relational databases:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Scalability: NoSQL databases can scale horizontally to handle large amounts of data.</li>\n  <li>Flexibility: NoSQL databases can handle unstructured or semi-structured data.</li>\n  <li>High performance: NoSQL databases can handle high query rates and large amounts of data.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task:</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Research and compare different NoSQL databases:</strong> Look into popular NoSQL databases like MongoDB, Cassandra, and Neo4j. Compare their features, scalability, and use cases.</li>\n  <li><strong>Choose a NoSQL database for a hypothetical project:</strong> Select a NoSQL database and design a project that utilizes its features. Consider the type of data you\'ll be storing and the queries you\'ll be running.</li>\n  <li><strong>Design a NoSQL data model:</strong> Use the chosen NoSQL database to design a data model that efficiently stores and retrieves data. Consider indexing, data partitioning, and other best practices.</li>\n  <li><strong>Write a short report:</strong> Summarize your research and design decisions in a short report. Include the following:</li>\n  <ul>\n    <li>NoSQL database chosen and its features.</li>\n    <li>Data model designed for the project.</li>\n    <li>Advantages and disadvantages of the chosen NoSQL database.</li>\n  </ul>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable:</h3>\n<p>Submit a short report (1-2 pages) that summarizes your research and design decisions. Include the following:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>NoSQL database chosen and its features.</li>\n  <li>Data model designed for the project.</li>\n  <li>Advantages and disadvantages of the chosen NoSQL database.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid:</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Not considering scalability:</strong> NoSQL databases can scale horizontally, but you need to design your database and data model to take advantage of this feature.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not using indexing:</strong> Indexing can improve query performance, but you need to use it correctly to avoid performance issues.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not considering data partitioning:</strong> Data partitioning can help you scale your database horizontally, but you need to design your data model to take advantage of this feature.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips:</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Use a flexible data model:</strong> NoSQL databases are designed to handle changing data structures, so use a flexible data model that can adapt to these changes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use data partitioning:</strong> Data partitioning can help you scale your database horizontally, but you need to design your data model to take advantage of this feature.</li>\n  <li><strong>Monitor performance:</strong> Monitor your database\'s performance to identify bottlenecks and optimize your data model and queries accordingly.</li>\n</ul>', 'NoSQL Database Introduction', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:43:03'),
(980, 993, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312985a3efd_1781606789.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Database Modeling and Design</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 59 of your web development internship! Today, you\'ll learn how to design and model a database, which is a crucial step in building a scalable and efficient web application. A well-designed database can save you time and effort in the long run, making it easier to manage and maintain your data.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>Entity-Relationship Modeling (ERM)</h4>\n<p>Entity-Relationship Modeling is a technique used to design a database by identifying the entities, attributes, and relationships between them. Think of it like building a house. You start with the foundation (entities), add walls (attributes), and then connect them with doors and windows (relationships).</p>\n<p>For example, let\'s say you\'re building a database for a library. The entities could be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Books</li>\n<li>AUTHORS</li>\n<li>READERS</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The attributes for each entity could be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Books: title, author_id, publication_date</li>\n<li>AUTHORS: name, email, phone_number</li>\n<li>READERS: name, email, password</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Now, let\'s talk about relationships. For example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A book is written by one author (one-to-one relationship)</li>\n<li>A reader can borrow multiple books (one-to-many relationship)</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Database Schema Design</h4>\n<p>Once you have your entities, attributes, and relationships, you can design your database schema. This involves creating tables, defining their structure, and specifying the relationships between them. Think of it like building a blueprint for your house.</p>\n<p>For our library database, the schema could look like this:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\nCREATE TABLE books (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  title VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">255</span>),\n  author_id INT,\n  publication_date DATE\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE authors (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  name VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">255</span>),\n  email VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">255</span>),\n  phone_number VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">20</span>)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE readers (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  name VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">255</span>),\n  email VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">255</span>),\n  password VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">255</span>)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE book_borrowers (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  book_id INT,\n  reader_id INT,\n  borrow_date DATE,\n  return_date DATE\n);\n</code></pre>\n<h4>Database Normalization</h4>\n<p>Database normalization is the process of organizing data in a database to minimize data redundancy and dependency. Think of it like decluttering your house. You want to get rid of any unnecessary items and keep only what\'s essential.</p>\n<p>There are several normalization rules:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First Normal Form (1NF): Each table cell should contain a single value.</li>\n<li>Second Normal Form (2NF): Each non-key attribute in a table should depend on the entire primary key.</li>\n<li>Third Normal Form (3NF): If a table is in 2NF, and a non-key attribute depends on another non-key attribute, then it should be moved to a separate table.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Database Denormalization</h4>\n<p>Database denormalization is the process of deliberately adding redundancy to a database to improve performance. Think of it like keeping a few favorite items in a separate room, even though they\'re not strictly necessary.</p>\n<p>Denormalization can be useful in certain situations, such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Improving query performance</li>\n<li>Reducing the number of joins required</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new database schema for a library using the entities, attributes, and relationships we discussed earlier.</li>\n<li>Design the schema to minimize data redundancy and dependency using database normalization techniques.</li>\n<li>Denormalize the schema to improve query performance by adding a few redundant attributes.</li>\n<li>Write a SQL query to retrieve the title, author name, and publication date of all books written by a specific author.</li>\n<li>Write a SQL query to retrieve the name and email of all readers who have borrowed a specific book.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>Please submit your database schema design, including the entities, attributes, and relationships. Also, include the SQL queries from steps 4 and 5.</p>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Not normalizing the database schema, leading to data redundancy and dependency issues.</li>\n<li>Over-normalizing the schema, making it too complex and difficult to maintain.</li>\n<li>Not considering the performance implications of denormalization.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use Entity-Relationship Modeling to design your database schema, making it easier to understand and maintain.</li>\n<li>Use database normalization techniques to minimize data redundancy and dependency issues.</li>\n<li>Consider denormalization only when necessary, and carefully evaluate the performance implications.</li>\n</ul>', 'Database Modeling and Design', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:46:35'),
(981, 994, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3129bee168c_1781606846.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Database Queries and Indexing</h3>\n<p>In this task, you\'ll learn about optimizing database queries, indexing and constraints, database views, and stored procedures. You\'ll apply these concepts to a real-world scenario, improving the performance of a database.</p>\n<p>This is a crucial skill in web development, as it allows you to write efficient database queries that scale with your application\'s growth.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need:</h3>\n<h4>SQL Query Optimization</h4>\n<p>A SQL query is like a recipe for your database. You give it a list of ingredients (data) and it returns a dish (result). But, just like a recipe, a SQL query can be optimized for better performance. Think of it like a chef perfecting a recipe to make it faster and more efficient.</p>\n<p>There are several ways to optimize SQL queries:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use indexes</strong>: An index is like a map that helps the database find the data it needs quickly. Without an index, the database has to search through all the data, which can be slow.</li>\n<li><strong>Limit the amount of data</strong>: Only retrieve the data you need, rather than bringing back too much data that you won\'t use.</li>\n<li><strong>Use efficient joins</strong>: Joins are like combining two recipes. You can use different types of joins to combine data from multiple tables.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid using SELECT *</strong>: Instead of selecting all columns (*), specify the exact columns you need.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Indexing and Constraints</h4>\n<p>An index is a data structure that improves the speed of data retrieval. It\'s like a map that helps the database find the data it needs quickly.</p>\n<p>There are two types of indexes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clustered index</strong>: This type of index rearranges the data in the table to match the index. It\'s like organizing a library by author.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-clustered index</strong>: This type of index creates a separate data structure that points to the data in the table. It\'s like creating a catalog of books.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A constraint is a rule that ensures data integrity. It\'s like a safety net that prevents invalid data from being inserted into the database.</p>\n<h4>Database Views and Stored Procedures</h4>\n<p>A database view is like a virtual table that\'s based on a query. It\'s like a recipe that\'s already been cooked and can be served immediately.</p>\n<p>A stored procedure is a set of SQL statements that are stored in the database. It\'s like a pre-cooked meal that can be served quickly.</p>\n<h4>Database Query Best Practices</h4>\n<p>Here are some best practices to keep in mind when writing database queries:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use meaningful table and column names</strong>: This makes your queries easier to read and understand.</li>\n<li><strong>Use comments to explain your queries</strong>: This helps others understand your code and makes it easier to maintain.</li>\n<li><strong>Test your queries</strong>: Before deploying your queries to production, test them to ensure they work correctly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Step-by-step task:</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new table called \'orders\' with the following columns: \'id\', \'customer_id\', \'order_date\', and \'total\'.</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  customer_id INT,\n  order_date DATE,\n  total DECIMAL(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>)\n);\n</code></pre>\n<p>This table represents an order in a database. The \'id\' column is the primary key, which uniquely identifies each order.</p>\n<li>Create an index on the \'customer_id\' column in the \'orders\' table.</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\nCREATE INDEX idx_customer_id ON orders (customer_id);\n</code></pre>\n<p>This index will improve the speed of queries that retrieve orders by customer.</p>\n<li>Create a view that shows the total amount spent by each customer.</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\nCREATE VIEW customer_spending AS\nSELECT customer_id, SUM(total) AS total_spent\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY customer_id;\n</code></pre>\n<p>This view will show the total amount spent by each customer, based on the orders in the \'orders\' table.</p>\n<li>Create a stored procedure that inserts a new order into the \'orders\' table.</li>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\nCREATE PROCEDURE insert_order\n  @customer_id INT,\n  @order_date DATE,\n  @total DECIMAL(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>)\nAS\nBEGIN\n  INSERT INTO orders (customer_id, order_date, total)\n  VALUES (@customer_id, @order_date, @total);\nEND;\n</code></pre>\n<p>This stored procedure will insert a new order into the \'orders\' table, based on the provided parameters.</p>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable:</h3>\n<p>Submit the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A screenshot of the \'orders\' table with an index on the \'customer_id\' column.</li>\n<li>A screenshot of the \'customer_spending\' view.</li>\n<li>A screenshot of the \'insert_order\' stored procedure.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Not using indexes</strong>: Failing to use indexes can lead to slow query performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Not optimizing queries</strong>: Queries that are not optimized can lead to slow performance and increased resource usage.</li>\n<li><strong>Not testing queries</strong>: Failing to test queries before deploying them to production can lead to errors and downtime.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use indexes on frequently used columns</strong>: This will improve the speed of queries that retrieve data from those columns.</li>\n<li><strong>Use efficient joins</strong>: This will improve the speed of queries that combine data from multiple tables.</li>\n<li><strong>Use stored procedures for complex queries</strong>: This will improve the speed and maintainability of complex queries.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Image query:</h3>\n<p>Database optimization</p>\n<h3>Quiz:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li></li>\n</ul>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>', 'Database Queries and Indexing', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:47:32'),
(982, 995, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312a0b3d927_1781606923.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Day 61: Web Security Fundamentals</h3>\n<p>Today, you will learn about web security basics, common web vulnerabilities, secure coding practices, and web security frameworks. This knowledge is crucial in real-world web development, as it helps protect users\' sensitive information and prevents malicious attacks.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need:</h4>\n<p>A web application is like a house with many doors and windows. Each door and window represents a potential entry point for an attacker. To keep the house secure, you need to lock all doors and windows, and also install a secure alarm system.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Web Security Basics</strong>: This refers to the fundamental principles of securing web applications, including encryption, secure protocols, and authentication.</li>\n  <li><strong>Common Web Vulnerabilities</strong>: These are weaknesses in web applications that can be exploited by attackers, such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting (XSS).</li>\n  <li><strong>Secure Coding Practices</strong>: These are guidelines for writing code that is secure and resistant to attacks, including input validation, error handling, and secure data storage.</li>\n  <li><strong>Web Security Frameworks</strong>: These are tools and libraries that help developers implement web security best practices, such as OWASP (Open Web Application Security Project) and Helmet.js.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task:</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Learn about web security basics</strong>: Read about encryption, secure protocols, and authentication, and how they work together to secure web applications.</li>\n  <li><strong>Understand common web vulnerabilities</strong>: Study SQL injection and XSS, and learn how to prevent them in your code.</li>\n  <li><strong>Implement secure coding practices</strong>: Write code that validates user input, handles errors securely, and stores sensitive data safely.</li>\n  <li><strong>Explore web security frameworks</strong>: Look into OWASP and Helmet.js, and learn how to use them to improve the security of your web applications.</li>\n  <li><strong>Build a simple web application</strong>: Create a basic web application that demonstrates secure coding practices and uses a web security framework.</li>\n  <li><strong>Test and debug</strong>: Test your web application for security vulnerabilities and debug any issues you find.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Here\'s a simple example of a web application that uses secure coding practices and a web security framework:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Secure Web Application&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to our secure web application&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;form&gt;\n      &lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"username\"</span>&gt;Username:&lt;/label&gt;\n      &lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"text\"</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"username\"</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"username\"</span> &gt;\n      &lt;label <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">for</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"password\"</span>&gt;Password:&lt;/label&gt;\n      &lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"password\"</span> id=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"password\"</span> name=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"password\"</span> &gt;\n      &lt;input <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">type</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"submit\"</span> value=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Login\"</span> &gt;\n    &lt;/form&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code uses a secure form to collect user input, and it validates the input using a web security framework (Helmet.js). The code also handles errors securely and stores sensitive data safely.</p>\n<h4>Deliverable:</h4>\n<p>Submit a simple web application that demonstrates secure coding practices and uses a web security framework. The application should have the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A secure form to collect user input</li>\n  <li>Input validation using a web security framework</li>\n  <li>Error handling that is secure and user-friendly</li>\n  <li>Sensitive data storage that is safe and secure</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid:</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Not validating user input</strong>: Failing to validate user input can lead to security vulnerabilities like SQL injection and XSS.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not handling errors securely</strong>: Failing to handle errors securely can lead to sensitive data exposure and other security issues.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not using a web security framework</strong>: Failing to use a web security framework can make it difficult to implement secure coding practices and protect against security vulnerabilities.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not testing and debugging</strong>: Failing to test and debug your web application can lead to security vulnerabilities and other issues.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips:</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Use a web security framework</strong>: A web security framework can help you implement secure coding practices and protect against security vulnerabilities.</li>\n  <li><strong>Validate user input</strong>: Validating user input can help prevent security vulnerabilities like SQL injection and XSS.</li>\n  <li><strong>Handle errors securely</strong>: Handling errors securely can help prevent sensitive data exposure and other security issues.</li>\n  <li><strong>Test and debug regularly</strong>: Testing and debugging regularly can help you identify and fix security vulnerabilities and other issues.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Quiz:</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>What is the primary goal of web security?</li>\n  <li>What is the difference between SQL injection and XSS?</li>\n  <li>What is a web security framework, and how can it help you secure your web application?</li>\n  <li>What are some common mistakes to avoid when implementing web security?</li>\n</ul>', 'Web Security Fundamentals', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:48:53');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(983, 996, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312a4f9f91d_1781606991.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\">&nbsp;</p></div><h3>Secure Coding Practices and Error Handling</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ll learn how to protect your web application from common security threats by implementing secure coding practices and error handling. This is a crucial skill for any web developer, as it ensures that your application is reliable, trustworthy, and complies with industry standards.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>Before we dive into the task, let\'s cover some essential concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Input Validation and Sanitization</strong>: This involves checking user input to ensure it\'s in the expected format and removing any malicious code. Think of it like a bouncer at a nightclub - they check your ID and make sure you\'re not carrying any prohibited items.</li>\n<li><strong>Error Handling and Logging</strong>: This is the process of catching and handling errors that occur during runtime, as well as logging them for future reference. It\'s like having a safety net that catches you if you fall.</li>\n<li><strong>Secure Password Storage</strong>: This involves storing passwords securely, so even if an attacker gains access to your database, they won\'t be able to reuse the passwords. It\'s like having a super-secure safe that only you can open.</li>\n<li><strong>Secure Session Management</strong>: This involves managing user sessions securely, so an attacker can\'t steal a user\'s session and gain unauthorized access. It\'s like having a secret handshake that only you and the user know.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<p>For this task, you\'ll build a simple web application that demonstrates secure coding practices and error handling. Follow these steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Set up a new project</strong>: Create a new project using your preferred framework (e.g., Express.js, Django). Set up a basic structure for your application, including a routes directory, a models directory, and a views directory.</li>\n<li><strong>Implement input validation and sanitization</strong>: In your routes directory, create a new file called <code>user.js</code>. In this file, create a function called <code>validateUserInput</code> that checks user input for the following:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> validateUserInput(req, res, next) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> username = req.body.username;\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> password = req.body.password;\n\n  <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Check if username and password are present</span>\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (!username || !password) {\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> res.status(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">400</span>).send({ error: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Username and password are required\'</span> });\n  }\n\n  <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Check if username and password are in the expected format</span>\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (typeof username !== <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'string\'</span> || typeof password !== <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'string\'</span>) {\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> res.status(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">400</span>).send({ error: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Username and password must be strings\'</span> });\n  }\n\n  <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Sanitize user input to remove any malicious code</span>\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> sanitizedUsername = sanitize(username);\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> sanitizedPassword = sanitize(password);\n\n  req.username = sanitizedUsername;\n  req.password = sanitizedPassword;\n\n  next();\n}\n\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Define the sanitize function</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> sanitize(input) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> input.replace(/&lt;|&gt;|&amp;/g, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'\'</span>);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function checks if the username and password are present, in the expected format, and removes any malicious code using the <code>sanitize</code> function.</p>\n</li><li><strong>Implement error handling and logging</strong>: In your routes directory, create a new file called <code>errorHandler.js</code>. In this file, create a function called <code>handleError</code> that catches and handles errors that occur during runtime. Log the error using a logging library (e.g., Winston).\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> handleError(err, req, res, next) {\n  console.error(err);\n  res.status(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">500</span>).send({ error: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Internal Server Error\'</span> });\n}\n\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Use the errorHandler middleware in your routes</span>\napp.use(errorHandler);\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function catches any errors that occur during runtime, logs them using the logging library, and returns a 500 Internal Server Error response.</p>\n</li><li><strong>Implement secure password storage</strong>: In your models directory, create a new file called <code>user.js</code>. In this file, define a function called <code>hashPassword</code> that hashes user passwords using a library like bcrypt.\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> bcrypt = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'bcrypt\'</span>);\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> hashPassword(password) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> salt = bcrypt.genSaltSync(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>);\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> hashedPassword = bcrypt.hashSync(password, salt);\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> hashedPassword;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function generates a salt using the bcrypt library and hashes the user password using the salt.</p>\n</li><li><strong>Implement secure session management</strong>: In your routes directory, create a new file called <code>session.js</code>. In this file, define a function called <code>createSession</code> that creates a secure session using a library like express-session.\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> session = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'express-session\'</span>);\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> createSession(req, res, next) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> session = req.session;\n  session.username = req.username;\n  session.password = req.password;\n  next();\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function creates a secure session using the express-session library and stores the username and password in the session.</p>\n</li><li><strong>Test your application</strong>: Use a tool like Postman to test your application. Send a POST request to the /login endpoint with a username and password. Verify that the application returns a 200 OK response and stores the user\'s session securely.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>For this task, you\'ll submit the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Your completed project with secure coding practices and error handling implemented</li>\n<li>A test report showing that your application returns a 200 OK response and stores the user\'s session securely</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<p>Be careful not to make the following mistakes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Not validating user input</strong>: Failing to validate user input can lead to security vulnerabilities like SQL injection and cross-site scripting (XSS).</li>\n<li><strong>Not sanitizing user input</strong>: Failing to sanitize user input can lead to security vulnerabilities like SQL injection and cross-site scripting (XSS).</li>\n<li><strong>Not handling errors properly</strong>: Failing to handle errors properly can lead to security vulnerabilities like information disclosure.</li>\n<li><strong>Not storing passwords securely</strong>: Failing to store passwords securely can lead to security vulnerabilities like password cracking.</li>\n<li><strong>Not managing sessions securely</strong>: Failing to manage sessions securely can lead to security vulnerabilities like session hijacking.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<p>Here are some additional tips to help you complete this task:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use a logging library</strong>: Use a logging library like Winston to log errors and other important events in your application.</li>\n<li><strong>Use a secure password storage library</strong>: Use a library like bcrypt to store passwords securely.</li>\n<li><strong>Use a secure session management library</strong>: Use a library like express-session to manage sessions securely.</li>\n</ul>', 'Secure Coding Practices and Error Handling', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:50:16'),
(984, 997, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: code review process</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=code+review+process\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/code+review+process\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/code+review+process/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/code+review+process/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=code+review+process\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you\'ll practice reviewing code for quality and testing it for errors. This is critical in real jobs because it ensures code works reliably and follows team standards.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n\n<h4>Code Review Process</h4>\n<p>Imagine you and a friend both write recipes for cookies. Code review is when you swap recipes and check for mistakes, like missing ingredients or unclear steps. It helps catch errors and share knowledge.</p>\n\n<h4>Code Testing Frameworks</h4>\n<p>These are like training wheels for your code. They let you create mini-tests to check if your code works. Popular ones include Jest for JavaScript and Mocha.</p>\n\n<h4>Unit Testing</h4>\n<p>This is testing one small part of your code at a time, like checking if a single math function adds numbers correctly. It\'s like testing a single gear in a machine.</p>\n\n<h4>Integration Testing</h4>\n<p>This tests how multiple parts work together, like checking if your website\'s login form works with the database. It\'s like testing the whole machine with all its gears.</p>\n\n<h4>Code Review Best Practices</h4>\n<p>Think of this as a doctor\'s checklist: look for typos, check if code follows style rules, test if it works, and ask \"would I understand this in 6 months?\"</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Clone sample project:</strong>\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script&gt;\n    fetch(\'https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//api.github.com/repos/example-project/zipball\')</span>\n    .then(response =&gt; response.blob())\n    .then(blob =&gt; console.log(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Downloaded:\'</span>, blob));\n  &lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n  <p>This downloads a sample project to review and test.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Review code for issues:</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Check for clear variable names (like <code>userAge</code> instead of <code>uA</code>)</li>\n    <li>Look for missing error handling</li>\n    <li>Verify code follows style guides</li>\n  </ul>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Write unit tests with Jest:</strong>\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">describe(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Math functions\'</span>, () =&gt; {\n  test(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'adds 1 + 2 to equal 3\'</span>, () =&gt; {\n    expect(add(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>)).toBe(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>);\n  });\n});</code></pre>\n  <p>This tests a simple math function. <code>expect()</code> checks if the result matches what we want.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Create integration test:</strong>\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">test(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'login form submits correctly\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">async</span> () =&gt; {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> response = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">await</span> fetch(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/login\'</span>, {\n    method: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'POST\'</span>,\n    body: JSON.stringify({ username: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'test\'</span>, password: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'123\'</span> })\n  });\n  expect(response.status).toBe(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">200</span>);\n});</code></pre>\n  <p>This tests if the login form works with the server.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Run tests and review:</strong>\n  <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">npx jest</code></pre>\n  <p>This command runs all your tests and shows which ones pass/fail.</p>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Written code review comments in a document</li>\n  <li>3+ unit tests with explanations</li>\n  <li>1 integration test</li>\n  <li>Summary of changes you\'d make to improve the code</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Testing only \"happy path\" scenarios - always check edge cases (like empty inputs)</li>\n  <li>Reviewing code too quickly without reading every line</li>\n  <li>Writing tests that depend on specific data values</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Use automated tools:</strong> Linters like ESLint can catch basic style issues</li>\n  <li><strong>Test like a user:</strong> Think about how real people might use your code</li>\n  <li><strong>Review with fresh eyes:</strong> Take a 15-minute break between writing and reviewing code</li>\n</ul>', 'Code Review and Testing', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(985, 998, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312a9d97c90_1781607069.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you will learn how to protect a web server from attacks by configuring security settings. You\'ll set up encryption, firewalls, and access rules that keep user data safe and prevent unauthorized access. These skills are essential for any job that involves deploying websites or web apps.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>Secure Server Configuration</h4><p>Think of a server like a house. Just as you lock doors and reinforce windows, you must configure server software (like Nginx or Apache) with strong settings to prevent hackers from exploiting weaknesses.</p>\n\n<h4>Encryption Methods</h4><p>Encryption is like a secret language between a user\'s browser and your server. When you enable HTTPS, data becomes unreadable to anyone intercepting it (like someone eavesdropping on a phone call).</p>\n\n<h4>Firewall Setup</h4><p>A firewall acts like a bouncer at a club. It checks every incoming request and blocks suspicious traffic. For example, it can stop brute-force attacks where hackers guess login passwords thousands of times per second.</p>\n\n<h4>Access Control Lists (ACLs)</h4><p>ACLs are like a guest list for specific parts of your website. You can allow only certain IP addresses to access admin pages, or block entire regions of the world if they\'re not relevant to your users.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Configure secure server settings</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;blockquote&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># Example Nginx config to disable insecure HTTP methods</span>\nserver {\n    listen <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">80</span>;\n    server_name example.com;\n\n    <span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># Block methods like PUT and DELETE that aren\'t needed</span>\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> ($request_method !~ ^(GET|HEAD|POST)$ ) {\n        <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">405</span>;\n    }\n}\n&lt;/blockquote&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>This configuration restricts your server to only allow basic HTTP methods, reducing attack surfaces.</p>\n\n</li><li><strong>Enable HTTPS encryption</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;blockquote&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># Example Apache SSL configuration</span>\n&lt;VirtualHost *:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">443</span>&gt;\n    ServerName example.com\n    SSLEngine on\n    SSLCertificateFile <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"/etc/ssl/certs/example.com.crt\"</span>\n    SSLCertificateKeyFile <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"/etc/ssl/private/example.com.key\"</span>\n&lt;/VirtualHost&gt;\n&lt;/blockquote&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>This makes all data between users and your server encrypted. In real projects, you\'d get free SSL certificates from Let\'s Encrypt.</p>\n\n</li><li><strong>Set up a firewall with UFW</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;blockquote&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># Allow only HTTP, HTTPS, and SSH traffic</span>\nsudo ufw allow <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">80</span>/tcp\nsudo ufw allow <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">443</span>/tcp\nsudo ufw allow <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">22</span>/tcp\n\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># Block all other traffic</span>\nsudo ufw default deny incoming\nsudo ufw enable\n&lt;/blockquote&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>This command sequence creates a firewall that only permits essential traffic while blocking everything else by default.</p>\n\n</li><li><strong>Create access control rules</strong>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;blockquote&gt;\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># Example .htaccess file to restrict access by IP</span>\n&lt;Limit GET POST&gt;\n    Order Deny,Allow\n    Deny <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> all\n    Allow <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">203.0</span>.<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">113.45</span>\n    Allow <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">from</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">198.51</span>.<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">100.0</span>/<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">24</span>\n&lt;/Limit&gt;\n&lt;/blockquote&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>This file blocks all users except those from specific IP addresses, protecting sensitive admin areas.</p>\n</li></ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit four files showing your work:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Server configuration file (nginx.conf or apache2.conf)</li>\n<li>SSL configuration snippet</li>\n<li>Firewall rules output from <code>sudo ufw status verbose</code></li>\n<li>.htaccess file with access control rules</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Forgetting to test configurations before applying them live - always use <code>nginx -t</code> or <code>apachectl configtest</code> first</li>\n<li>Using default passwords for admin interfaces instead of generating strong, unique ones</li>\n<li>Leaving debug modes enabled in production servers</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use Let\'s Encrypt</strong> for free, automatic SSL certificates instead of self-signed ones</li>\n<li><strong>Monitor logs regularly</strong> with <code>tail -f /var/log/auth.log</code> to catch suspicious activity</li>\n<li><strong>Keep software updated</strong> - use <code>sudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt upgrade</code> weekly to patch security vulnerabilities</li>\n</ul>', 'Secure Deployment Strategies', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:51:21'),
(986, 999, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312adf5da17_1781607135.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Web Application Security Best Practices</h3>\n<p>Today, you will learn how to secure your web application from common attacks. You will implement input validation and sanitization, CSRF protection, SQL injection prevention, and cross-site scripting defense.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>Before we dive into the task, let\'s cover some essential concepts.</p>\n<h5>Input Validation and Sanitization</h5>\n<p>Input validation is the process of checking user input to ensure it meets the expected format. Think of it like a bouncer at a nightclub. The bouncer checks your ID and attire to make sure you fit the club\'s criteria. If you don\'t meet the requirements, you won\'t get in.</p>\n<p>Sanitization is the process of removing or modifying user input to prevent any malicious code from being executed. It\'s like the bouncer scanning your bag for any prohibited items before you enter the club.</p>\n<h5>CSRF Protection</h5>\n<p>CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) is an attack where an attacker tricks a user into performing an action on a web application without their consent. For example, an attacker might create a fake login form on a malicious website that, when submitted, sends a request to your web application to transfer funds to the attacker\'s account.</p>\n<p>CSRF protection involves including a token in the form that the user submits, which the web application verifies before processing the request.</p>\n<h5>SQL Injection Prevention</h5>\n<p>SQL injection is an attack where an attacker injects malicious SQL code into a web application\'s database queries. This can allow the attacker to access sensitive data or modify the database.</p>\n<p>SQL injection prevention involves parameterizing database queries to prevent attackers from injecting malicious code.</p>\n<h5>Cross-Site Scripting Defense</h5>\n<p>Cross-site scripting (XSS) is an attack where an attacker injects malicious JavaScript code into a web application, which is then executed by the user\'s browser. This can allow the attacker to steal user data or take control of the user\'s session.</p>\n<p>Cross-site scripting defense involves encoding user input to prevent malicious code from being executed.</p>\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new PHP file called <code>security.php</code> in your project directory.</li>\n<li>In <code>security.php</code>, create a simple form that asks the user for their name and email address.</li>\n<li>Add input validation and sanitization to the form using the <code>filter_var</code> function. For example:<code>\n&lt;?php\n$name = filter_var($_POST[\'name\'], FILTER_SANITIZE_STRING);\n$email = filter_var($_POST[\'email\'], FILTER_SANITIZE_EMAIL);\n?&gt;\n</code>\n<p>This code uses the <code>filter_var</code> function to sanitize the user input. The <code>FILTER_SANITIZE_STRING</code> filter removes any HTML tags from the input, while the <code>FILTER_SANITIZE_EMAIL</code> filter removes any characters that are not part of a valid email address.</p>\n</li><li>Add CSRF protection to the form using a token. You can use the <code>session_id</code> function to generate a token and store it in the user\'s session.<code>\n&lt;?php\nsession_start();\n$token = session_id();\n?&gt;\n</code>\n<p>This code starts a new session and generates a token using the <code>session_id</code> function.</p>\n</li><li>Add SQL injection prevention to the form by parameterizing the database query. For example:<code>\n&lt;?php\n$stmt = $pdo-&gt;prepare(\'SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = ? AND email = ?\');\n$stmt-&gt;execute([$name, $email]);\n?&gt;\n</code>\n<p>This code uses a prepared statement to parameterize the database query. The <code>?</code> placeholders are replaced with the actual values from the user input.</p>\n</li><li>Add cross-site scripting defense to the form by encoding the user input using the <code>htmlspecialchars</code> function.<code>\n&lt;?php\necho htmlspecialchars($name);\necho htmlspecialchars($email);\n?&gt;\n</code>\n<p>This code uses the <code>htmlspecialchars</code> function to encode the user input, preventing any malicious code from being executed.</p>\n</li><li>Test the form to ensure that input validation and sanitization, CSRF protection, SQL injection prevention, and cross-site scripting defense are working correctly.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>Submit the <code>security.php</code> file and a brief description of how you implemented input validation and sanitization, CSRF protection, SQL injection prevention, and cross-site scripting defense.</p>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Not validating user input before processing it.</li>\n<li>Not sanitizing user input to prevent malicious code from being executed.</li>\n<li>Not including a token in the form to prevent CSRF attacks.</li>\n<li>Not parameterizing database queries to prevent SQL injection attacks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Use a library like <code>filter</code> to simplify input validation and sanitization.</li>\n<li>Use a library like <code>csrf</code> to simplify CSRF protection.</li>\n<li>Use a library like <code>pdo</code> to simplify database interactions and prevent SQL injection attacks.</li>\n</ul>', 'Web Application Security Best Practices', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:52:27'),
(987, 1000, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312b4b57737_1781607243.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Introduction to Project Planning</h3>\n<p>In today\'s task, you\'ll learn the essential steps of project planning. This is a crucial skill in web development, as it helps you manage projects efficiently, meet deadlines, and ensure that your projects meet the required standards. As a beginner, mastering project planning will make you a more valuable asset to any development team.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>Before we dive into the task, let\'s cover some essential concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Project Requirements Gathering</strong>: This is the process of collecting and documenting the needs and expectations of all stakeholders involved in the project.</li>\n  <li><strong>Project Scope Definition</strong>: This is the process of defining the boundaries and limitations of the project, including what is included and excluded.</li>\n  <li><strong>Project Timeline Estimation</strong>: This is the process of estimating the time required to complete each task and the entire project.</li>\n  <li><strong>Project Resource Allocation</strong>: This is the process of assigning resources (people, equipment, materials) to each task and ensuring that they have the necessary skills and expertise.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let\'s break down each concept:</p>\n<h4>Project Requirements Gathering</h4>\n<p>Imagine you\'re planning a party. You need to know how many guests are coming, what type of food and drinks they\'ll want, and what kind of music they\'ll enjoy. This is similar to gathering requirements in a project. You need to understand the needs and expectations of all stakeholders, including the project sponsor, team members, and end-users.</p>\n<h4>Project Scope Definition</h4>\n<p>Going back to the party example, let\'s say you decide to have a BBQ. You need to define what\'s included in the BBQ, such as the type of food, drinks, and decorations. You also need to exclude things that aren\'t part of the BBQ, like a DJ or a photo booth. This is similar to defining the scope of a project. You need to clearly define what\'s included and excluded to avoid scope creep.</p>\n<h4>Project Timeline Estimation</h4>\n<p>Now, let\'s estimate how long it\'ll take to prepare for the party. You need to consider the time it takes to plan the menu, order supplies, and set up the decorations. This is similar to estimating the time required for each task in a project. You need to break down the project into smaller tasks, estimate the time required for each task, and add up the total time required for the entire project.</p>\n<h4>Project Resource Allocation</h4>\n<p>Finally, let\'s assign resources to the party planning tasks. You might need to allocate time and money for supplies, decorations, and food. You also need to assign tasks to team members, such as planning the menu, ordering supplies, and setting up the decorations. This is similar to allocating resources in a project. You need to assign the right resources to each task and ensure that they have the necessary skills and expertise.</p>\n<h3>Step-by-Step Task</h3>\n<p>Now that we\'ve covered the essential concepts, let\'s move on to the task. You\'ll be planning a fictional project to build a simple website for a local business. Follow these steps:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Gather project requirements</strong>: Create a list of project requirements, including the project scope, timeline, and resources. For example:<code>\n    &lt;a href=\'project_requirements.txt\'&gt;Project Requirements&lt;/a&gt;\n  </code><p>This file should contain the following information:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Project name: Local Business Website</li>\n  <li>Project scope: Build a simple website with a homepage, about page, and contact page</li>\n  <li>Project timeline: 2 weeks</li>\n  <li>Resources: 1 developer, 1 designer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Save this file as project_requirements.txt.</p>\n  </li><li><strong>Define project scope</strong>: Create a document that outlines the project scope, including what\'s included and excluded. For example:<code>\n    &lt;a href=\'project_scope.txt\'&gt;Project Scope&lt;/a&gt;\n  </code><p>This file should contain the following information:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Project scope: The project scope includes building a simple website with a homepage, about page, and contact page. The project excludes building a blog or e-commerce functionality.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Save this file as project_scope.txt.</p>\n  </li><li><strong>Estimate project timeline</strong>: Create a Gantt chart or a table that outlines the project timeline, including the start and end dates for each task. For example:<code>\n    &lt;a href=\'project_timeline.xlsx\'&gt;Project Timeline&lt;/a&gt;\n  </code><p>This file should contain the following information:</p>\n<table>\n  <tbody><tr>\n    <th>Task</th>\n    <th>Start Date</th>\n    <th>End Date</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Plan project scope</td>\n    <td>Monday</td>\n    <td>Wednesday</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Design website</td>\n    <td>Thursday</td>\n    <td>Friday</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Develop website</td>\n    <td>Monday</td>\n    <td>Wednesday</td>\n  </tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Save this file as project_timeline.xlsx.</p>\n  </li><li><strong>Allocate project resources</strong>: Create a document that outlines the project resources, including the team members and their roles. For example:<code>\n    &lt;a href=\'project_resources.txt\'&gt;Project Resources&lt;/a&gt;\n  </code><p>This file should contain the following information:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Team members:</li>\n  <ul>\n    <li>John (developer)</li>\n    <li>Jane (designer)</li>\n  </ul>\n  <li>Roles:</li>\n  <ul>\n    <li>John: Develop website</li>\n    <li>Jane: Design website</li>\n  </ul>\n</ul>\n<p>Save this file as project_resources.txt.</p>\n</li></ol>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Your deliverable for today\'s task is to submit the following files:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>project_requirements.txt</li>\n  <li>project_scope.txt</li>\n  <li>project_timeline.xlsx</li>\n  <li>project_resources.txt</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Not gathering enough project requirements</strong>: Make sure to gather all necessary project requirements, including the project scope, timeline, and resources.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not defining the project scope clearly</strong>: Make sure to clearly define what\'s included and excluded in the project scope.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not estimating the project timeline accurately</strong>: Make sure to estimate the project timeline accurately, including the start and end dates for each task.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not allocating project resources effectively</strong>: Make sure to allocate project resources effectively, including team members and their roles.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Use a project management tool</strong>: Use a project management tool, such as Asana or Trello, to help you manage your project.</li>\n  <li><strong>Break down large tasks into smaller ones</strong>: Break down large tasks into smaller ones to make them more manageable.</li>\n  <li><strong>Communicate with your team regularly</strong>: Communicate with your team regularly to ensure that everyone is on the same page.</li>\n</ul>', 'Introduction to Project Planning', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:54:18'),
(988, 1001, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312bb86c240_1781607352.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Agile Development Methodologies</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ll learn about four essential Agile development methodologies: Scrum Framework, Kanban Board, Lean Software Development, and Extreme Programming. These methodologies will help you work more efficiently and effectively with your team.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need:</h4>\n<p>Before we dive into the task, let\'s cover the key concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Agile Development</strong>: Agile development is a project management approach that emphasizes flexibility, collaboration, and rapid delivery. It\'s an iterative and incremental process that helps teams respond to change quickly.</li>\n<li><strong>Scrum Framework</strong>: Scrum is a framework for implementing Agile development. It\'s based on three roles: Product Owner, Scrum Master, and Development Team. Scrum emphasizes teamwork, accountability, and iterative progress toward well-defined goals.</li>\n<li><strong>Kanban Board</strong>: A Kanban board is a visual representation of your work process. It helps you track work items as they move through different stages. Kanban emphasizes continuous improvement, limiting work in progress, and delivering value to customers.</li>\n<li><strong>Lean Software Development</strong>: Lean software development is a methodology that aims to minimize waste and maximize value. It\'s based on the principles of Lean manufacturing and emphasizes continuous improvement, customer focus, and rapid delivery.</li>\n<li><strong>Extreme Programming</strong> (XP): XP is a software development methodology that emphasizes technical practices such as pair programming, continuous integration, and testing. It\'s designed to deliver high-quality software quickly and efficiently.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task:</h4>\n<p>Today, you\'ll create a Kanban board for a fictional project. Follow these steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Define the project scope</strong>: Identify the project\'s goals, objectives, and deliverables. Create a list of tasks that need to be completed.</li>\n<li><strong>Create a Kanban board</strong>: Use a tool like Trello, Asana, or Jira to create a Kanban board. Set up columns for different stages, such as To-Do, In Progress, and Done.</li>\n<li><strong>Add tasks to the board</strong>: Add the tasks you defined in step 1 to the Kanban board. Assign each task to a column based on its current status.</li>\n<li><strong>Configure card details</strong>: For each task, add a description, due date, and assignee. Use the tool\'s built-in features to customize the card details.</li>\n<li><strong>Set up workflows</strong>: Define the workflow for each column. For example, you can set up a workflow to move tasks from To-Do to In Progress when they\'re assigned to a team member.</li>\n<li><strong>Visualize the board\n<p>Use the tool\'s built-in features to customize the board\'s appearance. Add labels, colors, and images to make the board more visually appealing.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;a href=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'kanban-board.html\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'kanban-board\'</span>&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;Project Scope&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Task <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>: Define project goals&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Task <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>: Create project plan&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Task <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>: Assign team members&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/a&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code snippet creates a basic Kanban board with three tasks. You can customize the board further using the tool\'s features.</p>\n</strong></li><li><strong><strong>Save and share the board</strong>: Save the Kanban board and share it with your team. Use the tool\'s collaboration features to assign tasks and track progress.</strong></li><strong>\n</strong></ol><strong>\n<h4>Deliverable:</h4>\n<p>Submit the Kanban board you created in step 6. Make sure it includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A clear project scope</li>\n<li>A well-organized Kanban board with columns and tasks</li>\n<li>Customized card details for each task</li>\n<li>A visually appealing board with labels, colors, and images</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Not defining a clear project scope</strong>: Make sure you have a clear understanding of the project\'s goals and objectives before creating the Kanban board.</li>\n<li><strong>Not customizing card details</strong>: Add descriptions, due dates, and assignees to each task to ensure everyone knows what needs to be done.</li>\n<li><strong>Not setting up workflows</strong>: Define the workflow for each column to ensure tasks are moving through the board efficiently.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use labels and colors to differentiate tasks</strong>: Use labels and colors to highlight important tasks or tasks that require special attention.</li>\n<li><strong>Set up notifications for task assignments</strong>: Set up notifications to alert team members when they\'re assigned a task or when a task is due.</li>\n<li><strong>Regularly review and update the board</strong>: Regularly review the board to ensure it\'s up-to-date and accurate. Update the board as tasks are completed or new tasks are added.</li>\n</ul></strong>', 'Agile Development Methodologies', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:56:03');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(989, 1002, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312c1be13be_1781607451.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Version Control with Git</h3>\n<p>Welcome to Day 68 of our web development internship! Today we\'re going to explore the basics of version control using Git. This is a crucial skill for any web developer, as it allows you to track changes to your code, collaborate with others, and even roll back to previous versions if something goes wrong.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need:</h3>\n<p>Before we dive into the task, let\'s cover some essential concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Git</strong>: Git is a version control system that helps you track changes to your code over time. It\'s like a digital notebook that keeps a record of every change you make.</li>\n  <li><strong>Repository</strong> (or <strong>repo</strong>): A repository is a central location where all your project files are stored. Think of it like a file cabinet where you keep all your project files organized.</li>\n  <li><strong>Branch</strong>: A branch is like a separate folder within your repository. You can create different branches for different features or versions of your project.</li>\n  <li><strong>Merge</strong>: When you\'re working on a branch, you can merge it with the main branch (called <strong>master</strong>) to incorporate your changes into the main project.</li>\n  <li><strong>Remote repository</strong>: A remote repository is a copy of your repository stored on a server, like GitHub or GitLab. This allows you to collaborate with others and access your project from anywhere.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Step-by-step task:</h3>\n<p>Today\'s task is to create a simple Git repository, create a branch, make some changes, and then merge the branch back into the main branch. Here are the steps:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Install Git</strong>: If you haven\'t already, install Git on your computer. You can download it from the official Git website.</li>\n  <li><strong>Create a new repository</strong>: Open your terminal or command prompt and navigate to the directory where you want to create your new repository. Run the following command to create a new repository:<code>git add .</code>\n  <p>This will create a new Git repository in the current directory.</p>\n  </li><li><strong>Initialize the repository</strong>: Run the following command to initialize the repository:<code>git add . &amp;&amp; git commit -m \"Initial commit\"</code>\n  <p>This will create a new commit with a message \"Initial commit\".</p>\n  </li><li><strong>Create a new branch</strong>: Run the following command to create a new branch called \"feature/new-feature\":<code>git branch feature/new-feature</code>\n  <p>This will create a new branch called \"feature/new-feature\".</p></li>\n  <li><strong>Switch to the new branch</strong>: Run the following command to switch to the new branch:<code>git checkout feature/new-feature</code>\n  <p>This will switch you to the new branch.</p></li>\n  <li><strong>Make some changes</strong>: Make some changes to a file in your repository, like changing a word in a text file.</li>\n  <li><strong>Commit the changes</strong>: Run the following command to commit the changes:<code>git add . &amp;&amp; git commit -m \"Made some changes\"</code>\n  <p>This will create a new commit with a message \"Made some changes\".</p></li>\n  <li><strong>Merge the branch</strong>: Run the following command to merge the branch back into the main branch:<code>git checkout master &amp;&amp; git merge feature/new-feature</code>\n  <p>This will merge the changes from the branch into the main branch.</p></li>\n  <li><strong>Push the changes to a remote repository</strong>: Run the following command to push the changes to a remote repository like GitHub:<code>git add . &amp;&amp; git commit -m \"Merged changes\" &amp;&amp; git push origin master</code>\n  <p>This will push the changes to the remote repository.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable:</h3>\n<p>Your deliverable for today is to create a simple Git repository, create a branch, make some changes, and then merge the branch back into the main branch. You should also push the changes to a remote repository like GitHub.</p>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid:</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Don\'t forget to commit your changes</em>: Make sure to commit your changes regularly to avoid losing your work.</li>\n  <li><em>Don\'t merge a branch into the wrong branch</em>: Make sure to merge a branch into the correct branch to avoid overwriting changes.</li>\n  <li><em>Don\'t forget to push your changes to a remote repository</em>: Make sure to push your changes to a remote repository to collaborate with others.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips:</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><blockquote>Use <code>git status</code> to check the status of your repository</blockquote></li>\n  <li><blockquote>Use <code>git log</code> to view the commit history of your repository</blockquote></li>\n  <li><blockquote>Use <code>git branch -a</code> to view all branches in your repository</blockquote></li>\n</ul>', 'Version Control with Git', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 10:57:43'),
(990, 1003, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312c5b5c0dc_1781607515.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\n</div></div><h3>Deployment Strategies and Options</h3>\n<p>Today, you will learn about different deployment strategies and options used in web development. You will understand the concepts of cloud deployment, on-premise deployment, containerization with Docker, and serverless architecture. This knowledge will be essential in your future web development projects, as you will be able to choose the best deployment strategy for your application.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>Cloud Deployment</h4>\n<p>Cloud deployment refers to the practice of hosting and running applications on a cloud computing platform. Think of it like renting a house. Instead of buying a house and maintaining it, you rent a house and the landlord takes care of everything. In cloud deployment, you rent virtual servers and storage from a cloud provider, and they handle the maintenance and scaling of your application.</p>\n<h4>On-Premise Deployment</h4>\n<p>On-premise deployment refers to the practice of hosting and running applications on a physical server or a local network. Think of it like owning a house. You buy the house, maintain it, and are responsible for everything. In on-premise deployment, you own the physical servers and are responsible for their maintenance, updates, and scaling.</p>\n<h4>Containerization with Docker</h4>\n<p>Containerization with Docker is a way to package and deploy applications in a lightweight and portable way. Think of it like packing a suitcase. You put all your belongings in a suitcase, and it becomes a self-contained unit that can be easily moved from one place to another. In containerization, you create a container that includes the application code, dependencies, and configuration, and it can be easily deployed and scaled.</p>\n<h4>Serverless Architecture</h4>\n<p>Serverless architecture refers to the practice of building applications without managing servers. Think of it like a restaurant. You don\'t own the kitchen or the servers, but you can still serve food to customers. In serverless architecture, you write code that runs on a cloud provider\'s servers, and you only pay for the resources you use.</p>\n<h3>Step-by-Step Task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new directory for your project and navigate to it in your terminal or command prompt.</li>\n<li>Initialize a new Node.js project using the command <code>npm init</code>.</li>\n<li>Create a new file called <code>dockerfile</code> and add the following code:<code>\nFROM node:14\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package*.json ./\nRUN npm install\nCOPY . .\nRUN npm run build\nEXPOSE 3000\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n</code>\n<p>This Dockerfile creates a new image based on the Node.js 14 image, sets the working directory to /app, copies the package.json file, installs the dependencies, copies the application code, runs the build script, exposes port 3000, and sets the default command to run the server.js file.</p>\n</li><li>Build the Docker image using the command <code>docker build -t my-app</code>.</li>\n<li>Run the Docker container using the command <code>docker run -p 3000:3000 my-app</code>.</li>\n<li>Create a new file called <code>serverless.yml</code> and add the following code:<code>\nservice:\n  name: my-app\n\nprovider:\n  name: aws\n  runtime: nodejs14\n\nfunctions:\n  hello:\n    handler: handler.hello\n    events:\n      - http:\n          path: hello\n          method: get\n</code>\n<p>This serverless.yml file defines a new service called my-app, sets the provider to AWS and the runtime to Node.js 14, and defines a new function called hello that handles GET requests to the /hello path.</p></li>\n<li>Deploy the serverless function using the command <code>serverless deploy</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>You should have a working Docker container and a deployed serverless function. You should also have a clear understanding of the concepts of cloud deployment, on-premise deployment, containerization with Docker, and serverless architecture.</p>\n<h3>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Not properly configuring the Dockerfile to match the application code.</li>\n<li>Not exposing the correct port in the Dockerfile.</li>\n<li>Not properly configuring the serverless.yml file to match the application code.</li>\n<li>Not deploying the serverless function correctly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro Tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use a consistent naming convention for your Docker images and containers.</li>\n<li>Use environment variables to configure your application code.</li>\n<li>Use a CI/CD pipeline to automate the deployment of your application.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><br></p>', 'Deployment Strategies and Options', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 12:41:28'),
(991, 1004, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CI/CD pipeline</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CI%2FCD+pipeline\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CI%2FCD+pipeline\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CI%2FCD+pipeline/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CI%2FCD+pipeline/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CI%2FCD+pipeline\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Introduction to Continuous Integration and Delivery</h3>\n<p>In this task, you\'ll learn about the importance of Continuous Integration and Delivery in web development. You\'ll understand how to set up a CI/CD pipeline, automate testing and building, and deploy your application continuously. This is a crucial skill to have in any web development job, as it ensures that your code is always in a deployable state and reduces the risk of errors.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>A CI/CD pipeline is a series of automated processes that help you build, test, and deploy your application. It\'s like a production line where your code is the product, and the pipeline is the process that takes it from raw material to finished product.</p>\n<p>Automated testing is a crucial part of the pipeline. It\'s a way to ensure that your code works as expected by running a series of tests. Think of it like a quality control check at a factory. If your code passes the test, it\'s good to go. If it fails, you need to fix it before it can be deployed.</p>\n<p>Continuous deployment is the process of deploying your application to production as soon as it\'s built and tested. It\'s like a conveyor belt that takes your product from the production line to the customer\'s hands.</p>\n<p>Continuous monitoring is the process of keeping an eye on your application in production. It\'s like a quality control team that checks on your product after it\'s been delivered to the customer.</p>\n<p>CI/CD tools and services are software that help you set up and manage your pipeline. They\'re like the machines that run the production line.</p>\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Create a new GitHub repository</strong> for your project. This will be the central location for all your code.</li>\n  <li><strong>Install Git</strong> on your local machine if you haven\'t already. Git is a version control system that helps you keep track of changes to your code.</li>\n  <li><strong>Initialize a new Git repository</strong> in your project directory. This will create a new Git repository for your project.</li>\n  <li><strong>Write a simple script</strong> that uses the Git command-line interface to perform the following actions:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\ngit add .\ngit commit -m <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">&quot;Initial commit&quot;</span>\ngit push origin master\n</code></pre>\n  This script will add all files in the current directory to the Git repository, commit the changes with a message, and push the changes to the remote repository.</li>\n  <li><strong>Set up a CI/CD pipeline</strong> using a tool like Jenkins or Travis CI. This will automate the process of building, testing, and deploying your application.</li>\n  <li><strong>Write a simple build script</strong> that uses a tool like Maven or Gradle to build your application. This script will be used by the CI/CD pipeline to build your application.</li>\n  <li><strong>Write a simple test script</strong> that uses a testing framework like JUnit or PyUnit to test your application. This script will be used by the CI/CD pipeline to test your application.</li>\n  <li><strong>Set up continuous deployment</strong> using a tool like Jenkins or Travis CI. This will automatically deploy your application to production after it\'s built and tested.</li>\n  <li><strong>Set up continuous monitoring</strong> using a tool like New Relic or Datadog. This will keep an eye on your application in production and alert you to any issues.</li>\n  <li><strong>Test your CI/CD pipeline</strong> by making changes to your code and pushing them to the remote repository. The pipeline should automatically build, test, and deploy your application.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>You should have a working CI/CD pipeline that automates the process of building, testing, and deploying your application. You should also have continuous monitoring set up to keep an eye on your application in production.</p>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Not setting up a CI/CD pipeline</strong>: This can lead to errors and delays in deployment.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not automating testing</strong>: This can lead to errors and bugs in production.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not setting up continuous monitoring</strong>: This can lead to issues going undetected in production.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not testing the CI/CD pipeline</strong>: This can lead to errors and delays in deployment.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Use a CI/CD tool that integrates with your version control system</strong>. This will make it easier to set up and manage your pipeline.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use a build tool that automates the process of building your application</strong>. This will save you time and reduce errors.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use a testing framework that automates the process of testing your application</strong>. This will save you time and reduce errors.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Quiz</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>\n    <p>What is the purpose of a CI/CD pipeline?</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>A. To automate the process of building, testing, and deploying an application.</li>\n      <li>B. To reduce the risk of errors in production.</li>\n      <li>C. To improve the quality of an application.</li>\n      <li>D. To increase the speed of deployment.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Correct answer: A</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p>What is the purpose of automated testing?</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>A. To ensure that an application works as expected.</li>\n      <li>B. To reduce the risk of errors in production.</li>\n      <li>C. To improve the quality of an application.</li>\n      <li>D. To increase the speed of deployment.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Correct answer: A</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p>What is the purpose of continuous deployment?</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>A. To automate the process of building, testing, and deploying an application.</li>\n      <li>B. To reduce the risk of errors in production.</li>\n      <li>C. To improve the quality of an application.</li>\n      <li>D. To increase the speed of deployment.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Correct answer: D</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p>What is the purpose of continuous monitoring?</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>A. To ensure that an application works as expected.</li>\n      <li>B. To reduce the risk of errors in production.</li>\n      <li>C. To improve the quality of an application.</li>\n      <li>D. To keep an eye on an application in production.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Correct answer: D</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p>What is the purpose of a CI/CD tool?</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>A. To automate the process of building, testing, and deploying an application.</li>\n      <li>B. To reduce the risk of errors in production.</li>\n      <li>C. To improve the quality of an application.</li>\n      <li>D. To set up and manage a CI/CD pipeline.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Correct answer: D</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p>What is the purpose of a build tool?</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>A. To automate the process of building an application.</li>\n      <li>B. To reduce the risk of errors in production.</li>\n      <li>C. To improve the quality of an application.</li>\n      <li>D. To increase the speed of deployment.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Correct answer: A</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p>What is the purpose of a testing framework?</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>A. To automate the process of testing an application.</li>\n      <li>B. To reduce the risk of errors in production.</li>\n      <li>C. To improve the quality of an application.</li>\n      <li>D. To increase the speed of deployment.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Correct answer: A</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p>What is the purpose of continuous integration?</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>A. To automate the process of building, testing, and deploying an application.</li>\n      <li>B. To reduce the risk of errors in production.</li>\n      <li>C. To improve the quality of an application.</li>\n      <li>D. To integrate code changes frequently.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Correct answer: D</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p>What is the purpose of continuous delivery?</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>A. To automate the process of building, testing, and deploying an application.</li>\n      <li>B. To reduce the risk of errors in production.</li>\n      <li>C. To improve the quality of an application.</li>\n      <li>D. To deliver code changes frequently.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Correct answer: D</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p>What is the purpose of continuous monitoring?</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>A. To ensure that an application works as expected.</li>\n      <li>B. To reduce the risk of errors in production.</li>\n      <li>C. To improve the quality of an application.</li>\n      <li>D. To keep an eye on an application in production.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Correct answer: D</p>\n  </li>\n</ul>', 'Introduction to Continuous Integration and Delivery', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(992, 1005, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\"><img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312cbdee7cd_1781607613.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you will learn how to plan a web development project from start to finish. This skill is critical for real jobs because it helps teams stay organized, meet deadlines, and build exactly what users need.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n\n<h4>Project Goal and Scope</h4>\n<p><strong>Goal</strong> = What you want to achieve (e.g., \"Build a website to sell handmade soap\"). <strong>Scope</strong> = What is and isn\'t included (e.g., \"Include product pages but no payment system\").</p>\n<p>Analogy: Planning a road trip. Your goal is \"Drive to the beach\". Scope = \"Pack swimsuits but not hiking gear\".</p>\n\n<h4>Project Requirements</h4>\n<p>Specific features or rules the project must follow. Example: \"Users must be able to filter products by price\".</p>\n<p>Analogy: A shopping list for your road trip - \"Don’t forget sunscreen\".</p>\n\n<h4>Project Stakeholders</h4>\n<p>People who care about the project’s success: clients, users, managers, developers.</p>\n\n<h4>Timeline and Budget</h4>\n<p><strong>Timeline</strong> = Deadlines for each task. <strong>Budget</strong> = Money available for development.</p>\n<p>Analogy: Planning a party. Timeline: \"Decorate by 5pm\". Budget: $200 for food.</p>\n\n<h4>Roles and Responsibilities</h4>\n<p>Who does what on the team. Example: \"Designer creates buttons, Developer builds the code\".</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Create a project plan document</strong>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;h1&gt;My Project Plan&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;h2&gt;Goal <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">and</span> Scope&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Goal: Build a website to sell handmade soap&lt;br&gt;\nScope: Include product pages, shopping cart, but no payment system&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>This HTML structure helps organize your thinking about what the project will achieve.</p>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Identify stakeholders</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Client: Sarah (soap maker)</li>\n      <li>Users: People buying soap</li>\n      <li>Developer: You!</li>\n      <li>Designer: Person who creates the website’s look</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Write requirements</strong>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;h2&gt;Requirements&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Users can view product photos&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Users can add items to a shopping cart&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Mobile-friendly design&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Create a timeline</strong>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;h2&gt;Timeline&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;table&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Task&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Deadline&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Design homepage&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;March <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Build shopping cart&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;March <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">15</span>&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n&lt;/table&gt;</code></pre>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Assign roles</strong>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;h2&gt;Team Roles&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Project Manager: Oversees everything&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Frontend Developer: Builds the website’s look&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Backend Developer: Makes the shopping cart work&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Create and submit a text file or HTML document containing:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Project goal and scope</li>\n  <li>List of stakeholders</li>\n  <li>At least 3 requirements</li>\n  <li>Timeline with 2+ deadlines</li>\n  <li>Team roles with responsibilities</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Writing vague goals like \"Make a good website\" - always be specific</li>\n  <li>Forgetting to include important stakeholders like end-users</li>\n  <li>Creating unrealistic timelines (building a website takes time!)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<blockquote>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Interview stakeholders to understand their needs</li>\n    <li>Use tools like Trello or Google Sheets to track timelines</li>\n    <li>Document everything in plain text files for easy sharing</li>\n  </ul>\n</blockquote>', 'Project Planning and Requirements', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 11:00:22'),
(993, 1006, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312d1fc3648_1781607711.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you will learn how professional teams build websites and apps using Agile methods. You\'ll create a project plan for a fictional app using Scrum and Kanban tools. This skill is critical for working in tech teams and delivering projects on time.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>Scrum Framework</h4><p>Scrum is like a sports team that works in short bursts called <strong>sprints</strong> (usually 2-4 weeks). The team has specific roles: <em>Product Owner</em> (decides what to build), <em>Scrum Master</em> (keeps the team on track), and <em>Developers</em> (build the work). They meet daily for 15 minutes in <strong>stand-ups</strong> to share progress.</p>\n\n<h4>Kanban Methodology</h4><p>Kanban is like a restaurant kitchen that visualizes work on a board. Tasks move through columns like: <em>To Do</em> → <em>In Progress</em> → <em>Done</em>. It focuses on limiting work-in-progress to avoid overload.</p>\n\n<h4>Agile Principles and Values</h4><p>Agile values: </p><ul><li>People and interactions over processes</li><li>Working software over documentation</li><li>Customer collaboration over contract negotiation</li><li>Responding to change over following a plan</li></ul>Think of it like cooking a meal - you adjust the recipe based on taste as you go, not just following a static plan.<p></p>\n\n<h4>Agile Project Planning and Estimation</h4><p>Teams break work into <strong>user stories</strong> like: \"As a user, I want to log in so I can save my progress.\" They estimate size using <strong>story points</strong> (like 1=small, 5=medium, 8=large) instead of hours.</p>\n\n<h4>Agile Team Roles and Responsibilities</h4><p>A typical Agile team has: </p><ul><li><strong>Product Owner</strong>: Manages the list of features (product backlog)</li><li><strong>Scrum Master</strong>: Removes obstacles and protects the team</li><li><strong>Development Team</strong>: Cross-functional members who do the work</li></ul><p></p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a text document named <code>project-plan.txt</code> and add: <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;Project&gt;\n  Name: Personal Finance Tracker App\n  Goal: Help users track income/expenses\n  Timeframe: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">12</span> weeks\n&lt;/Project&gt;</code></pre></li>\n\n<li>Write 5 user stories using this format: <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>. As a user, I want to add income so I can track my earnings.\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>. As a user, I want to categorize expenses so I can see spending patterns.\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>. As a user, I want to generate monthly reports so I can review my finances.\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>. As a user, I want to set budget limits so I can avoid overspending.\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>. As a user, I want to access the app on mobile so I can track anytime.</code></pre></li>\n\n<li>Estimate story points (1-5) for each task: <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>. Add income - <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span> points\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>. Categorize expenses - <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span> points\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>. Generate reports - <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span> points\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>. Set budgets - <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span> points\n<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>. Mobile access - <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">8</span> points</code></pre></li>\n\n<li>Create a basic HTML Scrum board: <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'scrum-board\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'column\'</span>&gt;\n    &lt;h3&gt;To Do&lt;/h3&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Add income&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Categorize expenses&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'column\'</span>&gt;\n    &lt;h3&gt;In Progress&lt;/h3&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Generate reports&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'column\'</span>&gt;\n    &lt;h3&gt;Done&lt;/h3&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Set budgets&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre><p>This shows how tasks move through development phases.</p></li>\n\n<li>Create a Kanban-style CSS for your board: <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">div.scrum-board {\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 20px;\n}\n\n.column {\n  border: 1px solid <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#ccc;</span>\n  padding: 10px;\n  width: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">30</span>%;\n}\n\nul {\n  list-style: none;\n  padding-left: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>;\n}\n\nli {\n  background: <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#f0f0f0;</span>\n  margin: 5px <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>;\n  padding: 8px;\n  border-radius: 4px;\n}</code></pre></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3><p>Submit: </p><ul><li>Your <code>project-plan.txt</code> file with all planning details</li><li>The HTML+CSS files for your Scrum/Kanban board</li></ul><p></p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3><ul><li>Not completing all 5 user stories and estimates</li><li>Using hours instead of story points for estimation</li><li>Mixing up Scrum roles (Scrum Master vs Product Owner)</li></ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3><ul><li><blockquote>Use free tools like Trello or Azure Boards to practice visualizing workflows</blockquote></li><li><blockquote>Do daily 5-minute stand-ups with teammates to practice Scrum</blockquote></li><li><blockquote>Keep story points consistent - if one task is 5 points, similar tasks should match</blockquote></li></ul>', 'Agile Development Methodologies', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 11:02:02'),
(994, 1007, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: git repository</p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&amp;q=git+repository\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/git+repository\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/git+repository/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/git+repository/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=git+repository\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\n</div></div><h3>Version Control with Git - Basic Concepts</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ll learn the basics of Git, a powerful version control system used by developers worldwide. By the end of this task, you\'ll understand how to install and configure Git, initialize a repository, stage and commit changes, branch and merge, and connect to remote repositories.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need:</h4>\n<p>Before we dive into the task, let\'s cover some essential concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Version Control:</strong> Version control systems help you manage changes to your codebase over time. They allow you to track changes, collaborate with others, and revert to previous versions if needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Git:</strong> Git is a popular version control system developed by Linus Torvalds. It\'s free, open-source, and widely used in the industry.</li>\n<li><strong>Repository (Repo):</strong> A repository is a central location where your code and its history are stored. You can think of it as a digital filing cabinet.</li>\n<li><strong>Commit:</strong> A commit is a snapshot of your code at a particular point in time. It\'s like taking a photo of your code\'s state.</li>\n<li><strong>Branch:</strong> A branch is a separate line of development in your repository. You can think of it as a parallel universe where you can experiment without affecting the main codebase.</li>\n<li><strong>Remote Repository:</strong> A remote repository is a copy of your repository stored on a remote server, such as GitHub or GitLab. It allows you to collaborate with others and access your code from anywhere.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task:</h4>\n<p>Now that you\'ve learned the basics, let\'s practice with a step-by-step task:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Install Git:</strong> Go to the official Git website (<a href=\"https://git-scm.com/downloads\">&lt;a href=\'https://git-scm.com/downloads\'&gt;</a>) and download the installer for your operating system. Follow the installation instructions to install Git on your machine.</li>\n<li><strong>Verify Git Installation:</strong> Open a terminal or command prompt and type <code>git --version</code> to verify that Git is installed correctly. You should see the version number of Git.</li>\n<li><strong>Initialize a Repository:</strong> Create a new directory for your project and navigate to it in the terminal or command prompt. Type <code>git add .</code> to initialize a new repository. This will create a new .git folder in your project directory.</li>\n<li><strong>Stage Changes:</strong> Create a new file called <code>hello.txt</code> in your project directory with the following content:<code>\n# Hello World!\n</code>\n<p>Then, type <code>git add hello.txt</code> to stage the new file.</p></li>\n<li><strong>Commit Changes:</strong> Type <code>git commit -m \'Initial commit\'</code> to commit the changes with a meaningful message.</li>\n<li><strong>Create a Branch:</strong> Type <code>git branch feature/new-feature</code> to create a new branch called <code>feature/new-feature</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Switch to the Branch:</strong> Type <code>git checkout feature/new-feature</code> to switch to the new branch.</li>\n<li><strong>Make Changes:</strong> Create a new file called <code>new-feature.txt</code> in your project directory with the following content:<code>\n# This is a new feature\n</code>\n<p>Then, type <code>git add new-feature.txt</code> to stage the new file.</p></li>\n<li><strong>Commit Changes:</strong> Type <code>git commit -m \'Added new feature\'</code> to commit the changes with a meaningful message.</li>\n<li><strong>Merge Branch:</strong> Type <code>git checkout master</code> to switch back to the master branch. Then, type <code>git merge feature/new-feature</code> to merge the changes from the <code>feature/new-feature</code> branch into the master branch.</li>\n<li><strong>Push to Remote Repository:</strong> Create a new remote repository on GitHub or GitLab. Then, type <code>git remote add origin &lt;a href=\'https://github.com/your-username/your-repo-name.git\'&gt;</code> to link your local repository to the remote one. Finally, type <code>git push -u origin master</code> to push the changes to the remote repository.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable:</h4>\n<p>You should have a local repository with a master branch and a feature branch. You should also have a remote repository on GitHub or GitLab with the same changes.</p>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Not committing changes regularly:</strong> Make sure to commit changes frequently to avoid losing your work.</li>\n<li><strong>Not using meaningful commit messages:</strong> Use descriptive commit messages to explain the changes you made.</li>\n<li><strong>Not merging branches properly:</strong> Make sure to merge branches correctly to avoid conflicts.</li>\n<li><strong>Not pushing changes to the remote repository:</strong> Remember to push changes to the remote repository to collaborate with others.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use Git hooks:</strong> Git hooks are scripts that run automatically before or after certain Git commands. You can use them to automate tasks, such as formatting code or running tests.</li>\n<li><strong>Use Git submodules:</strong> Git submodules allow you to include other repositories as subdirectories within your main repository. You can use them to manage dependencies or create complex projects.</li>\n<li><strong>Use Git blame:</strong> Git blame shows you who made changes to a particular line of code and when. You can use it to track changes and collaborate with others.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><br></h3>\n<ul>\n</ul>', 'Version Control with Git - Basic Concepts', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 12:42:47');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(995, 1008, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312d81efc80_1781607809.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Version Control with Git - Advanced Topics</h3>\n<p>Today, we\'re going to explore some advanced topics in Git that will help you work more efficiently with your codebase. By the end of this task, you\'ll be able to use Git to manage complex workflows and resolve conflicts with ease.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need:</h4>\n<p>In this task, we\'ll be covering several advanced Git concepts. Let\'s start by defining each one:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rebasing</strong>: Rebasing is a process in Git that allows you to reapply your commits on top of another base commit. It\'s like rewriting history, but it\'s a more efficient way to manage your codebase.</li>\n<li><strong>Merging</strong>: Merging is the process of combining two or more branches of your codebase into a single branch. It\'s like taking two different versions of your code and combining them into one.</li>\n<li><strong>Cherry-Picking</strong>: Cherry-picking is a process in Git that allows you to take a commit from one branch and apply it to another branch. It\'s like copying a single commit from one branch and pasting it into another.</li>\n<li><strong>Resetting</strong>: Resetting is a process in Git that allows you to undo changes to your codebase. It\'s like hitting the \"undo\" button in your code editor.</li>\n<li><strong>Git Hooks</strong>: Git Hooks are scripts that run automatically when you perform certain Git actions, such as committing or merging. They\'re like automated reminders to follow best practices.</li>\n<li><strong>Git Filters</strong>: Git Filters are tools that allow you to manipulate your codebase in various ways, such as renaming files or removing sensitive information. They\'re like powerful editing tools.</li>\n<li><strong>Git Submodules</strong>: Git Submodules are a way to include another Git repository inside your own repository. They\'re like nested folders that contain their own Git repositories.</li>\n<li><strong>Gitignore</strong>: Gitignore is a file that tells Git which files to ignore in your codebase. It\'s like a list of files that you don\'t want Git to track.</li>\n<li><strong>Git Rebase and Git Amend</strong>: Git Rebase and Git Amend are two Git commands that allow you to modify your commit history. They\'re like editing your commit messages or squashing multiple commits into one.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task:</h4>\n<p>For this task, you\'ll be working with a sample codebase that contains several branches and commits. Your goal is to use the advanced Git concepts we\'ve covered to manage your codebase and resolve conflicts.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Clone the sample codebase from <code>https://github.com/example/sample-codebase.git</code> using the command <code>git clone https://github.com/example/sample-codebase.git</code>.</li>\n<li>Switch to the <code>feature/new-feature</code> branch using the command <code>git checkout feature/new-feature</code>.</li>\n<li>Rebase your commits on top of the <code>master</code> branch using the command <code>git rebase master</code>.</li>\n<li>Merge the <code>feature/new-feature</code> branch into the <code>master</code> branch using the command <code>git merge feature/new-feature</code>.</li>\n<li>Cherry-pick the commit <code>abc123</code> from the <code>feature/new-feature</code> branch into the <code>master</code> branch using the command <code>git cherry-pick abc123</code>.</li>\n<li>Reset your changes to the <code>master</code> branch using the command <code>git reset --hard</code>.</li>\n<li>Create a new file called <code>.gitignore</code> in the root of your codebase and add the following line: <code>.env</code>.</li>\n<li>Commit the changes to the <code>.gitignore</code> file using the command <code>git add .gitignore &amp;&amp; git commit -m \"Added .env to .gitignore\"</code>.</li>\n<li>Create a new Git Hook called <code>pre-commit</code> that runs the command <code>echo \"Hello, World!\"</code> when you commit changes.</li>\n<li>Apply the Git Filter <code>filter-branch</code> to rename all files with the extension <code>.txt</code> to <code>.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Include the Git Submodule <code>https://github.com/example/submodule.git</code> in your codebase.</li>\n<li>Use the Git Rebase command to modify your commit history and squash multiple commits into one.</li>\n<li>Use the Git Amend command to modify your commit message.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable:</h4>\n<p>Your deliverable for this task is a report that describes the steps you took to complete each part of the task. Include screenshots or code snippets to illustrate your work.</p>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Don\'t forget to commit your changes</strong>: Make sure to commit your changes after each step to avoid losing your work.</li>\n<li><strong>Be careful with rebasing</strong>: Rebasing can be a powerful tool, but it can also overwrite your changes. Make sure to use it carefully.</li>\n<li><strong>Don\'t ignore important files</strong>: Make sure to add important files to your Gitignore file to avoid losing them.</li>\n<li><strong>Test your Git Hooks</strong>: Make sure to test your Git Hooks to ensure they\'re working correctly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><blockquote><strong>Use Git Hooks to automate repetitive tasks</strong></blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote><strong>Use Git Filters to manipulate your codebase</strong></blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote><strong>Use Git Submodules to include external repositories</strong></blockquote></li>\n</ul>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># .gitignore</span>\n.env\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># .gitmodules</span>\n[submodule <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"submodule\"</span>]\n	path = submodule\n	url = https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//github.com/example/submodule.git</span>\n</code></pre>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># .pre-commit</span>\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">#!/bin/sh</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">echo</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"Hello, World!\"</span>\n</code></pre>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># filter-branch</span>\ngit filter-branch <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">--tree-filter \"mv *.txt *.md\" HEAD</span>\n</code></pre>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># .gitattributes</span>\n*.txt filter-branch\n</code></pre>\n<p></p>', 'Version Control with Git - Advanced Topics', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 11:03:39'),
(996, 1009, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: deployment strategies and options</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=deployment+strategies+and+options\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/deployment+strategies+and+options\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/deployment+strategies+and+options/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/deployment+strategies+and+options/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=deployment+strategies+and+options\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Deployment Strategies and Options</h3>\n<p>In today\'s task, you\'ll learn about different deployment methods and strategies used in web development. You\'ll understand the importance of continuous integration and continuous deployment (CI/CD) pipelines and how to automate them using tools like Jenkins and Docker. By the end of this task, you\'ll be able to deploy your web application to cloud platforms like AWS and Azure, as well as containerization platforms like Kubernetes.</p>\n\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>Before we dive into the task, let\'s cover some essential concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Deployment Methods</strong>: These are the ways you can deploy your web application to a production environment. There are two main types:</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Manual Deployment</strong>: This involves manually copying files from your local machine to the production server. It\'s a time-consuming and error-prone process, but it\'s still used in some cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Automated Deployment</strong>: This involves using tools to automate the deployment process. It\'s faster, more reliable, and reduces the risk of human error.</p>\n<p><strong>Deployment Tools</strong>: These are software tools used to automate the deployment process. Some popular deployment tools include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Jenkins</strong>: An open-source automation server that automates the build, test, and deployment process.</li>\n<li><strong>Docker</strong>: A containerization platform that allows you to package your application and its dependencies into a single container.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cloud Platforms</strong>: These are online platforms that provide computing resources, storage, and services over the internet. Some popular cloud platforms include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AWS (Amazon Web Services)</strong>: A comprehensive cloud platform that offers a wide range of services, including computing, storage, and analytics.</li>\n<li><strong>Azure</strong>: A cloud platform that offers a range of services, including computing, storage, and artificial intelligence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Containerization Platforms</strong>: These are platforms that allow you to package your application and its dependencies into a single container. Some popular containerization platforms include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kubernetes</strong>: An open-source container orchestration platform that automates the deployment, scaling, and management of containers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment (CI/CD)</strong>: These are software development practices that involve automating the build, test, and deployment process. CI/CD pipelines automate the testing and deployment of code changes, reducing the risk of errors and improving the overall quality of the application.</p>\n\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<p>Here\'s a step-by-step guide to deploying your web application to a cloud platform using Jenkins and Docker:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Set up a Jenkins server</strong>: Install Jenkins on your local machine or a remote server. You can download the Jenkins WAR file from the official Jenkins website.</code>&lt;a href=\'https://www.jenkins.io/download/&gt;</code></p>\n<p><strong>Configure Jenkins</strong>: Configure Jenkins to connect to your Git repository and automate the build process.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\n&lt;?xml version=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"1.0\"</span> encoding=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"UTF-8\"</span>?&gt;\n&lt;jobs&gt;\n  &lt;job&gt;\n    &lt;name&gt;My Job&lt;/name&gt;\n    &lt;description&gt;This <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">is</span> my job&lt;/description&gt;\n    &lt;scm <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"hudson.scm.CVSSCM\"</span>&gt;\n      &lt;host&gt;github.com&lt;/host&gt;\n      &lt;username&gt;myuser&lt;/username&gt;\n      &lt;password&gt;mypassword&lt;/password&gt;\n      &lt;repository&gt;myrepo&lt;/repository&gt;\n    &lt;/scm&gt;\n    &lt;builders&gt;\n      &lt;hudson.tasks.Shell&gt;\n        &lt;command&gt;/bin/bash -exc \'git clone https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//github.com/myuser/myrepo.git && cd myrepo && npm install && npm run build\'&lt;/command&gt;</span>\n      &lt;/hudson.tasks.Shell&gt;\n    &lt;/builders&gt;\n  &lt;/job&gt;\n&lt;/jobs&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>This configuration tells Jenkins to clone the Git repository, install the dependencies, and run the build script.</p>\n<li><strong>Set up a Dockerfile</strong>: Create a Dockerfile that defines the base image, copies the application code, and sets the working directory.</code>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\nFROM node:<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">14</span>\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">package</span>*.json ./\nRUN npm install\nCOPY . .\nRUN npm run build\nEXPOSE <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3000</span>\nCMD [<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"node\"</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"server.js\"</span>]\n</code></pre>\n<p>This Dockerfile tells Docker to use the Node.js 14 image, copy the application code, install the dependencies, and run the build script.</p>\n<li><strong>Build and push the Docker image</strong>: Use the Docker CLI to build and push the Docker image to a container registry like Docker Hub.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\ndocker build -t myimage .\ndocker tag myimage:latest myuser/myimage:latest\ndocker push myuser/myimage:latest\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code builds the Docker image, tags it with the latest version, and pushes it to Docker Hub.</p>\n<li><strong>Deploy the application to AWS</strong>: Use the AWS CLI to deploy the Docker image to an Amazon ECS cluster.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">\naws ecs create-service <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">--cluster mycluster --service-name myservice --task-definition mytask --desired-count 1</span>\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code creates a new service in the Amazon ECS cluster, specifies the task definition, and sets the desired count to 1.</p>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>For this task, you\'ll need to submit the following deliverables:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A screenshot of the Jenkins dashboard showing the successful build and deployment of the application.</li>\n<li>A Dockerfile that defines the base image, copies the application code, and sets the working directory.</li>\n<li>A Docker image that can be deployed to a container registry like Docker Hub.</li>\n<li>A screenshot of the AWS ECS dashboard showing the deployed application.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<p>Here are some common mistakes to avoid when deploying your web application:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Incorrect configuration</strong>: Make sure to configure Jenkins and Docker correctly to avoid errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Insufficient testing</strong>: Ensure that you\'ve tested your application thoroughly before deploying it to production.</li>\n<li><strong>Incorrect permissions</strong>: Make sure that the Jenkins server and Docker have the correct permissions to access the Git repository and container registry.</li>\n<li><strong>Outdated dependencies</strong>: Ensure that your dependencies are up-to-date to avoid security vulnerabilities.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<p>Here are some pro tips to help you improve your deployment process:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use a CI/CD pipeline</strong>: Automate your build, test, and deployment process using a CI/CD pipeline to improve efficiency and reduce errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Use a containerization platform</strong>: Use a containerization platform like Docker to package your application and its dependencies into a single container.</li>\n<li><strong>Use a cloud platform</strong>: Use a cloud platform like AWS to deploy your application and take advantage of scalability and reliability features.</li>\n</ul>', 'Deployment Strategies and Options', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(997, 1010, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312dcf96ce5_1781607887.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Testing and Debugging Techniques</h3>\n<p>In today\'s task, you\'ll learn the fundamentals of testing and debugging in web development. This is a crucial skill to master, as it will help you identify and fix bugs in your code, ensuring your application works as expected. In a real-world job, you\'ll use these techniques to test and debug complex web applications.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need:</h4>\n<p>To complete today\'s task, you\'ll need to understand the following concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Testing Types:</strong> Unit Testing and Integration Testing</li>\n<li><strong>Testing Frameworks:</strong> Jest and Mocha</li>\n<li><strong>Debugging Tools:</strong> Chrome DevTools and Visual Studio Code</li>\n<li><strong>Debugging Techniques:</strong> Console Logging and Error Handling</li>\n<li><strong>Testing and Debugging Best Practices</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-Step Task:</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Create a new JavaScript file called <code>math.js</code> and add the following code:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// math.js</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> add(x, y) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> x + y;\n}\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> subtract(x, y) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> x - y;\n}\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> multiply(x, y) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> x * y;\n}\n\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> divide(x, y) {\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (y === <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>) {\n    <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">throw</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">new</span> Error(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Cannot divide by zero!\'</span>);\n  }\n  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> x / y;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code defines four functions for basic arithmetic operations: addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.</p></li>\n<li><p>Create a new JavaScript file called <code>math.test.js</code> and add the following code:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// math.test.js</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> math = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'./math\'</span>);\n\ndescribe(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'math module\'</span>, () =&gt; {\n  it(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'adds two numbers\'</span>, () =&gt; {\n    expect(math.add(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>)).toBe(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>);\n  });\n\n  it(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'subtracts two numbers\'</span>, () =&gt; {\n    expect(math.subtract(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>)).toBe(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>);\n  });\n\n  it(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'multiplies two numbers\'</span>, () =&gt; {\n    expect(math.multiply(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>)).toBe(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">20</span>);\n  });\n\n  it(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'divides two numbers\'</span>, () =&gt; {\n    expect(math.divide(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>)).toBe(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>);\n  });\n\n  it(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'throws an error when dividing by zero\'</span>, () =&gt; {\n    expect(() =&gt; math.divide(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>)).toThrowError(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Cannot divide by zero!\'</span>);\n  });\n});\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code uses Jest to write unit tests for the math module. We\'re testing each function with a specific input and expected output.</p></li>\n<li><p>Run the tests using Jest. You should see four passing tests and one failing test (the one that tries to divide by zero).</p></li>\n<li><p>Open the Chrome DevTools and navigate to the Console tab. Create a new error by calling the <code>divide</code> function with a divisor of zero:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Create a new error</span>\nmath.divide(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>);\n</code></pre>\n<p>This will throw an error in the console. Inspect the error object to see its properties:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Inspect the error object</span>\nconsole.error(error);\n</code></pre>\n<p>Notice the error message and the stack trace.</p></li>\n<li><p>Use Visual Studio Code to debug the math module. Set a breakpoint on the line that throws the error:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// Set a breakpoint</span>\ndebugger;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Run the code and inspect the variables in the Variables panel. You should see the error object and its properties.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable:</h4>\n<p>Submit the following files:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>math.js</code></li>\n<li><code>math.test.js</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Common Mistakes to Avoid:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Not using a testing framework:</strong> Jest and Mocha are popular testing frameworks that make it easy to write and run tests.</li>\n<li><strong>Not testing for edge cases:</strong> Make sure to test your code with different inputs, including edge cases like dividing by zero.</li>\n<li><strong>Not using debugging tools:</strong> Chrome DevTools and Visual Studio Code are powerful debugging tools that can help you identify and fix bugs.</li>\n<li><strong>Not following best practices:</strong> Keep your code organized, use meaningful variable names, and follow standard coding conventions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro Tips:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use a testing framework to write and run tests:</strong> Jest and Mocha make it easy to write and run tests, and they provide a lot of features out of the box.</li>\n<li><strong>Test for edge cases:</strong> Make sure to test your code with different inputs, including edge cases like dividing by zero.</li>\n<li><strong>Use debugging tools to identify and fix bugs:</strong> Chrome DevTools and Visual Studio Code are powerful debugging tools that can help you identify and fix bugs.</li>\n</ul>', 'Testing and Debugging Techniques', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 11:04:57'),
(998, 1011, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: CI/CD pipeline tools</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=CI%2FCD+pipeline+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/CI%2FCD+pipeline+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/CI%2FCD+pipeline+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/CI%2FCD+pipeline+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=CI%2FCD+pipeline+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Continuous Integration and Delivery</h3>\n<p>In today\'s task, you will learn about Continuous Integration and Delivery (CI/CD) pipelines, tools, and best practices. You will understand how to automate testing and deployment, and the benefits and challenges of implementing CI/CD in a real-world project.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need:</h4>\n<p>A <strong>pipeline</strong> is a series of automated processes that take your code from writing to deployment. It\'s like a factory assembly line where each stage builds upon the previous one.</p>\n<p>A <strong>CI/CD tool</strong> is a software that helps you automate and manage your pipeline. Think of it as a robot that takes care of all the tedious tasks for you.</p>\n<p>A <strong>job</strong> in a pipeline is a single task that is executed by the CI/CD tool. It\'s like a recipe that the robot follows to complete a specific task.</p>\n<p><strong>Automated testing</strong> is the process of running tests on your code automatically, without manual intervention. It\'s like having a quality control team that checks your code for bugs.</p>\n<p><strong>Deployment</strong> is the process of making your code available to the public. It\'s like sending your code to a printing press to print physical copies.</p>\n<p><strong>Scalability</strong> refers to the ability of your pipeline to handle increased traffic and workload. It\'s like building a highway that can handle more cars as the population grows.</p>\n<p><strong>Security</strong> refers to the measures taken to protect your pipeline and code from unauthorized access. It\'s like building a fortress around your code to keep it safe.</p>\n<p><strong>AI and Machine Learning</strong> integration refers to the use of artificial intelligence and machine learning algorithms to improve your pipeline and code. It\'s like having a super-smart robot that can learn and adapt to new situations.</p>\n<h4>Step-by-step task:</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new Jenkinsfile in your project directory. This file will contain the configuration for your pipeline.</li>\n<li>Define a new job in your Jenkinsfile that builds your project using Maven. Here\'s an example:</pre><code>\npipeline {\n    agent any\n\n    stages {\n        stage(\'Build\') {\n            steps {\n                sh \'mvn clean package\'\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This job will run the Maven build command to compile and package your project.</li>\n<li>Define a new job in your Jenkinsfile that deploys your project to a remote server using SSH. Here\'s an example:</pre><code>\npipeline {\n    agent any\n\n    stages {\n        stage(\'Deploy\') {\n            steps {\n                sshagent([\'your_ssh_key\']) {\n                    sh \'scp target/*.war user@remote_server:/var/www/html\'\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This job will use SSH to connect to the remote server and deploy your project.</li>\n<li>Run your pipeline using Jenkins and verify that it completes successfully.</li>\n<li>Use CircleCI to create a new pipeline that builds and deploys your project. Here\'s an example:</pre><code>\nversion: 2.1\n\njobs:\n  build-and-deploy:\n    docker:\n      - image: circleci/node:14\n    steps:\n      - checkout\n      - run: npm install\n      - run: npm run build\n      - run: sshagent([\'your_ssh_key\']) {\n          scp target/*.war user@remote_server:/var/www/html\n        }\n</code></pre>\n<p>This pipeline will build your project using Node.js and deploy it to the remote server using SSH.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable:</h4>\n<p>You should have two pipelines set up: one using Jenkins and one using CircleCI. Both pipelines should build and deploy your project successfully.</p>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Not defining a pipeline for your project, leading to manual deployment and potential errors.</li>\n<li>Not using automated testing, leading to bugs and errors in production.</li>\n<li>Not securing your pipeline and code, leading to unauthorized access and potential security breaches.</li>\n<li>Not scaling your pipeline to handle increased traffic and workload, leading to performance issues and errors.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Use Jenkins or CircleCI to automate your pipeline and reduce manual effort.</li>\n<li>Use automated testing to catch bugs and errors early in the development process.</li>\n<li>Use SSH to securely deploy your project to a remote server.</li>\n<li>Use Docker to containerize your application and improve scalability.</li>\n</ul>', 'Continuous Integration and Delivery', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(999, 1012, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; <img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312e227eba1_1781607970.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you will build a small database, run queries, and see how adding indexes changes the speed of those queries. In a real job you often need to read data quickly from large tables, and the right indexes can make the difference between a lagging page and a snappy one.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SQL Query Basics</strong> – The language you use to ask a database for data. Think of it like a question you ask a librarian: \"Show me the books about gardening that were published after 2010.\"</li>\n<li><strong>SELECT</strong> – The part of the query that tells the database which columns you want.</li>\n<li><strong>FROM</strong> – Indicates which table(s) to look in.</li>\n<li><strong>WHERE</strong> – Filters rows based on conditions, similar to a filter on a spreadsheet.</li>\n<li><strong>JOIN</strong> – Combines rows from two tables when they share a common column, like matching a customer to their orders.</li>\n<li><strong>GROUP BY</strong> – Groups rows so you can aggregate data (SUM, COUNT, etc.), like counting how many orders each customer placed.</li>\n<li><strong>ORDER BY</strong> – Sorts the result set, just like arranging a list alphabetically.</li>\n<li><strong>HAVING</strong> – Filters groups after aggregation, similar to WHERE but for groups.</li>\n<li><strong>Execution Plan</strong> – A map the database builds to decide how to run a query. Think of it as a GPS route plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Index</strong> – A data structure that lets the database find rows faster, like an index in a book that points to page numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>B‑Tree</strong> – The most common index type; it keeps data sorted so lookups are quick.</li>\n<li><strong>Hash Index</strong> – Uses a hash table for equality lookups, great for exact matches but not for ranges.</li>\n<li><strong>Composite Index</strong> – An index on multiple columns, useful when you filter on more than one column.</li>\n<li><strong>Covering Index</strong> – An index that contains all the columns a query needs, so the database never has to touch the main table.</li>\n<li><strong>Partial Index</strong> – An index that only includes rows that meet a condition, saving space and improving speed for common queries.</li>\n<li><strong>Query Optimization</strong> – The process of improving query performance through better SQL, indexes, or database settings.</li>\n<li><strong>Query Performance Tuning</strong> – Fine‑tuning steps like adding indexes, rewriting queries, or adjusting statistics.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Step‑by‑step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Set up a sample database</strong>\n<p>Use SQLite for simplicity. Open a terminal and run:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">sqlite3 demo.db</code></pre>\n<p>Now you are inside the SQLite shell.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Create tables</strong>\n<p>Run the following statements to create two tables: <code>users</code> and <code>orders</code>.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE TABLE users (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  name TEXT NOT NULL,\n  email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  signup_date TEXT NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  user_id INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  product TEXT NOT NULL,\n  quantity INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  status TEXT NOT NULL,\n  order_date TEXT NOT NULL,\n  FOREIGN KEY(user_id) REFERENCES users(id)\n);\n</code></pre>\n<p>These tables mimic a small e‑commerce system.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Insert sample data</strong>\n<p>Insert 5 users and 20 orders. For brevity, only a few rows are shown; you can copy the pattern to create more.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">INSERT INTO users (name, email, signup_date) VALUES\n  (<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Alice\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'alice@example.com\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'2022-01-15\'</span>),\n  (<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Bob\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'bob@example.com\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'2022-02-20\'</span>),\n  (<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Carol\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'carol@example.com\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'2022-03-05\'</span>),\n  (<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Dave\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'dave@example.com\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'2022-04-12\'</span>),\n  (<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Eve\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'eve@example.com\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'2022-05-01\'</span>);\n\nINSERT INTO orders (user_id, product, quantity, status, order_date) VALUES\n  (<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Book\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'shipped\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'2022-06-01\'</span>),\n  (<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Pen\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'pending\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'2022-06-03\'</span>),\n  (<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Notebook\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">5</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'shipped\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'2022-06-02\'</span>),\n  (<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Book\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'shipped\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'2022-06-04\'</span>),\n  (<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">4</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Pencil\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">20</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'canceled\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'2022-06-05\'</span>);\n</code></pre>\n<p>Feel free to add more rows to make the dataset larger.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Run a basic SELECT query</strong>\n<p>Get all orders for user 1:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>;</code></pre>\n<p>Observe the result and note the time it takes.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Analyze the query plan</strong>\n<p>Use <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code> to see how SQLite will execute the query.</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>;</code></pre>\n<p>The output shows whether SQLite will scan the whole table or use an index.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Add an index on <code>user_id</code></strong>\n<p>Because we filter by <code>user_id</code>, an index will help:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE INDEX idx_orders_user_id ON orders(user_id);</code></pre>\n<p>Re‑run the <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code> and compare.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Rewrite the query for better performance</strong>\n<p>Instead of <code>SELECT *</code>, request only needed columns:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">SELECT id, product, quantity, status FROM orders WHERE user_id = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>;</code></pre>\n<p>This reduces data transfer and can improve speed.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Create a composite index for a common query</strong>\n<p>Suppose you often query by <code>status</code> and <code>order_date</code>:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">SELECT * FROM orders\nWHERE status = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'shipped\'</span> AND order_date &gt; <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'2022-06-01\'</span>;</code></pre>\n<p>Create an index covering both columns:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE INDEX idx_orders_status_date ON orders(status, order_date);</code></pre>\n<p>Run <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code> again to confirm the index is used.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Use a covering index</strong>\n<p>If the query only needs <code>product</code> and <code>quantity</code>, add those to the index:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE INDEX idx_orders_status_date_cover ON orders(status, order_date, product, quantity);</code></pre>\n<p>Now the database can answer the query entirely from the index.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Experiment with a partial index</strong>\n<p>If most queries target shipped orders, index only those rows:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE INDEX idx_orders_shipped ON orders(user_id)\nWHERE status = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'shipped\'</span>;</code></pre>\n<p>Test the performance of a shipped‑only query.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Measure performance</strong>\n<p>Use <code>.timing on</code> in SQLite to see how long each query takes. Record the times before and after adding indexes.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Write a short report</strong>\n<p>Summarize:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Initial query times</li>\n<li>Effect of each index added</li>\n<li>Which index gave the biggest improvement</li>\n<li>Any trade‑offs you noticed (e.g., insert speed)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit a ZIP file containing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>demo.db</code> – the SQLite database after all changes.</li>\n<li><code>queries.sql</code> – a SQL file that recreates the tables, inserts data, creates indexes, and runs the sample queries with <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code> comments.</li>\n<li><code>performance_report.txt</code> – your written report with timings and observations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Using <code>SELECT *</code> in production – it pulls unnecessary data and can slow queries.</li>\n<li>Adding indexes without testing – indexes speed reads but slow writes and take storage.</li>\n<li>Ignoring the execution plan – you may think an index works when it isn’t actually used.</li>\n<li>Not keeping statistics up to date – the optimizer relies on accurate data distribution.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Always start with <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code> before adding indexes to see the current strategy.</li>\n<li>Use composite indexes that match the order of columns in your <code>WHERE</code> clause for best results.</li>\n<li>For very large tables, consider partitioning or sharding if single indexes cannot keep up.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>', 'Database Queries and Indexing', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 11:06:33');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(1000, 1013, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312e735fa43_1781608051.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you will design a small library database and then apply normalization rules to eliminate redundancy. In a real job you’ll often receive a messy schema from legacy systems and your job is to clean it up so updates, deletes and inserts work reliably.</p><h3>Concepts you need</h3><h4>First Normal Form (1NF)</h4><p>1NF means every column contains only atomic values and each row is unique. Think of a spreadsheet where each cell holds a single piece of information, not a list.</p><h4>Second Normal Form (2NF)</h4><p>2NF builds on 1NF by removing partial dependencies: a non‑key column must depend on the whole primary key, not just part of it. Imagine a table where a book’s author is stored with the book ID; if the key is a composite of book_id and chapter_id, the author depends only on book_id.</p><h4>Third Normal Form (3NF)</h4><p>3NF removes transitive dependencies: a non‑key column should not depend on another non‑key column. For example, storing a city name and the state name together creates a dependency between city and state.</p><h4>Data Redundancy and Dependency</h4><p>Redundancy is duplicate data that can lead to inconsistencies. Dependency is the relationship that forces one piece of data to be derived from another. Reducing redundancy by normalizing reduces the chance of contradictory data.</p><h4>Denormalization</h4><p>Denormalization is the intentional re‑introduction of redundancy to speed up read queries. It’s a trade‑off: fewer joins but more write complexity.</p><h4>Query Optimization</h4><p>After normalization you’ll often need to join many tables. Query optimization involves indexing, selecting only needed columns, and rewriting joins to be efficient.</p><h4>Database Normalization Techniques</h4><p>Techniques include identifying candidate keys, decomposing tables, and using foreign keys. The goal is a set of tables that each represents a single entity or relationship.</p><h3>Step-by-step task</h3><ol><li><strong>Create the initial table.</strong> Write a SQL script that creates a single table <code>library_books</code> with columns: <code>book_id</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>author</code>, <code>publisher</code>, <code>pub_year</code>, <code>category</code>, <code>category_description</code>. Use <code>INT</code> for IDs and <code>VARCHAR</code> for text. <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE TABLE library_books (book_id INT PRIMARY KEY, title VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">255</span>), author VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">255</span>), publisher VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">255</span>), pub_year INT, category VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">100</span>), category_description VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">255</span>));</code></pre></li><li><strong>Insert sample data.</strong> Add five rows that contain duplicate category descriptions and authors. <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">INSERT INTO library_books VALUES (<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Book A\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Author X\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Pub1\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2020</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Fiction\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Stories about adventure\'</span>);</code></pre></li><li><strong>Check for 1NF violations.</strong> Ensure each column holds atomic values. If a cell contains a list (e.g., multiple authors separated by commas), split them into separate rows.</li><li><strong>Apply 2NF.</strong> Identify the composite key that might exist if you later split the table. For now, create a new table <code>authors</code> with <code>author_id</code> and <code>author_name</code>, and replace the <code>author</code> column in <code>library_books</code> with <code>author_id</code>. <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE TABLE authors (author_id INT PRIMARY KEY, author_name VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">255</span>));</code></pre></li><li><strong>Apply 3NF.</strong> Notice <code>category_description</code> depends on <code>category</code>. Create a <code>categories</code> table and replace the description column with a foreign key. <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE TABLE categories (category_id INT PRIMARY KEY, category_name VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">100</span>), category_description VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">255</span>));</code></pre></li><li><strong>Update foreign keys.</strong> Alter <code>library_books</code> to include <code>category_id</code> and add foreign key constraints. <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">ALTER TABLE library_books ADD COLUMN category_id INT; ALTER TABLE library_books ADD CONSTRAINT fk_category FOREIGN KEY (category_id) REFERENCES categories(category_id);</code></pre></li><li><strong>Populate new tables.</strong> Insert unique authors and categories into their tables and update <code>library_books</code> with the correct IDs.</li><li><strong>Check for denormalization opportunities.</strong> If you anticipate frequent queries for <code>author_name</code> and <code>category_description</code> together, create a view that joins the three tables. <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE VIEW book_details AS SELECT lb.book_id, lb.title, a.author_name, c.category_name, c.category_description FROM library_books lb JOIN authors a ON lb.author_id = a.author_id JOIN categories c ON lb.category_id = c.category_id;</code></pre></li><li><strong>Optimize a query.</strong> Write a SELECT that retrieves all books with their authors and categories, and add indexes on <code>author_id</code> and <code>category_id</code>. <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE INDEX idx_author ON library_books(author_id); CREATE INDEX idx_category ON library_books(category_id); SELECT * FROM book_details WHERE category_name = <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Fiction\'</span>;</code></pre></li></ol><h3>Deliverable</h3><p>Submit a single SQL file named <code>library_normalization.sql</code> that contains: </p><ul><li>Table creation statements for <code>library_books</code>, <code>authors</code>, <code>categories</code>, and the view.</li><li>Insert statements with sample data.</li><li>Alter statements adding foreign keys.</li><li>Index creation and a sample SELECT query.</li></ul> Also include a short diagram (hand‑drawn or digital) showing the relationships between the three tables.<p></p><h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3><ul><li>Leaving non‑atomic values in a column; always split lists into separate rows.</li><li>Using a composite key without fully decomposing dependent columns; check that every non‑key column depends on the whole key.</li><li>Creating circular foreign key references; keep relationships one‑to‑many.</li><li>Forgetting to add indexes after adding foreign keys; this can slow down joins.</li></ul><h3>Pro tips</h3><blockquote><p>Use <code>EXPLAIN</code> to see how the database executes your query and adjust indexes accordingly.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>When denormalizing, document the rationale so future developers understand the trade‑off.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Keep your normalization steps in a version control system; you can revert if a denormalization proves too costly.</p></blockquote>', 'Database Normalization', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 11:07:41'),
(1001, 1014, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: database security diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=database+security+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/database+security+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/database+security+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/database+security+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=database+security+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you will add a layer of protection to a simple user database. You’ll encrypt sensitive data, set up role‑based access, validate input, audit changes, and design the schema to keep data safe. These skills are essential for any developer who builds real‑world applications that store personal or financial information.</p><h3>Concepts you need</h3><h4>Data Encryption</h4><p>Encryption turns readable data into unreadable code. Think of it like putting a secret message in a locked box. Only someone with the key can open the box and read the message again.</p><h4>Access Control</h4><p>Access control decides who can see or change data. Imagine a library where only librarians can add new books, while readers can only borrow them.</p><h4>Data Validation</h4><p>Validation checks that data fits expected rules before it’s stored. It’s like a security guard checking a visitor’s ID before letting them enter a building.</p><h4>Data Authorization</h4><p>Authorization is the logic that says whether a user is allowed to perform a specific action. Think of it as a parking permit that lets a car only into certain areas of a campus.</p><h4>Database Auditing</h4><p>Auditing records who did what and when. It’s similar to a CCTV camera that records every entry and exit in a store.</p><h4>Database Compliance</h4><p>Compliance ensures the database follows legal and industry rules (e.g., GDPR, PCI‑DSS). Think of it as a building that must meet fire‑safety regulations.</p><h4>Secure Database Design</h4><p>Secure design means choosing tables, columns, and relationships that reduce risk. It’s like building a house with reinforced walls and secure windows.</p><h3>Step‑by‑step task</h3><ol><li><strong>Create a sample database</strong><p>Open your SQL client and run:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE DATABASE secure_demo;</code></pre><p>Use it:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">USE secure_demo;</code></pre></li><li><strong>Build a user table with encrypted password</strong><p>We’ll store passwords as encrypted blobs:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE TABLE users (</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  username VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">50</span>) NOT NULL UNIQUE,</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  password VARBINARY(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">255</span>) NOT NULL,</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  role ENUM(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'admin\'</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'editor\'</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'viewer\'</span>) DEFAULT <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'viewer\'</span>,</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">);</code></pre></li><li><strong>Insert a user with encrypted password</strong><p>Use MySQL’s AES_ENCRYPT. Replace <em>my_secret_key</em> with a strong key you keep safe.</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">INSERT INTO users (username, password, role) VALUES (</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'alice\'</span>,</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  AES_ENCRYPT(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'P@ssw0rd\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'my_secret_key\'</span>),</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'admin\'</span></code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">);</code></pre></li><li><strong>Set up role‑based access control</strong><p>Create a simple view that only admins can see all users:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE VIEW admin_users AS SELECT id, username, role, created_at FROM users;</code></pre><p>Grant the view to the admin role:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">GRANT SELECT ON admin_users TO <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'admin_user\'</span>@<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'localhost\'</span>;</code></pre></li><li><strong>Validate data before insertion</strong><p>In your application layer, check that usernames are alphanumeric and passwords are at least 8 characters. Example in PHP:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span> validate($username,$password){</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (!preg_match(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'/^[a-zA-Z0-9_]+$/\'</span>,$username)) <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">false</span>;</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> (strlen($password) &lt; <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">8</span>) <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">false</span>;</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">return</span> <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">true</span>;</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">}</code></pre></li><li><strong>Implement data authorization logic</strong><p>When a user requests to update a record, check their role. Example pseudo‑code:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">if</span> ($user_role == <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'admin\'</span> || $user_id == $record_owner) {</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// allow update</span></code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">} <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">else</span> {</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">// deny</span></code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">}</code></pre></li><li><strong>Set up auditing</strong><p>Create an audit table:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE TABLE audit_log (</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  audit_id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  user_id INT,</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  action VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">50</span>),</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  table_name VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">50</span>),</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  record_id INT,</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  old_value TEXT,</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  new_value TEXT,</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  changed_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">);</code></pre><p>Use a trigger to log updates:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE TRIGGER log_user_update AFTER UPDATE ON users FOR EACH ROW BEGIN</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  INSERT INTO audit_log (user_id, action, table_name, record_id, old_value, new_value)</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  VALUES (NEW.id, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'UPDATE\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'users\'</span>, NEW.id, OLD.password, NEW.password);</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">END;</code></pre></li><li><strong>Check compliance with a simple rule set</strong><p>For GDPR, ensure no personally identifiable data is stored without consent. Add a consent column and enforce NOT NULL:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN consent TINYINT(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>) NOT NULL DEFAULT <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>;</code></pre></li><li><strong>Design secure schema</strong><p>Separate sensitive data into its own table with stricter permissions:</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">CREATE TABLE user_secrets (</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  user_id INT PRIMARY KEY,</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  ssn VARBINARY(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">255</span>),</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">  FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id)</code></pre><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">);</code></pre></li><li><strong>Test everything</strong><p>Insert a second user, try to view the admin view as a non‑admin, attempt to update a record without proper role, and confirm audit logs record the changes.</p></li></ol><h3>Deliverable</h3><p>Submit a single SQL file named <code>secure_demo.sql</code> that contains:</p><ul><li>Database creation</li><li>All table and view definitions</li><li>Insert statements for at least two users</li><li>Triggers for auditing</li><li>Comments explaining each part</li></ul><p>Also provide a short README (plain text) describing how to set up the database and run the tests.</p><h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3><ul><li>Storing plain text passwords – always encrypt or hash.</li><li>Using weak encryption keys – keep keys out of source code.</li><li>Granting broad SELECT rights – limit permissions to the minimum needed.</li><li>Ignoring audit triggers – they must fire on every DML operation.</li></ul><h3>Pro tips</h3><blockquote>Use environment variables for encryption keys so they never hit the repository.</blockquote><blockquote>Apply least‑privilege principle: create separate database users for admin, editor, and viewer roles.</blockquote><blockquote>Regularly review audit logs; set up alerts for suspicious activity.</blockquote>', 'Database Security and Integrity', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(1002, 1015, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: database backup process</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=database+backup+process\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/database+backup+process\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/database+backup+process/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/database+backup+process/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=database+backup+process\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you\'ll learn how to protect web app data using database backups and recovery. You\'ll create backup files, clone databases, and practice restoring data—skills every developer needs to prevent disasters like accidental data deletion or server crashes.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>Database Backup</h4>\n<p>A <strong>backup</strong> is like a time capsule for your data. It saves a copy of your database at a specific moment. If your main database breaks, you can use the backup to restore everything.</p>\n\n<h4>Database Restore</h4>\n<p>Restoring is like opening a time capsule. You take a backup file and use it to rebuild your database when there\'s a problem.</p>\n\n<h4>Cloning</h4>\n<p>Cloning creates an exact copy of a database, like photocopying a notebook. Developers use clones for testing changes without risking real data.</p>\n\n<h4>Snapshot</h4>\n<p>A snapshot is an instant photo of your database. It\'s faster than regular backups and lets you \"rewind\" to that exact moment.</p>\n\n<h4>Replication</h4>\n<p>Replication keeps multiple database copies in sync, like mirrors reflecting the same image. It helps with both performance and safety.</p>\n\n<h4>Backup Strategies</h4>\n<p>Strategies are your safety plan. You might keep daily backups for a week, weekly backups for a month, and yearly archives—like keeping different types of savings accounts.</p>\n\n<h4>Data Loss Recovery</h4>\n<p>This is your emergency plan when data disappears. It combines backups, snapshots, and replication to fix problems quickly.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Create a test database named <code>webstore</code> with sample data:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;code&gt;CREATE DATABASE webstore;\nUSE webstore;\n\nCREATE TABLE products (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  name VARCHAR(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">50</span>),\n  price DECIMAL(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">10</span>,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>)\n);\n\nINSERT INTO products VALUES\n(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Laptop\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">999.99</span>),\n(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Phone\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">699.49</span>);&lt;/code&gt;</code></pre></li>\n\n<li><p>Make a full backup using <code>mysqldump</code>:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;code&gt;mysqldump -u root -p webstore &gt; webstore_backup_$(date +%F).sql&lt;/code&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>This creates a dated backup file. The <code>$(date +%F)</code> adds today\'s date to the filename.</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Create a database clone:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;code&gt;mysql -u root -p -e <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\"CREATE DATABASE webstore_clone;\"</span>\nmysql -u root -p webstore_clone &lt; webstore_backup_2023-<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">11</span>-<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">01</span>.sql&lt;/code&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>This builds a new database from your backup file.</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Test snapshot recovery (PostgreSQL example):</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;code&gt;pg_dump -U username -Fc webstore &gt; webstore_snapshot.dump\npg_restore -U username -d webstore_recovery webstore_snapshot.dump&lt;/code&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>These commands create and restore a PostgreSQL snapshot.</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Set up basic replication (MySQL example):</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;code&gt;<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">-- On main server</span>\nGRANT REPLICATION SLAVE ON *.* TO <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'replica_user\'</span>@<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'%\'</span> IDENTIFIED BY <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'password\'</span>;\n\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">-- On backup server</span>\nCHANGE MASTER TO\n  MASTER_HOST=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'main-server-ip\'</span>,\n  MASTER_USER=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'replica_user\'</span>,\n  MASTER_PASSWORD=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'password\'</span>,\n  MASTER_LOG_FILE=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'mysql-bin.000001\'</span>,\n  MASTER_LOG_POS=  <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">12345</span>;\nSTART SLAVE;&lt;/code&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>This connects a backup server to automatically copy changes from the main server.</p></li>\n\n<li><p>Test your backup strategy:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;code&gt;<span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># Delete test data</span>\nDELETE FROM webstore.products;\n\n<span style=\"color:#5c6370\"># Restore from backup</span>\nmysql -u root -p webstore &lt; webstore_backup_2023-<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">11</span>-<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">01</span>.sql&lt;/code&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>This proves your backup works by recovering deleted data.</p></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit a text document with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Screen shots of each step\'s output</li>\n<li>Explanations of what each command does</li>\n<li>A 3-sentence summary of your backup strategy</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Forgetting to test backups - create backups but never check if they work</li>\n<li>Storing backups in the same location as your main database (what if both get deleted?)</li>\n<li>Not documenting your backup strategy - you need to remember where files are and how to use them</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Automate everything:</strong> Use cron jobs or task scheduler to run backups automatically</li>\n<li><strong>Version your backups:</strong> Add dates to filenames so you can find older versions</li>\n<li><strong>Test restores monthly:</strong> A backup is only useful if you can actually use it</li>\n</ul>', 'Database Backup and Recovery', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(1003, 1016, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312f1981af3_1781608217.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you will learn how to gather and document project requirements for a web development project. This skill is critical for real jobs because it ensures everyone agrees on what the project will deliver before writing any code.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>Project Scope Definition</h4><p>Like planning a road trip: you define the start and end points, major stops, and what you’ll bring. In projects, this means writing clear boundaries for what the project will and won’t include.</p>\n\n<h4>Requirements Elicitation Techniques</h4><p>Imagine asking friends questions to plan a party. In projects, you interview stakeholders, observe workflows, or run surveys to find out what they need.</p>\n\n<h4>Use Cases and User Stories</h4><p>A use case is like a movie script showing how a user interacts with a system. A user story is a short phrase from the user’s perspective. Example: <em>\"As a user, I want to log in so I can save my progress.\"</em></p>\n\n<h4>Project Requirements Document</h4><p>This is a written contract between developers and stakeholders. It includes scope, features, constraints, and acceptance criteria—all written clearly so no one gets surprised later.</p>\n\n<h4>Requirements Prioritization</h4><p>Like packing a suitcase: you decide which items are most important. In projects, you rank requirements as Must-Have, Should-Have, or Nice-to-Have using methods like MoSCoW (Must, Should, Could, Won’t).</p>\n\n<h4>Stakeholder Management</h4><p>Stakeholders are people who care about the project (e.g., clients, users, managers). Managing them means understanding their goals, resolving conflicts, and keeping them updated.</p>\n\n<h4>Project Requirements Gathering Tools</h4><p>Tools like Trello, Miro, or Google Docs help organize requirements visually. Think of them as digital sticky notes you can sort, group, and share with others.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Create a Project Requirements Document outline</strong><p>Use Google Docs or Notion to create a new document with these sections:\n</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;h2&gt;Project Title&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;h3&gt;<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>. Purpose&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;One paragraph explaining the project’s goal.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;h3&gt;<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>. Stakeholders&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Client: [Name/Role]&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;End Users: [Description]&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre></li>\n\n<li><strong>Write 3 user stories</strong><p>Use the format: \"As a [role], I want [goal] so that [benefit].\" Example:\n</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;h4&gt;User Story <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/h4&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;As a student, I want to track my quiz scores so I can review my progress.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre></li>\n\n<li><strong>Make one use case diagram</strong><p>Draw this in Miro or paper: a user interacting with a system. For example, a customer placing an order on a website. Label actors (user roles) and actions (steps).</p></li>\n\n<li><strong>Prioritize requirements</strong><p>Add a table to your document:\n</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;table&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Requirement&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Priority&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;User login&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;Must-Have&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Dark mode&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;Nice-to-Have&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n&lt;/table&gt;</code></pre></li>\n\n<li><strong>Identify stakeholders</strong><p>List 3 stakeholders and their goals. Example:\n</p><pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;h4&gt;Stakeholder <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/h4&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Client: Wants the site to load <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> under <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span> seconds.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit your completed Project Requirements Document (as a Google Docs link or PDF) with all 5 sections filled in. Include at least 3 user stories, one use case description, and a prioritization table.</p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Writing vague requirements like \"make it fast\"—always add measurable criteria (e.g., \"load in 2 seconds\").</li>\n<li>Ignoring stakeholder conflicts: if two users want different features, document the tradeoff clearly.</li>\n<li>Skipping the scope section—this is where you define what’s <strong>not</strong> included to avoid scope creep.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><blockquote><strong>Start with user needs:</strong> Ask \"who will use this?\" before jumping to technical solutions.</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote><strong>Use templates:</strong> Search \"Project Requirements Document template\" on Google Docs to save time.</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote><strong>Sketch first:</strong> Draw rough diagrams of user flows before writing detailed text.</blockquote></li>\n</ul>', 'Project Requirements Gathering', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 11:10:33'),
(1004, 1017, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: Agile project management tools</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=Agile+project+management+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/Agile+project+management+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/Agile+project+management+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/Agile+project+management+tools/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=Agile+project+management+tools\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3>\n<p>Today you\'ll create a project plan for a web development project using Agile methods. This skill helps teams build better software faster by breaking work into manageable pieces and adapting to changes quickly.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n\n<h4>Agile Methodologies</h4>\n<p>Agile is like a flexible roadmap for software projects. Instead of planning everything upfront, teams work in short cycles (called sprints) and adjust as they learn what works best.</p>\n\n<h4>User Story Estimation</h4>\n<p>User stories describe features from the user\'s perspective. We estimate their complexity using \"story points\" like 1 (easy), 3 (medium), or 5 (hard). It\'s like guessing how many Lego blocks you need to build something.</p>\n\n<h4>Velocity Measurement</h4>\n<p>Velocity shows how much work a team can complete in a sprint. If your team finishes 15 story points in one week, your velocity is 15. It helps predict how long projects will take.</p>\n\n<h4>Kanban Board Management</h4>\n<p>A Kanban board tracks work visually with columns like \"To Do,\" \"In Progress,\" and \"Done.\" It\'s like a to-do list that shows everyone\'s current tasks at a glance.</p>\n\n<h4>Prioritization Techniques</h4>\n<p>Teams use methods like MoSCoW (Must have, Should have, Could have, Won\'t have) to decide which features are most important. It\'s like choosing which groceries to buy when you have limited space in your cart.</p>\n\n<h4>Project Planning and Tracking</h4>\n<p>This means creating a schedule and monitoring progress. You\'ll plan which features to build each week and check if you\'re on track to meet deadlines.</p>\n\n<h4>Agile Project Management Tools</h4>\n<p>Tools like Jira, Trello, or Asana help manage Agile projects. They let teams create user stories, track velocity, and update Kanban boards all in one place.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Create user stories</strong> for a simple website project:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;As a visitor, I want to see a homepage <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">with</span> our logo&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;As a user, I want to create an account&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;As an admin, I want to view user reports&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>Estimate each story with story points (1-5).</p>\n  </li>\n  \n  <li><strong>Calculate team velocity</strong>:\n    <p>If your team completed 10 story points last week and 12 the week before, your average velocity is (10+12)/2 = 11 story points per week.</p>\n  </li>\n  \n  <li><strong>Build a Kanban board</strong> using HTML:\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'board\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'column\'</span>&gt;\n    &lt;h4&gt;To Do&lt;/h4&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Design homepage&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'column\'</span>&gt;\n    &lt;h4&gt;In Progress&lt;/h4&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Create login form&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'column\'</span>&gt;\n    &lt;h4&gt;Done&lt;/h4&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Add logo&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>This simple board shows your team\'s current work status.</p>\n  </li>\n  \n  <li><strong>Apply MoSCoW prioritization</strong> to your user stories:\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Must have:</em> Homepage and login</li>\n      <li><em>Should have:</em> User profile page</li>\n      <li><em>Could have:</em> Social media links</li>\n      <li><em>Won\'t have:</em> Chat feature</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  \n  <li><strong>Plan your first sprint</strong> using your velocity and priorities:\n    <p>If your velocity is 11 and \"Must have\" stories total 8 points, you can also include 3 \"Should have\" points in this sprint.</p>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit these three items:\n<ol>\n  <li>A text document with your user stories and story points</li>\n  <li>Your HTML Kanban board code</li>\n  <li>A short summary of your sprint plan (what you\'ll build in the next 2 weeks)</li>\n</ol>\n</p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Using exact hours for estimation instead of relative story points</li>\n  <li>Forgetting to update your Kanban board as tasks complete</li>\n  <li>Trying to include too many low-priority items in a sprint</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use free tools like <a href=\'https://trello.com\' target=\'_blank\'>Trello</a> or <a href=\'https://www.clickup.com\' target=\'_blank\'>ClickUp</a> for real Kanban boards</li>\n  <li>Hold 15-minute daily stand-ups to update your team on progress</li>\n  <li>Re-estimate stories during sprint planning if team members change</li>\n</ul>', 'Agile Project Planning and Estimation', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(1005, 1018, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: git branching diagram</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=git+branching+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/git+branching+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/git+branching+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/git+branching+diagram/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=git+branching+diagram\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you\'ll learn to use Git branches to work on multiple features without breaking your main code. This skill is essential for real-world teamwork where developers work on different tasks simultaneously.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>1. Git Branch</h4><p>A branch is like a separate room in a library where you can work on changes without affecting others. The <code>main</code> branch is the main room everyone uses.</p>\n\n<h4>2. Merging</h4><p>Merging combines changes from two branches. Think of it as combining two sets of notes into one document.</p>\n\n<h4>3. Conflict</h4><p>A conflict happens when two people edit the same part of a file. Git asks you to choose which changes to keep, like deciding who wins a board game.</p>\n\n<h4>4. Branching Strategies</h4><p>Plans for how teams use branches. Common ones include Git Flow (for big projects) and Feature Branches (for small tasks).</p>\n\n<h4>5. Best Practices</h4><p>Rules like keeping branches short-lived, using descriptive names (<code>feature-login</code>), and always pulling latest changes before merging.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new Git repository:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">git init my-project\n</code></pre>\n<p>This makes a new folder with Git version control enabled.</p></li>\n\n<li>Add a <code>README.md</code> file and commit:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">echo</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Hello World\'</span> > README.md\ngit add README.md\ngit commit -m <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Initial commit\'</span></code></pre></li>\n\n<li>Create a feature branch:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">git branch feature-<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>\ngit checkout feature-<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span></code></pre>\n<p>This creates a new branch and switches to it. You\'re now in the \"feature-1\" room.</p></li>\n\n<li>Edit <code>README.md</code> and commit:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">echo</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Added feature 1\'</span> >> README.md\ngit add README.md\ngit commit -m <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Add feature 1\'</span></code></pre></li>\n\n<li>Return to main branch:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">git checkout main</code></pre></li>\n\n<li>Create another branch and add a different feature:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">git branch feature-<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>\ngit checkout feature-<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">echo</span> <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Added feature 2\'</span> >> README.md\ngit add README.md\ngit commit -m <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Add feature 2\'</span></code></pre></li>\n\n<li>Merge both branches into main:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">git checkout main\ngit merge feature-<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>\ngit merge feature-<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span></code></pre>\n<p>This combines all changes into the main branch.</p></li>\n\n<li>Create a conflict:\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">git checkout main\ngit branch conflict-test\ngit checkout conflict-test\n</code></pre>\n<p>Edit the same line in <code>README.md</code> in both branches and try to merge. Git will show conflict markers like:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; HEAD\nThis line was <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> main\n=======\nThis line was <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">in</span> the other branch\n&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; conflict-test</code></pre>\n<p>Edit the file to choose which changes to keep, then run:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">git add README.md\ngit commit -m <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Resolved conflict\'</span></code></pre></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit: 1) Your Git repository as a ZIP file. 2) A text file showing how you resolved conflicts in step 8.</p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Forgetting to <code>git pull</code> before merging - this causes more conflicts</li>\n<li>Using vague branch names like <code>branch1</code> instead of <code>fix-bug-42</code></li>\n<li>Merging without checking <code>git status</code> to see what\'s changed</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><blockquote><strong>Use <code>git branch -v</code> to see what each branch contains</strong></blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>Rebase instead of merge for cleaner history: <code>git pull --rebase</code></blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>Always write <code>git status</code> before doing anything else to see your current state</blockquote></li>\n</ul>', 'Version Control with Git - Branching and Merging', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(1006, 1019, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312f9bac01b_1781608347.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\"><br>\n</div></div><h3>Intro</h3><p>Today you will build a responsive web layout using modern front-end tools. You’ll learn to create designs that adapt to any screen size, use CSS preprocessors for cleaner code, add JavaScript libraries for interactivity, and optimize performance. These skills are essential for building fast, user-friendly websites.</p>\n\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n\n<h4>Responsive Design</h4>\n<p>Responsive design means your website changes its layout based on the screen size (like a coat that adjusts to fit different body types). This uses <strong>media queries</strong> in CSS to apply different styles for mobile, tablet, and desktop views.</p>\n\n<h4>CSS Preprocessors</h4>\n<p>CSS preprocessors like <strong>Sass</strong> or <strong>Less</strong> let you write advanced CSS with features like variables, nested rules, and mixins (think of it like a recipe book that simplifies cooking). You write code in .scss files, then compile it into regular CSS for browsers.</p>\n\n<h4>JavaScript Library Integration</h4>\n<p>A JavaScript library (like jQuery or Lodash) is a pre-written collection of code that makes common tasks easier (like a toolbox with ready-made tools). You’ll add one to your project to handle interactions like button clicks or animations.</p>\n\n<h4>Front-end Performance Optimization</h4>\n<p>Optimization means making your website load faster. Techniques include minifying CSS/JS files (removing extra spaces and comments), compressing images, and reducing HTTP requests. A faster site improves user experience and search engine rankings.</p>\n\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>\n    <p><strong>Create a project folder</strong> named <code>responsive-portfolio</code>. Inside it, make these files: <code>index.html</code>, <code>styles.scss</code>, <code>script.js</code>, and a <code>images/</code> folder.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p><strong>Write basic HTML</strong> in <code>index.html</code>. Add a navigation bar, hero section, and project grid. Example:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;header&gt;\n  &lt;nav&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Home&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Projects&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/nav&gt;\n&lt;/header&gt;\n\n&lt;section <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'hero\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to My Portfolio&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;/section&gt;\n\n&lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'projects\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'project\'</span>&gt;Project <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">class</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'project\'</span>&gt;Project <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">2</span>&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>This creates a simple structure with sections for navigation, a hero headline, and project cards.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p><strong>Make it responsive</strong> in <code>styles.scss</code>. Use media queries to adjust layout for small screens:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">$breakpoint-mobile: 600px;\n\n.projects {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">3</span>, 1fr);\n  gap: 20px;\n}\n\n@media (max-width: $breakpoint-mobile) {\n  .projects {\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr;\n  }\n\n  nav ul {\n    flex-direction: column;\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n    <p>This uses a CSS preprocessor variable (<code>$breakpoint-mobile</code>) and changes the layout to a single column on small screens.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p><strong>Add a JavaScript library</strong>. In <code>index.html</code>, include jQuery from a CDN:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;script src=\'https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js\'&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</span>\n&lt;script src=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'script.js\'</span>&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>In <code>script.js</code>, add this code to animate a button click:</p>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">$(document).ready(<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span>() {\n  $(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'.project\'</span>).click(<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span>() {\n    $(<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">this</span>).fadeOut(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">300</span>).fadeIn(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">300</span>);\n  });\n});</code></pre>\n    <p>This makes each project card fade in and out when clicked using jQuery.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p><strong>Optimize performance</strong>:\n    </p><ol>\n      <li>Use <code>gulp</code> or <code>webpack</code> to minify <code>styles.scss</code> into <code>styles.min.css</code>.</li>\n      <li>Compress images with <code>tinypng.com</code> or an image optimizer tool.</li>\n      <li>Add <code>&lt;picture&gt;</code> elements for responsive images:</li>\n    </ol>\n    <pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;picture&gt;\n  &lt;source media=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'(min-width: 600px)\'</span> srcset=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'images/large.jpg\'</span>&gt;\n  &lt;img src=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'images/small.jpg\'</span> alt=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'Project image\'</span>&gt;\n&lt;/picture&gt;</code></pre>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <p><strong>Test your work</strong>:\n    </p><ul>\n      <li>Open <code>index.html</code> in a browser.</li>\n      <li>Resize the window to see responsive changes.</li>\n      <li>Click project cards to test the jQuery animation.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit a ZIP file named <code>day85-portfolio.zip</code> containing:\n</p><ul>\n  <li>All project files (<code>index.html</code>, <code>styles.min.css</code>, <code>script.js</code>)</li>\n  <li>A <code>README.md</code> explaining your responsive design choices</li>\n  <li>Screenshots of your layout on mobile and desktop views</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Forgetting to compile Sass files into regular CSS before testing</li>\n  <li>Not using a mobile-first approach in media queries (start with small-screen styles first)</li>\n  <li>Adding too many JavaScript libraries, which slows down performance</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use browser developer tools (<strong>Ctrl+Shift+I</strong> or <strong>Cmd+Option+I</strong>) to test responsive designs</li>\n  <li>Start with a mobile layout, then add larger-screen styles</li>\n  <li>Use CDNs for libraries like jQuery to reduce file size and load time</li>\n</ul>', 'Front-end Implementation', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 11:12:46'),
(1007, 1020, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: express js node js postgresql</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=express+js+node+js+postgresql\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/express+js+node+js+postgresql\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/express+js+node+js+postgresql/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/express+js+node+js+postgresql/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=express+js+node+js+postgresql\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Back-end Implementation</h3>\n<p>Today, you will set up a server-side environment, implement RESTful API endpoints, integrate a database with your back-end, and learn how to handle errors and exceptions. This is a crucial step in building a robust and scalable web application.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need:</h3>\n<p>To complete this task, you need to understand the following concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Server-side environment:</strong> This refers to the software and tools used to run your server-side code. In this case, we will use Node.js.</li>\n  <li><strong>RESTful API endpoints:</strong> These are URLs that your server responds to, providing data or performing actions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Database integration:</strong> This involves connecting your database to your server-side code, allowing you to store and retrieve data.</li>\n  <li><strong>Handling errors and exceptions:</strong> This is the process of catching and handling unexpected errors that may occur during execution.</li>\n  <li><strong>Node.js:</strong> This is a JavaScript runtime environment that allows you to run JavaScript on the server-side.</li>\n  <li><strong>Express.js:</strong> This is a popular Node.js framework for building web applications.</li>\n  <li><strong>PostgreSQL:</strong> This is a popular open-source database management system.</li>\n  <li><strong>RESTful API:</strong> This is an architectural style for designing networked applications.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Step-by-step task:</h3>\n<p>Here are the steps to complete this task:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Install Node.js and npm:</strong> Go to the Node.js website and download the latest version of Node.js and npm (the package manager for Node.js). Follow the installation instructions to install Node.js and npm on your system.</li>\n  <li><strong>Create a new Express.js project:</strong> Open a terminal or command prompt and run the following command to create a new Express.js project:</pre><code>express my-app</code></pre>\n    <p>This will create a new directory called <code>my-app</code> with the basic structure for an Express.js project.</p>\n  <li><strong>Install required packages:</strong> Run the following command to install the required packages:</pre><code>cd my-app\nnpm install express pg</code></pre>\n    <p>This will install the Express.js and PostgreSQL packages.</p>\n  <li><strong>Set up PostgreSQL:</strong> Go to the PostgreSQL website and download the latest version of PostgreSQL. Follow the installation instructions to install PostgreSQL on your system.</li>\n  <li><strong>Create a new PostgreSQL database:</strong> Open a terminal or command prompt and run the following command to create a new PostgreSQL database:</pre><code>createdb my-app</code></pre>\n    <p>This will create a new database called <code>my-app</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Connect to the database:</strong> Open the <code>app.js</code> file and add the following code to connect to the database:</pre><code>\nconst { Pool } = require(\'pg\');\nconst pool = new Pool({\n  user: \'your_username\',\n  host: \'localhost\',\n  database: \'my-app\',\n  password: \'your_password\',\n  port: 5432,\n});\n</code></pre>\n    <p>Replace <code>your_username</code>, <code>your_password</code>, and <code>my-app</code> with your actual PostgreSQL username, password, and database name.</p>\n  <li><strong>Create a RESTful API endpoint:</strong> Open the <code>app.js</code> file and add the following code to create a RESTful API endpoint:</pre><code>\nconst express = require(\'express\');\nconst app = express();\napp.get(\'/users\', (req, res) => {\n  pool.query(\'SELECT * FROM users\', (err, results) => {\n    if (err) {\n      res.status(500).send({ message: \'Error fetching users\' });\n    } else {\n      res.send(results.rows);\n    }\n  });\n});\n</code></pre>\n    <p>This will create a new RESTful API endpoint at <code>/users</code> that returns a list of users from the database.</p>\n  <li><strong>Handle errors and exceptions:</strong> Open the <code>app.js</code> file and add the following code to handle errors and exceptions:</pre><code>\napp.use((err, req, res, next) => {\n  console.error(err);\n  res.status(500).send({ message: \'Internal Server Error\' });\n});\n</code></pre>\n    <p>This will catch any unexpected errors and return a 500 Internal Server Error response.</p>\n  <li><strong>Start the server:</strong> Run the following command to start the server:</pre><code>node app.js</code></pre>\n    <p>This will start the server and make it available at <code>http://localhost:3000</code>.</p>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable:</h3>\n<p>Your deliverable for this task is a working Express.js application with a RESTful API endpoint that returns a list of users from a PostgreSQL database. You should also include a README file that explains how to run the application and access the API endpoint.</p>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid:</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Forgetting to install required packages:</strong> Make sure to install all required packages before running the application.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not setting up PostgreSQL correctly:</strong> Make sure to install and set up PostgreSQL correctly before connecting to the database.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not handling errors and exceptions correctly:</strong> Make sure to handle errors and exceptions correctly to prevent unexpected behavior.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips:</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Use a linter to catch errors:</strong> Use a linter like ESLint to catch errors and improve code quality.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use a debugger to debug code:</strong> Use a debugger like Node.js Inspector to debug code and identify issues.</li>\n  <li><strong>Test code thoroughly:</strong> Test code thoroughly to ensure it works correctly and handles errors and exceptions properly.</li>\n</ul>', 'Back-end Implementation', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45');
INSERT INTO `internship_content_blocks` (`id`, `lesson_id`, `type`, `content`, `title`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(1008, 1021, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: debugging tools and techniques</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=debugging+tools+and+techniques\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/debugging+tools+and+techniques\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/debugging+tools+and+techniques/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/debugging+tools+and+techniques/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=debugging+tools+and+techniques\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Testing and Debugging</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ll learn how to test and debug your code to ensure it\'s working as expected. This is a crucial skill for any web developer, as it helps you identify and fix errors before they become major issues. On a real job, you\'ll be working on complex projects with multiple developers, and testing and debugging will be an essential part of your workflow.</p>\n<h3>Concepts you need</h3>\n<h4>Unit Testing</h4>\n<p>Unit testing is a way to test individual components of your code in isolation. Think of it like testing a single LEGO brick to make sure it\'s working correctly before building a whole castle. You can write unit tests to verify that a specific function or method is behaving as expected.</p>\n<h4>Integration Testing</h4>\n<p>Integration testing is a way to test how different components of your code work together. It\'s like building a whole castle with LEGO bricks and making sure it stands up. You can write integration tests to verify that multiple functions or methods are working together correctly.</p>\n<h4>Debugging Tools and Techniques</h4>\n<p>Debugging tools and techniques are used to identify and fix errors in your code. Think of it like using a magnifying glass to examine a tiny LEGO brick and figure out why it\'s not fitting together correctly. You can use tools like console.log() to print out the values of variables, or use a debugger to step through your code line by line.</p>\n<h4>Identifying and Fixing Common Errors</h4>\n<p>Identifying and fixing common errors is an essential part of debugging. It\'s like knowing the most common reasons why LEGO bricks don\'t fit together correctly and being able to fix them quickly. You\'ll learn how to identify and fix common errors like syntax errors, runtime errors, and logical errors.</p>\n<h4>Testing for Security Vulnerabilities</h4>\n<p>Testing for security vulnerabilities is an important part of ensuring your code is secure. It\'s like making sure your LEGO castle is safe from invaders. You\'ll learn how to identify and fix common security vulnerabilities like SQL injection, cross-site scripting (XSS), and cross-site request forgery (CSRF).</p>\n<h3>Step-by-step task</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new JavaScript file called <code>calculator.js</code> and add the following code:</pre><code>\nfunction add(x, y) {\n  return x + y;\n}\n\nfunction subtract(x, y) {\n  return x - y;\n}\n\nfunction multiply(x, y) {\n  return x * y;\n}\n\nfunction divide(x, y) {\n  if (y === 0) {\n    throw new Error(\"Cannot divide by zero!\");\n  }\n  return x / y;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code defines four functions for basic arithmetic operations: addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.</p>\n<li>Create a new test file called <code>calculator.test.js</code> and add the following code:</pre><code>\nconst assert = require(\'assert\');\n\ndescribe(\'Calculator\', () => {\n  it(\'should add two numbers\', () => {\n    assert.strictEqual(add(2, 3), 5);\n  });\n\n  it(\'should subtract two numbers\', () => {\n    assert.strictEqual(subtract(5, 2), 3);\n  });\n\n  it(\'should multiply two numbers\', () => {\n    assert.strictEqual(multiply(4, 5), 20);\n  });\n\n  it(\'should divide two numbers\', () => {\n    assert.strictEqual(divide(10, 2), 5);\n  });\n\n  it(\'should throw an error when dividing by zero\', () => {\n    try {\n      divide(10, 0);\n      assert.fail(\'Expected an error to be thrown\');\n    } catch (error) {\n      assert.strictEqual(error.message, \'Cannot divide by zero!\');\n    }\n  });\n});\n</code></pre>\n<p>This code uses the Jest testing framework to write unit tests for the calculator functions. Each test case checks a specific behavior of the functions.</p>\n<li>Run the tests using the command <code>jest</code> and verify that all tests pass.</p>\n<li>Use a debugger to step through the calculator functions and verify that they are working correctly.</p>\n</ol>\n<h3>Deliverable</h3>\n<p>Submit your <code>calculator.js</code> file and your <code>calculator.test.js</code> file as a single zip archive. Make sure that all tests pass and that the calculator functions are working correctly.</p>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Don\'t forget to require the necessary modules in your test file.</li>\n<li>Don\'t use the same variable names in your test file as in your production code.</li>\n<li>Don\'t forget to handle errors and edge cases in your production code.</li>\n<li>Don\'t use console.log() in your production code.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pro tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use a code formatter like Prettier to keep your code consistent and readable.</li>\n<li>Use a linter like ESLint to catch syntax errors and enforce coding standards.</li>\n<li>Use a code coverage tool like Istanbul to measure the coverage of your tests.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Quiz</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>{\"question\":\"What is unit testing?\",\"options\":[\"Testing individual components of code\",\"Testing how different components of code work together\",\"Testing the entire application\",\"Testing a single function\"],\"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"Unit testing is a way to test individual components of your code in isolation.\"}</li>\n<li>{\"question\":\"What is integration testing?\",\"options\":[\"Testing individual components of code\",\"Testing how different components of code work together\",\"Testing the entire application\",\"Testing a single function\"],\"correct_index\":1,\"explanation\":\"Integration testing is a way to test how different components of your code work together.\"}</li>\n<li>{\"question\":\"What is debugging?\",\"options\":[\"Testing individual components of code\",\"Testing how different components of code work together\",\"Identifying and fixing errors in code\",\"Testing the entire application\"],\"correct_index\":2,\"explanation\":\"Debugging is the process of identifying and fixing errors in your code.\"}</li>\n<li>{\"question\":\"What is a common security vulnerability?\",\"options\":[\"SQL injection\",\"Cross-site scripting (XSS)\",\"Cross-site request forgery (CSRF)\",\"Buffer overflow\"],\"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"SQL injection is a common security vulnerability that occurs when user input is not properly sanitized.\"}</li>\n<li>{\"question\":\"What is a code formatter?\",\"options\":[\"A tool that formats code for human readability\",\"A tool that checks code for syntax errors\",\"A tool that measures code coverage\",\"A tool that identifies security vulnerabilities\"],\"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"A code formatter is a tool that formats code for human readability.\"}</li>\n<li>{\"question\":\"What is a linter?\",\"options\":[\"A tool that checks code for syntax errors\",\"A tool that measures code coverage\",\"A tool that identifies security vulnerabilities\",\"A tool that formats code for human readability\"],\"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"A linter is a tool that checks code for syntax errors and enforces coding standards.\"}</li>\n<li>{\"question\":\"What is code coverage?\",\"options\":[\"A measure of how much of your code is tested\",\"A measure of how much of your code is executed\",\"A measure of how much of your code is maintained\",\"A measure of how much of your code is refactored\"],\"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"Code coverage is a measure of how much of your code is tested.\"}</li>\n<li>{\"question\":\"What is a code coverage tool?\",\"options\":[\"A tool that measures code coverage\",\"A tool that identifies security vulnerabilities\",\"A tool that formats code for human readability\",\"A tool that checks code for syntax errors\"],\"correct_index\":0,\"explanation\":\"A code coverage tool is a tool that measures the coverage of your tests.\"}</li>\n<li>{\"question\":\"What is a debugger?\",\"options\":[\"A tool that formats code for human readability\",\"A tool that checks code for syntax errors\",\"A tool that measures code coverage\",\"A tool that allows you to step through your code line by line\"],\"correct_index\":3,\"explanation\":\"A debugger is a tool that allows you to step through your code line by line.\"}</li>\n<li>{\"question\":\"What is a security vulnerability?\",\"options\":[\"A bug in your code\",\"A security threat to your application\",\"A tool that formats code for human readability\",\"A tool that checks code for syntax errors\"],\"correct_index\":1,\"explanation\":\"A security vulnerability is a security threat to your application.\"}</li>\n<li>{\"question\":\"What is a testing framework?\",\"options\":[\"A tool that formats code for human readability\",\"A tool that checks code for syntax errors\",\"A tool that measures code coverage\",\"A tool that allows you to write and run tests for your code\"],\"correct_index\":3,\"explanation\":\"A testing framework is a tool that allows you to write and run tests for your code.\"}</li>\n</ul>\n', 'Testing and Debugging', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(1009, 1022, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a312ff805b45_1781608440.jpg\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Deployment and Maintenance</h3>\n<p>Today we\'re going to learn how to deploy our web application to a cloud platform, configure server and database settings, implement monitoring and logging, and plan for future updates and maintenance.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>Before we start, let\'s cover some key concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Cloud Platform:</strong> A cloud platform is a remote computing environment where you can host your web application. Think of it like renting a virtual office space instead of owning a physical one.</li>\n  <li><strong>Server Settings:</strong> Server settings control how your web application interacts with the server. This includes settings like port numbers, protocol versions, and authentication methods.</li>\n  <li><strong>Database Settings:</strong> Database settings control how your web application interacts with the database. This includes settings like database username, password, and connection string.</li>\n  <li><strong>Monitoring and Logging:</strong> Monitoring and logging help you understand how your web application is performing. This includes tracking errors, performance metrics, and user activity.</li>\n  <li><strong>CI/CD Pipeline:</strong> A CI/CD pipeline is a series of automated processes that help you build, test, and deploy your web application.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Choose a Cloud Platform:</strong> Let\'s use AWS as our cloud platform for this task. Go to the AWS website and sign up for a free account.</li>\n  <li><strong>Create a New Project:</strong> Create a new project in AWS using the AWS CLI. You can do this by running the following command in your terminal:<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">aws cloudformation create-stack <span style=\"color:#5c6370\">--stack-name my-project --template-body file://my-project.yaml</span></code></pre>This will create a new stack in AWS with the name \"my-project\".</li>\n  <li><strong>Configure Server Settings:</strong> Configure the server settings for your project by creating a new file called \"server.config\" in the root of your project. Add the following code to the file:<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">server {\n  port: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">8080</span>\n}\n</code></pre>This will set the port number for your server to 8080.</li>\n  <li><strong>Configure Database Settings:</strong> Configure the database settings for your project by creating a new file called \"database.config\" in the root of your project. Add the following code to the file:<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">database {\n  username: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'myuser\'</span>\n  password: <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'mypassword\'</span>\n  connection_string: \'mongodb:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//localhost:27017\'</span>\n}\n</code></pre>This will set the database username, password, and connection string for your project.</li>\n  <li><strong>Implement Monitoring and Logging:</strong> Implement monitoring and logging for your project by adding the following code to your server configuration file:<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> express = <span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">require</span>(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'express\'</span>);\n<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">const</span> app = express();\n\napp.use(express.logger());\napp.use(express.errorHandler());\n</code></pre>This will enable logging and error handling for your server.</li>\n  <li><strong>Plan for Future Updates and Maintenance:</strong> Plan for future updates and maintenance by creating a CI/CD pipeline for your project. You can do this by using a tool like Jenkins or CircleCI. Create a new pipeline that builds and deploys your project to AWS whenever you push new code to your repository.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>For this task, you should have the following deliverables:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A deployed web application on AWS.</li>\n  <li>A server configuration file called \"server.config\" in the root of your project.</li>\n  <li>A database configuration file called \"database.config\" in the root of your project.</li>\n  <li>A CI/CD pipeline set up for your project.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Don\'t forget to configure your server settings and database settings correctly. This can cause issues with your web application.</li>\n  <li>Make sure to implement monitoring and logging correctly. This can help you understand how your web application is performing.</li>\n  <li>Don\'t forget to plan for future updates and maintenance. This can help you avoid issues with your web application in the future.</li>\n  <li>Use a CI/CD pipeline to automate the build and deployment process for your project.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use environment variables to store sensitive information like database credentials.</li>\n  <li>Use a containerization tool like Docker to package your web application and its dependencies.</li>\n  <li>Use a monitoring and logging tool like Prometheus and Grafana to track performance metrics and user activity.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><br></h3>', 'Deployment and Maintenance', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 12:44:48'),
(1010, 1023, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">🖼️ Suggested Images — Ek choose karke topic image set karo:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:10px;\">Search: project review and optimization</p>\r\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;\">\r\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?tbm=isch&q=project+review+and+optimization\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#4285f4;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🔍 Google Images</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/s/photos/project+review+and+optimization\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#111;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🌄 Unsplash</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.pexels.com/search/project+review+and+optimization/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#05a081;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">📸 Pexels</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://pixabay.com/images/search/project+review+and+optimization/\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#2ec66e;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🎨 Pixabay</a>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.freepik.com/search?query=project+review+and+optimization\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background:#ff5a5a;color:#fff;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;\">🖌️ Freepik</a>\r\n</div></div><h3>Project Review and Optimization</h3>\n<p>In today\'s task, you\'ll learn how to review and optimize your project to make it more efficient, secure, and maintainable. This is an essential skill for any web developer, as it helps you identify areas for improvement and refine your code to meet the project\'s requirements.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>To complete this task, you\'ll need to understand the following concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Project requirements and scope</strong>: This refers to the specific goals and objectives of your project, as well as the tasks and features that are required to complete it.</li>\n<li><strong>Performance optimization</strong>: This involves making changes to your code to improve its speed and efficiency, reducing the load on your server and improving the user experience.</li>\n<li><strong>Security measures</strong>: This includes implementing best practices to protect your project from common web vulnerabilities, such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting (XSS).</li>\n<li><strong>Code refactoring</strong>: This involves reorganizing and simplifying your code to make it more maintainable, readable, and efficient.</li>\n<li><strong>Database query optimization</strong>: This involves improving the performance of your database queries to reduce the load on your server and improve the user experience.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-step task</h4>\n<p>Follow these steps to complete the task:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Review project requirements and scope</strong>: Go back to the project brief and review the requirements and scope. Make a list of the tasks and features that are required to complete the project.</li>\n<li><strong>Analyze performance and security</strong>: Use tools such as Google PageSpeed Insights and Web Vulnerability Scanner to analyze your project\'s performance and security. Identify areas for improvement and make a list of the issues you need to address.</li>\n<li><strong>Identify areas for improvement</strong>: Based on your analysis, identify areas of the project that need improvement. This could include optimizing code, improving database queries, or implementing security measures.</li>\n<li><strong>Optimize code and database queries</strong>: Use the following code example to optimize your code and database queries:</pre><code>\n// Before\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE name = \'John\';\n</code></pre><p>This query retrieves all columns from the users table where the name is \'John\'. However, this can be optimized to retrieve only the necessary columns:</pre><code>\n// After\nSELECT id, name, email FROM users WHERE name = \'John\';\n</code></pre><p>This optimized query retrieves only the id, name, and email columns from the users table where the name is \'John\', reducing the amount of data transferred and improving performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Implement security measures</strong>: Use the following code example to implement security measures:</pre><code>\n// Before\n$username = $_POST[\'username\'];\n$password = $_POST[\'password\'];\n$query = \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = \'$username\' AND password = \'$password\'\";\n</code></pre><p>This code is vulnerable to SQL injection attacks. To prevent this, use prepared statements:</pre><code>\n// After\n$username = $_POST[\'username\'];\n$password = $_POST[\'password\'];\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = :username AND password = :password\");\n$stmt->bindParam(\':username\', $username);\n$stmt->bindParam(\':password\', $password);\n$stmt->execute();\n</code></pre><p>This code uses prepared statements to prevent SQL injection attacks.</li>\n<li><strong>Refactor code</strong>: Use the following code example to refactor your code:</pre><code>\n// Before\nfunction get_user_data($username) {\n  $query = \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = \'$username\'\";\n  $result = $pdo->query($query);\n  return $result->fetch();\n}\n</code></pre><p>This code is not efficient and can be refactored to use a more efficient query:</pre><code>\n// After\nfunction get_user_data($username) {\n  $stmt = $pdo->prepare(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = :username\");\n  $stmt->bindParam(\':username\', $username);\n  $stmt->execute();\n  return $stmt->fetch();\n}\n</code></pre><p>This refactored code uses a more efficient query and is more maintainable.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>Submit the following deliverables:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A list of the tasks and features that are required to complete the project.</li>\n<li>A list of the areas for improvement and the issues you need to address.</li>\n<li>The optimized code and database queries.</li>\n<li>The implemented security measures.</li>\n<li>The refactored code.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Common mistakes to avoid</h4>\n<p>Avoid the following common mistakes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Not reviewing project requirements and scope</strong>: Failing to review the project brief and requirements can lead to missed deadlines and scope creep.</li>\n<li><strong>Not analyzing performance and security</strong>: Failing to analyze performance and security can lead to a poor user experience and security vulnerabilities.</li>\n<li><strong>Not optimizing code and database queries</strong>: Failing to optimize code and database queries can lead to slow performance and increased server load.</li>\n<li><strong>Not implementing security measures</strong>: Failing to implement security measures can lead to security vulnerabilities and data breaches.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro tips</h4>\n<p>Use the following pro tips to improve your skills:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use performance optimization tools</strong>: Use tools such as Google PageSpeed Insights and Web Vulnerability Scanner to analyze performance and security.</li>\n<li><strong>Use code refactoring techniques</strong>: Use techniques such as code splitting, code reorganization, and code simplification to improve code maintainability and efficiency.</li>\n<li><strong>Use database query optimization techniques</strong>: Use techniques such as indexing, caching, and query optimization to improve database query performance.</li>\n</ul>\n</code></pre>', 'Project Review and Optimization', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-14 16:23:45'),
(1011, 1024, 'text', '<div style=\"background:#f0f4ff;border:1px solid #c7d7ff;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 20px 0;\"><p style=\"font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:#3b5bdb;margin-bottom:8px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"/public/uploads/images/media_6a3130355aca8_1781608501.png\" alt=\"image\" style=\"color: rgb(17, 24, 39); font-size: 0.9rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 8px 0px;\"></p></div><h3>Final Project Deployment and Launch</h3>\n<p>Today, you\'ll deploy your final project to a production environment, set up analytics and monitoring tools, and launch it to the world. This is a critical step in any web development project, as it ensures your site is live, secure, and performing well.</p>\n<h4>Concepts you need</h4>\n<p>Before we begin, let\'s cover some essential concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Production Environment:</strong> This is the live version of your website, where users will interact with your application.</li>\n    <li><strong>Deployment:</strong> This process involves moving your code from a development environment to a production environment.</li>\n    <li><strong>Analytics Tools:</strong> These are software applications that help you track user behavior, website performance, and other key metrics.</li>\n    <li><strong>Monitoring Tools:</strong> These tools help you identify and resolve issues with your website in real-time.</li>\n    <li><strong>CDN (Content Delivery Network):</strong> This is a network of servers that distribute your website\'s content across different geographical locations, improving load times and reducing latency.</li>\n    <li><strong>SSL Certificate:</strong> This is a digital certificate that ensures your website is secure and trustworthy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Step-by-Step Task</h4>\n<ol>\n    <li><strong>Finalize Project Details and Settings:</strong> Review your project\'s settings, including the database connection, API keys, and other sensitive information. Ensure that all necessary configuration files are in place and up-to-date.</li>\n    <li><strong>Deploy to Production Environment:</strong> Use a deployment tool like <code>git push</code> or a CI/CD pipeline to move your code from the development environment to the production environment. Make sure to update the production environment\'s configuration files accordingly.</li>\n    <li><strong>Configure Analytics Tools:</strong> Set up analytics tools like Google Analytics or Matomo to track user behavior, website performance, and other key metrics. You\'ll need to add tracking codes to your website\'s HTML files.</li>\n    <li><strong>Configure Monitoring Tools:</strong> Set up monitoring tools like New Relic or Datadog to identify and resolve issues with your website in real-time. You\'ll need to add monitoring codes to your website\'s HTML files.</li>\n    <li><strong>Set up a CDN:</strong> Configure a CDN like Cloudflare or MaxCDN to distribute your website\'s content across different geographical locations. This will improve load times and reduce latency.</li>\n    <li><strong>Obtain an SSL Certificate:</strong> Purchase an SSL certificate from a reputable provider like Let\'s Encrypt or GlobalSign. This will ensure your website is secure and trustworthy.</li>\n    <li><strong>Launch and Promote the Project:</strong> Once everything is set up, launch your project to the world. Promote it on social media, forums, and other online platforms to reach a wider audience.</li>\n    <li><strong>Test and Debug:</strong> Test your website thoroughly to ensure it\'s working as expected. Identify and resolve any issues that arise during testing.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Deliverable</h4>\n<p>Today, you should:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>Deploy your final project to a production environment.</li>\n    <li>Set up analytics and monitoring tools.</li>\n    <li>Configure a CDN.</li>\n    <li>Obtain an SSL certificate.</li>\n    <li>Launch and promote the project.</li>\n    <li>Test and debug the website.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h4>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Forget to update production environment configuration files:</strong> Make sure to update the production environment\'s configuration files after deployment.</li>\n    <li><strong>Not testing thoroughly:</strong> Test your website thoroughly to ensure it\'s working as expected.</li>\n    <li><strong>Not configuring analytics and monitoring tools correctly:</strong> Make sure to add tracking codes and monitoring codes to your website\'s HTML files.</li>\n    <li><strong>Not obtaining an SSL certificate:</strong> This will make your website insecure and untrustworthy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pro Tips</h4>\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Use a CI/CD pipeline:</strong> This will automate the deployment process and ensure consistency across environments.</li>\n    <li><strong>Configure logging and error tracking:</strong> This will help you identify and resolve issues with your website in real-time.</li>\n    <li><strong>Use a version control system:</strong> This will help you manage changes to your codebase and collaborate with team members.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Code Examples</h4>\n<p>Here\'s an example of how to configure Google Analytics:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;a href=\'https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//www.google-analytics.com/analytics.js\'&gt;&lt;script&gt;</span>\n  (<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span>(i,s,o,g,r,a,m){i[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'GoogleAnalyticsObject\'</span>]=r;i[r]=i[r]||<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">function</span>(){\n  (i[r].q=i[r].q||[]).push(arguments)},i[r].l=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>*<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">new</span> Date();a=s.createElement(o),\n  m=s.getElementsByTagName(o)[<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">0</span>];a.<span style=\"color:#0033b3;font-weight:600\">async</span>=<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">1</span>;a.src=g;m.parentNode.insertBefore(a,m)\n  })(window,document,<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'script\'</span>,\'https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//www.google-analytics.com/analytics.js\',\'ga\');</span>\n  ga(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'create\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'UA-XXXXX-X\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'auto\'</span>);\n  ga(<span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'send\'</span>, <span style=\"color:#c41a16\">\'pageview\'</span>);\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/a&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>And here\'s an example of how to configure a CDN:</p>\n<pre style=\"background:#d7dbdb;color:#1a1a1a;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #b9bfbf\"><code style=\"background:transparent;color:#1a1a1a;font-family:inherit\">&lt;a href=\'https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/jquery@3.6.0/dist/jquery.min.js\'&gt;</span>\n  &lt;script src=\'https:<span style=\"color:#5c6370\">//cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/jquery@3.6.0/dist/jquery.min.js\'&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</span>\n&lt;/a&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Remember to replace the placeholder values with your actual values.</p>\n<h4><br></h4>', 'Final Project Deployment and Launch', 1, '2026-06-14 16:23:45', '2026-06-16 12:45:05');

